    {
      "id": "semantic-audit:ASTIS-RT-20260913-PBPSResolventDistributional",
      "kind": "semantic-audit",
      "label": "ASTIS-RT-20260913-PBPSResolventDistributional",
      "status": "fidelity-exact",
      "subtitle": "companion:pbps-weighted-resolvent-prerequisite · equivalent-after-elaboration",
      "summary": "Blind reconstruction and seven-slot semantic comparison for AutoSamplingTheory.TechnicalLemmas.FunctionalInequalities.WeightedResolvent.weak_resolvent_distributional.",
      "column": 7,
      "url": "lean-foundations.html?view=semantic",
      "source_url": "https://arxiv.org/html/2609.06905v1#A3.SS1",
      "original_theorem": "Explicit Euclidean analytic-route prerequisite, not the full cited source theorem: PBPS Appendix C.1 uses gradient closedness and conditional Poincare. The chosen weighted-Poisson route needs an actual weak solution before elliptic regularity can be invoked. For a C1 potential W on finite-dimensional Euclidean space, with integrable exp(-W), fix a closable operator whose original graph is exactly the smooth compact gradient pairs in normalized Gibbs L2. For every epsilon>0 and scalar L2 forcing f, its same closed gradient has a weak resolvent u, with the all-domain equation epsilon<u,v>+<Du,Dv>=<f,v>. Its representatives u and G=Du have local volume integrability and the ordinary weak gradient identity against C1 compact tests, with both test products integrable. Against every smooth compact psi, all three volume products exp(-W)u psi, exp(-W)<G,gradient psi>, exp(-W)f psi are integrable and epsilon integral exp(-W)u psi + integral exp(-W)<G,gradient psi> = integral exp(-W)f psi. This is a distributional divergence-form equation only. Background Kolesnikov-Milman1310.2526v7 sections2.4-2.5 treats weighted Poisson/Sobolev theory on compact manifolds; no classical/H2 regularity or spectral theorem is transferred automatically, and no separately chosen conditional solution is definitionally identified.",
      "reconstructed_theorem": "For every finite-dimensional real inner-product space E, equipped with its Borel measurable structure and volume measure dx, and every continuously Fréchet differentiable W:E→ℝ satisfying ∫E exp(−W(x)) dx<∞, let Z=∫E exp(−W(x)) dx and let μ be the normalized tilted measure dμ=Z⁻¹exp(−W(x))dx. For every real-linear partial operator D from L²(μ;ℝ) to L²(μ;E), assume D is closable and assume that, for every a∈L²(μ;ℝ) and H∈L²(μ;E), (a,H) belongs to its graph if and only if there exists a smooth compactly supported φ:E→ℝ with a=φ μ-almost everywhere and H=∇φ μ-almost everywhere. Write D̄ for the partial operator whose graph is the topological closure of the graph of D. Then, for every ε>0 and every f∈L²(μ;ℝ), there exists u∈dom(D̄) such that, for every v∈dom(D̄), ε⟨u,v⟩L²(μ;ℝ)+⟨D̄u,D̄v⟩L²(μ;E)=⟨f,v⟩L²(μ;ℝ). Let U:E→ℝ, G:E→E, and F:E→ℝ denote the measurable representative functions selected by the Lp coercions of u, D̄u, and f, respectively; the original Lp elements u, D̄u, and f are equivalence classes modulo μ-almost-everywhere equality, whereas U, G, and F are functions representing those classes. The same u additionally satisfies: (1) U and G are locally integrable with respect to dx, meaning integrable on a neighborhood of each point; (2) for every continuously Fréchet differentiable compactly supported ψ:E→ℝ and every direction h∈E, the scalar functions x↦ψ(x)⟨G(x),h⟩E and x↦U(x)dψ(x)[h] are dx-integrable, and ∫E ψ(x)⟨G(x),h⟩E dx=−∫E U(x)dψ(x)[h] dx; (3) for every smooth compactly supported ψ:E→ℝ, each of x↦exp(−W(x))U(x)ψ(x), x↦exp(−W(x))⟨G(x),∇ψ(x)⟩E, and x↦exp(−W(x))F(x)ψ(x) is dx-integrable, and ε∫E exp(−W(x))U(x)ψ(x) dx+∫E exp(−W(x))⟨G(x),∇ψ(x)⟩E dx=∫E exp(−W(x))F(x)ψ(x) dx. All displayed pointwise gradients represent the Fréchet derivative through the inner product on E. The directional identity identifies G as the distributional gradient of U with respect to unweighted volume. The theorem asserts existence with these variational and distributional properties, not an explicit uniqueness conclusion, additional classical differentiability of U, a pointwise differential equation, or quantitative estimates. Closability and the exact graph description are hypotheses, not conclusions.",
      "fidelity_verdict": "equivalent-after-elaboration",
      "blindness": "source hidden from decoder",
