      "subtitle": "companion:pbps-distributional-gradient-prerequisite · equivalent-after-elaboration",
      "summary": "Blind reconstruction and seven-slot semantic comparison for AutoSamplingTheory.TechnicalLemmas.FunctionalInequalities.WeightedGradientDistribution.closed_gradient_distributional.",
      "column": 7,
      "url": "lean-foundations.html?view=semantic",
      "source_url": "https://arxiv.org/html/2609.06905v1#A3.SS1",
      "original_theorem": "Faithful analytic-route paraphrase, not the full cited theorem: the PBPS Appendix C.1 argument uses conditional Gibbs Sobolev gradients, closedness and conditional Poincare. To apply a full-space weak-resolvent PDE route, the completed weighted smooth gradient must agree with an ordinary distributional gradient. For a strictly positive C1 Gibbs density exp(-W) with finite normalization, pairs (u,G) in the completed smooth compact gradient graph have locally volume-integrable representatives and satisfy integral psi inner(G,v) dx = -integral u D_v psi dx for compact C1 tests and constant v, with meaningful integrable test products. This follows by inserting exp(W)psi in the weighted identity and removing the weight locally. Kolesnikov-Milman1310.2526v7§2.5 supplies weighted Sobolev background, not an automatic transfer of compact-manifold regularity or spectral conclusions to Euclidean full space.",
      "reconstructed_theorem": "For every type E equipped with a normed additive commutative group structure, a real inner-product-space structure, finite dimensionality over ℝ, and a measurable-space structure whose measurable sets are the Borel sets, and for every function W : E → ℝ, assume that W is continuously Fréchet differentiable and that x ↦ exp(-W(x)) is integrable with respect to volume on E. Let μ be volume tilted by the potential -W, namely the measure obtained by normalizing the density exp(-W) relative to volume. For every partial ℝ-linear map D from L²(μ; ℝ) to L²(μ; E), assume that D is closable and that, for every u ∈ L²(μ; ℝ) and G ∈ L²(μ; E), (u,G) belongs to the graph of D if and only if there exists a smooth function f : E → ℝ having compact support such that a chosen measurable representative of u equals f μ-almost everywhere and a chosen measurable representative of G equals ∇f μ-almost everywhere. Then, for every u ∈ L²(μ; ℝ) and G ∈ L²(μ; E) whose pair belongs to the graph of the closure of D, their chosen measurable representatives, denoted ũ : E → ℝ and G̃ : E → E, are both locally integrable with respect to volume. Furthermore, for every continuously Fréchet differentiable function ψ : E → ℝ having compact support and every vector v ∈ E, both functions x ↦ ψ(x)⟨G̃(x),v⟩ℝ and x ↦ ũ(x)(dψ(x))[v] are integrable with respect to volume, and ∫_E ψ(x)⟨G̃(x),v⟩ℝ dvol(x) = -∫_E ũ(x)(dψ(x))[v] dvol(x). Here dψ(x) is the real Fréchet derivative of ψ at x. This conclusion is only a forward implication from membership in the closed graph to the stated local integrability and distributional-gradient identity; no converse characterization of that graph is asserted.",
