      "search": "semantic-audit:astis-rt-20260913-pbpsgradientdistributional semantic-audit astis-rt-20260913-pbpsgradientdistributional companion:pbps-distributional-gradient-prerequisite · equivalent-after-elaboration blind reconstruction and seven-slot semantic comparison for autosamplingtheory.technicallemmas.functionalinequalities.weightedgradientdistribution.closed_gradient_distributional. equivalent-after-elaboration source hidden from decoder faithful analytic-route paraphrase, not the full cited theorem: the pbps appendix c.1 argument uses conditional gibbs sobolev gradients, closedness and conditional poincare. to apply a full-space weak-resolvent pde route, the completed weighted smooth gradient must agree with an ordinary distributional gradient. for a strictly positive c1 gibbs density exp(-w) with finite normalization, pairs (u,g) in the completed smooth compact gradient graph have locally volume-integrable representatives and satisfy integral psi inner(g,v) dx = -integral u d_v psi dx for compact c1 tests and constant v, with meaningful integrable test products. this follows by inserting exp(w)psi in the weighted identity and removing the weight locally. kolesnikov-milman1310.2526v7§2.5 supplies weighted sobolev background, not an automatic transfer of compact-manifold regularity or spectral conclusions to euclidean full space. for every type e equipped with a normed additive commutative group structure, a real inner-product-space structure, finite dimensionality over ℝ, and a measurable-space structure whose measurable sets are the borel sets, and for every function w : e → ℝ, assume that w is continuously fréchet differentiable and that x ↦ exp(-w(x)) is integrable with respect to volume on e. let μ be volume tilted by the potential -w, namely the measure obtained by normalizing the density exp(-w) relative to volume. for every partial ℝ-linear map d from l²(μ; ℝ) to l²(μ; e), assume that d is closable and that, for every u ∈ l²(μ; ℝ) and g ∈ l²(μ; e), (u,g) belongs to the graph of d if and only if there exists a smooth function f : e → ℝ having compact support such that a chosen measurable representative of u equals f μ-almost everywhere and a chosen measurable representative of g equals ∇f μ-almost everywhere. then, for every u ∈ l²(μ; ℝ) and g ∈ l²(μ; e) whose pair belongs to the graph of the closure of d, their chosen measurable representatives, denoted ũ : e → ℝ and g̃ : e → e, are both locally integrable with respect to volume. furthermore, for every continuously fréchet differentiable function ψ : e → ℝ having compact support and every vector v ∈ e, both functions x ↦ ψ(x)⟨g̃(x),v⟩ℝ and x ↦ ũ(x)(dψ(x))[v] are integrable with respect to volume, and ∫_e ψ(x)⟨g̃(x),v⟩ℝ dvol(x) = -∫_e ũ(x)(dψ(x))[v] dvol(x). here dψ(x) is the real fréchet derivative of ψ at x. this conclusion is only a forward implication from membership in the closed graph to the stated local integrability and distributional-gradient identity; no converse characterization of that graph is asserted. accepted appendix c.1 analytic prerequisite; weighted sobolev background1310.2526v7§2.5 autosamplingtheory.technicallemmas.functionalinequalities.weightedgradientdistribution.closed_gradient_distributional autosamplingtheory/technicallemmas/functionalinequalities/weightedgradientdistribution.lean true root anonymous_decoder_d 432e037d741bb19154f895cb29772ec93b173690c844fcaa2a1dd9f867bdea51 accepted distributional_source_review objects a strictly positive gibbs density exp(-w), its normalized weighted l2 spaces, and pairs (u,g) in the completed smooth compact gradient graph. normalized μ=volume.tilted(-w), scalar and vector l2 classes, and the same closable exact smooth compact gradient graph d and its closure. explicit-elaboration the graph biconditional identifies pairs represented by smooth compact (f,gradient f). the closure is that same d; the reconstruction preserves the normalized tilt and partial-linear-map interface. domains euclidean full space, scalar u and tests, vector g and constant test directions; local integrability and final integrals use ordinary volume. finite-dimensional real inner-product borel e, including dimension zero. l2 uses μ; final conclusions use volume. explicit-elaboration finitedimensional and innerproductspace provide coordinate-free euclidean space. the elaborated conclusions explicitly use volume, without boundary or compact ambient manifold. quantifiers every admissible potential and completed-graph pair, every compact c1 scalar test and constant vector. every e,w,d satisfying the graph conditions; every u,g in that closure; every c1 compact ψ and v:e. explicit-elaboration the explicit forall sequence preserves all tests and directions. none is existentially fixed and there is no unit-vector restriction. assumptions strictly positive c1 exp(-w), finite normalization, membership in completed weighted smooth compact gradient graph. c1 w, volume-integrable exp(-w), closable exact smooth compact graph d, and selected pair in its closure. explicit-elaboration positive c1 exponential density and c1 w are equivalent via minus log. positive normalization follows from integral_exp_pos. closability/graph identification elaborate completed-gradient wording, with no poincare, curvature, h2, classical differentiability of u or global volume integrability assumed. conclusion both representatives locally volume-integrable; both compact-test products integrable; integral ψ inner(g,v) = -integral u d_vψ. the same local integrabilities, two product integrabilities and signed volume identity for all tests. equivalent all four integrability conclusions and the negative sign are explicit. strict positive finite-normalized density makes μ and volume null sets coincide; selected representatives add no restriction. c1 compact tests include smooth distributional tests. scopes explicit euclidean analytic prerequisite, not full pbps or transferred compact-manifold regularity/spectral results. only forward closed-gradient membership to local integrability and ordinary weak-gradient identity. same the reconstruction denies the converse; module, lesson, statement and prerequisite binding exclude h2, resolvent pde, d*d operator core, poincare and complete paper. constant dependencies finite positive normalization, no quantitative estimate. z depends on w and volume; its inverse is canceled, no estimate constants. same ht rewrites the tilted integrals; mul_left_cancel₀ uses integral_exp_pos. no normalization, curvature, dimension, poincare or tail constant remains. assumptions informational explicit closable-operator and exact smooth-graph hypotheses elaborate the scoped source; the distributional identity is not assumed. d.isclosable and the graph biconditional are inputs; exp(w)ψ substitution and cancellation prove the desired identity. scopes informational the inverse-weight lemma is an authored prerequisite, not a quoted paper theorem. companion:pbps-distributional-gradient-prerequisite; attribution denies lemma b.1; binding role prerequisite.",
