      "summary": "Blind reconstruction and seven-slot semantic comparison for AutoSamplingTheory.ExampleCases.SmoothedPicardHMC.RecursiveVariance.variance_update_bounds.",
      "column": 7,
      "url": "lean-foundations.html?view=semantic",
      "source_url": "https://arxiv.org/html/2609.06906v1#S6.SS3",
      "original_theorem": "At a recursive stage let 0<η≤c₀<1/4. The well-conditioned branch κ_A<2 selects τ=c₀ and a=(η+c₀)/(1+A⁻¹), with A∈(0,∞]. The update (A⁺)⁻¹=A⁻¹+a⁻¹ has A⁺≤2c₀. For a finite previous A, it also satisfies A⁺≤ρ₀A, where ρ₀=2c₀/(1+2c₀)<1. These are the one-step parameter claims of Lemma 6.6(ii), not the later multi-stage algorithm theorem. ASTIS uses r=A⁻¹≥0 and proves A⁺>0 even at r=0. This selected scalar formula does not require c₀<1/4; that generalization is disclosed separately.",
      "reconstructed_theorem": "For every three real numbers r,h,c satisfying r≥0, h>0, and h≤c, define a=(h+c)/(1+r), r_p=r+a⁻¹, A_plus=r_p⁻¹, and ρ=2c/(1+2c). These are local abbreviations, not additional hypotheses. Then r_p>0, A_plus>0, A_plus≤2c, ρ>0, and ρ<1. In addition, if r>0, then A_plus≤ρ·r⁻¹. All variables, products, inequalities, reciprocals, and divisions are real. The reciprocal convention is totalized, with 0⁻¹=0. The stated assumptions give c>0 and make 1+r and 1+2c positive; they also make a positive, while the first conclusion gives positivity of r_p. Their reciprocals therefore have their ordinary nonzero-denominator meanings. The premise r≥0 allows r=0. At that boundary, all five unconditional conclusions still apply, but the final implication imposes no bound involving r⁻¹ because its separate premise r>0 is false. In particular, one must not extend A_plus≤ρ·r⁻¹ to r=0: its totalized right-hand side would be zero, whereas A_plus is strictly positive. No further iterative, asymptotic, or probabilistic conclusion is asserted.",
