      "url": "lean-foundations.html?view=semantic",
      "source_url": "https://arxiv.org/html/2609.06906v1#S6.SS2.SSS2",
      "original_theorem": "For a C² potential U on Euclidean space satisfying κ⁻¹I≤D²U≤I, use the positive condition-number convention κ>0. For A∈(0,∞] define U_A,u=U+||·−u||²/(2A). It is α_A=κ⁻¹+A⁻¹ strongly convex and β_A=1+A⁻¹ smooth; let K_A=β_A/α_A. For a>0 the normalized Gibbs law R^(U_A,u)_a,y equals R^U_A+,u+, where (A+)⁻¹=A⁻¹+a⁻¹ and u+=A+(u/A+y/a). Its condition-number bounds satisfy K_A+=(aβ_A+1)K_A/(aβ_A+K_A). ASTIS expands the omitted calculation by proving integrability, strictly positive normalization and actual probability laws, and uses precision r=A⁻¹ to include infinity. This is a mathematical restatement, not a quotation. The formal space is any finite-dimensional real inner-product Borel space, including zero dimension. No recursive algorithm, error or cost bound is included.",
      "reconstructed_theorem": "Let E be any finite-dimensional real inner-product space with its induced norm and Borel σ-algebra, and let λ be its canonical Lebesgue measure. For every U:E→ℝ and κ,a∈ℝ, assume κ>0, U is twice continuously Fréchet differentiable everywhere, and κ⁻¹‖v‖²≤D²U(x)[v,v]≤‖v‖² for every x,v∈E. For every nonnegative real r, if a>0, then for every u,y∈E define W(x)=U(x)+(r/2)‖x−u‖², α=κ⁻¹+r, β=1+r as a nonnegative real, K=β/α, r_p=r+a⁻¹, and w=r_p⁻¹(r u+a⁻¹ y). Real coercions of nonnegative parameters are understood. Write Q(x)=U(x)+(r_p/2)‖x−w‖², merely as an abbreviation for the displayed expression. All eight conclusions hold: (1) W is α-strongly convex on E: E is convex and W(tx+(1−t)z)≤tW(x)+(1−t)W(z)−(α/2)t(1−t)‖x−z‖² for every x,z∈E and t∈[0,1]. (2) Its canonical gradient is globally β-Lipschitz: ‖∇W(x)−∇W(z)‖≤β‖x−z‖ for every x,z∈E. (3) x↦exp(−W(x)) is integrable with respect to λ. (4) Z_W=∫_E exp(−W(x)) dλ(x) is strictly positive. (5) Tilt(λ,−W) has total mass one. (6) Tilt(λ,−Q) has total mass one. (7) The following equality of measures holds: Tilt(λ,x↦−(W(x)+(a⁻¹/2)‖x−y‖²))=Tilt(λ,−Q). (8) The exact scalar identity (β+a⁻¹)/(α+a⁻¹)=((aβ+1)K)/(aβ+K) holds. Here Tilt(λ,f)=λ.withDensity(x↦ENNReal.ofReal(exp(f(x))/∫_E exp(f) dλ)); all displayed laws are normalized relative to canonical Lebesgue measure, not an arbitrary reference measure. The normalizing real integral is totalized for nonintegrable exponential weights, so the asserted probability properties are conclusions, not implicit in the definition of Tilt. Integrability includes almost-everywhere strong measurability and finiteness of the norm integral. The expression D²U(x)[v,v] differentiates the continuous-linear-map-valued first derivative and evaluates the result on v twice; C² supplies genuine derivatives despite the derivative API's totalization where derivatives fail to exist. The gradient is the Riesz vector of the derivative, not a separately supplied field. Real reciprocal and division are totalized at zero; the stated positivity and nonnegativity conditions make the denominators in the scalar definitions and final identity nonzero. No r>0, κ≥1, positive-dimension, or separate probability-measure assumption is imposed. Equality (7) concerns normalized measures, not equality of the two potentials or their unnormalized weights. No optimality interpretation of K, algorithm, convergence rate, or sampling guarantee is concluded.",
      "fidelity_verdict": "equivalent-after-elaboration",
      "blindness": "source hidden from decoder",
