      "id": "semantic-audit:ASTIS-RT-20260910-MarkovKernelTVContraction",
      "kind": "semantic-audit",
      "label": "ASTIS-RT-20260910-MarkovKernelTVContraction",
      "status": "fidelity-exact",
      "subtitle": "companion:markov-tv-contraction · equivalent-after-elaboration",
      "summary": "Blind reconstruction and seven-slot semantic comparison for AutoSamplingTheory.TechnicalLemmas.Probability.KernelTotalVariation.abs_real_comp_sub_le.",
      "column": 7,
      "url": "lean-foundations.html?view=semantic",
      "source_url": "https://arxiv.org/html/2609.06906v1#S7.SS2",
      "original_theorem": "SPHMC v1 Section 7.2, proof of Theorem 1.3, passes the actual/proxy input TV error through the same proximal BPS Markov kernel. ASTIS expands only this data-processing step: on any two measurable spaces, if probability input laws μ and ν satisfy |μ(S)−ν(S)|≤δ for every measurable input event S, then for every Markov kernel K and every measurable output event T, |(μK)(T)−(νK)(T)|≤δ. In the paper the selected inputs are the implementable law and its comparison law, δ=ε/2, and K is K_{ε/2,1}. The generic measurable-space formulation and arbitrary real δ are an explicit generalization; the premise on the empty event implies δ≥0. This is an attributed faithful paraphrase with ASTIS's standard proof expansion, not copied prose. The construction of the paper's actual kernel, proxy warmness, its separate mixing error and actual-input expected query costs are not supplied by this declaration.",
      "reconstructed_theorem": "For any measurable spaces A and B with arbitrary σ-algebras, any probability measures μ and ν on A, any Markov kernel K from A to B, and any real number δ, suppose that |μ.real(S)−ν.real(S)| ≤ δ for every measurable set S ⊆ A. Then |(K∘ₘμ).real(T)−(K∘ₘν).real(T)| ≤ δ for every measurable set T ⊆ B. Here a Markov kernel assigns to every x ∈ A a probability measure K(x,·) on B, with x ↦ K(x,T) measurable for each measurable T. The measure K∘ₘρ on B is obtained by integrating the transition measures K(x,·) against ρ. The notation ρ.real(S) is the real value of the extended nonnegative event measure; the event measures in the displayed inequalities are finite under the stated probability and Markov assumptions. Absolute value and the inequalities are ordinary real operations. The same δ bounds every input event and every output event, without an additional factor. No separate sign condition on δ, topology, density, coupling, or further hypothesis is imposed.",
      "fidelity_verdict": "equivalent-after-elaboration",
      "blindness": "source hidden from decoder",
