    {
      "id": "semantic-audit:ASTIS-RT-20260910-QuadraticRegularization",
      "kind": "semantic-audit",
      "label": "ASTIS-RT-20260910-QuadraticRegularization",
      "status": "fidelity-exact",
      "subtitle": "companion:quadratic-regularization-curvature · equivalent-after-elaboration",
      "summary": "Blind reconstruction and seven-slot semantic comparison for AutoSamplingTheory.TechnicalLemmas.Analysis.QuadraticRegularization.strongConvexOn_and_lipschitzWith_gradient_add_quadratic.",
      "column": 7,
      "url": "lean-foundations.html?view=semantic",
      "source_url": "https://arxiv.org/html/2609.06906v1#S6.SS2.SSS2",
      "original_theorem": "SPHMC v1 Lemma 6.4 considers U_{A,u}(x)=U(x)+‖x−u‖²/(2A), where U is C² with κ^{-1}I≼∇²U(x)≼I and A∈(0,∞]. Its selected curvature/smoothness clause gives α_A=κ^{-1}+A^{-1} and β_A=1+A^{-1}. ASTIS expands precisely this clause: for a complete real inner-product space, a genuine C² potential U, and nonnegative real m,L,r with m‖v‖²≤D²U(x)[v,v]≤L‖v‖² everywhere, W(x)=U(x)+r‖x−u‖²/2 is (m+r)-strongly convex and its actual gradient is (L+r)-Lipschitz everywhere. The source specialization is m=κ^{-1}, L=1, r=A^{-1}; A=∞ is represented by r=0.\n\nThis is a faithful paraphrase and ASTIS's expanded proof, not copied prose. The complete-inner-product-space and nonnegative-constant generalization is confined to this analytic clause. PBPS v1 (2.9)-(2.10) has the same quadratic-potential mechanism with m=α,L=β,r=η^{-1}; the selected public conclusion is an analytic prerequisite/consequence of that setting, not a public Hessian-sandwich theorem or all of Proposition 2.1. The remaining RGO-law identification and parameter-ratio claims of Lemma 6.4, conditional laws, Poincare, covariance, sampler invariance, error and query cost are not asserted.",
      "reconstructed_theorem": "Let E be any complete real inner-product space with its induced norm. For every function U : E → ℝ and every three nonnegative real numbers m,L,r, assume that U is twice continuously Fréchet differentiable on all of E and that, for every x,v ∈ E,\n\nm ‖v‖² ≤ D²U(x)[v,v] ≤ L ‖v‖².\n\nThen, for every u ∈ E, define W : E → ℝ by W(x) = U(x) + (r/2)‖x−u‖². The following two conclusions hold simultaneously:\n\nFirst, W is (m+r)-strongly convex on E. Precisely, E is convex and, for every x,y ∈ E and all real a,b ≥ 0 with a+b = 1,\nW(a x+b y) ≤ a W(x)+b W(y) − ((m+r)/2) a b ‖x−y‖².\n\nSecond, the canonical gradient of W is globally Lipschitz with constant L+r: for every x,y ∈ E,\n‖∇W(x)−∇W(y)‖ ≤ (L+r)‖x−y‖.\n\nHere D²U(x)[v,v] is obtained by differentiating the continuous-linear-map-valued first derivative, applying the resulting derivative to v, and then applying the resulting linear functional to v again. The Fréchet derivative API is totalized to zero where differentiability fails, but the assumed C² regularity supplies genuine first and second derivatives of U. The canonical gradient ∇W(x) is the Riesz vector representing the totalized Fréchet derivative of W at x; its inner product with each direction v equals that derivative applied to v. Completeness supplies the inverse Riesz map, and no independently chosen gradient field is an input. The nonnegative parameters may be zero; their sums are coerced to ordinary real numbers in the strong-convexity and norm inequalities. Division by 2 is ordinary real division with nonzero denominator. The function W is the specified let-bound construction, not an extra assumption. No finite-dimensionality, measurable structure, or measure is assumed, and all bounds are global and pointwise.",
      "fidelity_verdict": "equivalent-after-elaboration",
      "blindness": "source hidden from decoder",
      "semantic_slots": [
        {
          "slot": "objects",
          "original": "The selected SPHMC clause concerns the actual potential U_A,u=U+norm(x-u)^2/(2A), its strong convexity and gradient smoothness. The selected expansion uses W=U+(r/2)norm(x-u)^2.",
          "reconstructed": "W is exactly the let-bound quadratic regularization. The second conclusion concerns Mathlib gradient W, the inverse-Riesz representative of DW, not an independently supplied field.",
          "relation": "explicit-elaboration",
          "evidence": "The pinned module proves W is C², computes its genuine derivatives, and proves HasFDerivAt (gradient W) using the fixed inverse Riesz map. The anonymous reconstruction identifies these same objects. SPHMC source: https://arxiv.org/html/2609.06906v1#S6.SS2.SSS2, Lemma 6.4."
        },
        {
          "slot": "domains",
          "original": "The papers use Euclidean finite-dimensional space. The selected extraction explicitly generalizes the deterministic clause to any complete real inner-product space.",
