      "status": "fidelity-exact",
      "subtitle": "pbps:v1:normalized-joint-density · equivalent-after-elaboration",
      "summary": "Blind reconstruction and seven-slot semantic comparison for AutoSamplingTheory.ExampleCases.ProximalBPS.GibbsAugmentation.normalized_augmentation_density.",
      "column": 7,
      "url": "lean-foundations.html?view=semantic",
      "source_url": "https://arxiv.org/html/2609.06905v1#S2.SS2",
      "original_theorem": "PBPS defines an augmented joint density proportional to exp(−V(x)−‖x−y‖²/(2η)) and generates it by independent X with the normalized Gibbs law of V and Z with standard Gaussian law, then Y=X+√η Z. Its source setting is V∈C²(R^d), 0<α≤β, αI≼∇²V≼βI everywhere, and 0<η≤1/β.\n\nSelected normalized-law certificate (ASTIS expanded mathematical restatement): write Z_V=∫exp(−V(x))dx and C_η=(2πη)^(−d/2). Then Z_V>0; the generated joint law is a probability measure; and it equals product volume with density (C_η/Z_V)exp(−V(x)−‖y−x‖²/(2η)). Integrability and normalization are derived, not supplied as assumptions. ASTIS proves this identity in finite-dimensional real inner-product Borel spaces, including dimension zero, from genuine C² regularity, a positive diagonal Hessian lower bound and any η>0. The unused upper Hessian and upper scale restrictions remain attached to the paper's other conclusions. This certificate does not identify conditional representatives, construct the process, prove invariance, give concentration or bound sampling error or query cost.",
      "reconstructed_theorem": "Let E be any finite-dimensional real inner-product space with its induced norm and Borel σ-algebra, and let λ be its canonical Lebesgue measure normalized by orthonormal coordinates. Put d = finrank_ℝ(E). For every V : E → ℝ and every α,η ∈ ℝ, assume α > 0, η > 0, V is twice continuously Fréchet differentiable everywhere, and\n\nα ‖v‖² ≤ D²V(x)[v,v] for every x,v ∈ E.\n\nDefine the following quantities, which are not additional assumptions:\nZ_V = ∫_E exp(−V(x)) dλ(x);\nμ = Tilt(λ,−V) = λ.withDensity(x ↦ ENNReal.ofReal(exp(−V(x))/Z_V));\nJ = (Φ_η)_*(μ ⊗ γ_E), where Φ_η(x,z) = (x, x + √η · z) and γ_E is the centered standard Gaussian probability measure with identity covariance.\n\nThen all three conclusions hold: (1) Z_V > 0; (2) J has total mass one; and (3), as measures on E × E,\n\nJ = (λ ⊗ λ).withDensity((x,y) ↦ ENNReal.ofReal(((((√(2πη))⁻¹)^d)/Z_V) · exp(−V(x) − ‖y−x‖²/(2η)))).\n\nThe reference measure in this final density is canonical Lebesgue measure in both coordinates. Pushforward assigns to a measurable set its preimage measure, and withDensity assigns the nonnegative integral of the displayed density over that set. The integral defining Z_V is a real totalized Bochner integral, equal to zero if the integrand is not integrable. Tilt uses exactly the displayed normalization and is zero if its exponential weight is not integrable. ENNReal.ofReal takes the nonnegative part of a real number and views it as an extended nonnegative real. Real inverse and division are totalized at zero; η > 0 and the concluded Z_V > 0 make the displayed denominators nonzero. The square root is the nonnegative real square root, π is the circle constant, and exponent d is natural-number power. D²V(x)[v,v] means differentiating the continuous-linear-map-valued first derivative, applying to v, then applying the resulting linear functional to v. Although the derivative API is totalized to zero where differentiability fails, C² regularity supplies actual first and second derivatives here. All derivative hypotheses are global and pointwise. Dimension zero is allowed, with canonical volume and standard Gaussian both unit mass on the one-point space.",
      "fidelity_verdict": "equivalent-after-elaboration",
      "blindness": "source hidden from decoder",
      "semantic_slots": [
        {
          "slot": "objects",
          "original": "PBPS v1 (2.6)-(2.7) relates the augmented Gibbs density to independent Gibbs X and standard Gaussian noise. The selected certificate names the actual Gibbs integral Z_V and makes the normalizing factor explicit.",
          "reconstructed": "Z_V is the integral of exp(-V) against canonical volume, mu is volume.tilted(-V), and J is the pushforward of mu.prod(stdGaussian E) by (x,z) ↦ (x,x+sqrt(eta) z). The final reference measure is volume.prod volume.",
          "relation": "explicit-elaboration",
          "evidence": "The pinned statement and current module define precisely these objects by let bindings. The anonymous reconstruction identifies the same objects, including the normalized tilt definition; no independent law-equality assumption is introduced. Primary context: https://arxiv.org/html/2609.06905v1#S2.SS2, equations (2.6)-(2.7)."
