      "url": "lean-foundations.html?view=semantic",
      "source_url": "https://arxiv.org/html/2609.06906v1#S6.SS2.SSS2",
      "original_theorem": "Supporting normalization theorem: on a finite-dimensional real inner-product Borel space, a differentiable potential V that is m-strongly convex on the whole space for m>0 has a volume-integrable Gibbs weight exp(−V). In the paper, normalized laws are specified by densities proportional to exp(−U_A,u), where U_A,u=U+‖·−u‖²/(2A), U is C² with positive Hessian lower bound, and A∈(0,infinity]. Establishing StrongConvexOn from those Hessian bounds and identifying the particular source potential are separate source adapters, not premises silently replaced by this prerequisite.\n\nSelected supporting theorem: V is differentiable, m>0, and V((1−t)x+ty)≤(1−t)V(x)+tV(y)−m t(1−t)‖x−y‖²/2 for all x,y and 0≤t≤1.\nThe integration space is finite-dimensional real inner-product space with its Borel sigma-algebra and canonical volume.\nNo minimizer, stationary point, gradient field, tail envelope or finite normalizer is an additional hypothesis.\nThe source's C²/Hessian potential conditions and RGO parameters remain attached to its own statements. The Hessian-to-StrongConvexOn adapter is not proved by this prerequisite.",
      "reconstructed_theorem": "For every finite-dimensional real inner-product space E, equipped with its induced norm, norm topology, and Borel σ-algebra, let λ_E denote the canonical Lebesgue measure normalized by orthonormal coordinates. For every real-valued function V : E → ℝ and every real number m > 0, assume that V is genuinely Fréchet differentiable over ℝ at every point of E and is m-strongly convex on all of E. Explicitly, for every x,y ∈ E and every pair of real numbers a,b ≥ 0 with a+b = 1, assume\n\nV(a x + b y) ≤ a V(x) + b V(y) − (m/2) a b ‖x−y‖².\n\nThen the function x ↦ exp(−V(x)) is integrable with respect to λ_E. This conclusion means both that the function is almost everywhere strongly measurable and that the nonnegative integral of its absolute value is finite. It is a genuine integrability assertion, not merely a statement about a real-valued, totalized Bochner integral expression.\n\nThe strong-convexity domain is the entire space E, which is convex. All weights and scalar multiplications in the displayed inequality are real, and the coefficient of the squared-distance correction is exactly m/2. The exponential is the positive real exponential. Canonical volume is not an arbitrary measure-space parameter. No positive-dimension or nontriviality assumption is imposed; dimension zero is allowed, with canonical volume equal to unit mass on the one-point space. Completeness is supplied by finite dimensionality, rather than imposed as a separate hypothesis. The differentiability premise asserts existence of an actual derivative everywhere, not a value assigned by a totalized derivative API.",
      "fidelity_verdict": "equivalent-after-elaboration",
      "blindness": "source hidden from decoder",
      "semantic_slots": [
        {
          "slot": "objects",
          "original": "The selected supporting theorem concerns a real potential V, a positive strong-convexity modulus m and its Gibbs weight exp(−V), integrated against canonical finite-dimensional volume. The source context separately contains particular Gibbs and quadratic-RGO potentials.",
