          "relation": "equivalent",
          "evidence": "The chord parameterizations are equivalent by b=t and a=1−t. The convexity of Set.univ is automatic. No point is assumed to be stationary or chosen as an existential minimizer; the proof simply evaluates the genuine gradient at the fixed point zero."
        },
        {
          "slot": "assumptions",
          "original": "The selected theorem assumes m>0, genuine differentiability and m-strong convexity with squared-distance coefficient m/2. No extra normalization, minimizer or tail premise is allowed. The source instead presents C² potentials with Hessian bounds, leaving that adapter separate.",
          "reconstructed": "Precisely positive m, Differentiable ℝ V and StrongConvexOn Set.univ m V, whose Mathlib modulus is r↦(m/2)r².",
          "relation": "explicit-elaboration",
          "evidence": "Each (hV z).hasGradientAt certifies the actual gradient of V; it is not merely a totalized derivative value. Differentiability also gives continuity and hence measurability of exp(−V). No supplied gradient field or zero-gradient assertion is used. A constant potential on ℝ confirms the lesson's warning that allowing m=0 would not guarantee integrability."
        },
        {
          "slot": "conclusion",
          "original": "The supporting conclusion is volume integrability of exp(−V), supplying the finite-normalizer prerequisite without assuming it.",
          "reconstructed": "MeasureTheory.Integrable (fun x↦Real.exp(−V x)) volume, including almost-everywhere strong measurability and finite absolute integral.",
          "relation": "equivalent",
          "evidence": "The proof establishes an integrable Gaussian dominating function and applies Integrable.mono'. It does not claim finiteness from a totalized Bochner integral. Since the weight is everywhere positive and canonical volume is nonzero, the compiled tests obtain a strictly positive real normalizer and IsProbabilityMeasure of the corresponding tilted volume. Those are downstream consumers, not extra production hypotheses."
        },
        {
          "slot": "scopes",
          "original": "The source extraction is explicitly an ASTIS mathematical expansion supporting normalized-density notation. The binding role is prerequisite, supporting only gibbs-weight-integrability; source-hessian-adapter and source-gibbs-identification remain separate.",
