      "summary": "Blind reconstruction and seven-slot semantic comparison for AutoSamplingTheory.ExampleCases.ProximalBPS.ConditionalScoreDomain.conditional_curvature_and_score_domain.",
      "column": 7,
      "url": "lean-foundations.html?view=semantic",
      "source_url": "https://arxiv.org/html/2609.06905v1#A3.SS1",
      "original_theorem": "The paper works on R^d, d>=1, with C2 potential V and genuine Hessian bounds alpha I<=D2V<=beta I, explicit 0<alpha<=beta, eta>0 and beta eta<=1.\nThe actual conditional law of Y_minus given Y_plus=y, with Y_plus/minus=X plus/minus sqrt(eta)Z and X Gibbs, has density proportional to exp(-W_y(u)), W_y(u)=V((y+u)/2)+norm(y-u)^2/(8eta). The score is s_y(u)=-grad V((y+u)/2)/2-(y-u)/(4eta).\nThe conditional negative log-density has Hessian (D2V((y+u)/2)+eta^-1 I)/4, bounded below by (alpha+eta^-1)I/4. The score spatial derivative is (eta^-1 I-D2V((y+u)/2))/4 and has operator norm at most (eta^-1-alpha)/4 under beta eta<=1. The conditional Poincare step uses directional scores a dot s_y, for unit a. This selected input packet makes the noncompact directional-score L2 and finite gradient-energy domain explicit; it does not prove the curvature-to-Poincare inequality or the resulting variance bound.",
      "reconstructed_theorem": "Let E be a finite-dimensional real inner-product space with its Borel measurable structure. Let V:E->R, alpha,beta nonnegative real and eta real. Assume alpha>0, alpha<=beta, V is C2, alpha norm(v)^2<=D2V(x)[v,v]<=beta norm(v)^2 for all x,v, eta>0 and beta eta<=1. Define mu as Lebesgue measure tilted by -V. Let J be the pushforward of mu tensor standard Gaussian by (x,z)->(x,x+sqrt(eta)z). Define W_y(u)=V((y+u)/2)+norm(u-y)^2/(8eta), and s_y(u)=-DV((y+u)/2)/2-inner(y-u,.)/(4eta), a continuous linear functional. There exist Markov kernels R,S such that R is a conditional kernel for swap pushforward J given the first coordinate, and S(y) is the pushforward of R(y) by x->2x-y for every y. Thus R represents the original first coordinate conditional on the noisy second coordinate. The same kernels satisfy, for every y: S(y) is volume tilted by -W_y; W_y is C2 and s_y C1; for every u,v,w, D2W_y(u)[v,w]=D2V((y+u)/2)[v,w]/4+inner(v,w)/(4eta), and Ds_y(u)[v][w]=inner(v,w)/(4eta)-D2V((y+u)/2)[v,w]/4; for every u,v, D2W_y(u)[v,v]>=(alpha+1/eta)norm(v)^2/4; and for every u, norm(Ds_y(u))<=(1/eta-alpha)/4. For every direction a define q_(y,a)(u)=s_y(u)[a]. Then q is C1, belongs to L2(S(y)), and is Admissible for S(y): exactly q integrable, its square centered by its S(y) expectation integrable, and norm(gradient q)^2 integrable. Admissible does not assert a variance inequality. For every u, norm(gradient q(u))<=((1/eta-alpha)/4)norm(a). Quantifiers: every space and tuple satisfying hypotheses, then one pair R,S, then every y and every indicated point and direction. Kernel identities and analytic conclusions are pointwise for every y, not merely almost everywhere. Norm constants depend only on alpha,eta; beta enters hypotheses. Gradient is the Riesz representative of the Frechet derivative. L2 includes almost-everywhere strong measurability and finite seminorm.",
