      "source_url": "https://arxiv.org/html/1310.2526v7",
      "original_theorem": "Selected Euclidean localization: d>=1, W:R^d->R C2 and f smooth compactly supported. Haar volume and actual derivatives are used; no global probability normalization is needed for this compact-test formula.\nFor the energy consequence only, a real m satisfies D2W(x)[v,v]>=m norm(v)^2 for every x,v. The full background source has compact manifold and boundary hypotheses outside this selected localized component.\nThe weighted Reilly identity in Theorem 1.1 (1.3) is a compact weighted manifold formula with boundary terms. The selected ASTIS background component is its Euclidean smooth compact-test localization, proved directly: on R^d for d>=1, W C2 and f smooth compactly supported, Lf=Delta f-gradient W dot gradient f satisfies integral exp(-W)(Lf)^2 = integral exp(-W)sum_i norm(gradient(D_i f))^2 + integral exp(-W)D2W[gradient f,gradient f]. All tested weighted products are integrable by compact support; global weight integrability is not required. If D2W>=m I, dropping the nonnegative Hessian-square term gives m times weighted gradient energy at most weighted square-generator energy. This selected component does not claim the entire manifold theorem or a spectral gap.",
      "reconstructed_theorem": "For every finite-dimensional real inner-product space E with its Borel measurable structure, every C2 W:E->R and every smooth compactly supported f:E->R, define L(x)=Delta f(x)-inner(gradient W(x),gradient f(x)), H(x)=sum_i norm(gradient(z->Df(z)[e_i])(x))^2, and C(x)=D2W(x)[gradient f(x),gradient f(x)], using the displayed standard orthonormal basis indexed by the dimension. With respect to canonical volume all four functions exp(-W)L^2, exp(-W)norm(gradient f)^2, exp(-W)H and exp(-W)C are integrable, including absolute integrability of the signed curvature contribution. The integral of exp(-W)L^2 equals the sum of the integrals of exp(-W)H and exp(-W)C. For every real m, if D2W(x)[v,v]>=m norm(v)^2 for every x,v, then m times the weighted gradient-energy integral is at most the weighted L^2 integral. All derivatives are genuine Frechet derivatives, gradients their Riesz representatives and Delta the trace of the genuine second derivative. There is no positivity requirement on m, no global integrability assumption on exp(-W), and no probability normalization. Zero-dimensional E is allowed with an empty basis sum. The integrability clauses are conclusions, not premises.",
