      "original_theorem": "Finite-dimensional real inner-product space with its Borel measurable structure, including dimension zero.\nTau>0 and arbitrary centers x,y. The covariance is tau I, not sqrt(tau) I.\nBoth Gaussian laws are actual scaled/translated standard Gaussian pushforwards, not arbitrary laws assumed to have a density ratio.\nThe source states reverse transport on Euclidean probabilities. This coordinate-free Gaussian ingredient is an explicit expanded prerequisite, not the mixture theorem.\nSPHMC Lemma6.3(ii) uses the heat-flow bound R_q(Pdag*gamma_tau || Q*gamma_tau)<=q Winfinity(Pdag,Q)^2/(2tau). The selected expanded Gaussian prerequisite, not the full mixture inequality, is the actual likelihood between equal-covariance translates: for G_a=Law(a+sqrt(tau)Z), tau>0, L(z)=exp(inner(x-y,z-y)/tau-norm(x-y)^2/(2tau)) satisfies G_x=G_y.withDensity(ofReal L), absolute continuity and a.e. RN identification under G_y. Measurability and the measure identity are proved rather than assumed.",
      "reconstructed_theorem": "For every finite-dimensional real inner-product space E equipped with its normed additive commutative group structure and a Borel measurable structure, all x,y in E and real tau>0, define G(a) as the pushforward of canonical centered standard Gaussian on E by z to a+sqrt(tau)z. Define L(z)=exp(inner(x-y,z-y)/tau-norm(x-y)^2/(2tau)). Then L is measurable; G(x)=G(y).withDensity(z to ENNReal.ofReal(L(z))); G(x) is absolutely continuous with respect to G(y); and the extended nonnegative RN derivative dG(x)/dG(y) equals ofReal L for G(y)-almost every z. The base measure is G(y) and the resulting weighted measure G(x). Dimension zero is included. There is no restriction on x,y, no extra regularity, integrability or normalization assumption, and no unspecified constant. The local definitions scope over the whole conjunction.",
