      "url": "lean-foundations.html?view=semantic",
      "source_url": "https://arxiv.org/html/2609.06906v1#S6.SS2.SSS1",
      "original_theorem": "Any measurable space and measure mu; no probability or sigma-finiteness assumption is required for this scalar inequality.\na,b are ENNReal-valued and a.e. measurable. The denominator b is nonzero and finite mu-a.e.\nThe integral of b is nonzero and finite, and real q>1.\nThe integral of a and of a^q/b^(q-1) may be infinite. This general measure inequality is an expanded ingredient, not a printed paper theorem.\nLet mu be any measure on a measurable space and a,b be almost-everywhere measurable extended nonnegative functions. Suppose b is positive and finite almost everywhere and its integral B is positive and finite. For real q>1, the qth power of integral a divided by B^(q-1) is at most the integral of a^q/b^(q-1). Neither the numerator integral nor the right-hand integral is assumed finite.",
      "reconstructed_theorem": "For every measurable space X, every extended nonnegative measure mu on X, and every pair of functions a,b:X to [0,infinity], assume a and b are measurable up to a mu-null set, 0<b(x)<infinity for mu-almost every x, and 0<integral b dmu<infinity. Then for every real q>1, (integral a dmu)^q/(integral b dmu)^(q-1) is at most integral a(x)^q/b(x)^(q-1) dmu(x). Every displayed integral is the extended nonnegative Lebesgue integral, and powers and division use extended nonnegative real conventions. No finiteness assumption is imposed on a, its integral, or the right-hand integral; in particular integral a=infinity is included. No probability, finite-total-mass, or sigma-finiteness hypothesis is imposed on mu. There are no additional constants. No ambiguity in the quantified inequality using the supplied extended-real conventions.",
