    {
      "id": "semantic-audit:ASTIS-RT-20260911-SPHMCGaussianMixture",
      "kind": "semantic-audit",
      "label": "ASTIS-RT-20260911-SPHMCGaussianMixture",
      "status": "fidelity-mismatch",
      "subtitle": "companion:sphmc-gaussian-mixture · domain-mismatch",
      "summary": "Blind reconstruction and seven-slot semantic comparison for AutoSamplingTheory.ExampleCases.SmoothedPicardHMC.GaussianMixture.bounded_displacement_reverse_transport.",
      "column": 7,
      "url": "lean-foundations.html?view=semantic",
      "source_url": "https://arxiv.org/html/2609.06906v1#S6.SS2.SSS1",
      "original_theorem": "Finite-dimensional real inner-product Borel E, including zero dimension; P,Q are probability measures.\ngamma has the actual P,Q marginals. Its probability property is derived from the coupling and P, not separately assumed.\ntau>0, real q>1, t>=0, and norm(x-y)<=t gamma-a.e.; t=0 is allowed.\nNo density identity, reverse-transport inequality, moment integrability or marginal p-moment assumption is supplied. Dropping source marginal Pp membership for this bounded ingredient is an explicit valid generalization.\nThe source uses this ingredient after choosing a truncation proxy. That choice, its all-q quantifier, full Wasserstein/Renyi API and warm-start recursion are separate.\nLet P,Q be probability measures on a finite-dimensional real inner-product Borel space E and gamma an actual coupling of P,Q. Suppose tau>0, real q>1, t>=0 and norm(x-y)<=t gamma-a.e. Set Hmu=GaussianSmoothing.gaussianSmoothing mu (sqrt tau), the existing law of independent additive Gaussian noise. Then HP is absolutely continuous with respect to HQ. Its actual ENNReal RN qth-power integral is bounded by ofReal exp(q(q-1)t^2/(2tau)); the real RN qth power is integrable, its integral has the same exponential upper bound, and its logarithm divided by q-1 is at most q t^2/(2tau).",
      "reconstructed_theorem": "For every finite-dimensional real inner-product space E, including dimension zero, equipped with its Borel measurable structure, let P,Q be probability measures on E and gamma a measure on E times E whose first-coordinate pushforward is P and second-coordinate pushforward is Q. For every triple of real tau,q,t, assume tau>0,q>1,t>=0 and norm(x-y)<=t for gamma-almost every (x,y). Define kappa as the pushforward of the measure named stdGaussian under z to sqrt(tau)z. For each measure mu, define Hmu as the pushforward of mu product kappa under (x,z) to x+z, the actual Gaussian-smoothing law defined through product measure and addition pushforward. Write r(z)=d(HP)/d(HQ)(z) for the extended nonnegative RN derivative and r_R(z)=toReal(r(z)), using the totalized conversion at infinity. All five conclusions hold: HP is absolutely continuous with respect to HQ; the extended nonnegative Lebesgue integral of r^q under HQ is at most ofReal(exp(q(q-1)t^2/(2tau))); the real-valued r_R^q is integrable under HQ, meaning a.e. strong measurability and finite integral of norm; its real Bochner integral is at most exp(q(q-1)t^2/(2tau)); and the real logarithm of that same real integral divided by q-1 is at most q t^2/(2tau). Every integral uses HQ and the derivative direction is d(HP)/d(HQ). The conclusions apply to each supplied coupling satisfying the a.e. displacement bound. No density, moment or positive-dimension assumption is imposed. The bounds depend only on tau,q,t with exactly the displayed constants. The extended integral is bounded by a finite exponential and the real integral has an explicit integrability conclusion. Definition-context limitation: the supplied context identifies stdGaussian by name without expanding its construction or normalization; this reconstruction retains that exact named measure without adding a covariance or density formula.",
