      "source_url": "",
      "original_theorem": "Let E be a complete real inner-product space, s a convex subset, f:E→ℝ, g:E→E and m∈ℝ. Suppose f has ambient gradient g(z) at every z∈s. If ⟨g(y)−g(x),y−x⟩≥m‖y−x‖² for every x,y∈s, then f is m-strongly convex on s, in the chord sense with quadratic correction m/2.",
      "reconstructed_theorem": "Let E be a complete real inner product space, let s be any convex subset of E, let f : E → ℝ and grad : E → E, and let m ∈ ℝ. Suppose that f is Fréchet differentiable in the ambient space at every z ∈ s, with derivative v ↦ ⟨grad(z), v⟩. Suppose further that for every x,y ∈ s, m‖y−x‖² ≤ ⟨grad(y)−grad(x), y−x⟩. Then f is strongly convex on s with parameter m in the following precise sense: for every x,y ∈ s and every a,b ∈ ℝ with a ≥ 0, b ≥ 0 and a+b=1, f(ax+by) ≤ af(x)+bf(y)−(m/2)ab‖x−y‖². Convexity of s is also part of the conclusion. No sign restriction is imposed on m.",
