ity nor 0≤m is present. the wider domain is a reusable generalization, not the literal source statement. quantifiers every pair x,y in the domain; chord parameter t∈[0,1]. every x,y in e; every pair of nonnegative real weights a,t with a+t=1. equivalent set a=1−t. conversely, nonnegative weights summing to one imply t∈[0,1]. the proof uses exactly this change of variables. assumptions c¹ regularity, nonnegative m, and the global gradient lower bound. c¹ regularity and the same global gradient bound; m may have either sign. weaker-in-lean the source sign hypothesis is absent from this reusable lemma. the proof only cancels s(1−t)>0 and multiplies by t≥0, so it does not need m≥0. no added regularity or integrability assumption is used. conclusion the chord bound with correction (m/2)t(1−t)‖y−x‖². strongconvexon univ m f with exactly that correction. equivalent expanding the mathlib definitions and replacing ‖x−y‖ by ‖y−x‖ yields the stated inequality. for m<0 it should be understood as the signed chord predicate, not ordinary positive strong convexity. scopes the (1.5)→(1.3) direction within proposition 1.6 part 1. the same direction proved by two segment ftc identities and integration. same the lesson and code compare a and b. at t=1 the chord is equality. for t<1, the proof cancels s(1−t) on s∈(0,1), integrates via the null-endpoint lemma, and covers t=0 by multiplication by zero. it asserts no hessian result. constant dependencies the same modulus in monotonicity and the half-modulus in the chord correction. m becomes m/2 through ∫₀¹s ds=1/2. same hpoly preserves the exact factor 1/2; no dimension constants, losses, or sign-dependent constants enter. domains informational the lean leaf generalizes the selected finite-dimensional euclidean source boundary to arbitrary complete real inner-product spaces. the publication lesson explicitly identifies this generalization; acceptance is restricted to the selected euclidean source boundary, and the unrestricted semantic v