      "url": "lean-foundations.html?view=semantic",
      "source_url": "",
      "original_theorem": "For every natural dimension d, every nonnegative real m and every continuously differentiable f on all of real Euclidean d-space, the m-strong-convexity chord condition is equivalent to each of: (i) for all x,y, f(y)>=f(x)+<grad f(x),y-x>+(m/2)||y-x||^2; (ii) for all x,y, <grad f(y)-grad f(x),y-x>>=m||y-x||^2. Only part 1 is selected, excluding the C2 Hessian condition.",
      "reconstructed_theorem": "For every natural number d (including zero), every f:ℝᵈ→ℝ on Euclidean space with its usual inner product and ℓ² norm, and every real m≥0, suppose f is continuously Fréchet differentiable on all of ℝᵈ. Then both equivalences hold: (1) f is m-strongly convex on all of ℝᵈ if and only if, for every x,y∈ℝᵈ, f(x)+⟨∇f(x),y−x⟩+(m/2)‖y−x‖²≤f(y); (2) f is m-strongly convex on all of ℝᵈ if and only if, for every x,y∈ℝᵈ, m‖y−x‖²≤⟨∇f(y)−∇f(x),y−x⟩. Here m-strong convexity means that for all x,y and real a,b≥0 with a+b=1, f(a x+b y)≤a f(x)+b f(y)−a b(m/2)‖x−y‖².",
