      "status": "fidelity-mismatch",
      "subtitle": "chewi-opt-v1-prop-1-6 · domain-mismatch",
      "summary": "Blind reconstruction and seven-slot semantic comparison for AutoSamplingTheory.TechnicalLemmas.Analysis.ConvexityC2.gradient_sub_inner_eq_integral_fderiv2.",
      "column": 7,
      "url": "lean-foundations.html?view=semantic",
      "source_url": "",
      "original_theorem": "For a twice continuously differentiable real-valued f on all real Euclidean d-space and every x,v, the inner product of grad f(x+v)-grad f(x) with v equals the integral from 0 to 1 of the Hessian quadratic form D2f(x+t v)[v,v] with respect to dt. This is the FTC identity used in the final proof paragraph.",
      "reconstructed_theorem": "Let E be any complete real inner-product space (a real Hilbert space), with no finite-dimensionality assumption, and let f:E→ℝ be twice continuously Fréchet differentiable on all of E. For every x,v∈E, the inner product of ∇f(x+v)−∇f(x) with v equals the Lebesgue integral from 0 to 1 of the second Fréchet derivative at x+t v evaluated on (v,v): ⟨∇f(x+v)−∇f(x),v⟩ = ∫₀¹ D²f(x+t v)[v,v] dt. The gradient is the Riesz representative of the first Fréchet derivative. The integral is the oriented real interval integral, here with endpoints 0 and 1. There are no restrictions on x or v and no convexity or sign assumptions.",
      "fidelity_verdict": "domain-mismatch",
      "blindness": "source hidden from decoder",
      "semantic_slots": [
        {
          "slot": "objects",
          "original": "Real-valued f, Euclidean gradient, and Hessian quadratic form.",
          "reconstructed": "Real-valued f, gradient as the Riesz representative of Df, and D²f(x)[v,v] as two evaluations of fderiv (fderiv f).",
          "relation": "explicit-elaboration",
          "evidence": "The actual module differentiates t ↦ Df(x+t v)[v]. In Euclidean space its derivative is the classical Hessian quadratic form; real inner-product symmetry accounts for the source pairing order. No independently postulated Hessian or gradient is substituted."
        },
        {
          "slot": "domains",
          "original": "All real Euclidean d-space.",
          "reconstructed": "Any complete real inner-product space, including infinite-dimensional spaces.",
          "relation": "stronger-in-lean",
          "evidence": "This is a genuine domain generalization, not literal source equality. The module preamble, lesson assumption 1 and candidate_assumptions explicitly disclose it. The scalar FTC proof only requires the displayed Fréchet regularity and the Riesz gradient interface."
        },
        {
          "slot": "quantifiers",
          "original": "The source identity uses arbitrary x,y and their displacement y−x.",
          "reconstructed": "For every x,v, with endpoints x and x+v.",
          "relation": "equivalent",
          "evidence": "On the source domain use v=y−x, or conversely y=x+v. There is no nonzero-direction restriction; v=0 gives both sides zero."
        },
        {
          "slot": "assumptions",
          "original": "Twice continuous differentiability on the whole Euclidean domain.",
          "reconstructed": "ContDiff ℝ 2 f everywhere on a complete real inner-product space.",
          "relation": "explicit-elaboration",
          "evidence": "In finite dimension C² and twice continuously Fréchet differentiable agree. The code obtains ContDiff ℝ 1 (fderiv ℝ f), differentiates its affine-segment evaluation, and obtains continuity of the second derivative. Compact-interval integrability follows from that continuity, with no omitted domination, boundary or boundedness assumption. The domain extension is recorded separately."
        },
        {
