          "relation": "equivalent",
          "evidence": "The lesson defines g(t)=Df(x+t v)[v]. The code proves its genuine derivative and applies FTC to obtain g(1)−g(0), then rewrites the endpoints using the Riesz gradient. Specialization to ℝ^d yields exactly the final source identity."
        },
        {
          "slot": "scopes",
          "original": "An exact global identity within the C² part of the proof, with ordinary dt on [0,1].",
          "reconstructed": "An exact equality of real numbers; an oriented interval integral against real Lebesgue measure with endpoints 0 and 1.",
          "relation": "explicit-elaboration",
          "evidence": "Continuity makes the scalar integral agree with the classical source integral. No distributional, almost-everywhere, local-domain or asymptotic interpretation is substituted. Although the proposition has a nonnegative modulus, the identity itself has no modulus and does not require monotonicity."
        },
        {
          "slot": "constant dependencies",
          "original": "Unit integration interval; no modulus or unspecified multiplicative constant.",
