      "original_theorem": "For a twice continuously differentiable f on all real Euclidean d-space and a nonnegative real m, the global condition <grad f(y)-grad f(x),y-x> >= m||y-x||^2 for all x,y is equivalent to <v,Hessian f(x)v> >= m||v||^2 for all x,v. Forward uses the positive directional increment and derivative limit; reverse integrates the Hessian along the segment.",
      "reconstructed_theorem": "Let E be any complete real inner-product space (a real Hilbert space), with no finite-dimensionality assumption. Let f:E→ℝ be twice continuously Fréchet differentiable on all of E, and let m be any real number, without a sign restriction. Then the following two global statements are equivalent: (1) for every x,y∈E, m‖y−x‖² ≤ ⟨∇f(y)−∇f(x),y−x⟩; (2) for every x,v∈E, m‖v‖² ≤ D²f(x)[v,v]. The gradient is the Riesz representative of the first Fréchet derivative, and D²f(x)[v,v] means the derivative of the first Fréchet derivative at x applied successively to v and v. The same fixed m is used in both inequalities.",
