      "source_url": "",
      "original_theorem": "For every natural dimension d, every nonnegative real m and every twice continuously differentiable real-valued f on all Euclidean d-space, the strong-convexity chord condition with correction (m/2)t(1-t)||y-x||^2 is equivalent to <v,Hessian f(x)v> >= m||v||^2 for every x,v. This selects Proposition 1.6 part 2; part 1 is already a dependency.",
      "reconstructed_theorem": "For every natural number d, including d=0, every function f:ℝᵈ→ℝ on real d-dimensional Euclidean space, and every real number m≥0, suppose f is twice continuously Fréchet differentiable on all of ℝᵈ. Then f is m-strongly convex on the whole space if and only if, for every x,v∈ℝᵈ, m‖v‖² ≤ D²f(x)[v,v]. Here m-strong convexity means exactly that for every x,y∈ℝᵈ and every real a,b≥0 with a+b=1, f(a x+b y) ≤ a f(x)+b f(y)−a b (m/2)‖x−y‖². The second derivative is the derivative of the first Fréchet derivative, evaluated twice on v. The same m appears in the interpolation inequality and the second-derivative bound; m=0 is included.",
