      "subtitle": "companion:sphmc-adaptive-center-rgo · domain-mismatch",
      "summary": "Blind reconstruction and seven-slot semantic comparison for AutoSamplingTheory.ExampleCases.SmoothedPicardHMC.AdaptiveCenterRGO.adaptive_center_recovery.",
      "column": 7,
      "url": "lean-foundations.html?view=semantic",
      "source_url": "https://arxiv.org/html/2609.06906v1#S6.SS2.SSS1",
      "original_theorem": "Finite-dimensional real inner-product Borel space, including dimension zero; arbitrary probability base mu.\nFixed b>=0 and a>0. No density, curvature, moments or extra normalizability premise.\nAll three kernels precede the universally quantified probability center law. Fiber formulas hold for every u,y.\nThe input conditional observation kernel is exactly H. Arbitrary center laws do not mean arbitrary center-observation joint laws.\nThis explicitly generalizes the source Gibbs/Euclidean setting and exposes its needed ideal joint semantics.\nLet mu be a probability measure on a finite-dimensional real inner-product Borel space, b>=0 and a>0. There exist Markov kernels T,H from E to E and B from E times E to E times E, chosen before every probability center law nu. For every u, T(u) is mu tilted by -b*norm(x-u)^2/2 and H(u) is its actual Gaussian smoothing at standard deviation sqrt(a). For every u,y, B(u,y) retains u and draws x from T(u) tilted by -norm(x-y)^2/(2a); equivalently it draws from mu with precision b+a^-1 and center (b+a^-1)^-1*(b*u+a^-1*y). For every probability nu, B composed with the actual joint measure nu compProd H equals nu compProd T.",
      "reconstructed_theorem": "For every finite-dimensional real inner-product space E equipped with its Borel measurable structure, every Borel probability measure mu on E, and real b,a satisfying b>=0,a>0, there exist Markov kernels T,H:E to E and B:E times E to E times E. For every u, T(u) is mu reweighted by exp(-(b/2)*norm(x-u)^2) and normalized by its integral against mu. For every u, H(u) is the law of X+sqrt(a)*Z, with X of law T(u) and independent centered identity-covariance Gaussian Z. Noise standard-deviation scale is sqrt(a), covariance aI. For every u,y, let Q(u,y) be T(u) reweighted by exp(-norm(x-y)^2/(2a)) and normalized by its integral against T(u). B(u,y) is the pushforward of Q(u,y) by x to (u,x), retaining u. The same B for every u,y also equals the pushforward by x to (u,x) of mu reweighted by exp(-(c/2)*norm(x-m(u,y))^2) and normalized by its mu integral, where c=b+a^-1 and m(u,y)=c^-1*(b*u+a^-1*y). Finally for every Borel probability nu on E, B composed with nu compProd H equals nu compProd T as actual measures on E times E. Explicitly sample U from nu, Y conditionally from H(U), then a pair from B(U,Y); its law equals sampling U from nu and X conditionally from T(U). This is equality of the full joint law of retained center and final sample, not merely sample marginal equality, and does not assert independence of center and sample. Quantifier order is all E,mu,b,a satisfying hypotheses, then exists T,H,B, then all probability nu. The same kernels precede and do not depend on nu. All fiber identities hold everywhere, not merely almost everywhere. Parameter measurability is included in the conclusion through kernel types, and probability of every fiber through Markov assertions. No separately supplied family measurability, density hypothesis on mu, moment, smoothness or convexity condition is assumed. The only numerical restrictions are b>=0 and a>0, with no unspecified constants. Reconstructed only from the anonymous statement and approved definitions; no source-fidelity or compilation verdict.",
