      "id": "semantic-audit:ASTIS-RT-20260911-SPHMCStateDependentRGO",
      "kind": "semantic-audit",
      "label": "ASTIS-RT-20260911-SPHMCStateDependentRGO",
      "status": "fidelity-mismatch",
      "subtitle": "companion:sphmc-state-dependent-rgo · domain-mismatch",
      "summary": "Blind reconstruction and seven-slot semantic comparison for AutoSamplingTheory.ExampleCases.SmoothedPicardHMC.StateDependentRGO.state_dependent_recovery.",
      "column": 7,
      "url": "lean-foundations.html?view=semantic",
      "source_url": "https://arxiv.org/html/2609.06906v1",
      "original_theorem": "E is a finite-dimensional real inner-product space with its Borel measurable structure, including dimension zero; mu is an arbitrary probability measure.\nS has only a measurable-space structure. No topology, standard Borel condition, or moment condition on the state law is needed.\nb,a,u are measurable on S, with b nonnegative and a strictly positive at every state. No fixed or deterministic schedule is imposed.\nAll global kernels precede the universally quantified probability state law. Both posterior formulas hold at every state and observation.\nRecovery assumes the actual ideal input nu compProd H. State may encode past history and its parameters are fixed conditional on the current call input. This does not assume one predetermined schedule for all calls or construct the actual approximate sampler.\nLet E be a finite-dimensional real inner-product Borel space, S any measurable space, and mu a probability measure on E. Let b,a:S to R and u:S to E be measurable, with b(s)>=0 and a(s)>0 everywhere. There exist Markov kernels T,H:S to E and B:S times E to S times E, chosen before every probability state law nu. Every T(s) is the normalized mu tilt by -b(s)*norm(x-u(s))^2/2; H(s) is its actual Gaussian smoothing at standard deviation sqrt(a(s)). Every B(s,y) retains s and draws x from the actual backward tilt of T(s) by -norm(x-y)^2/(2a(s)). Equivalently, it uses the original mu tilt with precision b(s)+1/a(s) and center (b(s)*u(s)+y/a(s))/(b(s)+1/a(s)). For every probability nu, B composed with nu compProd H equals nu compProd T.",
      "reconstructed_theorem": "Let E be any finite-dimensional real inner-product space equipped with its Borel measurable structure, S any measurable space, and mu any probability measure on E. Suppose b,a:S to R and u:S to E are measurable, and b(s)>=0,a(s)>0 for every s. Write P^f for the exponential tilt with density exp(f(x)) divided by its integral against P. There exist Markov kernels T,H:S to E and B:S times E to S times E. Every T(s) is mu tilted by -b(s)*norm(x-u(s))^2/2. Every H(s) is the law of X+sqrt(a(s))*Z, with X distributed as T(s) and independent centered identity-covariance Gaussian Z; sqrt(a(s)) is the standard-deviation scale. For every s,y, B(s,y) is the pushforward under x to (s,x) of T(s) tilted by -norm(x-y)^2/(2*a(s)). The same fiber is the pushforward under x to (s,x) of mu tilted by -(b(s)+a(s)^-1)*norm(x-m(s,y))^2/2, where m(s,y)=(b(s)+a(s)^-1)^-1*(b(s)*u(s)+a(s)^-1*y).\nThese kernels are chosen before and work simultaneously for every probability measure nu on S. For each such nu, B composed with nu compProd H equals nu compProd T. The input joint law samples s from nu and then y from H(s); applying B gives the same full S times E law as drawing s from nu and x from T(s). Equality holds on all measurable product-space subsets. All fiber identities hold everywhere, not merely almost everywhere. Every kernel is measurable in its input and has probability fibers everywhere. No additional density, moment, smoothness or topological assumptions on S, or moment/density assumptions on mu, are imposed. There are no unspecified constants; coefficients depend explicitly on b(s),a(s),u(s), and where applicable y.",
