      "source_url": "",
      "original_theorem": "In Section3 of Chewi Lectures on Optimization, f is C2 on Euclidean d-space, alpha-strongly convex and beta-smooth. The gradient step is T(x)=x-h grad f(x). Theorem3.3 states that for all x,y and h<=1/beta, ||T(y)-T(x)||<=sqrt(1-alpha h)||y-x||. The printed step restriction does not explicitly state h>=0. Definition3.2 uses kappa=beta/alpha>=1 in the positive condition-number setting. ",
      "reconstructed_theorem": "Let E be any complete real inner product space, with its compatible norm and additive group structure; no finite-dimensional hypothesis is imposed. Let f:E→ℝ and α,β,h∈ℝ be arbitrary. Assume (1) f is continuously Fréchet differentiable on all of E; (2) for every x,y∈E and every t∈[0,1], f((1−t)x+ty)≤(1−t)f(x)+tf(y)−(α/2)t(1−t)‖y−x‖²; (3) α≥0; (4) β≥0; (5) h≥0; (6) βh≤1; and (7) for every x,y∈E, f(y)≤f(x)+⟨∇f(x),y−x⟩+(β/2)‖y−x‖², where ∇f(x) is the Riesz vector representing the derivative of f at x. All these hypotheses hold globally and for the same fixed f,α,β,h. Define T(x)=x−h∇f(x), and q=Real.sqrt(1−αh), the nonnegative real square root with value zero on negative inputs. Then, for every x,y∈E, ‖T(y)−T(x)‖≤q‖y−x‖. Thus the declaration gives a uniform global one-step Lipschitz bound for the gradient update, with stated factor q depending only on α and h; β appears in the assumed upper quadratic model and step-size constraint. There are no other constants, restrictions on the two points, or dependence on dimension.",
      "fidelity_verdict": "possible-source-error",
      "blindness": "source hidden from decoder",
