    {
      "id": "semantic-audit:ASTIS-RT-ANDI-OPT-GradientDistance",
      "kind": "semantic-audit",
      "label": "ASTIS-RT-ANDI-OPT-GradientDistance",
      "status": "fidelity-mismatch",
      "subtitle": "chewi-opt-v1-theorem-3-3 · implicit-assumption-exposed",
      "summary": "Blind reconstruction and seven-slot semantic comparison for AutoSamplingTheory.TechnicalLemmas.Analysis.GradientDescentContraction.gradient_descent_distance_bound.",
      "column": 7,
      "url": "lean-foundations.html?view=semantic",
      "source_url": "",
      "original_theorem": "In Section3 of Chewi Lectures on Optimization, f is C2 on Euclidean d-space, alpha-strongly convex and beta-smooth. The gradient step is T(x)=x-h grad f(x). Theorem3.3 states that for all x,y and h<=1/beta, ||T(y)-T(x)||<=sqrt(1-alpha h)||y-x||. The printed step restriction does not explicitly state h>=0. Definition3.2 uses kappa=beta/alpha>=1 in the positive condition-number setting. Immediately after the proof, take y as a global minimizer xstar, h=1/beta and R=||x0-xstar||. Iteration gives ||x_N-xstar||<=(1-1/kappa)^(N/2)R<=exp(-N/(2kappa))R. The following logarithmic complexity consequence is outside this declaration.",
      "reconstructed_theorem": "Let E be any complete real inner product space, with its compatible norm and additive group structure; no finite-dimensional hypothesis is imposed. Let f:E→ℝ and α,β,h∈ℝ be arbitrary. Assume (1) f is continuously Fréchet differentiable on all of E; (2) for every x,y∈E and every t∈[0,1], f((1−t)x+ty)≤(1−t)f(x)+tf(y)−(α/2)t(1−t)‖y−x‖²; (3) α≥0; (4) β≥0; (5) h≥0; (6) βh≤1; and (7) for every x,y∈E, f(y)≤f(x)+⟨∇f(x),y−x⟩+(β/2)‖y−x‖², where ∇f(x) is the Riesz vector representing the derivative of f at x. All these hypotheses hold globally and for the same fixed f,α,β,h. Define T(x)=x−h∇f(x), and q=Real.sqrt(1−αh), the nonnegative real square root with value zero on negative inputs. For every xstar∈E satisfying f(xstar)≤f(y) for every y∈E, for every initial point x₀∈E, and for every natural number N≥0, let x_N=T^[N](x₀), using the same constant h at every step and taking T^[0] to be the identity. Then both inequalities hold: ‖x_N−xstar‖≤q^N‖x₀−xstar‖ and q^N‖x₀−xstar‖≤exp(−αhN/2)‖x₀−xstar‖. N in the exponential is interpreted as a real number. Equivalently these form the displayed chain ‖x_N−xstar‖≤[Real.sqrt(1−αh)]^N‖x₀−xstar‖≤exp(−αhN/2)‖x₀−xstar‖. The conclusion is uniform in the chosen global minimizer, initial point, and iteration number. Its geometric factor and exponential rate depend on α and h; the multiplicative initial-distance factor depends on x₀ and xstar; β occurs only in the hypotheses and admissibility of h. No dimension-dependent or other hidden constant is present.",
