      "search": "semantic-audit:astis-rt-andi-opt-gradientdistance semantic-audit astis-rt-andi-opt-gradientdistance chewi-opt-v1-theorem-3-3 · implicit-assumption-exposed blind reconstruction and seven-slot semantic comparison for autosamplingtheory.technicallemmas.analysis.gradientdescentcontraction.gradient_descent_distance_bound. implicit-assumption-exposed source hidden from decoder in section3 of chewi lectures on optimization, f is c2 on euclidean d-space, alpha-strongly convex and beta-smooth. the gradient step is t(x)=x-h grad f(x). theorem3.3 states that for all x,y and h<=1/beta, ||t(y)-t(x)||<=sqrt(1-alpha h)||y-x||. the printed step restriction does not explicitly state h>=0. definition3.2 uses kappa=beta/alpha>=1 in the positive condition-number setting. immediately after the proof, take y as a global minimizer xstar, h=1/beta and r=||x0-xstar||. iteration gives ||x_n-xstar||<=(1-1/kappa)^(n/2)r<=exp(-n/(2kappa))r. the following logarithmic complexity consequence is outside this declaration. let e be any complete real inner product space, with its compatible norm and additive group structure; no finite-dimensional hypothesis is imposed. let f:e→ℝ and α,β,h∈ℝ be arbitrary. assume (1) f is continuously fréchet differentiable on all of e; (2) for every x,y∈e and every t∈[0,1], f((1−t)x+ty)≤(1−t)f(x)+tf(y)−(α/2)t(1−t)‖y−x‖²; (3) α≥0; (4) β≥0; (5) h≥0; (6) βh≤1; and (7) for every x,y∈e, f(y)≤f(x)+⟨∇f(x),y−x⟩+(β/2)‖y−x‖², where ∇f(x) is the riesz vector representing the derivative of f at x. all these hypotheses hold globally and for the same fixed f,α,β,h. define t(x)=x−h∇f(x), and q=real.sqrt(1−αh), the nonnegative real square root with value zero on negative inputs. for every xstar∈e satisfying f(xstar)≤f(y) for every y∈e, for every initial point x₀∈e, and for every natural number n≥0, let x_n=t^[n](x₀), using the same constant h at every step and taking t^[0] to be the identity. then both inequalities hold: ‖x_n−xstar‖≤q^n‖x₀−xstar‖ and q^n‖x₀−xstar‖≤exp