      "column": 7,
      "url": "lean-foundations.html?view=semantic",
      "source_url": "https://arxiv.org/html/2609.06906v1",
      "original_theorem": "S, X and A are arbitrary measurable spaces. Q:S->X is a Markov kernel and nu is a fixed probability law on A.\nB>0 is fixed across parameters. W:((S times X) times A)->Real is jointly measurable and |W((s,x),z)|<=B for every s,x,z.\nA default x0:X is supplied for the parent output construction; the cost bound does not depend on its value.\nAt each parameter s an attempt independently draws x~Q(s), N~Poisson(2B), an infinite iid auxiliary sequence z~nu^ℕ, of which only the first N entries are read, and U~Uniform[0,1]. It accepts when U<=product_{i<N}(B+W((s,x),z_i))/(2B). Whole attempts are iid.\nC is the extended nonnegative sum of N_n over all attempts n whose preceding attempts failed, including the first successful batch. On never-success it is the sum over every batch.\nFor every parameter s and failure tolerance 0<delta<1, set L=log(2/delta) and m=ceil(exp(2B)L).\nUnder the displayed measurable bounded-estimator experiment, the actual full-batch query count satisfies P(C>2B(e-1)m+L)<=delta and P(C>K(B)L)<=delta, where K(B)=2B(e-1)(exp(2B)+1/log(2))+1. Acceptance probability is derived from the actual parent program and is not an extra hypothesis. The first inequality retains the integer ceiling; the second is an explicit single-log bound for fixed B. Both hold for every B>0, including arbitrarily small B, and every 0<delta<1.",
      "reconstructed_theorem": "Let S, X and A be arbitrary measurable spaces, Q a Markov kernel from S to X, nu a probability measure on A, and W: (S x X) x A -> R a measurable function. Suppose B > 0 and |W((s,x),z)| <= B for every s in S, x in X and z in A. For every x0 in X, s in S and real delta with 0 < delta < 1, make the following definitions. One attempt p=(x,N,z,u) has law Lambda=(Q s) x Poisson(2B) x nu^NAT x Uniform([0,1]), with independent coordinates; z is an infinite sequence in A. An attempt is accepted on E={p: u <= product_{i=0}^{N-1} (B+W((s,x),z_i))/(2B)}, where the empty product is 1. Let rho=Lambda^NAT be the law of an independent sequence of attempts omega. Define the extended nonnegative cost C(omega)=sum_{n=0}^infinity [N_n if omega_i is not in E for every i<n, and 0 otherwise]. Set L=log(2/delta) and m=ceil_NAT(exp(2B)L). Then both rho{C > 2B(exp(1)-1)m+L} <= delta and rho{C > [2B(exp(1)-1)(exp(2B)+1/log(2))+1]L} <= delta hold. In each inequality the real threshold and delta are embedded using ENNReal.ofReal, and probability and cost are extended nonnegative reals. The cost includes the count of the first accepted attempt, when one exists, and otherwise sums all attempt counts. The quantified x0 does not occur in any definition or bound. Acceptance uses a non-strict inequality and the tail events use strict inequalities.",
