    {
      "id": "semantic-audit:ASTIS-RT-20260911-SPHMCGaussianArcLaw",
      "kind": "semantic-audit",
      "label": "ASTIS-RT-20260911-SPHMCGaussianArcLaw",
      "status": "fidelity-mismatch",
      "subtitle": "companion:sphmc-gaussian-arc-law · domain-mismatch",
      "summary": "Blind reconstruction and seven-slot semantic comparison for AutoSamplingTheory.ExampleCases.SmoothedPicardHMC.GaussianArcLaw.gaussian_arc_law.",
      "column": 7,
      "url": "lean-foundations.html?view=semantic",
      "source_url": "https://arxiv.org/html/2602.01338v1",
      "original_theorem": "E is a finite-dimensional real inner product space with its Borel measurable structure; second countability is explicitly available. Zero dimension is allowed.\neta>0 and h in E. The input is the actual independent product law of X=h+sqrt(eta)G1 and Z=sqrt(eta)G2, where G1,G2 are standard Gaussian.\nFor r in the real line set theta=pi*r/2, gamma_r=h+sin(theta)(X-h)+cos(theta)Z and v_r=(pi/2)(cos(theta)(X-h)-sin(theta)Z). Source consumption only requires r in [0,1].\nThe square of sqrt(eta) is eta. The map (h,r,x,z) to (gamma_r,v_r) is jointly measurable. For every real r its pushforward of the displayed input product law is N(h,eta I) product N(0,(pi/2)^2 eta I); in particular the position and velocity are independent. For every deterministic input pair, gamma has derivative v at every real r. Its positions at 0 and 1 are h+z and x; its velocities are (pi/2)(x-h) and -(pi/2)z.",
      "reconstructed_theorem": "Let E be any finite-dimensional real inner product space, equipped with its Borel measurable space and a second-countable topology. Let gamma be the centered Gaussian probability measure on E with identity covariance. For every real eta > 0 and every h in E, put a = pi/2, c = sqrt(eta), mu_h = (x -> h + c x)_* gamma, nu = (z -> c z)_* gamma, and rho = (z -> a c z)_* gamma. For p = (x,z) in E x E and t in R define F_h(t,p) = h + sin(a t)(x-h) + cos(a t)z and V_h(t,p) = a[cos(a t)(x-h) - sin(a t)z]. Then all of the following hold: (1) c^2 = eta. (2) The map ((b,t),(x,z)) -> (F_b(t,(x,z)), V_b(t,(x,z))) from (E x R) x (E x E) to E x E is measurable. (3) For every r in R, the actual pushforward of the independent product measure mu_h x nu by p -> (F_h(r,p), V_h(r,p)) equals mu_h x rho. Thus this is equality of joint measures, including independence of the two output coordinates. (4) For every p in E x E and every r in R, the real-parameter E-valued function t -> F_h(t,p) has derivative V_h(r,p) at r. (5) For every p = (x,z) in E x E, F_h(0,p) = h+z, F_h(1,p) = x, V_h(0,p) = a(x-h), and V_h(1,p) = -a z. All scalar-vector products above are real scalar multiplication; c is the nonnegative square root. There are no time restrictions, extra regularity hypotheses, or additional distributional assumptions.",
      "fidelity_verdict": "domain-mismatch",
