          "necessity": "mathematically-necessary",
          "proposed_change": "Add h>=0 to the printed h<=1/beta condition for the descent and one-step energy assertions. Preserve the pinned source separately. Do not alter the signed-parameter Hilbert-space generalization or claim to repair the singular inverse-power rate.",
          "reconstructed_statement": "For a C2 alpha-convex beta-smooth Euclidean function with beta>0, restrict the step to 0<=h<=1/beta and set T(x)=x-h grad f(x). Then f(T(x))-f(x)<=-(h/2)||grad f(x)||^2. For every comparator z, ||T(x)-z||^2+2h(f(T(x))-f(z))<=(1-alpha h)||x-z||^2. This proposal corrects only the missing nonnegative-step domain in Lemma3.1 and (3.1)/(3.3); it makes no claim about the printed inverse-power rate at singular endpoints.",
          "statement_sha256": "8cecd69957c4f2dea39ebb815b5499f7bd3ada6c1396fbb1e6a900b41d9f25a1",
          "justification": "The source descent estimate and its general-comparator energy conclusion both fail for negative steps even with a smooth strongly convex quadratic; the proof multiplies inequalities by h or 2h and requires their signs.",
          "minimality_evidence": "Adding only h>=0 preserves the admissible equality case h=0. The beta>0 reciprocal domain is unchanged. No stronger curvature, smoothness or minimizer hypothesis is proposed. Removing this lower bound admits the explicit counterexamples.",
          "reference_or_counterexample": "{\"counterexample\": {\"descent_missing_nonnegative_step\": \"Take f(t)=t^2/2 on R, beta=1,h=-1,x=1. The printed h<=1/beta holds; T(1)=2 and f(2)-f(1)=3/2 exceeds -(h/2)|grad f(1)|^2=1/2.\", \"general_comparator_energy_missing_nonnegative_step\": \"Take f(t)=t^2/2, alpha=1/2,beta=1,h=-1,x=0,z=1. This f is alpha-strongly convex and beta-smooth. T(0)=0; ||T(0)-1||^2+2h(f(T(0))-f(1))=2 while (1-alpha h)||0-1||^2=3/2. Thus the printed arbitrary-comparator claim fails.\", \"general_comparator_weighted_missing_nonnegative_step\": \"Same f,alpha,beta,h, take x0=0,z=1,N=2. q=3/2, g_N=-1/2, S2=5/2. Left side is 5/2 while right side is 9/4. This concerns the general-comparator extension, not a claim that the minimizer weighted inequality itself fails for negative h.\"}, \"url\": \"https://arxiv.org/html/2605.07006v1#S3\"}",
          "review": {
            "state": "accepted",
            "reviewer": "converse_source_review",
            "independent_from_formalizer": true,
            "independent_from_decoder": true,
            "evidence": "Independent refreshed exact-proposal review. I read only the current candidate packet repair-reviewer-2-0.json; no registry, prior verdict or source-review output was read. I am distinct from andi-gdv-worker and value_blind. This decision is bound to the current packet and its current publication context, not automatically inherited from any earlier acceptance. Primary-source statements checked against my direct first-hand reading of the pinned official arXiv v1 Section 3: C² Euclidean setting; Lemma 3.1 and Theorem 3.4 print h≤1/β; (3.3) explicitly permits arbitrary z. The new shared-helper implementation does not change those source facts.\n\nThe reference_or_counterexample field is a valid sorted-key JSON string. Decoding preserves the primary source URL and all three explicit counterexamples. For f(t)=t²/2, β=1, h=−1, x=1, T(1)=2 and the descent increment 3/2 exceeds 1/2. For general h<0 its excess above the proposed bound is (h²−h)/2>0. For the energy example f(t)=t²/2, α=1/2, β=1, x=0, z=1, the valid but nonmaximal strong-convexity modulus α gives LHS=1−h and RHS=1−h/2. Thus every h<0 fails, and h=−1 yields 2>3/2. This uses the permitted negative comparator gap and a comparator that is not a minimizer. It does not purport to falsify every minimizer-only specialization. The N=2 weighted diagnostic has q=3/2, S₂=5/2 and gap −1/2, so 2hS₂ gap=5/2>9/4=q²R². This supplemental example is correctly confined to the arbitrary-comparator extension.\n\nNecessity and minimality are unchanged and independently valid: the preceding counterexamples require excluding every negative step for a guarantee uniform over the original objective and comparator classes. At h=0 the step is identity, descent is 0≤0 and the energy inequality is equality. Hence strict h>0 would unnecessarily remove a valid endpoint; no added curvature, regularity or minimizer hypothesis is warranted. β>0 is the retained source reciprocal domain, where βh≤1 is equivalent to h≤1/β. This conclusion is about the combined descent/arbitrary-comparator assertions, not a claim of necessity for every individual special case.\n\nSufficiency with exact normalization: write g=∇f(x), s=x−hg and v=x−z. The quadratic upper model gives f(s)−f(x)≤(−h+βh²/2)‖g‖²≤−h‖g‖²/2 for 0≤h≤1/β. Lower support gives f(z)≥f(x)−⟨g,v⟩+(α/2)‖v‖². Multiply the resulting gap estimate by 2h≥0 and add ‖v−hg‖²=‖v‖²−2h⟨g,v⟩+h²‖g‖². Both gradient terms cancel and leave exactly (1−αh)‖v‖². The proof permits every z and signed gaps. Descent alone uses only the upper model, so Lemma 3.1 must retain its smoothness-only objective assumption; the joint statement includes convexity because its energy conclusion needs lower support.\n\nCurrent-context review: the module now imports GradientDescentBasic and calls GradientDescentBasic.gradient_step_descent_of_quadratic_upper_bound hh hstep hu x for hd in the energy proof and at X n in the weighted proof. The private descent proof is no longer present. The authored lesson now names the canonical shared descent interface and the ASTIS dependency list includes both it and StrongConvexFirstOrder.firstOrder_lower_bound_of_strongConvexOn. Its formulas correctly explain the upper-model calculation supplied by that interface. The remaining proof still multiplies lower support and descent by 2h, expands the squared norm, and cancels. The weighted branch explicitly assumes αh≤1 for q≥0, derives decreasing function values using the same shared descent call, permits signed forcing, and uses nonnegative q powers before dropping the final squared norm. These code and lesson changes preserve the repaired mathematical boundary and do not turn this review into a certification of unprovided helper implementation/build artifacts. The local public statement and supplied decoder continue to agree on arbitrary comparator, signed α/β and h≥0.\n\nScope safeguards: C¹ Hilbert-space regularity, signed moduli and the division-free βh≤1 condition outside β>0 are local generalizations, not source repairs. The one-step bound needs no additional αh≤1 assumption. The repair remains confined to the missing nonnegative step in Lemma 3.1 and (3.1)/(3.3), with original source retained separately. It neither interprets inverse powers at q=0 or q=1 nor normalizes at h=0 or N=0; it does not certify the inverse-power or convex normalized rates, nor assert minimizer existence. No change in the revised module or lesson invalidates necessity, minimality, constants or source-domain preservation. Accept the exact proposal in this refreshed context.",
