      "source_url": "",
      "original_theorem": "Chewi arXiv:2605.07006v1 Section3 assumes C2 Euclidean functions. For an alpha-strongly convex beta-smooth function, the actual step is T(x)=x-h grad f(x), and the printed step restriction is h<=1/beta. Theorem3.4 (3.1) gives ||T(x)-xstar||² <= (1-alpha h)||x-xstar||²-2h(f(T(x))-f(xstar)). Remark (3.3) explicitly allows any comparator z in place of the minimizer. Lemma3.1 supplies f(T(x))-f(x)<=-h/2||grad f(x)||² from the quadratic upper model. The source does not explicitly state h>=0 in the step restriction. The proof after Lemma3.5 sets q=1-alpha h, applies the forced recurrence with negative function-gap forcing, then uses the nonincreasing function values to obtain 2h sum(k=0..N-1) q^k (f(xN)-f(xstar)) <= q^N ||x0-xstar||². Lemma3.5 assumes a strictly positive coefficient. Dividing the geometric sum gives alpha R²/[2(q^(-N)-1)]; alpha=0 is interpreted as R²/(2Nh). This audit covers the finite weighted inequality, not an inverse-power equality at singular endpoints.",
      "reconstructed_theorem": "For every real Hilbert space E (a complete real inner product space, with no finite-dimensional restriction), every function f:E→ℝ, and all real constants α, β, h, assume f is globally continuously Fréchet differentiable; for every x,y∈E and t∈[0,1], f((1−t)x+t y) ≤ (1−t)f(x)+t f(y)−(α/2)t(1−t)‖y−x‖²; h≥0; βh≤1; and for every x,y∈E, f(y) ≤ f(x)+⟨∇f(x),y−x⟩+(β/2)‖y−x‖². Here ∇f(x) is the actual Riesz representative of the Fréchet derivative of f at x, and define T(x)=x−h∇f(x). Assume additionally αh≤1, and write q=1−αh, so q≥0. Then for every x₀,z∈E and every natural number N≥0, with x_N=T^[N](x₀) denoting exactly N repetitions of T, (2h ∑_{k=0}^{N−1} q^k)(f(x_N)−f(z)) ≤ q^N ‖x₀−z‖². The empty sum for N=0 is zero, T^[0] is the identity, and q^0=1 even when q=0.",
