      "source_url": "",
      "original_theorem": "Chewi arXiv:2605.07006v1, Section3 assumes C2 Euclidean objectives. In the C² Euclidean setting with an attained minimum f★=f(x★), β-smoothness and the PL inequality with α>0 give the displayed last-iterate function-value bound, for the printed restriction h≤1/β. No convexity assumption appears. Definition2.5: alpha>0 and ||grad f(x)||²>=2alpha(f(x)-f(xstar)) for all x. Section2 assumes a minimizer xstar and fstar=f(xstar). Theorem3.6 conclusion: f(T^N x0)-fstar <= (1-alpha h)^N (f(x0)-fstar), T(x)=x-h grad f(x). The printed step condition is h<=1/beta, without explicit h>=0.",
      "reconstructed_theorem": "For every real inner-product space E whose induced norm makes it complete, every function f:E→ℝ, every triple of real parameters α,β,h, and every point z∈E, assume (i) z is a global minimizer, meaning f(z)≤f(x) for every x∈E; (ii) h≥0; (iii) βh≤1; (iv) for every x,y∈E, f(y)≤f(x)+⟨gradient f(x),y−x⟩+(β/2)‖y−x‖²; and (v) for every x∈E, 2α(f(x)−f(z))≤‖gradient f(x)‖². Then for every starting point x₀∈E and every natural number N, define the actual iterates by xₙ₊₁=xₙ−h gradient f(xₙ). The conclusion is f(x_N)−f(z)≤(1−αh)^N(f(x₀)−f(z)). Equivalently, x_N=T^[N](x₀) for T(x)=x−h gradient f(x), where T^[0] is the identity; thus N=0 is included. The gradient is the Riesz vector of the totalized Fréchet derivative, so the notation carries no differentiability assumption, and none is separately stated. α and β may be any real numbers satisfying the displayed assumptions: positivity of α or β, α≤β, and nonnegativity of 1−αh are not additional hypotheses. h may be zero. The bound concerns these concrete constant-step iterates, with the exact factor (1−αh)^N and coefficient 1 on the initial objective gap; no constants or iterates are existentially chosen.",
