      "original_theorem": "E is a finite-dimensional real inner product space with its Borel measurable structure and positive real dimension d=finrank(E). The real-valued function f is differentiable everywhere, and its actual gradient is beta-Lipschitz.\neta,beta,B>0, ell>=2, and 64 beta^2(ell*d/B+ell^2)<=1/eta^2. All denominators in the conclusion are strictly positive.\nh,x_plus in E satisfy norm(h-x_plus)<=sqrt(d eta). The independent input law is mu=N(h,eta I) product N(0,eta I), represented by the affine pushforwards of standard Gaussian.\nFor a fixed real r, theta=pi*r/2, gamma_r(x,z)=h+sin(theta)(x-h)+cos(theta)z, v_r(x,z)=(pi/2)(cos(theta)(x-h)-sin(theta)z), W_r=<v_r,grad f(x_plus)-grad f(gamma_r)>, tau_B(w)=max(abs(w)-B,0), and F_r=exp(2ell*tau_B(W_r))-1.\nW_r and F_r are measurable, F_r is integrable under the actual input law mu, and its integral is at most 2 exp(-min(B^2/(40 beta^2 d eta^2),B/(8 beta eta))).",
      "reconstructed_theorem": "Let E be any finite-dimensional real inner-product space equipped with its Borel measurable structure, and write d = dim_R(E). Let f: E -> R be differentiable everywhere, and let eta, beta, B, ell be real numbers satisfying eta > 0, beta > 0, B > 0, ell >= 2, and d > 0. Assume that the gradient of f is globally beta-Lipschitz. For every h, xp in E and every r in R, assume ||h - xp|| <= sqrt(d * eta) and 64 * beta^2 * (ell * d / B + ell^2) <= 1 / eta^2. Let gamma be the centered identity-covariance Gaussian probability measure on E, and let mu be the product of the pushforward of gamma by z -> h + sqrt(eta) z and the pushforward of gamma by z -> sqrt(eta) z. For p = (x, y) in E x E, define W(p) = <(pi/2) * (cos(pi*r/2) * (x-h) - sin(pi*r/2) * y), gradient f(xp) - gradient f(h + sin(pi*r/2) * (x-h) + cos(pi*r/2) * y)> and F(p) = exp(2 * ell * max(|W(p)| - B, 0)) - 1. Then W and F are measurable, F is Bochner integrable with respect to mu, and the integral of F with respect to mu is at most 2 * exp(-min(B^2 / (40 * beta^2 * d * eta^2), B / (8 * beta * eta))).",
