      "reconstructed_theorem": "Let E be any finite-dimensional real inner-product space equipped with its Borel measurable structure and canonical Lebesgue measure λ. Let f:E→ℝ be differentiable everywhere, with globally β-Lipschitz gradient for some β≥0. For any η>0 satisfying βη<1 and arbitrary x₀,xₚ∈E, define h=x₀−η∇f(xₚ), P=N(0,ηI), G=N(x₀,ηI), q=N(h,ηI), and ν=Unif((0,1])⊗P. Set m(x)=∫⟨(π/2)[cos(πt/2)(x−h)−sin(πt/2)z], ∇f(xₚ)−∇f(h+sin(πt/2)(x−h)+cos(πt/2)z)⟩ν(d(t,z)), and V(x)=f(x)+‖x−x₀‖²/(2η). Then exp(−f) is G-integrable with strictly positive integral, exp(m) is q-integrable with strictly positive integral, and exp(−V) is λ-integrable with strictly positive integral. Moreover, the normalized exponential tilt of q by m equals both the normalized exponential tilt of G by −f and the probability measure with Lebesgue density exp(−V)/∫exp(−V)dλ.",
