      "original_theorem": "E is a finite-dimensional real inner-product Borel space, d=finrank(E)>0. The actual potential f is differentiable everywhere and its actual gradient is globally beta-Lipschitz. eta,beta,B>0 and ell>=2.\nSet g=grad f(x_plus), h=x0-eta*g. Assume norm(h-x_plus)<=sqrt(d*eta) and 64*beta^2*(ell*d/B+ell^2)<=1/eta^2. These residual and step assumptions are retained exactly.\nLet P=(z->sqrt(eta) z)_*stdGaussian(E), U=volume restricted to (0,1], nu=U product P and q=(z->h+sqrt(eta) z)_*stdGaussian(E). For s=(r,z), theta=pi*r/2, gamma=h+sin(theta)(x-h)+cos(theta)z and v=(pi/2)(cos(theta)(x-h)-sin(theta)z), define W(x,s)=<v,g-grad f(gamma)>.\nDefine m(x)=integral W(x,s) dnu(s), mB(x)=integral min(B,max(-B,W(x,s))) dnu(s), V(x)=integral max(abs(W(x,s))-B,0) dnu(s), qhat=q.tilted(mB), and K=min(B^2/(40*beta^2*d*eta^2),B/(8*beta*eta)). All functions and measures are actual definitions, not abstract witnesses.\nm, mB and V are measurable. For every x, V(x)>=0 and abs(m(x)-mB(x))<=V(x). The function J(x)=exp(2*ell*V(x))-1 is integrable under q and qhat, with respective integrals at most 2*exp(-K) and 2*exp(2*B-K). The function exp(2*ell*abs(m(x)-mB(x)))-1 is qhat-integrable and its integral is at most 2*exp(2*B-K).",
      "reconstructed_theorem": "Let E be a finite-dimensional real inner-product Borel space of real dimension d>0, f differentiable everywhere, eta,beta,B>0,ell>=2, and the actual gradient globally beta-Lipschitz. For arbitrary x0,xp assume norm(x0-eta grad f(xp)-xp)<=sqrt(d eta) and 64 beta^2(ell d/B+ell^2)<=eta^(-2). Define h=x0-eta grad f(xp), q=N(h,eta I), P=N(0,eta I), nu=Lebesgue restricted to (0,1] product P. With theta=pi t/2 define Y=h+sin(theta)(x-h)+cos(theta)z and S=< (pi/2)(cos(theta)(x-h)-sin(theta)z), grad f(xp)-grad f(Y) >. Let m(x)=integral S dnu, mB(x)=integral min(B,max(-B,S)) dnu, V(x)=integral max(abs(S)-B,0) dnu, qhat=q.tilted(mB), and K=min(B^2/(40 beta^2 d eta^2),B/(8 beta eta)). Then m,mB,V are measurable, and every x satisfies V(x)>=0 and abs(m(x)-mB(x))<=V(x). The function exp(2ell V)-1 is Bochner integrable under q with integral<=2exp(-K), and under qhat with integral<=2exp(2B-K). The function exp(2ell abs(m-mB))-1 is Bochner integrable under qhat with integral<=2exp(2B-K).",
