    {
      "id": "semantic-audit:ASTIS-RT-20260912-SPHMCNormalizedReferenceCall",
      "kind": "semantic-audit",
      "label": "ASTIS-RT-20260912-SPHMCNormalizedReferenceCall",
      "status": "fidelity-mismatch",
      "subtitle": "companion:sphmc-normalized-reference-call · domain-mismatch",
      "summary": "Blind reconstruction and seven-slot semantic comparison for AutoSamplingTheory.ExampleCases.SmoothedPicardHMC.NormalizedReferenceCall.normalized_reference_call.",
      "column": 7,
      "url": "lean-foundations.html?view=semantic",
      "source_url": "https://arxiv.org/html/2609.06906v1",
      "original_theorem": "E is a complete finite-dimensional real inner-product space with its Borel measurable structure and canonical volume; d=dim(E)>0. U is C2 and its genuine Hessian lies between alpha*norm(v)^2 and beta*norm(v)^2 for every x,v, with 0<alpha<=beta.\neta>=0,tau>0,r>=0. The actual probability nu satisfies W2(nu,law(X+sqrt(eta)*Z))^2<=ofReal(r^2), where X has the normalized Gibbs law volume.tilted(-U) and Z is an independent standard Gaussian. This is the outer input to the existing actual reference construction.\nA=eta+tau,a=alpha+1/A,c=beta+1/A,k=c/a. For p=(y,z), u(p)=y+sqrt(tau)*z and F_p(x)=U(x)+norm(x-u(p))^2/(2*A). Here a is a curvature lower bound, not an RGO variance.\nT_p(x)=x-c^(-1)*grad F_p(x). N(p) is the least n with norm(grad F_p(T_p^n(y)))^2<=a*d if such an n exists, otherwise zero; out(p)=T_p^N(p)(y). The inherited finite-hit proof eliminates the default branch.\nS(x)=sqrt(c)*x,R(x)=x/sqrt(c),G_p(x)=F_p(R(x)). pi_p=volume.tilted(-F_p) and pibar_p=volume.tilted(-G_p). Write H(mu,sigma)=law(X+sigma*Z) for independent X~mu,Z~stdGaussian(E). W2 squared is the extended nonnegative infimum of quadratic coupling costs.\nN and p->S(out(p)) are measurable. For every p, the actual output satisfies norm(grad F_p(out))^2<=a*d and every earlier iterate strictly fails that threshold. The scaled reference satisfies norm(grad G_p(S(out)))^2<=d/k. G_p is C2 with genuine Hessian bounds [a/c,1]. Both exponential Gibbs weights are integrable with strictly positive integrals, and both tilted laws are probability measures. S#pi_p=pibar_p and grad G_p(S(x))=c^(-1/2)*grad F_p(x). For every next variance t>=0, R#H(pibar_p,sqrt(t))=H(pi_p,sqrt(t/c)). For every actual probability nubar and t,B>=0, a supplied W2(nubar,H(pibar_p,sqrt(t)))^2<=ofReal(B) implies W2(R#nubar,H(pi_p,sqrt(t/c)))^2<=ofReal(B/c).",
      "reconstructed_theorem": "Let \\(E\\) be a complete finite-dimensional real inner-product space, equipped with its Borel measurable structure and canonical Lebesgue measure \\(dx\\), and suppose its dimension \\(d=\\dim_{\\mathbb R}E\\) is positive.\n\nLet \\(U:E\\to\\mathbb R\\) be \\(C^2\\), and let \\(0<\\alpha\\le\\beta\\) be real constants such that, for every \\(x,v\\in E\\),\n\\[\n\\alpha\\|v\\|^2\\le D^2U(x)[v,v]\\le\\beta\\|v\\|^2.\n\\]\n\nFor measures \\(\\mu,\\rho\\), write\n\\[\n\\mathcal W_2^2(\\mu,\\rho)\n=\n\\left[\n\\left(\n\\inf_{\\gamma:\\,\\gamma_1=\\mu,\\ \\gamma_2=\\rho}\n\\int\\|x-y\\|^2\\,d\\gamma(x,y)\n\\right)^{1/2}\n\\right]^2.\n\\]\nThe infimum, integral and powers here are taken in the extended nonnegative reals. Write \\(H_s\\mu\\) for the pushforward of \\(\\mu\\otimes\\mathcal N(0,sI)\\) under addition, for \\(s\\ge0\\).\n\nFor a real function \\(V\\), define its tilted Lebesgue measure by\n\\[\n\\operatorname{Tilt}(V)(dx)\n=\n\\frac{e^{-V(x)}}{\\int e^{-V(y)}\\,dy}\\,dx,\n\\]\ninterpreted using the supplied totalized integral and division conventions. This definition alone does not assert integrability or probability normalization.\n\nAssume \\(\\eta\\ge0\\), \\(\\tau>0\\), \\(r\\ge0\\), and that \\(\\nu\\) is a probability measure satisfying\n\\[\n\\mathcal W_2^2\\bigl(\\nu,H_\\eta\\operatorname{Tilt}(U)\\bigr)\\le r^2.\n\\]\n\nSet\n\\[\nA=\\eta+\\tau,\\qquad\na=\\alpha+A^{-1},\\qquad\nc=\\beta+A^{-1},\\qquad\nk=c/a.\n\\]\nFor each pair \\(p=(y,z)\\in E\\times E\\), define\n\\[\nu_p=y+\\sqrt\\tau\\,z,\\qquad\nF_p(x)=U(x)+\\frac{\\|x-u_p\\|^2}{2A},\n\\qquad\nT_p(x)=x-c^{-1}\\nabla F_p(x).\n\\]\nStarting at \\(x_{p,0}=y\\), let \\(x_{p,n}=T_p^{\\,n}(y)\\). Define\n\\[\nN(p)=\n\\begin{cases}\n\\min\\{n\\in\\mathbb N:\\|\\nabla F_p(x_{p,n})\\|^2\\le ad\\},\n&\\text{if this set is nonempty},\\\\\n0,&\\text{otherwise},\n\\end{cases}\n\\qquad\n\\operatorname{out}(p)=x_{p,N(p)}.\n\\]\nAlso define\n\\[\nSx=\\sqrt c\\,x,\\qquad Rx=c^{-1/2}x,\\qquad\nG_p(x)=F_p(Rx),\\qquad\n\\pi_p=\\operatorname{Tilt}(F_p),\\qquad\n\\bar\\pi_p=\\operatorname{Tilt}(G_p).\n\\]\n\nThen \\(N:E\\times E\\to\\mathbb N\\) and \\(p\\mapsto S(\\operatorname{out}(p))\\) are measurable. Moreover, **for every pair \\(p\\in E\\times E\\)**:\n\n1. The output satisfies the stopping criterion, and every earlier iterate fails it strictly:\n   \\[\n   \\|\\nabla F_p(\\operatorname{out}(p))\\|^2\\le ad,\\qquad\n   \\forall j<N(p),\\quad \\|\\nabla F_p(x_{p,j})\\|^2>ad.\n   \\]\n   Consequently a successful index exists for every \\(p\\); the fallback branch cannot represent a trajectory that never meets the criterion.\n\n2. The scaled output satisfies\n   \\[\n   \\|\\nabla G_p(S(\\operatorname{out}(p)))\\|^2\\le d/k.\n   \\]\n\n3. \\(G_p\\) is \\(C^2\\), and for every \\(x,v\\in E\\),\n   \\[\n   \\frac ac\\|v\\|^2\\le D^2G_p(x)[v,v]\\le\\|v\\|^2.\n   \\]\n\n4. Both \\(e^{-F_p}\\) and \\(e^{-G_p}\\) are Lebesgue integrable, and both of their integrals are strictly positive. Thus \\(\\pi_p\\) and \\(\\bar\\pi_p\\) are probability measures, and\n   \\[\n   S_\\#\\pi_p=\\bar\\pi_p.\n   \\]\n\n5. For every \\(x\\in E\\),\n   \\[\n   \\nabla G_p(Sx)=c^{-1/2}\\nabla F_p(x).\n   \\]\n\n6. For every real \\(t\\ge0\\),\n   \\[\n   R_\\#(H_t\\bar\\pi_p)=H_{t/c}\\pi_p.\n   \\]\n\n7. For every probability measure \\(\\bar\\nu\\) on \\(E\\) and every pair of real numbers \\(t,B\\ge0\\),\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal W_2^2(\\bar\\nu,H_t\\bar\\pi_p)\\le B\n   \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\n   \\mathcal W_2^2(R_\\#\\bar\\nu,H_{t/c}\\pi_p)\\le B/c.\n   \\]\n\nInterpretation and scope:\n\n- The stopping threshold is exactly \\(ad\\), with equality accepted and iteration zero eligible. There is no arbitrary accuracy parameter, iteration bound, expected runtime bound, or stated computational implementation of the existence test.\n- The conclusions hold pointwise for all pairs \\((y,z)\\); the statement does not draw \\(y\\) from \\(\\nu\\), draw \\(z\\) from a Gaussian, or impose any joint distribution on the input pair.\n- The initial measure \\(\\nu\\) and radius \\(r\\) appear only in the assumptions. They do not enter the iteration, stopping threshold, or subsequent conclusions. The parameter \\(\\eta\\) enters through \\(A\\).\n- The final \\(B\\) bounds a **squared** transport distance. Rescaling divides that bound and Gaussian variance by \\(c\\).\n- No separate finite-second-moment hypotheses are stated. The transport costs retain their extended-real meaning, with the displayed finite upper bounds imposed where required.\n- The explicit integrability and normalization conclusions concern \\(F_p\\) and \\(G_p\\). The statement does not separately list these conclusions for \\(U\\).\n- Nothing here asserts that the deterministic stopping output itself has distribution \\(\\pi_p\\) or approximates it in transport distance.",
