e product m(s) x g means the new noise is independent conditional on the complete state. for every state,v>0 and bplus=b+1/v. n,out and update are jointly measurable. for every state/sample/noise triple,the actual stopped gradient-square is at most (alpha+bplus)*d and every earlier iterate strictly exceeds it; grad f_p(w)=grad v(w)+bplus*(w-uplus). the true source potential satisfies w_p(w)=f_p(w)+b*v^(-1)/(2*(b+v^(-1)))*norm(u-y)^2,so their gradients and all gd iterates from x with step1/(beta+bplus) coincide. the scaled reference sqrt(beta+bplus)*out has gradient-square at most d/((beta+bplus)/(alpha+bplus)) for f_p(./sqrt(beta+bplus)). there exists an actual markov state transition p with p(s)=dirac(s) when theta<=b,and otherwise p(s)=update(s,.)#(m(s) x g). its single-step reference-deleted marginal is dirac(project(s)) in the first branch and oldupdate(s,.)#(m(s) x g) in the second. let \\(e\\) be a finite-dimensional real inner-product space, equipped with its borel measurable structure, and let \\(d=\\dim_{\\mathbb r}e>0\\). let \\(v:e\\to\\mathbb r\\) be \\(c^2\\). assume nonnegative real constants \\(\\alpha,\\beta\\) satisfy \\(0<\\alpha\\le\\beta\\), and for every \\(x,w\\in e\\), \\[ \\alpha\\|w\\|^2\\le d^2v(x)[w,w]\\le\\beta\\|w\\|^2. \\] define the state space \\[ s=\\mathbb r_{\\ge0}\\times e\\times e\\times\\mathbb n\\times e^{\\mathbb n}. \\] write a state as \\(s=(b,c,r,k,h)\\): one nonnegative scalar, two vectors, one nonnegative integer, and one infinite vector sequence. let \\(\\eta,\\tau:s\\to\\mathbb r\\) be arbitrary measurable functions that are strictly positive at every state. let \\(m:s\\rightsquigarrow e\\) be any markov kernel, and let \\(a\\ge0\\) be any threshold. for each \\(s\\), define \\[ v(s)=\\frac{\\eta(s)+\\tau(s)}{\\beta+b}, \\qquad b^+(s)=b+\\max\\{v(s)^{-1},0\\}, \\] where the latter is a nonnegative real. for \\(p=(s,y,g)\\in s\\times e\\times e\\), define \\[ o(p)=y+\\sqrt{\\frac{\\tau(s)}{\\beta+b}}\\,g, \\qquad c^+(p)=\\frac{bc+v(s)^{-1}o(p)}{b^+(s)}, \\] and the two functions of \\(x\\in e\\) \\[ f_p(x)=v(x)+\\frac{b^+(s)}2\\|x-c^+(p)\\|^2, \\] \\[ w_p(x)=v(x)+\\frac b2\\|x-c\\|^2+ \\frac{v(s)^{-1}}2\\|x-o(p)\\|^2. \\] set \\[ t_p(x)=x-\\frac1{\\beta+b^+(s)}\\nabla f_p(x), \\qquad q_n(p)=\\|\\nabla f_p(t_p^n(y))\\|^2. \\] here \\(t_p^0(y)=y\\). define \\(n(p)\\) to be the least \\(n\\in\\mathbb n\\) satisfying \\[ q_n(p)\\le(\\alpha+b^+(s))d \\] if such an \\(n\\) exists, and \\(0\\) otherwise. define the output \\[ u(p)=t_p^{n(p)}(y). \\] define the full state update by \\[ u(p)=(b^+(s),c^+(p),u(p),k+1,h^+_p), \\] where \\[ h^+_p(0)=o(p),\\qquad h^+_p(n+1)=h(n). \\] define the projection that deletes the third state coordinate, \\[ \\pi(b,c,r,k,h)=(b,c,k,h), \\] and the corresponding reduced update \\[ u_0(p)=(b^+(s),c^+(p),k+1,h^+_p). \\] the theorem asserts all of the following. 1. for every \\(s\\), \\(v(s)>0\\), and consequently \\[ b^+(s)=b+v(s)^{-1} \\] as real numbers. 2. the maps \\(n:s\\times e\\times e\\to\\mathbb n\\), \\(u:s\\times e\\times e\\to e\\), and \\(u:s\\times e\\times e\\to s\\) are measurable. 3. for every \\(p=(s,y,g)\\), the selected index actually satisfies its predicate: \\[ q_{n(p)}(p)\\le(\\alpha+b^+(s))d. \\] every earlier index fails it strictly: \\[ j<n(p)\\implies q_j(p)>(\\alpha+b^+(s))d. \\] thus a satisfying finite index exists for every input; the fallback in the definition does not conceal a failure to meet the predicate. 4. for every \\(p\\) and every \\(x\\in e\\), \\[ \\nabla f_p(x)=\\nabla v(x)+b^+(s)(x-c^+(p)), \\] and \\[ w_p(x)=f_p(x)+ \\frac{b\\,v(s)^{-1}}{2(b+v(s)^{-1})}\\|c-o(p)\\|^2. \\] in particular, \\(\\nabla w_p(x)=\\nabla f_p(x)\\). for every \\(n\\in\\mathbb n\\), gradient descent on \\(w_p\\), with step size \\(1/(\\beta+b^+(s))\\) and initial point \\(y\\), has exactly the same \\(n\\)-th iterate as \\(t_p^n(y)\\). 5. if \\(l=\\beta+b^+(s)\\) and \\(\\widetilde f_p(x)=f_p(x/\\sqrt l)\\), then \\[ \\|\\nabla\\widetilde f_p(\\sqrt l\\,u(p))\\|^2 \\le \\frac{d}{l/(\\alpha+b^+(s))}. \\] the gradient here differentiates the displayed spatial argument, with \\(p\\) fixed. 6. there exists a markov kernel \\(p:s\\rightsquigarrow s\\) satisfying both kernel identities below, for every state \\(s\\): \\[ p(s)= \\begin{cases} \\delta_s,&a\\le b,\\\\ u(s,\\cdot,\\cdot)_\\#\\bigl(m(s)\\otimes\\gamma_e\\bigr),&b<a, \\end{cases} \\] where \\(\\gamma_e\\) is the centered gaussian probability measure with identity covariance. thus, conditional on the input state in the explicit sense of its transition law, an active transition draws \\(y\\sim m(s)\\) and \\(g\\sim\\gamma_e\\) independently, then applies \\(u\\). a state already at or above threshold stays fixed. the projected law satisfies \\[ \\pi_\\#p(s)= \\begin{cases} \\delta_{\\pi(s)},&a\\le b,\\\\ u_0(s,\\cdot,\\cdot)_\\#\\bigl(m(s)\\otimes\\gamma_e\\bigr),&b<a. \\end{cases} \\] **scope and ambiguity notes** - all assertions are universally quantified over the admissible space, potential, constants, functions, markov kernel, and threshold. the deterministic assertions hold for every input triple, not merely almost surely. - the vector \\(r\\) stored in the third state coordinate is replaced by \\(u(p)\\). the inner iteration starts at the newly supplied \\(y\\), not at \\(r\\). nevertheless, \\(\\eta,\\tau\\), and \\(m\\) may depend on every coordinate of the full state, including \\(r\\), the counter, and the entire sequence. - the predicate defining \\(n\\) concerns the squared gradient norm. the threshold test \\(a\\le b\\) is a separate test governing the outer transition. - the projected identity remains indexed by the full state \\(s\\). it does not assert that the projected process is an autonomous markov chain: the right side may depend on the deleted coordinate through \\(\\eta,\\tau\\), or \\(m\\). - no assumptions relate \\(m(s)\\) to \\(v\\) or to a target distribution. the theorem gives no invariance, sampling accuracy, mixing, convergence in law, estimator error, outer threshold-reaching guarantee, or quantitative bound on the number of inner iterations. - the sequence update prepends an observation to an infinite sequence; no assumption identifies the sequence with a valid past trajectory. - the supplied definitions use total real inversion and division. the hypotheses and positivity conclusion ensure the denominators appearing in the displayed mathematical constructions are positive. - no source identity or intended application is determined by this reconstruction. accepted algorithm3.3 and section6.3: actual reference-carrying recursive state transition autosamplingtheory.examplecases.smoothedpicardhmc.referencecarryingkernel.reference_carrying_kernel autosamplingtheory/examplecases/smoothedpicardhmc/referencecarryingkernel.lean true root-samplinglib-writer blind_reconstruction_v 8ba10d6ab1b63287a4c5e4cf3bf243dc1411de21e91af17df2aa82d85ccc0e19 accepted log_depth_source_review objects original-coordinate sampler output,new gaussian,rgo update and reference. full state,actual product kernel,same pre-noise initial point,true potentials and first-hit output. explicit-elaboration decoder recovered noise scale,updated center and stored reference. domains normalized euclidean valid recursive states. general beta,coordinate-free positive dimension,arbitrary old reference and history. different disclosed extensions; positive curvature and positive eta,tau retained. quantifiers call and update under current full state. deterministic conclusions for all states,inputs and noise; exists full-state markov kernel. explicit-elaboration projection remains indexed by full state,no autonomous projected chain. assumptions actual source call and stopping cutoff. arbitrary actual markov m,measurable positive parameters,supplied nonnegative precision threshold. different no m accuracy,old reference admissibility or valid-history premise. conclusion next rgo parameters and actual reference update. joint measu