    {
      "id": "semantic-audit:ASTIS-RT-20260912-SPHMCTerminalFORSKernel",
      "kind": "semantic-audit",
      "label": "ASTIS-RT-20260912-SPHMCTerminalFORSKernel",
      "status": "fidelity-mismatch",
      "subtitle": "companion:sphmc-terminal-fors-kernel · domain-mismatch",
      "summary": "Blind reconstruction and seven-slot semantic comparison for AutoSamplingTheory.ExampleCases.SmoothedPicardHMC.TerminalFORSKernel.terminal_fors_kernel.",
      "column": 7,
      "url": "lean-foundations.html?view=semantic",
      "source_url": "https://arxiv.org/html/2609.06906v1",
      "original_theorem": "E is a finite-dimensional real inner-product Borel space with d=dim(E)>0; S is any measurable state space. V:E->R is fixed and C2. Its genuine Hessian lies between alpha*norm(v)^2 and beta*norm(v)^2, with nonnegative alpha<=beta and beta>0.\nThe functions b:S->R and u,initial:S->E are measurable and b(s)>0. The fixed parameters ell,eps satisfy ell>=2 and0<eps<=1/2. Every state satisfies 64*beta*(sqrt(d*(ell+log(1/eps)))+ell+log(1/eps))<=b(s). No execution reachability of this terminal domain is assumed or concluded.\nSet A(s)=1/b(s), F_s(x)=V(x)+b(s)/2*norm(x-u(s))^2 and T_s(x)=x-(beta+b(s))^(-1)*grad F_s(x). N(s) is the least n with norm(grad F_s(T_s^n(initial(s))))^2<=d*b(s), with fallback zero if no n exists. Set xp(s)=T_s^N(s)(initial(s)), g(s)=grad V(xp(s)) and h(s)=u(s)-A(s)*g(s). Finite attainment and the reference residual are proved, not additional hypotheses.\nLet nu0 be Lebesgue restricted to Ioc(0,1) product standardGaussian, and q_s the law of h(s)+sqrt(A(s))*Z. For z=(t,zeta), the cached arc estimator is inner((pi/2)*(cos(pi*t/2)*(x-h)-sin(pi*t/2)*zeta),g-grad V(h+sin(pi*t/2)*(x-h)+cos(pi*t/2)*zeta)). W_s uses zeta=sqrt(A(s))*Z and clips this scalar to[-1,1]. The arc time uniform is distinct from the acceptance uniform on Icc(0,1).\nAn attempt p=(x,n,(zseq,v)) has law q_s product Poisson(2) product (infinitePi nu0 product uniformIcc(0,1)). It accepts iff v<=product over i:Fin n of(1+W_s(x,zseq(i)))/2. rho_s is the infinite product of independent attempts. output_s is the proposal at the first accepting attempt, or0 if none. queryCount sums each batch size when all earlier attempts failed, including the successful batch. The charged sampling-stage count is1+queryCount.\nThe target pi_s is the Gaussian law u(s)+sqrt(A(s))*Z tilted by -V. The original auxiliary law nua_s uses Gaussian variance A(s) instead of the standard Gaussian. Wa_s clips the same cached estimator without scaling inside. The stream map Phi_s scales each auxiliary Gaussian coordinate and preserves every proposal, batch size, arc time and acceptance uniform. The old program default is u(s).\nN and xp are measurable, the actual terminal gradient threshold holds and every earlier iterate fails it, and norm(h-xp)<=sqrt(d*A). There exists a single Markov kernel R on the whole state space whose fiber is the measurable first-success program output law; never-hit has rho_s measure zero and the actual sampling-stage integral of1+queryCount is at most1+2*exp(2). Each pi_s is a probability, pi_s and R(s) are mutually absolutely continuous, both RN ell-powers are integrable under their respective denominator laws with strictly positive integrals at most1+eps^2, and both log-integral/(ell-1) bounds are at most eps^2. Phi_s pushes rho_s to the original retry law, preserves acceptance and full-batch counts on every stream, and makes old default-u output equal to new default-zero output almost everywhere.",
      "reconstructed_theorem": "The statement concerns a measurable family of explicitly defined rejection-sampling programs, including their deterministic initialization, output probability laws, moment bounds relative to a tilted Gaussian target, and a change of auxiliary coordinates.\n\nLet E be any finite-dimensional real inner-product space with its normed additive group structure and Borel measurable structure. Write d = dimℝ E, regarded as a real number, and assume d > 0. Let S be any measurable space; no measure or probability law on S is assumed.\n\nLet V : E → ℝ be C². Let α, β be nonnegative real numbers such that α ≤ β and β > 0. Assume, pointwise for every x, v ∈ E,\nα‖v‖² ≤ D²V(x)[v,v] ≤ β‖v‖².\nStrict positivity of α is not required.\n\nLet b : S → ℝ and u, initial : S → E be measurable, with b(s) > 0 for every s. Let ell and eps be real numbers satisfying ell ≥ 2 and 0 < eps ≤ 1/2. Assume, for every s,\nb(s) ≥ 64β [√(d(ell + log(1/eps))) + ell + log(1/eps)].\n\nAll the following definitions use these data.\n\nFor s ∈ S and x ∈ E, define\nF_s(x) = V(x) + b(s)‖x − u(s)‖²/2,\nT_s(x) = x − (β + b(s))⁻¹ ∇F_s(x),\nQ_n(s) = ‖∇F_s(T_s^[n](initial(s)))‖²,\nwhere the zeroth iterate is the identity.\n\nDefine N(s) to be the least n ∈ ℕ with Q_n(s) ≤ d b(s), if such an n exists, and otherwise define N(s) = 0. Define\nxp(s) = T_s^[N(s)](initial(s)),\nA(s) = 1/b(s),\ng(s) = ∇V(xp(s)),\nh(s) = u(s) − A(s)g(s).\n\nLet γ be the centered Gaussian probability on E with identity covariance. Let λ₊ be Lebesgue measure restricted to (0,1], and let λ₀ be Lebesgue measure restricted to [0,1]. Define\nν = λ₊ ⊗ γ,\nq_s = (z ↦ h(s) + √A(s) z)₊γ,\nwhere the subscript denotes pushforward.\n\nFor x,z ∈ E and t ∈ ℝ, put θ = πt/2 and define the clipped weight\nW_s(x,(t,z)) =\nmin(1, max(−1,\n  ⟨(π/2)[cos(θ)(x−h(s)) − sin(θ)√A(s)z],\n    g(s) − ∇V(h(s) + sin(θ)(x−h(s)) + cos(θ)√A(s)z)\n  ⟩)).\n\nA trial is a tuple p = (x,k,a,r) with x ∈ E, k ∈ ℕ, a : ℕ → ℝ × E, and r ∈ ℝ. Its law is\nq_s ⊗ [Poisson(2) ⊗ (ν^ℕ ⊗ λ₀)].\nLet ρ_s be the countable product of this trial law over n ∈ ℕ. Thus a sample path ω is a sequence of trials ω_n = (x_n,k_n,a_n,r_n). Under the specified product law, trial coordinates have the stated independent distributions, and the auxiliary pairs within each trial have law ν independently.\n\nThe base acceptance predicate is\nAcc_s(x,k,a,r) ⇔\nr ≤ ∏_{i=0}^{k−1} (1 + W_s(x,a(i)))/2.\nThe empty product for k = 0 is 1.\n\nDefine Y_s(ω) to be x_n at the least accepted trial n, if any trial is accepted, and otherwise define Y_s(ω) = 0 ∈ E.\n\nDefine the extended nonnegative cost\nC_s(ω) =\n1 + ∑_{n=0}^∞\n  1_{all trials i<n fail Acc_s} k_n.\nThe indicator condition includes the successful trial’s count, since it requires only earlier trials to fail. The initial additive constant is 1. This cost definition does not explicitly count the number N(s) of deterministic iterations, the number of trial proposals, or any other operations.\n\nDefine the target measure π_s by exponentially tilting\n(z ↦ u(s) + √A(s)z)₊γ\nwith the function −V. In other words, the definition normalizes exp(−V) against that Gaussian using the stipulated total-inverse convention. The definition itself does not assume that the normalizing integral is finite and positive.\n\nFor the alternate auxiliary coordinates, define\nνa_s = λ₊ ⊗ (z ↦ √A(s)z)₊γ,\nand\nWa_s(x,(t,z)) =\nmin(1, max(−1,\n  ⟨(π/2)[cos(πt/2)(x−h(s)) − sin(πt/2)z],\n    g(s) − ∇V(h(s) + sin(πt/2)(x−h(s)) + cos(πt/2)z)\n  ⟩)).\n\nLet ρa_s be the countable product of trial laws\nq_s ⊗ [Poisson(2) ⊗ ((νa_s)^ℕ ⊗ λ₀)].\nDefine Acca_s exactly like Acc_s, with Wa_s replacing W_s.\n\nDefine the path transformation Φ_s by retaining each trial’s proposal, Poisson count, and acceptance uniform, and scaling every auxiliary Gaussian coordinate:\nΦ_s(ω)_n =\n(x_n,k_n, [j ↦ (a_n(j).first, √A(s)a_n(j).second)], r_n).\n\nThe conclusions are as follows.\n\n1. N : S → ℕ and xp : S → E are measurable.\n\n2. For every s, all three deterministic assertions hold:\nQ_{N(s)}(s) ≤ d b(s);\nfor every j < N(s), Q_j(s) > d b(s);\n‖h(s) − xp(s)‖ ≤ √(d A(s)).\nIn particular, the threshold is actually attained for every s, so the “no threshold exists” fallback in the definition of N is never needed under the assumptions. No numerical upper bound on N is asserted.\n\n3. There exists a Markov kernel R from S to E such that, for every s:\nY_s is measurable;\n(Y_s)₊ρ_s = R_s;\nρ_s({ω : every trial fails Acc_s}) = 0; and\n∫⁻ C_s(ω) dρ_s(ω) ≤ ENNReal.ofReal(1 + 2 exp(2)).\nHere the integral is the extended nonnegative integral. R is one measurable family of probability measures valid for all s. The statement separately asserts measurability of Y_s for each fixed s; it does not explicitly assert joint measurability of (s,ω) ↦ Y_s(ω).\n\n4. For every s, π_s is a probability measure and π_s and R_s are mutually absolutely continuous.\n\n5. For every s, both real-valued functions\nx ↦ ((dπ_s/dR_s)(x).toReal)^ell\nand\nx ↦ ((dR_s/dπ_s)(x).toReal)^ell\nare Bochner integrable with respect to R_s and π_s, respectively. The Radon–Nikodym derivatives are extended nonnegative derivatives; “toReal” sends infinity to zero according to the provided convention, and the exponent ell is real.\n\nWriting\nI_s = ∫ ((dπ_s/dR_s).toReal)^ell dR_s,\nJ_s = ∫ ((dR_s/dπ_s).toReal)^ell dπ_s,\nthe conclusion asserts\n0 < I_s ≤ 1 + eps²,\n0 < J_s ≤ 1 + eps²,\nlog(I_s)/(ell−1) ≤ eps²,\nlog(J_s)/(ell−1) ≤ eps².\nThese integrability and positivity facts are conclusions, not additional assumptions. The bounds are on moments and logarithmic moment expressions; no pointwise bound on either density ratio is asserted.\n\n6. For every s, the alternate path law is exactly the pushforward:\n(Φ_s)₊ρ_s = ρa_s.\n\n7. For every s, every sample path ω, and every n ∈ ℕ, acceptance is preserved pointwise:\nAcca_s(Φ_s(ω)_n) ⇔ Acc_s(ω_n).\nThis assertion is not restricted to paths sampled from ρ_s or to coordinates within the supports of their laws.\n\n8. For every s and every sample path ω, the countable sum of retained Poisson counts is preserved pointwise:\n∑_{n=0}^∞ 1_{all i<n fail Acca_s after Φ_s} (Φ_s(ω)_n).count\n=\n∑_{n=0}^∞ 1_{all i<n fail Acc_s} k_n.\nThe displayed equality concerns the sums without the initial additive 1. The only explicit expected-cost bound is the base-law bound in item 3.\n\n9. Define the alternate output on Φ_s(ω) as the proposal at its least Acca_s-accepted trial, falling back to u(s) if none exists. Then this alternate output equals Y_s(ω) for ρ_s-almost every ω. The output equality is expressly almost everywhere, unlike the pointwise acceptance and count-sum identities. Its two fallback values differ: u(s) for the alternate program and 0 for the base program.\n\nAll conclusions following the existence of R hold for every s, without exceptional parameter values. The assumptions impose no boundedness on initial(s) or u(s), and no parameter-space integrability assumption. No separate normalizer formula, pointwise density identity, explicit bound on deterministic initialization work, or exact equality R_s = π_s is asserted. The numerical constants in the displayed threshold and bounds are 64, 2, 1/2, and 1 + 2 exp(2); the threshold depends on d, β, ell, and eps, while the final expected-cost bound is independent of those quantities and of s.",
      "fidelity_verdict": "domain-mismatch",
