rs are integrable under their respective denominator laws, with strictly positive integrals at most1+eps^2, and their logarithms divided by q-1 are at most eps^2. packet sha-256: `f0ba78fb133847d01b0d1b756cd7091d44eb106355d47d4e80af73287abef62b` expanded-statement sha-256: `fec9ed0bd1777b77c64f297ed7a09967f1a2094cede1eb251059464c60add721` the following reconstruction uses only the packet’s expanded type and approved notation context. **quantified objects and hypotheses.** let \\(e\\) be a finite-dimensional real inner-product space with its borel measurable structure, and let \\[ d=\\dim_{\\mathbb r}e>0. \\] let \\(v:e\\to\\mathbb r\\) be \\(c^2\\), and let \\(\\kappa\\) be a nonnegative real satisfying \\(\\kappa\\ge1\\). assume, for every \\(x,w\\in e\\), \\[ \\kappa^{-1}\\|w\\|^2 \\le d^2v(x)[w,w] \\le \\|w\\|^2. \\] define the state space \\[ s=\\mathbb r_{\\ge0}\\times e\\times e\\times\\mathbb n\\times e^{\\mathbb n}, \\] and write \\(s=(b,a,u,k,h)\\). let \\(\\eta:s\\to\\mathbb r\\) be measurable. let \\(c,q,\\delta\\in\\mathbb r\\) satisfy \\[ 0<c<\\tfrac14,\\qquad 0<\\eta(s)\\le c\\quad(s\\in s),\\qquad q\\ge2,\\qquad 0<\\delta\\le\\tfrac12. \\] fix an arbitrary initial state \\(s_0=(b_0,a_0,u_0,k_0,h_0)\\) and an arbitrary markov kernel \\(m:s\\rightsquigarrow e\\). in particular, no approximation, invariance, or accuracy hypothesis on \\(m\\) appears. **definitions depending on these inputs.** for real \\(r\\), set \\[ k(r)=\\frac{1+r}{\\kappa^{-1}+r},\\qquad \\tau(s)= \\begin{cases} k(b),&2\\le k(b),\\\\ c,&k(b)<2. \\end{cases} \\] define \\[ l=q+\\log\\!\\left(\\frac{k(b_0)dq}{\\delta}\\right),\\qquad b=\\frac{1/1024}{\\sqrt{dl}+l}, \\] \\[ j=\\left\\lceil 8\\log\\!\\left(\\frac{ek(b_0)}b\\right)\\right\\rceil_{\\mathbb n}, \\qquad \\varepsilon=\\frac{\\delta}{\\sqrt{j+1}}. \\] here the ceiling is a natural-number ceiling. the following definitions specify a state update. for \\(s=(b,a,u,k,h)\\), put \\[ v(s)=\\frac{\\eta(s)+\\tau(s)}{1+b},\\qquad b^+(s)=b+\\operatorname{tonnreal}(v(s)^{-1}). \\] for a pair \\((x,z)\\in e\\times e\\), define \\[ y(s,x,z)=x+\\sqrt{\\frac{\\tau(s)}{1+b}}\\,z, \\] \\[ a^+(s,x,z)=b^+(s)^{-1} \\left(ba+v(s)^{-1}y(s,x,z)\\right), \\] \\[ f_{s,x,z}(w)=v(w)+\\frac{b^+(s)}2 \\|w-a^+(s,x,z)\\|^2, \\] \\[ t_{s,x,z}(w)=w-\\frac{1}{1+b^+(s)}\\nabla f_{s,x,z}(w). \\] let \\[ q_n(s,x,z)= \\left\\|\\nabla f_{s,x,z}\\!\\left(t_{s,x,z}^{\\,n}(x)\\right)\\right\\|^2. \\] define \\(n(s,x,z)\\) by an explicit existence branch: \\[ n(s,x,z)= \\begin{cases} \\min\\{n\\in\\mathbb n: q_n(s,x,z)\\le(\\kappa^{-1}+b^+(s))d\\}, &\\text{if this set is nonempty},\\\\ 0,&\\text{otherwise}. \\end{cases} \\] iteration with exponent zero is the identity. set \\[ u(s,x,z)= \\left( b^+(s),\\ a^+(s,x,z),\\ t_{s,x,z}^{\\,n(s,x,z)}(x),\\ k+1,\\ h^+ \\right), \\] where \\[ h^+(0)=y(s,x,z),\\qquad h^+(n+1)=h(n). \\] for the terminal construction, define, for every state \\(s=(b,a,u,k,h)\\), \\[ \\bar b(s)=\\max\\{b,b^{-1}\\},\\qquad f_s^{\\mathrm t}(w)=v(w)+\\frac{\\bar b(s)}2\\|w-a\\|^2, \\] \\[ t_s^{\\mathrm t}(w) =w-\\frac{1}{1+\\bar b(s)}\\nabla f_s^{\\mathrm t}(w), \\qquad q_n^{\\mathrm t}(s) =\\left\\|\\nabla f_s^{\\mathrm t} \\left((t_s^{\\mathrm t})^n(u)\\right)\\right\\|^2. \\] define \\[ n_{\\mathrm t}(s)= \\begin{cases} \\min\\{n\\in\\mathbb n:q_n^{\\mathrm t}(s)\\le d\\bar b(s)\\}, &\\text{if this set is nonempty},\\\\ 0,&\\text{otherwise}. \\end{cases} \\] then put \\[ x_{\\mathrm t}(s)=(t_s^{\\mathrm t})^{n_{\\mathrm t}(s)}(u),\\quad a(s)=\\bar b(s)^{-1},\\quad g(s)=\\nabla v(x_{\\mathrm t}(s)),\\quad m(s)=a-a(s)g(s). \\] let \\(\\gamma_e\\) denote the standard gaussian probability on \\(e\\), and let \\[ \\nu=\\lambda|_{(0,1]}\\otimes\\gamma_e, \\] where \\(\\lambda\\) is real lebesgue measure. for \\(x\\in e\\) and \\((t,z)\\in\\mathbb r\\times e\\), write \\[ \\theta=\\frac{\\pi t}{2},\\qquad z_a=\\sqrt{a(s)}\\,z, \\] and define \\[ w_s(x,t,z)= \\min\\!\\left\\{1,\\max\\!\\left\\{-1,\\ \\left\\langle \\frac{\\pi}{2}\\bigl(\\cos\\theta(x-m(s))-\\sin\\theta\\,z_a\\bigr), \\ g(s)-\\nabla v\\bigl(m(s)+\\sin\\theta(x-m(s))+\\cos\\theta\\,z_a\\bigr) \\right\\rangle \\right\\}\\right\\}. \\] thus the displayed inner product is clipped to \\([-1,1]\\). define the proposal probability \\[ g_s=(z\\mapsto m(s)+\\sqrt{a(s)}z)_\\#\\gamma_e. \\] one rejection-sampling attempt is a tuple \\[ p=(x,h,\\xi,r)\\in e\\times\\mathbb n\\times(\\mathbb r\\times e)^{\\mathbb n}\\times\\mathbb r \\] with independent coordinates having laws \\[ x\\sim g_s,\\qquad h\\sim\\operatorname{poisson}(2),\\qquad \\xi\\sim\\nu^{\\otimes\\mathbb n},\\qquad r\\sim\\lambda|_{[0,1]}. \\] let \\(\\rho_s\\) be the countable product law of independent attempts with this distribution. an attempt is accepted exactly when \\[ r\\le \\prod_{i=0}^{h-1}\\frac{1+w_s(x,\\xi_i)}2. \\] the product is over \\(\\operatorname{fin}(h)\\), and equals \\(1\\) when \\(h=0\\). denote this acceptance set by \\(\\mathcal a_s\\). for an attempt sequence \\(\\omega\\), define the returned point \\[ \\phi_s(\\omega)= \\begin{cases} x_{n_*},& n_*=\\min\\{n\\in\\mathbb n:\\omega_n\\in\\mathcal a_s\\} \\text{ exists},\\\\ 0_e,&\\text{no attempt is accepted}. \\end{cases} \\] finally define the target measure \\[ \\pi_s= \\left((z\\mapsto a+\\sqrt{a(s)}z)_\\#\\gamma_e\\right) \\text{ exponentially tilted by }-v. \\] this is the normalized exponential-tilting measure defined with total inverse conventions. its being a probability is a conclusion below, rather than an additional premise. **asserted conclusions.** all of the following hold: 1. the constants satisfy \\[ b>0,\\qquad 0<\\varepsilon\\le\\tfrac12. \\] for every state \\(s=(b,a,u,k,h)\\), \\[ b\\ge b^{-1}\\longrightarrow \\bar b(s)=b. \\] 2. the maps \\(n_{\\mathrm t}:s\\to\\mathbb n\\) and \\(x_{\\mathrm t}:s\\to e\\) are measurable. for every \\(s\\), \\[ q_{n_{\\mathrm t}(s)}^{\\mathrm t}(s)\\le d\\bar b(s), \\] \\[ j<n_{\\mathrm t}(s) \\longrightarrow d\\bar b(s)<q_j^{\\mathrm t}(s), \\] and \\[ \\|m(s)-x_{\\mathrm t}(s)\\|\\le\\sqrt{da(s)}. \\] these conclusions establish that the terminal stopping index attains its criterion and is its first attaining index, despite the fallback branch in its definition. 3. there exist markov kernels \\(l_{\\mathrm t}:s\\rightsquigarrow e\\) and \\(p:s\\rightsquigarrow s\\) such that, for every state \\(s=(b,a,u,k,h)\\), \\[ p(s)= \\begin{cases} \\delta_s,&b\\ge b^{-1},\\\\[1mm] \\bigl((x,z)\\mapsto u(s,x,z)\\bigr)_\\# \\bigl(m(s)\\otimes\\gamma_e\\bigr),&b<b^{-1}. \\end{cases} \\] thus the specified threshold states are absorbing. moreover, \\[ p^j(s_0)\\{s=(b,a,u,k,h):b\\ge b^{-1}\\}=1. \\] for every \\(m\\in\\mathbb n\\), equality of output measures holds: \\[ (l_{\\mathrm t}\\circ p^{j+m})(s_0) =(l_{\\mathrm t}\\circ p^j)(s_0). \\] composition here first applies the state kernel and then \\(l_{\\mathrm t}\\). 4. for every state \\(s\\), the function \\(\\phi_s\\) is measurable, and \\[ (\\phi_s)_\\#\\rho_s=l_{\\mathrm t}(s). \\] nontermination has measure zero: \\[ \\rho_s\\{\\omega:\\forall n,\\ \\omega_n\\notin\\mathcal a_s\\}=0. \\] there is also the precise extended nonnegative integral bound \\[ \\int \\left[ 1+\\sum_{n=0}^{\\infty} \\mathbf1_{\\{\\forall i<n:\\omega_i\\notin\\mathcal a_s\\}}h_n \\right]\\,d\\rho_s(\\omega) \\le \\operatorname{ofreal}(1+2e^2). \\] the count adds one, then the complete poisson batch sizes of every attempted batch through and including the first accepted attempt. it is not a count of attempts, and does not count just an inspected prefix of a batch. for \\(n=0\\), the preceding-failures condition is vacuous. 5. for every state \\(s\\), \\(\\pi_s\\) is a probability measure, and it is mutually absolutely continuous with \\(l_{\\mathrm t}(s)\\): \\[ \\pi_s\\ll l_{\\mathrm t}(s),\\qquad l_{\\mathrm t}(s)\\ll\\pi_s. \\] define the real-valued functions \\[ f_s(x)=\\operatorname{toreal} \\left(\\frac{d\\pi_s}{dl_{\\mathrm t}(s)}(x)\\right), \\qquad r_s(x)=\\operatorname{toreal} \\left(\\frac{dl_{\\mathrm t}(s)}{d\\pi_s}(x)\\right). \\] the conversion sends an infinite extended nonnegative value to zero. both \\(f_s^q\\) and \\(r_s^q\\) are bochner integrable with respect to their respective denominator measures: \\[ f_s^q\\in l^1(l_{\\mathrm t}(s)),\\qquad r_s^q\\in l^1(\\pi_s). \\] their integrals satisfy \\[ 0<\\int_e f_s(x)^q\\,dl_{\\mathrm t}(s)(x) \\le1+\\varepsilon^2, \\] \\[ 0<\\int_e r_s(x)^q\\,d\\pi_s(x) \\le1+\\varepsilon^2, \\] and \\[ \\frac{\\log\\!\\left(\\int_e f_s(x)^q\\,dl_{\\mathrm t}(s)(x)\\right)} {q-1}\\le\\varepsilon^2, \\] \\[ \\frac{\\log\\!\\lef…",
