      "original_theorem": "E is a finite-dimensional real inner-product Borel space of positive dimension d. V:E->R is C2 and its genuine Hessian satisfies alpha*norm(w)^2 <= D2V(x)[w,w] <= beta*norm(w)^2 for all x,w. The nonnegative reals alpha,beta satisfy0<alpha<=beta.\nThe full state is S=NNReal x E x E x Nat x (Nat->E), written s=(b,u,xref,k,history). M:S->E is an actual Markov kernel. eta,tau:S->R are measurable and strictly positive at every state; they may depend on all coordinates. Fix any nonnegative precision threshold. No source schedule,initial-reference admissibility,history origin,accuracy or moment assumption is imposed.\nPut v_s=(eta_s+tau_s)/(beta+b), bp_s=b+toNNReal(v_s^(-1)), and obs(s,(x,z))=x+sqrt(tau_s/(beta+b))*z. The next center is center(s,(x,z))=bp_s^(-1)*(b*u+v_s^(-1)*obs(s,(x,z))). The observation variance tau_s/(beta+b) is distinct from the RGO update variance v_s.\nDefine F_p(w)=V(w)+bp_s/2*norm(w-center(p))^2 and T_p(w)=w-(beta+bp_s)^(-1)*grad F_p(w), where p=(s,(x,z)). N_p is the least n with norm(grad F_p(T_p^n(x)))^2 <= (alpha+bp_s)*d, or zero if none exists. The actual update is U(p)=(bp_s,center(p),T_p^N_p(x),k+1,prepend(obs(p),history)). It uses the pre-noise sample x to initialize GD, and retains the full reference and history.\ngamma_E denotes standard Gaussian measure. Gaussian smoothing G(mu,sigma) is the pushforward of mu product the law of sigma*Z under addition. J.fst denotes the observation marginal of a joint kernel J:S->(E x S). J.condKernel is the parameterized Markov conditional state kernel (s,y)->S supplied by standard-Borel disintegration. R composed with P means first draw a state from P and then an output from R.\nThere exist a Markov joint kernel J:S->(E x S) and a Markov absorbed transition P:S->S. For every s, J_s is the pushforward of M_s product gamma_E by (x,z) -> (obs(s,(x,z)),U(s,(x,z))). Its first marginal is exactly G(M_s,sqrt(tau_s/(beta+b))). The kernel identity J.fst compProd J.condKernel=J holds. For each s, for J.fst_s-almost every y, for J.condKernel(s,y)-almost every t, t.precision=bp_s and t.center=bp_s^(-1)*(b*u+v_s^(-1)*y). For every actual Markov R:S->E, every s and every measurable A, the nonnegative integral of R(U(s,(x,z)),A) under M_s product gamma_E equals the iterated nonnegative integral of R(t,A) first under J.condKernel(s,y), then under J.fst_s. P_s is dirac_s when threshold<=b and the pushforward by U otherwise. If b<threshold, (R composed with P)(s,A) equals the same conditional iterated integral.",
      "reconstructed_theorem": "Packet ID: `ASTIS-BLIND-b97f548aa346d3c3`\n\nPacket SHA-256 recorded in the packet: `549c3fb5aa2db39058c8373ba545c3bb32dcafe1198d9449d0f21c14d069ffb9`\n\nLean statement SHA-256 recorded in the packet: `b97f548aa346d3c3e98ddaf00086c08c90b44462cc5f4931f165afebfc9c8bc8`\n\nThese are the packet-supplied hashes; I have not independently recomputed them. I read only the designated anonymous packet. No source text was visible. The reconstruction below identifies no source theorem or intended application.\n\n**Objects, assumptions, and definitions**\n\nLet \\(E\\) be any finite-dimensional real inner-product space, equipped with its Borel measurable structure, and let\n\\[\nd=\\dim_{\\mathbb R}E\\in\\mathbb R.\n\\]\nLet \\(V:E\\to\\mathbb R\\) and \\(\\alpha,\\beta\\in\\mathbb R_{\\ge0}\\). Assume:\n\n1. \\(0<\\alpha\\) and \\(\\alpha\\le\\beta\\).\n2. \\(V\\) is twice continuously Fréchet differentiable.\n3. For every \\(x,w\\in E\\),\n   \\[\n   \\alpha\\|w\\|^2\n   \\le (D(DV)(x)[w])[w]\n   \\le \\beta\\|w\\|^2.\n   \\]\n4. \\(0<d\\).\n\nNonnegative real parameters are coerced to real numbers wherever real arithmetic is displayed below.\n\nDefine the state space\n\\[\nS=\\mathbb R_{\\ge0}\\times E\\times E\\times\\mathbb N\\times E^{\\mathbb N}.\n\\]\nIts products are right-associated. Write a state as\n\\(s=(a,m,b,k,h)\\), with \\(h:\\mathbb N\\to E\\) a full infinite sequence. Equip this space with the product measurable structure.\n\nFor every pair of measurable functions \\(\\eta,\\tau:S\\to\\mathbb R\\) satisfying\n\\[\n\\forall s\\in S,\\quad \\eta(s)>0,\\qquad \\tau(s)>0,\n\\]\nevery Markov kernel \\(M:S\\rightsquigarrow E\\), and every threshold\n\\(\\theta\\in\\mathbb R_{\\ge0}\\), make the following definitions.\n\nFor \\(s=(a,m,b,k,h)\\), put\n\\[\nv(s)=\\frac{\\eta(s)+\\tau(s)}{\\beta+a},\n\\qquad\na^+(s)=a+\\operatorname{toNNReal}(v(s)^{-1}).\n\\]\nHere \\(\\operatorname{toNNReal}\\) truncates a real number below at zero. In these hypotheses \\(v(s)>0\\), so this truncation does not change the positive inverse.\n\nFor \\(p=(s,(x,g))\\in S\\times(E\\times E)\\), define\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\no(p)&=x+\\sqrt{\\frac{\\tau(s)}{\\beta+a}}\\,g,\\\\\nc(p)&=(a^+(s))^{-1}\\bigl(a\\,m+v(s)^{-1}o(p)\\bigr),\\\\\nF_p(u)&=V(u)+\\frac{a^+(s)}2\\|u-c(p)\\|^2,\\\\\nT_p(u)&=u-(\\beta+a^+(s))^{-1}\\nabla F_p(u),\\\\\nq(n,p)&=\\left\\|\\nabla F_p\\bigl(T_p^{[n]}(x)\\bigr)\\right\\|^2.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nAll scalar-vector products use the real vector-space structure. \\(T_p^{[n]}\\) denotes \\(n\\)-fold iteration, including the identity for \\(n=0\\). The starting point is the newly drawn \\(x\\), not either stored state coordinate \\(m\\) or \\(b\\).\n\nDefine\n\\[\nN(p)=\n\\begin{cases}\n\\min\\{n\\in\\mathbb N:q(n,p)\\le(\\alpha+a^+(s))d\\},\n &\\text{if this set is nonempty},\\\\\n0,&\\text{otherwise},\n\\end{cases}\n\\qquad\nu(p)=T_p^{[N(p)]}(x).\n\\]\nDefine an updated sequence by\n\\[\nh_p^+(0)=o(p),\\qquad h_p^+(n+1)=h(n)\n\\quad(n\\in\\mathbb N),\n\\]\nand the state update\n\\[\nU(p)=\\bigl(a^+(s),c(p),u(p),k+1,h_p^+\\bigr).\n\\]\nThus the observation is prepended to the stored infinite sequence.\n\nLet \\(\\gamma_E\\) be standard Gaussian measure on \\(E\\). All measures below use the indicated product/Borel structures. All integrals are nonnegative Lebesgue integrals with values in \\([0,\\infty]\\).\n\n**Reconstructed conclusion**\n\nThere exists a Markov kernel\n\\[\nJ:S\\rightsquigarrow E\\times S\n\\]\nwith the following five properties.\n\n1. **Joint observation and updated-state distribution.** For every \\(s\\in S\\),\n   \\[\n   J(s)=\n   \\bigl[(x,g)\\mapsto(o(s,(x,g)),U(s,(x,g)))\\bigr]_{\\#}\n   \\bigl(M(s)\\otimes\\gamma_E\\bigr).\n   \\]\n   The product measure means \\(x\\) and \\(g\\) are drawn independently, with respective laws \\(M(s)\\) and \\(\\gamma_E\\).\n\n2. **First-coordinate marginal.** Writing \\(J_1=J.\\mathrm{fst}\\), for every \\(s=(a,m,b,k,h)\\in S\\),\n   \\[\n   J_1(s)\n   =\n   [(x,e)\\mapsto x+e]_{\\#}\n   \\left(\n      M(s)\\otimes\n      \\left[g\\mapsto\\sqrt{\\frac{\\tau(s)}{\\beta+a}}\\,g\\right]_{\\#}\\gamma_E\n   \\right).\n   \\]\n\n3. **Exact kernel disintegration identity.** With\n   \\[\n   C=J.\\mathrm{condKernel}:\n   S\\times E\\rightsquigarrow S,\n   \\]\n   the equality\n   \\[\n   J_1.\\mathrm{compProd}\\,C=J\n   \\]\n   holds as an equality of kernels. The operation first draws \\(y\\) from \\(J_1(s)\\), then draws the updated state from \\(C(s,y)\\).\n\n4. **Conditional first two state coordinates.** For every \\(s=(a,m,b,k,h)\\in S\\), for \\(J_1(s)\\)-almost every \\(y\\in E\\), and then for \\(C(s,y)\\)-almost every \\(t\\in S\\),\n   \\[\n   t_1=a^+(s)\n   \\quad\\text{and}\\quad\n   t_2=(a^+(s))^{-1}\\bigl(a\\,m+v(s)^{-1}y\\bigr),\n   \\]\n   where \\(t_1\\) is the nonnegative-real state coordinate and \\(t_2\\) is its first \\(E\\)-coordinate.\n\n5. **Integration identity for every subsequent Markov kernel.** For every Markov kernel \\(R:S\\rightsquigarrow E\\), every \\(s\\in S\\), and every measurable set \\(A\\subseteq E\\),\n   \\[\n   \\int_{E\\times E}\n      R\\bigl(U(s,(x,g))\\bigr)(A)\\,\n      d\\bigl(M(s)\\otimes\\gamma_E\\bigr)(x,g)\n   =\n   \\int_E\n      \\left[\\int_S R(t)(A)\\,dC(s,y)(t)\\right]\n      dJ_1(s)(y).\n   \\]\n\nFurthermore, there exists a Markov kernel \\(P:S\\rightsquigarrow S\\) such that, for every \\(s=(a,m,b,k,h)\\in S\\),\n\\[\nP(s)=\n\\begin{cases}\n\\delta_s,&\\theta\\le a,\\\\\n[(x,g)\\mapsto U(s,(x,g))]_{\\#}\n  \\bigl(M(s)\\otimes\\gamma_E\\bigr),&\\text{otherwise}.\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\nFor every Markov kernel \\(R:S\\rightsquigarrow E\\), every \\(s=(a,m,b,k,h)\\in S\\) satisfying \\(a<\\theta\\), and every measurable set \\(A\\subseteq E\\),\n\\[\n(R.\\mathrm{comp}\\,P)(s)(A)\n=\n\\int_E\n  \\left[\\int_S R(t)(A)\\,dC(s,y)(t)\\right]\n  dJ_1(s)(y).\n\\]\nHere \\(R.\\mathrm{comp}\\,P\\) first draws a state according to \\(P(s)\\), then an output according to \\(R\\).\n\n**Quantifier scopes and boundaries**\n\nAll assumptions on \\(V,\\alpha,\\beta,E,\\eta,\\tau\\) precede the universal choice of \\(M\\) and \\(\\theta\\). The existential \\(J\\) is chosen after those data and before every subsequent universal choice of \\(s,R,A\\). The same \\(J\\), its marginal \\(J_1\\), and its conditional kernel \\(C\\) appear throughout. The existential \\(P\\) is also chosen before the final universal choices of \\(R,s,A\\); neither \\(J\\) nor \\(P\\) may be chosen separately for each \\(R\\) or \\(A\\).\n\nThe displayed marginal and joint distribution equalities hold for every input state, and the disintegration identity is exact as a kernel equality. The coordinate assertion is specifically\n\\[\n\\forall s,\\quad\n\\text{for }J_1(s)\\text{-a.e. }y,\\quad\n\\text{for }C(s,y)\\text{-a.e. }t.\n\\]\nIt is not a pointwise assertion for every \\(y,t\\). Exceptional observation sets may depend on \\(s\\), and exceptional state sets may depend on \\(s,y\\). No value of the conditional kernel at an exceptional observation is prescribed by that coordinate assertion. The explicit conditional-coordinate conclusion constrains only the first two coordinates; it does not separately state a conditional formula for the remaining coordinates.\n\nThe definition of \\(N\\) includes a fallback to zero when the stopping predicate has no witness. The conclusion does not separately assert existence of such a witness, a bound on \\(N\\), or the gradient inequality at \\(u(p)\\). These must not be added to the reconstructed conclusion merely from the definition.\n\nThe final composition identity is conditional on \\(a<\\theta\\). For \\(a\\ge\\theta\\), including equality, \\(P(s)=\\delta_s\\); the displayed final identity is not asserted there. If \\(\\theta=0\\), no state has \\(a<\\theta\\), so that conditional clause is vacuous, and \\(P\\) is the identity/Dirac transition at every state.\n\nNo distributional constraint on \\(M(s)\\) beyond being a Markov probability kernel is assumed. No approximation error, invariant law, convergence rate, or source-paper theorem is claimed.",
