      "original_theorem": "E is a finite-dimensional real inner-product Borel space with positive dimension d. V:E->R is C2 and its genuine Hessian satisfies alpha*norm(w)^2<=D2V(x)[w,w]<=beta*norm(w)^2 for every x,w. The nonnegative parameters satisfy0<alpha<=beta.\nThe full state is S=NNReal x E x E x Nat x (Nat->E), written s=(b,u,xref,k,history). eta,tau:S->R are measurable and strictly positive at every state. M:S->E is an actual Markov kernel, and threshold is any nonnegative real. M,eta,tau may depend on all coordinates. No source schedule,reference admissibility,history origin,approximation or moment premise is imposed.\nSet v_s=(eta_s+tau_s)/(beta+b), bp_s=b+toNNReal(v_s^(-1)), obs(s,(x,z))=x+sqrt(tau_s/(beta+b))*z and center(s,(x,z))=bp_s^(-1)*(b*u+v_s^(-1)*obs). For p=(s,(x,z)), define F_p(w)=V(w)+bp_s/2*norm(w-center(p))^2 and D_p(w)=w-(beta+bp_s)^(-1)*grad F_p(w). N_p is the least n with norm(grad F_p(D_p^n(x)))^2<=(alpha+bp_s)*d, or0 if none exists. U(p)=(bp_s,center(p),D_p^N_p(x),k+1,prepend(obs(p),history)). The actual reference is initialized at the pre-noise x.\nDefine the actual Gibbs base muV=volume.tilted(-V), using canonical volume. Gaussian smoothing G(mu,sigma) is the law of X+sigma*Z under mu product standardGaussian. All KL values and all displayed integrals are extended nonnegative reals, including infinity. R composed with P first draws the retained state from P and then the output from R.\nmuV is a probability measure. There exist Markov kernels T,H:S->E and P:S->S, chosen before every remaining-output kernel R, such that for every state s, T_s=muV.tilted(x->-b/2*norm(x-u)^2), H_s=G(T_s,sqrt(v_s)), and P_s=dirac_s when threshold<=b, otherwise P_s is the pushforward of M_s product standardGaussian by U(s,·). Put Q_s=G(M_s,sqrt(tau_s/(beta+b))) and e(s)=0 when threshold<=b, otherwise KL(Q_s||H_s). Then e is measurable. For every actual Markov kernel R:S->E, the function s->KL(R_s||T_s) is measurable and, for every s, KL((R composed with P)_s||T_s)<=e(s)+the nonnegative integral of KL(R_t||T_t) under P_s.",
      "reconstructed_theorem": "The following reconstruction uses only the anonymous packet and its approved definition context. No source text, source identity, source anchor, declaration identity, or intended application was consulted.\n\nPacket ID: `ASTIS-BLIND-ef8fe6c5207e6d86`.\n\nPacket SHA-256: `072cbb91955d2703b4647891caa9a14cae0561663df0f2b25ac129a62bb972d6` (supplied by the packet; not independently recomputed because the packet-hashing serialization convention is not specified).\n\nLean statement SHA-256: `ef8fe6c5207e6d86a83543fe5f303ea66d06073a0b5762f90517c2b7607e3626` (independently recomputed from the UTF-8 bytes of the decoded statement string; matches the supplied hash).\n\nThe packet labels the statement compiled. This decoder did not independently compile it.\n\nLet \\(E\\) be a finite-dimensional real inner-product space with its normed additive commutative group structure, a measurable-space structure, and the assertion that this structure is its Borel measurable structure. Write\n\\[\nd=\\dim_{\\mathbb R}E,\n\\]\nviewed as a real number, and assume \\(d>0\\).\n\nLet \\(V:E\\to\\mathbb R\\), and let \\(\\alpha,\\beta\\) be nonnegative real numbers. Assume:\n\n1. \\(0<\\alpha\\le\\beta\\).\n2. \\(V\\) is \\(C^2\\) over \\(\\mathbb R\\).\n3. For every \\(x,w\\in E\\),\n   \\[\n   \\alpha\\|w\\|^2\n   \\le D(DV)(x)[w][w]\n   \\le\\beta\\|w\\|^2.\n   \\]\n\nDefine the state space\n\\[\nS=\\mathbb R_{\\ge0}\\times E\\times E\\times\\mathbb N\\times E^{\\mathbb N}.\n\\]\nFor clarity, write a state as \\(s=(a,c,u,k,h)\\), where \\(h:\\mathbb N\\to E\\) is a full infinite sequence. Use the corresponding product measurable structure.\n\nLet \\(\\eta,\\tau:S\\to\\mathbb R\\) be any measurable functions satisfying\n\\[\n\\eta(s)>0,\\qquad \\tau(s)>0\n\\quad\\text{for every }s\\in S.\n\\]\nLet \\(M:S\\rightsquigarrow E\\) be any Markov kernel, and let \\(\\theta\\in\\mathbb R_{\\ge0}\\) be any threshold.\n\nIn the following formulas, nonnegative real numbers are coerced to real numbers when used in real arithmetic. Define\n\\[\nv(s)=\\frac{\\eta(s)+\\tau(s)}{\\beta+a},\n\\qquad\nb(s)=a+\\operatorname{toNNReal}\\bigl(v(s)^{-1}\\bigr).\n\\]\nHere \\(\\operatorname{toNNReal}(r)=\\max(r,0)\\), interpreted as a nonnegative real. The stated assumptions imply \\(\\beta+a>0\\) and \\(v(s)>0\\), so the truncation does not change the inverse.\n\nFor \\(p=(s,(y,z))\\in S\\times(E\\times E)\\), define\n\\[\no(p)=y+\\sqrt{\\frac{\\tau(s)}{\\beta+a}}\\,z,\n\\]\n\\[\nm(p)=b(s)^{-1}\\bigl(ac+v(s)^{-1}o(p)\\bigr),\n\\]\n\\[\nF_p(x)=V(x)+\\frac{b(s)}2\\|x-m(p)\\|^2,\n\\]\nand the map\n\\[\nG_p(x)=x-\\frac1{\\beta+b(s)}\\nabla F_p(x).\n\\]\nScalar multiplication is the real scalar action on \\(E\\). All square roots are real square roots.\n\nFor every \\(n\\in\\mathbb N\\), set\n\\[\nq_n(p)=\\left\\|\\nabla F_p\\bigl(G_p^{[n]}(y)\\bigr)\\right\\|^2,\n\\]\nwhere \\(G_p^{[n]}\\) is the \\(n\\)-fold iterate and the zeroth iterate is the identity. Define a total natural-valued function by\n\\[\nN(p)=\n\\begin{cases}\n\\min\\{n\\in\\mathbb N:q_n(p)\\le(\\alpha+b(s))d\\},\n&\\text{if this set is nonempty},\\\\\n0,&\\text{otherwise}.\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\nDefine\n\\[\nr(p)=G_p^{[N(p)]}(y).\n\\]\nThe update map \\(U:S\\times(E\\times E)\\to S\\) is\n\\[\nU(p)=\\bigl(b(s),m(p),r(p),k+1,h_p'\\bigr),\n\\]\nwhere\n\\[\nh_p'(0)=o(p),\\qquad h_p'(n+1)=h(n)\\quad(n\\in\\mathbb N).\n\\]\nIn particular, the iteration starts at the newly supplied coordinate \\(y\\); the existing state coordinate \\(u\\) does not explicitly appear in these update formulas. The functions \\(\\eta,\\tau\\) and the kernel \\(M\\) may nevertheless depend on every coordinate of \\(s\\).\n\nLet \\(\\lambda_E\\) denote canonical Lebesgue volume on \\(E\\), and define\n\\[\n\\mu=\\operatorname{Tilt}(\\lambda_E,-V).\n\\]\nThe notation \\(\\operatorname{Tilt}(\\nu,f)\\) means the total, normalized exponential reweighting operation in the provided Lean API. Its notation alone is not an assumption of finite normalization or probability.\n\nThen \\(\\mu\\) is a probability measure. Moreover, there exist Markov kernels\n\\[\nA:S\\rightsquigarrow E,\\qquad\nH:S\\rightsquigarrow E,\\qquad\nP:S\\rightsquigarrow S\n\\]\nwith all of the following properties.\n\nFor every \\(s=(a,c,u,k,h)\\in S\\),\n\\[\nA(s)=\\operatorname{Tilt}\n\\left(\\mu,\\;x\\mapsto-\\frac a2\\|x-c\\|^2\\right).\n\\]\n\nWriting \\(\\gamma_E\\) for the standard Gaussian probability measure on \\(E\\), for every \\(s\\in S\\),\n\\[\nH(s)=\n\\operatorname{Law}(X+\\sqrt{v(s)}Z),\n\\]\nwhere \\(X\\sim A(s)\\) and \\(Z\\sim\\gamma_E\\) are independent. Precisely, this is the pushforward under addition of the product of \\(A(s)\\) with the pushforward of \\(\\gamma_E\\) under \\(z\\mapsto\\sqrt{v(s)}z\\).\n\nFor every \\(s=(a,c,u,k,h)\\in S\\),\n\\[\nP(s)=\n\\begin{cases}\n\\delta_s,&\\theta\\le a,\\\\\n\\operatorname{Law}\\bigl(U(s,(Y,Z))\\bigr),&a<\\theta,\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\nwhere in the second branch \\(Y\\sim M(s)\\) and \\(Z\\sim\\gamma_E\\) independently.\n\nDefine, for all states, the probability measure\n\\[\nQ(s)=\n\\operatorname{Law}\n\\left(Y+\\sqrt{\\frac{\\tau(s)}{\\beta+a}}Z\\right),\n\\]\nwith \\(Y\\sim M(s)\\) and \\(Z\\sim\\gamma_E\\) independently. Define\n\\[\ne(s)=\n\\begin{cases}\n0,&\\theta\\le a,\\\\\nD_{\\mathrm{KL}}(Q(s)\\|H(s)),&a<\\theta.\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\nThen \\(e:S\\to[0,\\infty]\\) is measurable.\n\nFor every Markov kernel \\(R:S\\rightsquigarrow E\\), the function\n\\[\ns\\longmapsto D_{\\mathrm{KL}}(R(s)\\|A(s))\n\\]\nis measurable, and for every \\(s\\in S\\),\n\\[\nD_{\\mathrm{KL}}\\bigl((R\\circ P)(s)\\|A(s)\\bigr)\n\\le\ne(s)+\n\\int_S D_{\\mathrm{KL}}\\bigl(R(t)\\|A(t)\\bigr)\\,P(s,dt).\n\\]\nHere \\((R\\circ P)(s)\\) first draws \\(t\\sim P(s)\\) and then draws an \\(E\\)-valued output from \\(R(t)\\). The reference measure on the left is \\(A(s)\\) at the original state; the reference measure inside the integral is \\(A(t)\\) at the intermediate state.\n\nThe existential choice of \\(A,H,P\\) precedes the universal quantification over \\(R\\): the same three kernels work for every Markov kernel \\(R\\). All conclusions hold pointwise for every state, not merely almost everywhere.\n\nRelative entropy and the integral take values in the extended nonnegative reals. There is no assumed finiteness, absolute continuity, or integrability of the displayed relative entropies. If the right-hand side is infinite, the asserted inequality has its usual extended-order meaning. The hypotheses require no additional approximation, density, regularity, or accuracy condition on \\(M\\) or \\(R\\) beyond their being Markov kernels.\n\nThe displayed theorem does not separately assert that the set defining \\(N(p)\\) is nonempty, a bound on \\(N(p)\\), or a finite evaluation cost. Its definition expressly includes the empty-set fallback \\(N(p)=0\\). No quantitative iteration bound or additional assertion about the fallback is part of the reconstructed conclusion.\n\nThe formulas contain no unspecified constants. Their numerical dependencies are exactly the displayed dimension \\(d\\), curvature parameters \\(\\alpha,\\beta\\), functions \\(\\eta,\\tau\\), current state coordinates, and supplied potential \\(V\\). The threshold affects the branch defining \\(P\\) and \\(e\\); \\(M\\) supplies the random first input to the active update and to \\(Q\\).",
