      "subtitle": "companion:sphmc-enhanced-finite-output-kl · domain-mismatch",
      "summary": "Blind reconstruction and seven-slot semantic comparison for AutoSamplingTheory.ExampleCases.SmoothedPicardHMC.EnhancedFiniteOutputKL.finite_output_kl.",
      "column": 7,
      "url": "lean-foundations.html?view=semantic",
      "source_url": "https://arxiv.org/html/2609.06906v1",
      "original_theorem": "E is a positive-dimensional finite-dimensional real inner-product Borel space; d=dim_R E. The nonnegative real kappa satisfies kappa>=1. V:E->R is C2 and kappa^(-1)*norm(w)^2<=D2V(x)[w,w]<=norm(w)^2 for every x,w. This is the normalized beta=1 setting.\nThe full state S=NNReal x E x E x Nat x (Nat->E) is written s=(b,u,xref,k,history). eta:S->R is measurable,0<eta_s<=c,0<c<1/4,0<Delta<=1/2. The initial state s0 is arbitrary and M:S->E is any actual Markov kernel. No initial-history origin,reference admissibility,stage approximation or moment guarantee is assumed.\nWrite K(r)=(1+r)/(kappa^(-1)+r),tau_s=K(b_s) if K(b_s)>=2 and c otherwise,L=2+log(K(b_s0)*d*2/Delta),B=(1/1024)/(sqrt(d*L)+L),J=ceil(8*log(exp(1)*K(b_s0)/B)),eps=Delta/sqrt(J+1). Real inverses and square roots use their total Lean definitions; the proof establishes the required positive denominators.\nFor p=(s,(x,z)),set v_s=(eta_s+tau_s)/(1+b_s),bp_s=b_s+toNNReal(v_s^(-1)),obs(p)=x+sqrt(tau_s/(1+b_s))*z,center(p)=bp_s^(-1)*(b_s*u_s+v_s^(-1)*obs(p)),F_p(w)=V(w)+bp_s/2*norm(w-center(p))^2 and D_p(w)=w-(1+bp_s)^(-1)*grad F_p(w). N_p is the least n with norm(grad F_p(D_p^n(x)))^2<=(kappa^(-1)+bp_s)*d,or0 if no such n exists. U(p)=(bp_s,center(p),D_p^N_p(x),k+1,prepend(obs(p),history)). The actual reference starts at the pre-noise x.\nFor the terminal program,bb_s=max(b_s,B^(-1)),A_s=bb_s^(-1),TF_s(w)=V(w)+bb_s/2*norm(w-u_s)^2 and TD_s(w)=w-(1+bb_s)^(-1)*grad TF_s(w). TN_s is the least n with norm(grad TF_s(TD_s^n(xref_s)))^2<=d*bb_s,or0 if none exists. Put xp_s=TD_s^TN_s(xref_s),g_s=grad V(xp_s),h_s=u_s-A_s*g_s.\nFor an auxiliary draw a=(t,z) and terminal candidate x,let theta=pi*t/2 and Z=sqrt(A_s)*z. Define W_s(x,a)=clip[-1,1](inner((pi/2)*(cos(theta)*(x-h_s)-sin(theta)*Z),g_s-grad V(h_s+sin(theta)*(x-h_s)+cos(theta)*Z))). The auxiliary law nu is volume restricted to(0,1] times standardGaussian; the proposal q_s is the law of h_s+sqrt(A_s)*Z0 for standardGaussian Z0.\nOne terminal attempt independently draws X~q_s,N~Poisson(2),an infinite iid nu sequence a_i,and U~Uniform[0,1]. It accepts when U<=product(i<N,(1+W_s(X,a_i))/2),with empty product1. rho_s is the law of an infinite iid sequence of such attempts. O_s returns X from the first accepted attempt,or the zero vector if no attempt accepts. This is the actual terminal stream program,with its full independent-product law.\nLet muV=volume.tilted(-V),using canonical volume. Gaussian smoothing G(mu,sigma) is the law of X+sigma*Z under mu times standardGaussian. Tilt is the total normalized exponential reweighting operation; probability is proved,not implicit. Kernel composition Lt composed P first draws a state from P then an output from Lt. KL and nonnegative integrals take extended nonnegative values,possibly infinity.\nmuV is a probability measure. There exist Markov kernels Lt:S->E,P:S->S,TG:S->E and H:S->E such that P_s=dirac_s when B^(-1)<=b_s and otherwise P_s is the law of U(s,(X,Z)) under M_s times standardGaussian; the actual terminal stream output law (rho_s).map O_s equals Lt_s; TG_s=muV.tilted(x->-(b_s/2)*norm(x-u_s)^2); and H_s=G(TG_s,sqrt(v_s)). Define Q_s=G(M_s,sqrt(tau_s/(1+b_s))) and e(s)=0 when B^(-1)<=b_s,otherwise KL(Q_s||H_s). Then e is measurable,and for every natural m,KL((Lt composed P^(J+m))(s0)||TG_s0)<=sum(j<J,the nonnegative integral of e under(P^j)(s0))+Delta^2/(J+1). The final real term is embedded in the extended nonnegative reals.",
      "reconstructed_theorem": "I read only the anonymous packet and its approved notation context. I did not consult a source, declaration identity, or other repository files. The packet reports `compiled: true`; I did not independently run Lean.\n\nThe following is the complete reconstructed mathematical statement.\n\nLet \\(E\\) be a finite-dimensional real inner-product space, equipped with its normed additive-group structure and its Borel measurable-space structure. Let \\(V:E\\to\\mathbb R\\), and let \\(\\kappa\\in\\mathbb R_{\\ge0}\\). Assume\n\\[\n1\\le\\kappa,\\qquad V\\in C^2(E;\\mathbb R),\n\\]\nand, for every \\(x,w\\in E\\),\n\\[\n\\kappa^{-1}\\|w\\|^2\n\\le D^2V(x)[w,w]\\le \\|w\\|^2.\n\\]\nAssume the real dimension\n\\[\nd=\\dim_{\\mathbb R}E\n\\]\nis strictly positive. In formulas below, \\(d\\) denotes this dimension cast to a real number, and nonnegative-real state coordinates and \\(\\kappa\\) are likewise cast to reals where needed.\n\nThe state space is\n\\[\nS=\\mathbb R_{\\ge0}\\times E\\times E\\times\\mathbb N\\times E^{\\mathbb N}.\n\\]\nWrite a state as \\(s=(b,a,x,k,h)\\), where \\(h:\\mathbb N\\to E\\) is a full infinite sequence. Let \\(\\eta:S\\to\\mathbb R\\) be measurable. Let \\(c,\\Delta\\in\\mathbb R\\) satisfy\n\\[\n0<c<\\tfrac14,\\qquad\n\\forall s\\in S,\\quad 0<\\eta(s)\\le c,\\qquad\n0<\\Delta\\le\\tfrac12.\n\\]\nFix any initial state \\(s_0=(b_0,a_0,x_0,k_0,h_0)\\), without further restrictions, and any Markov kernel \\(M:S\\rightsquigarrow E\\). In particular, no approximation-quality, absolute-continuity, or finite-relative-entropy assumption is imposed on \\(M\\).\n\nDefine\n\\[\nK(r)=\\frac{1+r}{\\kappa^{-1}+r},\n\\qquad\n\\tau(s)=\n\\begin{cases}\nK(b),&2\\le K(b),\\\\\nc,&K(b)<2,\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\nand\n\\[\nL=2+\\log\\!\\left(\\frac{2K(b_0)d}{\\Delta}\\right),\\qquad\nB=\\frac{1/1024}{\\sqrt{dL}+L},\n\\]\n\\[\nJ=\\left\\lceil 8\\log\\!\\left(\\frac{eK(b_0)}B\\right)\\right\\rceil_{\\mathbb N},\n\\qquad\n\\varepsilon=\\frac{\\Delta}{\\sqrt{J+1}}.\n\\]\nThe displayed natural ceiling is the total natural-number ceiling operation. The local name \\(\\varepsilon\\) does not occur subsequently in the formal conclusion.\n\nFor each \\(s=(b,a,x,k,h)\\), define\n\\[\nv(s)=\\frac{\\eta(s)+\\tau(s)}{1+b},\\qquad\nb^+(s)=b+\\operatorname{toNNReal}\\!\\left(v(s)^{-1}\\right).\n\\]\nHere \\(\\operatorname{toNNReal}\\) truncates below at zero. Keep this operation as displayed rather than silently replacing it in the formal specification.\n\nFor \\(p=(s,(y,z))\\in S\\times(E\\times E)\\), define\n\\[\no(p)=y+\\sqrt{\\frac{\\tau(s)}{1+b}}\\,z,\n\\]\n\\[\na^+(p)=b^+(s)^{-1}\\bigl(ba+v(s)^{-1}o(p)\\bigr),\n\\]\n\\[\nF_p(u)=V(u)+\\frac{b^+(s)}2\\|u-a^+(p)\\|^2,\n\\qquad\nT_p(u)=u-\\frac{1}{1+b^+(s)}\\nabla F_p(u).\n\\]\nInverses involving \\(b^+(s)\\) above use its real coercion. Set\n\\[\nq_n(p)=\\left\\|\\nabla F_p\\!\\left(T_p^{\\,n}(y)\\right)\\right\\|^2\n\\]\nand define the total first-hit index\n\\[\nN(p)=\n\\begin{cases}\n\\min\\{n\\in\\mathbb N:q_n(p)\\le(\\kappa^{-1}+b^+(s))d\\},\n&\\text{if this set is nonempty},\\\\\n0,&\\text{otherwise}.\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\nDefine the state update\n\\[\nU(p)=\\bigl(b^+(s),\\,a^+(p),\\,T_p^{\\,N(p)}(y),\\,k+1,\\,h^+(p)\\bigr),\n\\]\nwhere\n\\[\nh^+(p)(0)=o(p),\\qquad h^+(p)(n+1)=h(n).\n\\]\nThus this iteration starts at the newly sampled \\(y\\), not at the third coordinate \\(x\\) of the incoming state.\n\nThe terminal construction uses, for each state \\(s=(b,a,x,k,h)\\),\n\\[\n\\bar b(s)=\\max\\{b,B^{-1}\\},\n\\]\n\\[\nF_s^{\\mathrm t}(u)=V(u)+\\frac{\\bar b(s)}2\\|u-a\\|^2,\n\\qquad\nT_s^{\\mathrm t}(u)=u-\\frac{1}{1+\\bar b(s)}\\nabla F_s^{\\mathrm t}(u),\n\\]\n\\[\nq_n^{\\mathrm t}(s)\n=\\left\\|\\nabla F_s^{\\mathrm t}\\!\\left((T_s^{\\mathrm t})^n(x)\\right)\\right\\|^2,\n\\]\n\\[\nN^{\\mathrm t}(s)=\n\\begin{cases}\n\\min\\{n\\in\\mathbb N:q_n^{\\mathrm t}(s)\\le d\\bar b(s)\\},\n&\\text{if this set is nonempty},\\\\\n0,&\\text{otherwise}.\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\nThen set\n\\[\nx^{\\mathrm t}(s)=(T_s^{\\mathrm t})^{N^{\\mathrm t}(s)}(x),\n\\quad\nA(s)=\\bar b(s)^{-1},\n\\quad\ng(s)=\\nabla V(x^{\\mathrm t}(s)),\n\\quad\n\\theta(s)=a-A(s)g(s).\n\\]\nThis terminal iteration starts at the third state coordinate \\(x\\).\n\nLet \\(\\gamma_E\\) denote standard Gaussian measure on \\(E\\), and let\n\\[\n\\nu=\\bigl(\\mathrm{Leb}_{\\mathbb R}|_{(0,1]}\\bigr)\\otimes\\gamma_E.\n\\]\nFor a candidate \\(y\\in E\\) and an auxiliary pair \\((t,z_0)\\in\\mathbb R\\times E\\), put \\(z=\\sqrt{A(s)}\\,z_0\\) and define\n\\[\n\\begin{split}\nW_s(y,(t,z_0))\n=\\min\\Biggl\\{1,\\max\\Biggl\\{-1,\\,\n\\Biggl\\langle&\n\\frac{\\pi}{2}\\left(\n\\cos\\!\\left(\\frac{\\pi t}{2}\\right)(y-\\theta(s))\n-\\sin\\!\\left(\\frac{\\pi t}{2}\\right)z\n\\right),\\\\\n&g(s)-\\nabla V\\!\\left(\n\\theta(s)\n+\\sin\\!\\left(\\frac{\\pi t}{2}\\right)(y-\\theta(s))\n+\\cos\\!\\left(\\frac{\\pi t}{2}\\right)z\n\\right)\n\\Biggr\\rangle_{\\mathbb R}\n\\Biggr\\}\\Biggr\\}.\n\\end{split}\n\\]\nThe expression is explicitly clipped to \\([-1,1]\\).\n\nDefine the candidate law\n\\[\nR_s=(z\\mapsto\\theta(s)+\\sqrt{A(s)}\\,z)_\\#\\gamma_E.\n\\]\nOne trial is a tuple\n\\[\np=(Y,N,((T_i,Z_i))_{i\\in\\mathbb N},U)\n\\]\nwith independent coordinates distributed according to\n\\[\nR_s\\otimes\n\\left(\n\\operatorname{Poisson}(2)\\otimes\n\\left(\\nu^{\\otimes\\mathbb N}\n\\otimes(\\mathrm{Leb}_{\\mathbb R}|_{[0,1]})\\right)\n\\right).\n\\]\nThe tuple grouping in this expression is the grouping of the displayed nested products. Let \\(\\rho_s\\) be the independent countable product of this trial law. Thus \\(\\omega\\) is an infinite sequence of independent trials.\n\nDeclare a trial accepted exactly when\n\\[\nU\\le\n\\prod_{i=0}^{N-1}\\frac{1+W_s(Y,(T_i,Z_i))}{2}.\n\\]\nThe product is \\(1\\) for \\(N=0\\). Define the total output function\n\\[\n\\mathcal A_s(\\omega)=\n\\begin{cases}\nY_{n_*},&\nn_*=\\min\\{n\\in\\mathbb N:\\omega_n\\text{ is accepted}\\}\n\\text{ exists},\\\\\n0_E,&\\text{if no trial is accepted}.\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\n\nA further local measure is defined in the statement:\n\\[\n\\pi_s^{\\mathrm t}\n=\n\\left((z\\mapsto a+\\sqrt{A(s)}z)_\\#\\gamma_E\\right)\n.\\operatorname{tilted}(-V).\n\\]\nThis local measure is unused in the asserted conclusion; in particular, there is no displayed assertion identifying the terminal output law with \\(\\pi_s^{\\mathrm t}\\).\n\nLet\n\\[\n\\mu_V=\\mathrm{Leb}_E.\\operatorname{tilted}(-V),\n\\]\nwhere `tilted` is the given total normalized exponential-reweighting operation.\n\nThe theorem concludes that \\(\\mu_V\\) is a probability measure and that there exist Markov kernels\n\\[\nL_{\\mathrm t}:S\\rightsquigarrow E,\\qquad\nP:S\\rightsquigarrow S,\\qquad\nTG:S\\rightsquigarrow E,\\qquad\nH:S\\rightsquigarrow E\n\\]\nwith all of the following properties.\n\nFor every state \\(s=(b,a,x,k,h)\\),\n\\[\nP(s)=\n\\begin{cases}\n\\delta_s,&B^{-1}\\le b,\\\\\nU(s,\\cdot)_\\#\\bigl(M(s)\\otimes\\gamma_E\\bigr),&b<B^{-1}.\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\nThe Gaussian used here is independent of the draw \\(y\\sim M(s)\\). Above the threshold the entire state is fixed, including its counter and history sequence.\n\nFor every state \\(s\\),\n\\[\nL_{\\mathrm t}(s)=(\\mathcal A_s)_\\#\\rho_s,\n\\]\n\\[\nTG(s)=\n\\mu_V.\\operatorname{tilted}\n\\left(u\\mapsto-\\frac b2\\|u-a\\|^2\\right),\n\\]\nand\n\\[\nH(s)=\n(u,w\\mapsto u+w)_\\#\n\\left[\nTG(s)\\otimes\n(z\\mapsto\\sqrt{v(s)}z)_\\#\\gamma_E\n\\right].\n\\]\n\nDefine\n\\[\nQ(s)=\n(u,w\\mapsto u+w)_\\#\n\\left[\nM(s)\\otimes\n\\left(z\\mapsto\n\\sqrt{\\frac{\\tau(s)}{1+b}}z\\right)_\\#\\gamma_E\n\\right]\n\\]\nand the extended-nonnegative error\n\\[\ne(s)=\n\\begin{cases}\n0,&B^{-1}\\le b,\\\\\n\\operatorname{KL}(Q(s)\\|H(s)),&b<B^{-1}.\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\nThe theorem asserts that \\(e\\) is measurable and, for every \\(m\\in\\mathbb N\\),\n\\[\n\\boxed{\n\\operatorname{KL}\n\\left(\n(L_{\\mathrm t}\\circ P^{\\,J+m})(s_0)\n\\,\\middle\\|\\,TG(s_0)\n\\right)\n\\le\n\\sum_{j=0}^{J-1}\n\\int_S^{-}e(s)\\,d(P^j(s_0))(s)\n+\n\\operatorname{ofReal}_{\\mathbb R_{\\ge0}\\cup\\{\\infty\\}}\n\\left(\\frac{\\Delta^2}{J+1}\\right).\n}\n\\]\nHere composition means first applying \\(P^{J+m}\\), then \\(L_{\\mathrm t}\\). Kernel exponent zero is the identity kernel, so \\(P^0(s_0)=\\delta_{s_0}\\). The target on the left is the measure \\(TG(s_0)\\) at the original initial state. The sum on the right stops at \\(J-1\\), independently of \\(m\\).\n\nAll KL divergences and displayed nonnegative integrals take values in the extended nonnegative reals. The assertion includes the possibility of infinite KL or an infinite right-hand side; it contains no implicit finiteness or absolute-continuity hypothesis. When the right-hand side is finite, the inequality bounds the final KL by that finite quantity; when it is infinite, the displayed inequality remains an extended-real assertion.\n\nThe statement retains three explicit fallback branches: the ordinary gradient-iteration first hit defaults to \\(0\\) if absent, the terminal gradient-iteration first hit defaults to \\(0\\) if absent, and the repeated-trial output defaults to \\(0_E\\) if there is no accepted trial. There is no separate displayed termination-probability, iteration-count, expected-cost, or runtime bound. The existence of the asserted Markov kernels includes probability of their fibers, but the text does not separately assert that these fallback events are null.\n\nThe constants \\(L,B,J\\) depend on \\(\\kappa,d,b_0,\\Delta\\); \\(\\tau\\) also uses \\(c\\), and \\(v\\) uses \\(\\eta\\). The transition kernel can depend on every state coordinate through arbitrary measurable \\(\\eta\\) and \\(M\\), including the full history sequence and counter. No finite-memory restriction is imposed.\n\nHash evidence:\n\n- Recomputed SHA-256 of the decoded `lean.statement` string, encoded as UTF-8 without adding a newline: `40ecc48bc7c1419a3e1417c02a53c1822978e8415b4a3714fc5f478cc7d53129`. It matches the supplied `statement_sha256`.\n- Supplied packet hash: `24328dfe607681b01201ac66ec8ee785e1af4217f9e7c0f7f4adb14cf392e795`. I have not recomputed this hash or inferred its serialization convention.\n- I have not computed a hash of this reconstructed prose. No source-fidelity verdict is given.",
