      "search": "semantic-audit:astis-rt-20260912-sphmcenhancedfiniteoutputkl semantic-audit astis-rt-20260912-sphmcenhancedfiniteoutputkl companion:sphmc-enhanced-finite-output-kl · domain-mismatch blind reconstruction and seven-slot semantic comparison for autosamplingtheory.examplecases.smoothedpicardhmc.enhancedfiniteoutputkl.finite_output_kl. domain-mismatch source hidden from decoder e is a positive-dimensional finite-dimensional real inner-product borel space; d=dim_r e. the nonnegative real kappa satisfies kappa>=1. v:e->r is c2 and kappa^(-1)*norm(w)^2<=d2v(x)[w,w]<=norm(w)^2 for every x,w. this is the normalized beta=1 setting. the full state s=nnreal x e x e x nat x (nat->e) is written s=(b,u,xref,k,history). eta:s->r is measurable,0<eta_s<=c,0<c<1/4,0<delta<=1/2. the initial state s0 is arbitrary and m:s->e is any actual markov kernel. no initial-history origin,reference admissibility,stage approximation or moment guarantee is assumed. write k(r)=(1+r)/(kappa^(-1)+r),tau_s=k(b_s) if k(b_s)>=2 and c otherwise,l=2+log(k(b_s0)*d*2/delta),b=(1/1024)/(sqrt(d*l)+l),j=ceil(8*log(exp(1)*k(b_s0)/b)),eps=delta/sqrt(j+1). real inverses and square roots use their total lean definitions; the proof establishes the required positive denominators. for p=(s,(x,z)),set v_s=(eta_s+tau_s)/(1+b_s),bp_s=b_s+tonnreal(v_s^(-1)),obs(p)=x+sqrt(tau_s/(1+b_s))*z,center(p)=bp_s^(-1)*(b_s*u_s+v_s^(-1)*obs(p)),f_p(w)=v(w)+bp_s/2*norm(w-center(p))^2 and d_p(w)=w-(1+bp_s)^(-1)*grad f_p(w). n_p is the least n with norm(grad f_p(d_p^n(x)))^2<=(kappa^(-1)+bp_s)*d,or0 if no such n exists. u(p)=(bp_s,center(p),d_p^n_p(x),k+1,prepend(obs(p),history)). the actual reference starts at the pre-noise x. for the terminal program,bb_s=max(b_s,b^(-1)),a_s=bb_s^(-1),tf_s(w)=v(w)+bb_s/2*norm(w-u_s)^2 and td_s(w)=w-(1+bb_s)^(-1)*grad tf_s(w). tn_s is the least n with norm(grad tf_s(td_s^n(xref_s)))^2<=d*bb_s,or0 if none exists. put xp_s=td_s^tn_s(xref_s),g_s=grad v(xp_s),h_s=u_s-a_s*g_s. for an auxiliary draw a=(t,z) and terminal candidate x,let theta=pi*t/2 and z=sqrt(a_s)*z. define w_s(x,a)=clip[-1,1](inner((pi/2)*(cos(theta)*(x-h_s)-sin(theta)*z),g_s-grad v(h_s+sin(theta)*(x-h_s)+cos(theta)*z))). the auxiliary law nu is volume restricted to(0,1] times standardgaussian; the proposal q_s is the law of h_s+sqrt(a_s)*z0 for standardgaussian z0. one terminal attempt independently draws x~q_s,n~poisson(2),an infinite iid nu sequence a_i,and u~uniform[0,1]. it accepts when u<=product(i<n,(1+w_s(x,a_i))/2),with empty product1. rho_s is the law of an infinite iid sequence of such attempts. o_s returns x from the first accepted attempt,or the zero vector if no attempt accepts. this is the actual terminal stream program,with its full independent-product law. let muv=volume.tilted(-v),using canonical volume. gaussian smoothing g(mu,sigma) is the law of x+sigma*z under mu times standardgaussian. tilt is the total normalized exponential reweighting operation; probability is proved,not implicit. kernel composition lt composed p first draws a state from p then an output from lt. kl and nonnegative integrals take extended nonnegative values,possibly infinity. muv is a probability measure. there exist markov kernels lt:s->e,p:s->s,tg:s->e and h:s->e such that p_s=dirac_s when b^(-1)<=b_s and otherwise p_s is the law of u(s,(x,z)) under m_s times standardgaussian; the actual terminal stream output law (rho_s).map o_s equals lt_s; tg_s=muv.tilted(x->-(b_s/2)*norm(x-u_s)^2); and h_s=g(tg_s,sqrt(v_s)). define q_s=g(m_s,sqrt(tau_s/(1+b_s))) and e(s)=0 when b^(-1)<=b_s,otherwise kl(q_s||h_s). then e is measurable,and for every natural m,kl((lt composed p^(j+m))(s0)||tg_s0)<=sum(j<j,the nonnegative integral of e under(p^j)(s0))+delta^2/(j+1). the final real term is embedded in the extended nonnegative reals. i read only the anonymous packet and its approved notation context. i did not consult a source, declaration identity, or other repository files. the packet reports `compiled: true`; i did not independently run lean. the following is the complete reconstructed mathematical statement. let \\(e\\) be a finite-dimensional real inner-product space, equipped with its normed additive-group structure and its borel measurable-space structure. let \\(v:e\\to\\mathbb r\\), and let \\(\\kappa\\in\\mathbb r_{\\ge0}\\). assume \\[ 1\\le\\kappa,\\qquad v\\in c^2(e;\\mathbb r), \\] and, for every \\(x,w\\in e\\), \\[ \\kappa^{-1}\\|w\\|^2 \\le d^2v(x)[w,w]\\le \\|w\\|^2. \\] assume the real dimension \\[ d=\\dim_{\\mathbb r}e \\] is strictly positive. in formulas below, \\(d\\) denotes this dimension cast to a real number, and nonnegative-real state coordinates and \\(\\kappa\\) are likewise cast to reals where needed. the state space is \\[ s=\\mathbb r_{\\ge0}\\times e\\times e\\times\\mathbb n\\times e^{\\mathbb n}. \\] write a state as \\(s=(b,a,x,k,h)\\), where \\(h:\\mathbb n\\to e\\) is a full infinite sequence. let \\(\\eta:s\\to\\mathbb r\\) be measurable. let \\(c,\\delta\\in\\mathbb r\\) satisfy \\[ 0<c<\\tfrac14,\\qquad \\forall s\\in s,\\quad 0<\\eta(s)\\le c,\\qquad 0<\\delta\\le\\tfrac12. \\] fix any initial state \\(s_0=(b_0,a_0,x_0,k_0,h_0)\\), without further restrictions, and any markov kernel \\(m:s\\rightsquigarrow e\\). in particular, no approximation-quality, absolute-continuity, or finite-relative-entropy assumption is imposed on \\(m\\). define \\[ k(r)=\\frac{1+r}{\\kappa^{-1}+r}, \\qquad \\tau(s)= \\begin{cases} k(b),&2\\le k(b),\\\\ c,&k(b)<2, \\end{cases} \\] and \\[ l=2+\\log\\!\\left(\\frac{2k(b_0)d}{\\delta}\\right),\\qquad b=\\frac{1/1024}{\\sqrt{dl}+l}, \\] \\[ j=\\left\\lceil 8\\log\\!\\left(\\frac{ek(b_0)}b\\right)\\right\\rceil_{\\mathbb n}, \\qquad \\varepsilon=\\frac{\\delta}{\\sqrt{j+1}}. \\] the displayed natural ceiling is the total natural-number ceiling operation. the local name \\(\\varepsilon\\) does not occur subsequently in the formal conclusion. for each \\(s=(b,a,x,k,h)\\), define \\[ v(s)=\\frac{\\eta(s)+\\tau(s)}{1+b},\\qquad b^+(s)=b+\\operatorname{tonnreal}\\!\\left(v(s)^{-1}\\right). \\] here \\(\\operatorname{tonnreal}\\) truncates below at zero. keep this operation as displayed rather than silently replacing it in the formal specification. for \\(p=(s,(y,z))\\in s\\times(e\\times e)\\), define \\[ o(p)=y+\\sqrt{\\frac{\\tau(s)}{1+b}}\\,z, \\] \\[ a^+(p)=b^+(s)^{-1}\\bigl(ba+v(s)^{-1}o(p)\\bigr), \\] \\[ f_p(u)=v(u)+\\frac{b^+(s)}2\\|u-a^+(p)\\|^2, \\qquad t_p(u)=u-\\frac{1}{1+b^+(s)}\\nabla f_p(u). \\] inverses involving \\(b^+(s)\\) above use its real coercion. set \\[ q_n(p)=\\left\\|\\nabla f_p\\!\\left(t_p^{\\,n}(y)\\right)\\right\\|^2 \\] and define the total first-hit index \\[ n(p)= \\begin{cases} \\min\\{n\\in\\mathbb n:q_n(p)\\le(\\kappa^{-1}+b^+(s))d\\}, &\\text{if this set is nonempty},\\\\ 0,&\\text{otherwise}. \\end{cases} \\] define the state update \\[ u(p)=\\bigl(b^+(s),\\,a^+(p),\\,t_p^{\\,n(p)}(y),\\,k+1,\\,h^+(p)\\bigr), \\] where \\[ h^+(p)(0)=o(p),\\qquad h^+(p)(n+1)=h(n). \\] thus this iteration starts at the newly sampled \\(y\\), not at the third coordinate \\(x\\) of the incoming state. the terminal construction uses, for each state \\(s=(b,a,x,k,h)\\), \\[ \\bar b(s)=\\max\\{b,b^{-1}\\}, \\] \\[ f_s^{\\mathrm t}(u)=v(u)+\\frac{\\bar b(s)}2\\|u-a\\|^2, \\qquad t_s^{\\mathrm t}(u)=u-\\frac{1}{1+\\bar b(s)}\\nabla f_s^{\\mathrm t}(u), \\] \\[ q_n^{\\mathrm t}(s) =\\left\\|\\nabla f_s^{\\mathrm t}\\!\\left((t_s^{\\mathrm t})^n(x)\\right)\\right\\|^2, \\] \\[ n^{\\mathrm t}(s)= \\begin{cases} \\min\\{n\\in\\mathbb n:q_n^{\\mathrm t}(s)\\le d\\bar b(s)\\}, &\\text{if this set is nonempty},\\\\ 0,&\\text{otherwise}. \\end{cases} \\] then set \\[ x^{\\mathrm t}(s)=(t_s^{\\mathrm t})^{n^{\\mathrm t}(s)}(x), \\quad a(s)=\\bar b(s)^{-1}, \\quad g(s)=\\nabla v(x^{\\mathrm t}(s)), \\quad \\theta(s)=a-a(s)g(s). \\] this terminal iteration starts at the third state coordinate \\(x\\). let \\(\\gamma_e\\) denote standard gaussian measure on \\(e\\), and let \\[ \\nu=\\bigl(\\mathrm{leb}_{\\mathbb r}|_{(0,1]}\\bigr)\\otimes\\gamma_e. \\] for a candidate \\(y\\in e\\) and an auxiliary pair \\((t,z_0)\\in\\mathbb r\\times e\\), put \\(z=\\sqrt{a(s)}\\,z_0\\) and define \\[ \\begin{split} w_s(y,(t,z_0)) =\\min\\biggl\\{1,\\max\\biggl\\{-1,\\, \\biggl\\langle& \\frac{\\pi}{2}\\left( \\cos\\!\\left(\\frac{\\pi t}{2}\\right)(y-\\theta(s)) -\\sin\\!\\left(\\frac{\\pi t}{2}\\right)z \\right),\\\\ &g(s)-\\nabla v\\!\\left( \\theta(s) +\\sin\\!\\left(\\frac{\\pi t}{2}\\right)(y-\\theta(s)) +\\cos\\!\\left(\\frac{\\pi t}{2}\\right)z \\right) \\biggr\\rangle_{\\mathbb r} \\biggr\\}\\biggr\\}. \\end{split} \\] the expression is explicitly clipped to \\([-1,1]\\). define the candidate law \\[ r_s=(z\\mapsto\\theta(s)+\\sqrt{a(s)}\\,z)_\\#\\gamma_e. \\] one trial is a tuple \\[ p=(y,n,((t_i,z_i))_{i\\in\\mathbb n},u) \\] with independent coordinates distributed according to \\[ r_s\\otimes \\left( \\operatorname{poisson}(2)\\otimes \\left(\\nu^{\\otimes\\mathbb n} \\otimes(\\mathrm{leb}_{\\mathbb r}|_{[0,1]})\\right) \\right). \\] the tuple grouping in this expression is the grouping of the displayed nested products. let \\(\\rho_s\\) be the independent countable product of this trial law. thus \\(\\omega\\) is an infinite sequence of independent trials. declare a trial accepted exactly when \\[ u\\le \\prod_{i=0}^{n-1}\\frac{1+w_s(y,(t_i,z_i))}{2}. \\] the product is \\(1\\) for \\(n=0\\). define the total output function \\[ \\mathcal a_s(\\omega)= \\begin{cases} y_{n_*},& n_*=\\min\\{n\\in\\mathbb n:\\omega_n\\text{ is accepted}\\} \\text{ exists},\\\\ 0_e,&\\text{if no trial is accepted}. \\end{cases} \\] a further local measure is defined in the statement: \\[ \\pi_s^{\\mathrm t} = \\left((z\\mapsto a+\\sqrt{a(s)}z)_\\#\\gamma_e\\right) .\\operatorname{tilted}(-v). \\] this local measure is unused in the asserted conclusion; in particular, there is no displayed assertion identifying the terminal output law with \\(\\pi_s^{\\mathrm t}\\). let \\[ \\mu_v=\\mathrm{leb}_e.\\operatorname{tilted}(-v), \\] where `tilted` is the given total normalized exponential-reweighting operation. the theorem concludes that \\(\\mu_v\\) is a probability measure and that there exist markov kernels \\[ l_{\\mathrm t}:s\\rightsquigarrow e,\\qquad p:s\\rightsquigarrow s,\\qquad tg:s\\rightsquigarrow e,\\qquad h:s\\rightsquigarrow e \\] with all of the following properties. for every state \\(s=(b,a,x,k,h)\\), \\[ p(s)= \\begin{cases} \\delta_s,&b^{-1}\\le b,\\\\ u(s,\\cdot)_\\#\\bigl(m(s)\\otimes\\gamma_e\\bigr),&b<b^{-1}. \\end{cases} \\] the gaussian used here is independent of the draw \\(y\\sim m(s)\\). above the threshold the entire state is fixed, including its counter and history sequence. for every state \\(s\\), \\[ l_{\\mathrm t}(s)=(\\mathcal a_s)_\\#\\rho_s, \\] \\[ tg(s)= \\mu_v.\\operatorname{tilted} \\left(u\\mapsto-\\frac b2\\|u-a\\|^2\\right), \\] and \\[ h(s)= (u,w\\mapsto u+w)_\\# \\left[ tg(s)\\otimes (z\\mapsto\\sqrt{v(s)}z)_\\#\\gamma_e \\right]. \\] define \\[ q(s)= (u,w\\mapsto u+w)_\\# \\left[ m(s)\\otimes \\left(z\\mapsto \\sqrt{\\frac{\\tau(s)}{1+b}}z\\right)_\\#\\gamma_e \\right] \\] and the extended-nonnegative error \\[ e(s)= \\begin{cases} 0,&b^{-1}\\le b,\\\\ \\operatorname{kl}(q(s)\\|h(s)),&b<b^{-1}. \\end{cases} \\] the theorem asserts that \\(e\\) is measurable and, for every \\(m\\in\\mathbb n\\), \\[ \\boxed{ \\operatorname{kl} \\left( (l_{\\mathrm t}\\circ p^{\\,j+m})(s_0) \\,\\middle\\|\\,tg(s_0) \\right) \\le \\sum_{j=0}^{j-1} \\int_s^{-}e(s)\\,d(p^j(s_0))(s) + \\operatorname{ofreal}_{\\mathbb r_{\\ge0}\\cup\\{\\infty\\}} \\left(\\frac{\\delta^2}{j+1}\\right). } \\] here composition means first applying \\(p^{j+m}\\), then \\(l_{\\mathrm t}\\). kernel exponent zero is the identity kernel, so \\(p^0(s_0)=\\delta_{s_0}\\). the target on the left is the measure \\(tg(s_0)\\) at the original initial state. the sum on the right stops at \\(j-1\\), independently of \\(m\\). all kl divergences and displayed nonnegative integrals take values in the extended nonnegative reals. the assertion includes the possibility of infinite kl or an infinite right-hand side; it contains no implicit finiteness or absolute-continuity hypothesis. when the right-hand side is finite, the inequality bounds the final kl by that finite quantity; when it is infinite, the displayed inequality remains an extended-real assertion. the statement retains three explicit fallback branches: the ordinary gradient-iteration first hit defaults to \\(0\\) if absent, the terminal gradient-iter…",
