      "original_theorem": "Chewi arXiv:2605.07006v1 Theorem3.7. In the smooth Euclidean setting, let f be beta-smooth and h<=1/beta. For actual iterates x_(k+1)=x_k-h grad f(x_k), min_{k=0,...,N-1} ||grad f(x_k)|| <= sqrt(2(f(x0)-fstar)/(N*h)). Section2 fixes an attained global minimum fstar=f(xstar). Its proof telescopes Lemma3.1: (h/(2N)) sum_{k<N} ||grad f(x_k)||^2 <= (1/N) sum_{k<N}(f(x_k)-f(x_(k+1))) <= (f(x0)-fstar)/N. The theorem line does not explicitly state h>0 or N>=1.",
      "reconstructed_theorem": "For every complete real inner product space E, every function f:E→ℝ, every pair of real numbers β,h, and every z∈E, assume f(z)≤f(x) for every x∈E, h>0, βh≤1, and, for all x,y∈E, f(y)≤f(x)+⟨gradient f(x),y−x⟩+(β/2)‖y−x‖². Here gradient f(x) denotes the Riesz vector of the totalized Fréchet derivative; no separate differentiability hypothesis is imposed, and no positivity hypothesis on β is imposed. For every initial point x₀∈E, define xₖ=T^[k](x₀), where T(x)=x−h·gradient f(x), h·v is real scalar multiplication, and the zeroth iterate is x₀. For every natural number N>0, there exists a natural number k with 0≤k<N such that ‖gradient f(xₖ)‖≤sqrt(2(f(x₀)−f(z))/((N:ℝ)h)). The square root is the nonnegative real square root, totalized to zero on negative arguments; here its argument is nonnegative because z is a global minimizer and (N:ℝ)h>0. The index set includes k=0 and excludes k=N. N=1 is included and forces k=0. N=0 and h=0 are excluded by the hypotheses. If f(x₀)=f(z), the bound is zero.",
