      "source_url": "",
      "original_theorem": "Chewi arXiv:2605.07006v1 Exercise3.2. In the Section3 C2 Euclidean setting, assume f is alpha-convex and beta-smooth. Write T_h(x)=x-h grad f(x). The fundamental theorem of calculus gives ||T_h(y)-T_h(x)|| <= integral_0^1 ||DT_h((1-t)x+t y)||op ||y-x|| dt. Bound the eigenvalues of DT_h and show that h=2/(alpha+beta) minimizes the resulting bound. The resulting factor is (kappa-1)/(kappa+1), with kappa=beta/alpha, improving the preceding convergence bound. The condition-number contraction interpretation uses positive strong convexity and smoothness. ASTIS-authored faithful paraphrase; no source wording is silently replaced by a Lean extension.",
      "reconstructed_theorem": "Let E be any complete real inner-product space, with its induced norm (no finite-dimensionality assumption), and let f:E→ℝ be twice continuously Fréchet differentiable. Write ∇f(x) for the Riesz vector representing its Fréchet derivative. The global α-strong-convexity assumption means: for every u,v∈E and every s,t∈ℝ with s≥0, t≥0 and s+t=1, f(su+tv)≤s f(u)+t f(v)−(α/2)st‖u−v‖². The global quadratic upper bound means: for every u,v∈E, f(v)≤f(u)+⟨∇f(u),v−u⟩+(β/2)‖v−u‖². For every choice of real α,β,h, if these two global assumptions hold and h≥0, then for every x,y∈E, ‖(y−h∇f(y))−(x−h∇f(x))‖≤max{|1−hα|,|1−hβ|}‖y−x‖. The constant depends only on h,α,β. There is no explicit positivity assumption on α or β, no explicit α≤β premise, no upper bound on h, and no strict contraction assertion. The case h=0 gives the identity-map norm bound. The case x=y is included, and the space may be the zero space.",
