      "status": "fidelity-mismatch",
      "subtitle": "chewi-opt-v1-exercise-3-3 · lean-weakened-conclusion",
      "summary": "Blind reconstruction and seven-slot semantic comparison for AutoSamplingTheory.TechnicalLemmas.Analysis.QuadraticGradientDescent.quadratic_eigenmode.",
      "column": 7,
      "url": "lean-foundations.html?view=semantic",
      "source_url": "",
      "original_theorem": "Chewi arXiv:2605.07006v1 Exercise3.3. Let f(x)=inner(x,A*x)/2 for a positive-definite symmetric matrix A on Euclidean space. Derive the gradient-descent iterates explicitly and check how sharp the results of this section are. The actual update is x_(n+1)=x_n-h gradient f(x_n). The exercise prints no solution. ASTIS-authored faithful paraphrase. The operator-power and eigenmode identities are selected proof components; full assessment of every Section3 rate remains an uncovered source obligation.",
      "reconstructed_theorem": "For every complete real inner product space E, every continuous real-linear endomorphism H:E→E satisfying ⟨Hu,v⟩=⟨u,Hv⟩ for all u,v∈E, every μ∈ℝ and every x∈E with Hx=μx, define f(y)=⟨y,Hy⟩/2. For every h∈ℝ and N∈ℕ, put z=T^[N](x), where T(y)=y−h∇f(y). Then simultaneously z=(1−hμ)^N x, ‖z‖=|1−hμ|^N‖x‖, and f(z)=(1−hμ)^(2N)f(x). Here iteration at N=0 is the identity and every zeroth power, including 0^0, is 1. Neither x≠0 nor μ>0 nor h>0 is assumed, and there is no step-size restriction. Thus zero starting vectors, zero step size, zero μ, and 1−hμ=0 are included. In the last case z=0 for N≥1, whereas N=0 still gives z=x. These are exact identities and assert no contraction, decay rate, convergence, or sign of f.",
      "fidelity_verdict": "lean-weakened-conclusion",
      "blindness": "source hidden from decoder",
