    {
      "id": "semantic-audit:ASTIS-RT-ANDI-OPT-GradientSharpness",
      "kind": "semantic-audit",
      "label": "ASTIS-RT-ANDI-OPT-GradientSharpness",
      "status": "fidelity-exact",
      "subtitle": "chewi-opt-v1-exercise-3-3-fixed-step-sharpness · equivalent-after-elaboration",
      "summary": "Blind reconstruction and seven-slot semantic comparison for AutoSamplingTheory.TechnicalLemmas.Analysis.GradientDescentSharpness.exists_quadratic_worst_case.",
      "column": 7,
      "url": "lean-foundations.html?view=semantic",
      "source_url": "",
      "original_theorem": "The quadratic exercise asks the reader to test Section3 estimates using positive-definite quadratics. This item isolates the sharpness of the uniform constant-step distance contraction from Exercise3.2: its balanced step has factor (beta−alpha)/(alpha+beta) over the class with curvature bounded below by alpha and above by beta. The exact quadratic witness is an ASTIS derivation, not a printed source theorem or an assertion that every Section3 estimate is sharp. Source: Chewi arXiv:2605.07006v1 Exercises3.2-3.3, positive-curvature twice-differentiable Euclidean setting. The source asks for positive-definite quadratic sharpness checks, not only an inequality supplied as an assumption. The selected component concerns uniform constant-step distance rates; remaining comparisons are explicitly outside this item.",
      "reconstructed_theorem": "For every pair of real numbers α and β satisfying 0 < α ≤ β, and for every real number h, there exists a real number μ, equal to either α or β and strictly positive, with the following properties. Define f: ℝ → ℝ by f(x) = μ x²/2. The function f is twice continuously Fréchet differentiable on ℝ. It is globally strongly convex with modulus α: for all real x,y and every t in [0,1], f((1−t)x+ty) ≤ (1−t)f(x)+t f(y)−(α/2)t(1−t)|y−x|². This asserts a lower bound α on the strong convexity modulus, not necessarily its maximal value. For all real x,y, f(y) ≤ f(x)+f′(x)(y−x)+(β/2)|y−x|². Also 0 is a global minimizer: f(0) ≤ f(x) for all real x. Here the gradient is the actual derivative represented as a real vector, so gradient f(x)=f′(x)=μx, the real inner product is multiplication, and the real norm is absolute value.\nFor this one choice of μ and f, define T_h(x)=x−h f′(x), and define T_*(x)=x−[2/(α+β)]f′(x). For every natural number N, including N=0, all three conclusions hold simultaneously:\n(1) |T_h^[N](1)| = [max{|1−hα|, |1−hβ|}]^N;\n(2) [(β−α)/(α+β)]^N ≤ |T_h^[N](1)|;\n(3) |T_*^[N](1)| = [(β−α)/(α+β)]^N.\nThe notation T^[N] means N-fold function iteration, with T^[0] the identity. Every scalar zeroth power, including 0^0, is 1, so at N=0 both iterates are evaluated as 1 and each displayed power is 1. The assumptions imply β>0 and α+β>0, and allow α=β. There is no positivity or stability restriction on h: it may be zero, negative, or any positive real. The witness μ may depend on α, β, and h, but is chosen before N and works for all N at once; the optimal-step identity in (3) holds for that same witness even if it was selected for another value of h. The theorem constructs a one-dimensional endpoint-curvature quadratic with the stated certificates and iteration properties, starting at 1, whose distance to its minimizer 0 is the displayed norm. It makes no universal claim for arbitrary functions or arbitrary starting points.",
      "fidelity_verdict": "equivalent-after-elaboration",
      "blindness": "source hidden from decoder",
      "semantic_slots": [
        {
          "slot": "objects",
          "original": "The selected exercise component uses positive-definite quadratic GD witnesses.",
          "reconstructed": "A real objective f(x)=mu*x^2/2 with actual gradient, certified minimizer 0, and its GD iterates from 1.",
          "relation": "explicit-elaboration",
          "evidence": "The complete module constructs f rather than taking a contraction inequality as a hypothesis. Its local hg proves gradient f x = mu*x by differentiation; positivity of mu makes the scalar matrix positive definite."
        },
        {
          "slot": "domains",
          "original": "The source is Euclidean and twice continuously differentiable; the selected regime has positive curvature.",
          "reconstructed": "All points and parameters are real, f is on all of R, 0<alpha<=beta, N is natural.",
          "relation": "explicit-elaboration",
          "evidence": "Dimension one is an admissible Euclidean witness for a class-level existential sharpness result. This does not claim the general matrix trajectory theorem or a result about every starting point. ContDiff R 2 f is constructed explicitly."
        },
        {
          "slot": "quantifiers",
          "original": "The selected component tests uniform constant-step distance sharpness.",
          "reconstructed": "For every alpha,beta with 0<alpha<=beta and every real h there exists mu in {alpha,beta}, with all certificates and all three conclusions for every N.",
          "relation": "explicit-elaboration",
          "evidence": "The endpoint is selected by comparing the two absolute factors before intro N. Hence mu may depend on h but not on N. The balanced-step equality is inside that same existential witness. Negative and zero h are included; their inclusion gives a valid explicit algebraic extension, not an adaptive-step claim."
        },
        {
          "slot": "assumptions",
          "original": "The source uses strong convexity, smoothness, and positive-definite quadratics; the item selects curvature bounds alpha,beta.",
          "reconstructed": "Only 0<alpha and alpha<=beta are assumed. C2, alpha-strong convexity, the beta quadratic upper model and minimization at 0 are conclusions for the constructed function.",
          "relation": "explicit-elaboration",
          "evidence": "The proof establishes alpha<=mu<=beta. Subtracting mu*norm(x)^2/2 leaves a constant convex function, then monotonicity lowers the convexity modulus to alpha. The exact remainder is mu*(y-x)^2/2 <= beta*(y-x)^2/2. There is no assumed trajectory inequality, assumed gradient identity, or hidden regularity parameter."
        },
        {
          "slot": "conclusion",
          "original": "Exercise 3.2 gives the balanced contraction factor; Exercise 3.3 requests quadratic sharpness checks. The pinned item selects only this distance component.",
          "reconstructed": "The h trajectory has norm M_h^N >= q^N and the balanced-step trajectory has norm q^N, with M_h=max(abs(1-h*alpha),abs(1-h*beta)) and q=(beta-alpha)/(alpha+beta).",
          "relation": "explicit-elaboration",
          "evidence": "Endpoint choice gives abs(1-h*mu)=M_h. The exact eigenmode identity supplies every iterate. The envelope lower bound and nonnegativity permit powers; balanced endpoint multipliers are q and -q. At N=0 both sides equal 1, including alpha=beta. This realizes the selected sharpness witness; it is not itself the universal upper-bound theorem."
        },
        {
          "slot": "scopes",
          "original": "The full exercise asks about Section 3 estimates; this publication expressly selects uniform fixed-step distance sharpness.",
          "reconstructed": "A class-level endpoint witness and exact norm identities for constant-step GD, with other comparisons excluded.",
          "relation": "explicit-elaboration",
          "evidence": "The source statement, whole-module header, lesson, source scope and unbound other-section-comparisons obligation consistently retain the restricted coverage. With the already separate uniform upper bound, the witness establishes sharpness of that bound. It is neither completion of the whole exercise nor a lower bound for adaptive steps or arbitrary first-order oracles."
        },
        {
          "slot": "constant dependencies",
          "original": "Definition 3.2 describes quadratic condition number by extremal eigenvalues; the selected item uses class curvature bounds.",
          "reconstructed": "alpha and beta are admissible moduli for a scalar witness of curvature mu. The fixed-step witness can depend on h. q and hstar depend only on alpha,beta.",
          "relation": "explicit-elaboration",
          "evidence": "When alpha<beta, the scalar witness has tight lower and upper curvature both mu, so its intrinsic condition number is 1, not beta/alpha. The publication explicitly avoids assigning the class ratio to that witness. Thus sharpness is uniform across the class for a step chosen from its bounds, not optimality of that step for a known scalar objective. alpha+beta>0; alpha=beta gives q=0 without division by zero."
        }
