      "search": "semantic-audit:astis-rt-andi-opt-gradientsharpness semantic-audit astis-rt-andi-opt-gradientsharpness chewi-opt-v1-exercise-3-3-fixed-step-sharpness · equivalent-after-elaboration blind reconstruction and seven-slot semantic comparison for autosamplingtheory.technicallemmas.analysis.gradientdescentsharpness.exists_quadratic_worst_case. equivalent-after-elaboration source hidden from decoder the quadratic exercise asks the reader to test section3 estimates using positive-definite quadratics. this item isolates the sharpness of the uniform constant-step distance contraction from exercise3.2: its balanced step has factor (beta−alpha)/(alpha+beta) over the class with curvature bounded below by alpha and above by beta. the exact quadratic witness is an astis derivation, not a printed source theorem or an assertion that every section3 estimate is sharp. source: chewi arxiv:2605.07006v1 exercises3.2-3.3, positive-curvature twice-differentiable euclidean setting. the source asks for positive-definite quadratic sharpness checks, not only an inequality supplied as an assumption. the selected component concerns uniform constant-step distance rates; remaining comparisons are explicitly outside this item. for every pair of real numbers α and β satisfying 0 < α ≤ β, and for every real number h, there exists a real number μ, equal to either α or β and strictly positive, with the following properties. define f: ℝ → ℝ by f(x) = μ x²/2. the function f is twice continuously fréchet differentiable on ℝ. it is globally strongly convex with modulus α: for all real x,y and every t in [0,1], f((1−t)x+ty) ≤ (1−t)f(x)+t f(y)−(α/2)t(1−t)|y−x|². this asserts a lower bound α on the strong convexity modulus, not necessarily its maximal value. for all real x,y, f(y) ≤ f(x)+f′(x)(y−x)+(β/2)|y−x|². also 0 is a global minimizer: f(0) ≤ f(x) for all real x. here the gradient is the actual derivative represented as a real vector, so gradient f(x)=f′(x)=μx, the real inner product is multiplication, and the real norm is absolute value. for this one choice of μ and f, define t_h(x)=x−h f′(x), and define t_*(x)=x−[2/(α+β)]f′(x). for every natural number n, including n=0, all three conclusions hold simultaneously: (1) |t_h^[n](1)| = [max{|1−hα|, |1−hβ|}]^n; (2) [(β−α)/(α+β)]^n ≤ |t_h^[n](1)|; (3) |t_*^[n](1)| = [(β−α)/(α+β)]^n. the notation t^[n] means n-fold function iteration, with t^[0] the identity. every scalar zeroth power, including 0^0, is 1, so at n=0 both iterates are evaluated as 1 and each displayed power is 1. the assumptions imply β>0 and α+β>0, and allow α=β. there is no positivity or stability restriction on h: it may be zero, negative, or any positive real. the witness μ may depend on α, β, and h, but is chosen before n and works for all n at once; the optimal-step identity in (3) holds for that same witness even if it was selected for another value of h. the theorem constructs a one-dimensional endpoint-curvature quadratic with the stated certificates and iteration properties, starting at 1, whose distance to its minimizer 0 is the displayed norm. it makes no universal claim for arbitrary functions or arbitrary starting points. accepted https://arxiv.org/html/2605.07006v1#s3; exercise3.3, selected sharpness check for exercise3.2; definition3.2 convention autosamplingtheory.technicallemmas.analysis.gradientdescentsharpness.exists_quadratic_worst_case autosamplingtheory/technicallemmas/analysis/gradientdescentsharpness.lean true andi-sharp-worker sharp_blind 1a801e889ad950e92af0d17bbebd6fc66bd7a7bdfe2dedb77054c3c90dbcffb9 accepted sharp_source objects the selected exercise component uses positive-definite quadratic gd witnesses. a real objective f(x)=mu*x^2/2 with actual gradient, certified minimizer 0, and its gd iterates from 1. explicit-elaboration the complete module constructs f rather than taking a contraction inequality as a hypothesis. its local hg proves gradient f x = mu*x by differentiation; positivity of mu makes the scalar matrix positive definite. domains the source is euclidean and twice continuously differentiable; the selected regime has positive curvature. all points and parameters are real, f is on all of r, 0<alpha<=beta, n is natural. explicit-elaboration dimension one is an admissible euclidean witness for a class-level existential sharpness result. this does not claim the general matrix trajectory theorem or a result about every starting point. contdiff r 2 f is constructed explicitly. quantifiers the selected component tests uniform constant-step distance sharpness. for every alpha,beta with 0<alpha<=beta and every real h there exists mu in {alpha,beta}, with all certificates and all three conclusions for every n. explicit-elaboration the endpoint is selected by comparing the two absolute factors before intro n. hence mu may depend on h but not on n. the balanced-step equality is inside that same existential witness. negative and zero h are included; their inclusion gives a valid explicit algebraic extension, not an adaptive-step claim. assumptions the source uses strong convexity, smoothness, and positive-definite quadratics; the item selects curvature bounds alpha,beta. only 0<alpha and alpha<=beta are assumed. c2, alpha-strong convexity, the beta quadratic upper model and minimization at 0 are conclusions for the constructed function. explicit-elaboration the proof establishes alpha<=mu<=beta. subtracting mu*norm(x)^2/2 leaves a constant convex function, then monotonicity lowers the convexity modulus to alpha. the exact remainder is mu*(y-x)^2/2 <= beta*(y-x)^2/2. there is no assumed trajectory inequality, assumed gradient identity, or hidden regularity parameter. conclusion exercise 3.2 gives the balanced contraction factor; exercise 3.3 requests quadratic sharpness checks. the pinned item selects only this distance component. the h trajectory has norm m_h^n >= q^n and the balanced-step trajectory has norm q^n, with m_h=max(abs(1-h*alpha),abs(1-h*beta)) and q=(beta-alpha)/(alpha+beta). explicit-elaboration endpoint choice gives abs(1-h*mu)=m_h. the exact eigenmode identity supplies every iterate. the envelope lower bound and nonnegativity permit powers; balanced endpoint multipliers are q and -q. at n=0 both sides equal 1, including alpha=beta. this realizes the selected sharpness witness; it is not itself the universal upper-bound theorem. scopes the full exercise asks about section 3 estimates; this publication expressly selects uniform fixed-step distance sharpness. a class-level endpoint witness and exact norm identities for constant-step gd, with other comparisons excluded. explicit-elaboration the source statement, whole-module header, lesson, source scope and unbound other-section-comparisons obligation consistently retain the restricted coverage. with the already separate uniform upper bound, the witness establishes sharpness of that bound. it is neither completion of the whole exercise nor a lower bound for adaptive steps or arbitrary first-order oracles. constant dependencies definition 3.2 describes quadratic condition number by extremal eigenvalues; the selected item uses class curvature bounds. alpha and beta are admissible moduli for a scalar witness of curvature mu. the fixed-step witness can depend on h. q and hstar depend only on alpha,beta. explicit-elaboration when alpha<beta, the scalar witness has tight lower and upper curvature both mu, so its intrinsic condition number is 1, not beta/alpha. the publication explicitly avoids assigning the class ratio to that witness. thus sharpness is uniform across the class for a step chosen from its bounds, not optimality of that step for a known scalar objective. alpha+beta>0; alpha=beta gives q=0 without division by zero. scopes informational accepted coverage is the explicitly selected constant-step distance component, not all section 3 comparisons. the pinned source paraphrase and publication obligation map both preserve the omitted comparisons; the lesson claims exactly the constructed witness. constant_dependencies informational class curvature bounds are not both intrinsic tight constants of the scalar witness unless alpha=beta. mu belongs to {alpha,beta}; the module and publication assumptions explicitly identify alpha,beta as class bounds and prohibit the intrinsic-condition-number interpretation. quantifiers informational the exact witness theorem makes the order of quantifiers and all-real fixed-step scope explicit. the lean statement is forall h, exists mu, forall n; the proof picks an endpoint independent of n and the same endpoint also attains the balanced factor.",
