      "subtitle": "chewi-opt-v1-exercise-3-3-convex-gap-sharpness · equivalent-after-elaboration",
      "summary": "Blind reconstruction and seven-slot semantic comparison for AutoSamplingTheory.TechnicalLemmas.Analysis.ConvexGradientGapSharpness.quadratic_gap_lower_bound.",
      "column": 7,
      "url": "lean-foundations.html?view=semantic",
      "source_url": "",
      "original_theorem": "Exercise3.3 asks the reader to examine Section3 bounds with positive-definite quadratic objectives. This item selects the convex function-value estimate from Theorem3.4: at step1/beta and initial distance R, the upper gap is beta R²/(2N) for positive N. An explicit quadratic family demonstrates its order sharpness at R=1. The exercise does not print the witness or a lower-bound constant. Source: Chewi arXiv:2605.07006v1 Exercise3.3, Theorem3.4 convex function-value specialization and Table1. The selected component is a positive-definite quadratic order-sharpness witness at prescribed step1/beta, initial distance1. Other section comparisons and exact optimal constants are explicitly outside this item.",
      "reconstructed_theorem": "For every real number β>0 and every natural number N (including N=0), define μ=β/[2(N+1)] and f:ℝ→ℝ by f(x)=μx²/2. Then μ>0; f is twice continuously Fréchet differentiable; f is globally μ-strongly convex, meaning that for every x,y∈ℝ and t∈[0,1], f((1−t)x+ty)≤(1−t)f(x)+tf(y)−(μ/2)t(1−t)|y−x|²; for every x,y∈ℝ it satisfies f(y)≤f(x)+f′(x)(y−x)+(β/2)|y−x|²; and 0 is a global minimizer, i.e. f(0)≤f(x) for every x∈ℝ. In addition, let T:ℝ→ℝ be T(x)=x−(1/β)f′(x), and let z=T iterated N times starting at 1 (equivalently x₀=1, xₖ₊₁=xₖ−(1/β)f′(xₖ), z=x_N). Then f(z)−f(0)=β/[4(N+1)]·(1−1/[2(N+1)])^(2N), and β/[16(N+1)]≤f(z)−f(0). Here μ and f depend on both β and the chosen horizon N; the same fixed f is used throughout those N iterations. At N=0 iteration is the identity and z=1; the displayed exponent 2N is zero and its power equals 1. The gradient in the formal statement is the actual Fréchet-gradient, identified here with f′ for this smooth real scalar function. The modulus μ is asserted to be a valid strong convexity lower bound, with no assertion that it is maximal.",
      "fidelity_verdict": "equivalent-after-elaboration",
      "blindness": "source hidden from decoder",
      "semantic_slots": [
        {
