      "original_theorem": "Section2 considers a supplied gradient flow X prime(t)=-gradient f(X(t)) at nonnegative times, assumes a twice continuously differentiable Euclidean objective with an attained minimum, and leaves well-posedness aside. Lemma2.1 derives d f(X(t))/dt=-norm gradient f(X(t)) squared by the chain rule. Definition2.5 uses a positive modulus alpha with 2 alpha(f(x)-f(z))<=norm gradient f(x) squared. Corollary2.6 then gives f(X(t))-f(z)<=(f(X(0))-f(z)) exp(-2 alpha t) for all t>=0.",
      "reconstructed_theorem": "Let E be a complete real inner-product space and let f:E→ℝ be Fréchet differentiable everywhere. Let X:ℝ→E, z∈E, and α,T∈ℝ. Assume α>0 and T≥0; X is continuous on [0,T]; for every t∈[0,T), the right derivative of X at t, taken within [t,∞), exists and equals −∇f(X(t)); z is a global minimizer of f; and for every x∈E, 2α(f(x)−f(z))≤‖∇f(x)‖². Then for each t∈[0,T), the right derivative of f∘X at t within [t,∞) equals −‖∇f(X(t))‖². Moreover, for every t∈[0,T], 0≤f(X(t))−f(z)≤(f(X(0))−f(z)) exp(−2αt).",
