      "original_theorem": "In the Section2 Euclidean C2 setting, let f be alpha-convex with alpha>=0 as in Definition1.5. Given two gradient flows X prime=-gradient f(X) and Y prime=-gradient f(Y) at nonnegative times, their distance at t is at most exp(-alpha*t) times their initial distance. The proof differentiates squared separation, applies the gradient monotonicity characterization (1.5), then Gronwall with coefficient -2 alpha. The section assumes an attained minimum generally, but this pairwise proof does not use it; well-posedness is left aside.",
      "reconstructed_theorem": "Let E be any complete real inner-product space (with its induced norm), let f:E→ℝ, let X,Y:ℝ→E, and let α,T∈ℝ. Suppose α≥0 and T≥0, f is Fréchet differentiable at every point of E, and f is globally α-strongly convex in the convention f((1−λ)x+λy)≤(1−λ)f(x)+λf(y)−(α/2)λ(1−λ)‖y−x‖² for all x,y∈E and λ∈[0,1]. Suppose X and Y are continuous on [0,T] in the relative topology. For every t∈[0,T), suppose their right derivatives exist, using the domain [t,∞), and satisfy X′₊(t)=−∇f(X(t)) and Y′₊(t)=−∇f(Y(t)), where ∇f is the Riesz representative of the Fréchet derivative. Then for every t∈[0,T], ‖Y(t)−X(t)‖≤exp(−αt)‖Y(0)−X(0)‖. Both the initial and final endpoints are included in the conclusion. No derivative condition at T is assumed; if T=0 the derivative assumptions are empty. No finite-dimensionality, continuity of the derivative, Lipschitz gradient, minimizer, or global existence assertion is imposed.",
