      "source_url": "",
      "original_theorem": "In the Section2 Euclidean C2 setting with a supplied gradient flow and attained minimizer z, Theorem2.4 assumes alpha-convexity with alpha>=0 and states for all t>=0 that f(X(t))-f(z)<=alpha norm(X(0)-z) squared/[2(exp(alpha*t)-1)]. For alpha=0 it explicitly instructs reading the coefficient as the limiting value 1/(2t). The proof uses squared-distance dissipation, Gronwall and objective descent. The printed all-nonnegative-time wording is preserved here: no finite real-valued coefficient is specified at t=0.",
      "reconstructed_theorem": "Let E be any complete real inner-product space, f:E→ℝ an everywhere Fréchet differentiable function, X:ℝ→E a curve, z∈E, and α,T∈ℝ with α≥0 and T≥0. Suppose f is α-strongly convex on all of E in the convention\nf((1−λ)x+λy) ≤ (1−λ)f(x)+λf(y) − (α/2)λ(1−λ)‖y−x‖²\nfor every x,y∈E and λ∈[0,1]. Suppose z is a global minimizer: f(z)≤f(x) for every x∈E. Suppose X is continuous on the closed interval [0,T], and for every s∈[0,T) its right derivative at s exists and equals −∇f(X(s)), where ∇f is the Riesz representative of the derivative of f. Then for every t∈(0,T],\n0 ≤ f(X(t))−f(z) ≤ B(t),\nwhere B(t)=‖X(0)−z‖²/(2t) when α=0, and B(t)=[α/(2(exp(αt)−1))]‖X(0)−z‖² when α≠0 (hence α>0 under the hypotheses).\nThe differential equation is assumed at time 0 and at every time strictly below T, but not at T. The conclusion includes T when T>0 and excludes 0; for T=0 there are no conclusion times and the differential equation hypothesis has empty time domain. No finite-dimensionality, second differentiability, gradient Lipschitz continuity, or uniqueness of the minimizer is assumed.",
