      "source_url": "",
      "original_theorem": "In the standing Section2 C2 Euclidean setting with a supplied actual gradient flow and global minimizer z, Exercise2.1 assumes convexity. It asks to show that L(t)=t squared times gradient norm squared+2t times objective gap+squared distance to z has nonpositive derivative, and to deduce gradient norm squared<=squared initial distance/t squared. First-order support then yields the improved objective gap bound squared initial distance/(4t). The exercise also asks for a sharpness witness using a scaled positive-part function; that separate nonsmooth witness is not included in the current formalized upper-bound component.",
      "reconstructed_theorem": "Let E be any complete real inner-product space with its associated norm, let f:E→ℝ, X:ℝ→E, z∈E and T∈ℝ. Assume T≥0, f is twice continuously Fréchet differentiable on E, f is convex on all of E, and z is a global minimizer of f. Assume X is continuous on [0,T], including relative endpoint continuity, and for every t∈[0,T) the right derivative of X at t, taken within [t,∞), exists and equals −∇f(X(t)). Define L(t)=t²‖∇f(X(t))‖²+2t(f(X(t))−f(z))+‖X(t)−z‖². Then L is nonincreasing on [0,T]: for every a,b∈[0,T] with a≤b, L(b)≤L(a). Moreover, for every t∈(0,T], both ‖∇f(X(t))‖²≤‖X(0)−z‖²/t² and f(X(t))−f(z)≤‖X(0)−z‖²/(4t). The gradient is the Riesz representative of the Fréchet derivative. No derivative assumption at T is required. For T=0, the rate assertions have empty time domain and the monotonicity assertion is on a singleton. No finite-dimensionality, positive lower bound on T, uniqueness of z or X, Lipschitz constant, or existence assertion for a trajectory is included.",
