      "source_url": "",
      "original_theorem": "Assume an algorithm optimizes every convex beta-smooth Euclidean objective from a point within radius R of a minimizer to objective error epsilon in phi(beta R²/epsilon) first-order queries. For an alpha-strongly convex beta-smooth objective, Lemma4.1 constructs a restarted method with stated order O(phi(8kappa) log(alpha R²/epsilon)), kappa=beta/alpha. Its proof calls the base method at radii R/2^k and tolerances alpha(R/2^k)²/8; quadratic growth halves the radius after each call. After reaching radius at most sqrt(epsilon/alpha), one final call at tolerance epsilon gives the desired objective accuracy. The proof records per-stage phi(8kappa) and final phi(kappa) costs. The finite construction, final call and full asymptotic/oracle conclusion are distinct obligations.",
      "reconstructed_theorem": "Let E be any complete real inner-product space (with its normed additive commutative group structure), and let f:E→ℝ be continuously Fréchet differentiable on E. Let α, β, R, ε be arbitrary strictly positive real numbers. Assume f is α-strongly convex on all E, in the convention f((1−t)x+ty) ≤ (1−t)f(x)+tf(y)−(α/2)t(1−t)‖x−y‖² for every x,y∈E and t∈[0,1]. Let z∈E be a global minimizer of f and x₀∈E satisfy ‖x₀−z‖≤R.\nLet A:E×ℝ×ℝ→E×ℕ and φ:ℝ→ℕ be arbitrary maps satisfying the following uniform contract: for every y∈E and every strictly positive r,δ∈ℝ with ‖y−z‖≤r, if A(y,r,δ)=(a,c), then f(a)−f(z)≤δ and c≤φ(βr²/δ). Counts are natural numbers, including zero. No monotonicity or regularity of A or φ is assumed; in particular β is not asserted to be a gradient Lipschitz constant.\nFor every N∈ℕ satisfying α(R/2^N)²≤ε, define r_k=R/2^k and a sequence (x_k,C_k)∈E×ℕ by (x_0,C_0)=(x₀,0) and, for each k≥0, (a_k,c_k)=A(x_k,r_k,αr_k²/8), x_{k+1}=a_k, C_{k+1}=C_k+c_k. Finally define (x_fin,c_fin)=A(x_N,√(ε/α),ε), where the square root is the nonnegative real square root.\nThen all four conclusions hold simultaneously: ‖x_N−z‖≤R/2^N; C_N≤N·φ(8(β/α)); f(x_fin)−f(z)≤ε; and C_N+c_fin≤N·φ(8(β/α))+φ(β/α). These count products and sums are in ℕ. The theorem allows N=0: its condition is αR²≤ε, the recursive state remains (x₀,0), and the final call alone achieves objective gap at most ε and count at most φ(β/α). None of α,β,R,ε may be zero. The positivity assumptions make all recursive radii, recursive tolerances and the final square-root radius strictly positive. The conclusion is asserted for any N satisfying the displayed inequality; no formula selecting a minimal N, logarithmic iteration bound, or existence of A is asserted.",
