    {
      "id": "semantic-audit:ASTIS-RT-ANDI-OPT-RegularizationFirstOrder",
      "kind": "semantic-audit",
      "label": "ASTIS-RT-ANDI-OPT-RegularizationFirstOrder",
      "status": "fidelity-mismatch",
      "subtitle": "chewi-opt-v1-lemma-4-2 · lean-weakened-conclusion",
      "summary": "Blind reconstruction and seven-slot semantic comparison for AutoSamplingTheory.TechnicalLemmas.Analysis.QuadraticRegularizationFirstOrder.curvature_gradient_and_smoothness.",
      "column": 7,
      "url": "lean-foundations.html?view=semantic",
      "source_url": "",
      "original_theorem": "Assume a first-order method optimizes the alpha-strongly convex beta-smooth class in phi(kappa) log(alpha R²/epsilon) queries, where kappa=beta/alpha. Lemma4.2 reduces the convex beta-smooth class to it with stated order O(phi(2 beta R²/epsilon)). The proof forms W=f+delta||x-x0||²/2 with delta=epsilon/R², solves W to tolerance epsilon/2, and compares its minimizer w with an original minimizer z. W(w)<=W(z) and f(z)<=f(w) show ||w-x0||<=||z-x0||<=R; the two half-error terms then give f(x)-f(z)<=epsilon. In the regime epsilon<=beta R², the regularized smoothness is beta+delta<=2beta and its condition number is at most2 beta R²/epsilon. The existence/comparison, curvature/smoothness and oracle-complexity obligations are separate.",
      "reconstructed_theorem": "Let E be any complete real inner product space, equipped with its induced norm, and let f:E→ℝ be everywhere Fréchet differentiable and convex on E. For every pair of nonnegative real numbers β and δ, suppose the actual Riesz gradient of f is β-Lipschitz: ‖∇f(x)−∇f(y)‖≤β‖x−y‖ for all x,y∈E. Then for every u∈E, define W:E→ℝ by W(x)=f(x)+(δ/2)‖x−u‖². The function W is everywhere Fréchet differentiable and δ-strongly convex on E: for all x,y∈E and t∈[0,1], W((1−t)x+ty)≤(1−t)W(x)+tW(y)−(δ/2)t(1−t)‖x−y‖². For every x∈E its actual Riesz gradient satisfies ∇W(x)=∇f(x)+δ(x−u). Furthermore this gradient is (β+δ)-Lipschitz on E: ‖∇W(x)−∇W(y)‖≤(β+δ)‖x−y‖ for every x,y∈E. Both β and δ may equal zero; the theorem assumes neither finite dimensionality nor existence of any minimizer.",
