      "original_theorem": "Assume a first-order method optimizes the alpha-strongly convex beta-smooth class in phi(kappa) log(alpha R²/epsilon) queries, where kappa=beta/alpha. Lemma4.2 reduces the convex beta-smooth class to it with stated order O(phi(2 beta R²/epsilon)). The proof forms W=f+delta||x-x0||²/2 with delta=epsilon/R², solves W to tolerance epsilon/2, and compares its minimizer w with an original minimizer z. W(w)<=W(z) and f(z)<=f(w) show ||w-x0||<=||z-x0||<=R; the two half-error terms then give f(x)-f(z)<=epsilon. In the regime epsilon<=beta R², the regularized smoothness is beta+delta<=2beta and its condition number is at most2 beta R²/epsilon. The existence/comparison, curvature/smoothness and oracle-complexity obligations are separate.",
      "reconstructed_theorem": "Let E be any complete real inner-product space, S any state type, and X any output type. Let next:S→X⊕E and update:S→E→(ℝ×E)→S be arbitrary pure functions. Suppose f:E→ℝ is everywhere Frechet differentiable and convex on all of E, and its genuine Riesz gradient is β-Lipschitz, where β≥0. For every δ≥0, center u∈E, natural fuel N, and initial state s, define W(x)=f(x)+(δ/2)‖x−u‖². Modify the update to transform an answer (v,g) at query x into (v+(δ/2)‖x−u‖², g+δ(x−u)) before passing it to update. Executing next with this modified update and the oracle x↦(f(x),∇f(x)) yields exactly the same nested result ((final state, optional output), explicit oracle-query count) as executing next with the original update and the oracle x↦(W(x),∇W(x)), with identical fuel and initial state. The count in the first result is at most N. Consequently equality also gives that bound for the second result. The count charges only the explicit oracle branches described in the execution definition.",
