      "search": "semantic-audit:astis-rt-andi-opt-regularizationoracle semantic-audit astis-rt-andi-opt-regularizationoracle chewi-opt-v1-lemma-4-2 · lean-weakened-conclusion blind reconstruction and seven-slot semantic comparison for autosamplingtheory.technicallemmas.analysis.quadraticregularizationoracle.simulate_regularized. lean-weakened-conclusion source hidden from decoder assume a first-order method optimizes the alpha-strongly convex beta-smooth class in phi(kappa) log(alpha r²/epsilon) queries, where kappa=beta/alpha. lemma4.2 reduces the convex beta-smooth class to it with stated order o(phi(2 beta r²/epsilon)). the proof forms w=f+delta||x-x0||²/2 with delta=epsilon/r², solves w to tolerance epsilon/2, and compares its minimizer w with an original minimizer z. w(w)<=w(z) and f(z)<=f(w) show ||w-x0||<=||z-x0||<=r; the two half-error terms then give f(x)-f(z)<=epsilon. in the regime epsilon<=beta r², the regularized smoothness is beta+delta<=2beta and its condition number is at most2 beta r²/epsilon. the existence/comparison, curvature/smoothness and oracle-complexity obligations are separate. let e be any complete real inner-product space, s any state type, and x any output type. let next:s→x⊕e and update:s→e→(ℝ×e)→s be arbitrary pure functions. suppose f:e→ℝ is everywhere frechet differentiable and convex on all of e, and its genuine riesz gradient is β-lipschitz, where β≥0. for every δ≥0, center u∈e, natural fuel n, and initial state s, define w(x)=f(x)+(δ/2)‖x−u‖². modify the update to transform an answer (v,g) at query x into (v+(δ/2)‖x−u‖², g+δ(x−u)) before passing it to update. executing next with this modified update and the oracle x↦(f(x),∇f(x)) yields exactly the same nested result ((final state, optional output), explicit oracle-query count) as executing next with the original update and the oracle x↦(w(x),∇w(x)), with identical fuel and initial state. the count in the first result is at most n. consequently equality also gives that bound for the second result. the count charges only the explicit oracle branches described in the execution definition. accepted https://arxiv.org/html/2605.07006v1#s4.ss1; section4.1 lemma4.2 statement and proof; regularized minimum and radius comparisons autosamplingtheory.technicallemmas.analysis.quadraticregularizationoracle.simulate_regularized autosamplingtheory/technicallemmas/analysis/quadraticregularizationoracle.lean true andi-reg-oracle-worker reg_oracle_blind 6eafa2d2bc9220681973e3e5777efdcb3aae6b42a66586947d1a364390127c15 accepted uniform_source objects regularized first-order oracle implemented from the original objective. original and actual w oracles; answer correction; equal executions including state, optional output and count. explicit-elaboration the original value and actual gradient are corrected by the explicit quadratic. the module proves the actual w gradient through the reviewed regularity parent. the execute spelling in the retained statement denotes this same run recurrence. domains finite-dimensional euclidean first-order setting. complete real inner-product space with arbitrary deterministic state and output types. explicit-elaboration the enlarged hilbert setting is valid for this finite equality; no minimizer existence is asserted. the current lesson states the domain accurately. quantifiers an oracle simulation usable by the reduction algorithm. for every fixed next,update,f,beta,delta,u,fuel and state satisfying analytic premises, both executions agree. explicit-elaboration induction generalizes over state, permitting adaptive future queries. fixed-program equality alone does not assert one successful algorithm for every objective. assumptions convex beta-smooth objective and known quadratic regularization. differentiable convex objective with beta-lipschitz genuine gradient, beta,delta>=0, arbitrary center. explicit-elaboration convexity and lipschitzness are inherited from the reused parent; they do not require c2. candidate differences record hilbert domain, nonnegative delta and finite deterministic scope. conclusion the full source theorem provides a uniform accuracy and complexity reduction. exact full execution equality and explicit query count at most fuel. weaker-in-lean neither success nor output quality is inferred from none/exhaustion. binding supports only finite-oracle-simulation, leaving finite-uniform-reduction and oracle-cost-class separate. scopes first-order implementation step of lemma 4.2 under the section 1.1 query model. finite deterministic execution and its explicit counter. explicit-elaboration the statement, lesson formulas and induction agree. corrected original execution and true w execution share next and the same initial state; local corrections use x,u,delta. constant dependencies a regularized reply requires one original first-order reply. exact count preservation and count<=fuel, with no extra interactions for corrections. same the positive query branch invokes one oracle and adds one to equal recursive counters. this does not yet certify the source phi/log query budget or generic oracle-isolation discipline. conclusion informational this declaration does not constitute the complete source lemma 4.2 reduction. neither success nor output quality is inferred from none/exhaustion. binding supports only finite-oracle-simulation, leaving finite-uniform-reduction and oracle-cost-class separate. scopes informational the current scoped publication binding is consistent with the declaration, lesson and explicit remaining obligations. the statement, lesson formulas and induction agree. corrected original execution and true w execution share next and the same initial state; local corrections use x,u,delta. assumptions informational the lean gradient-lipschitz interface is explicit; a compiled bridge from the source smoothness convention remains outside this binding. convexity and lipschitzness are inherited from the reused parent; they do not require c2. candidate differences record hilbert domain, nonnegative delta and finite deterministic scope.",
