    {
      "id": "semantic-audit:ASTIS-RT-ANDI-OPT-UniformRegularization",
      "kind": "semantic-audit",
      "label": "ASTIS-RT-ANDI-OPT-UniformRegularization",
      "status": "fidelity-mismatch",
      "subtitle": "chewi-opt-v1-lemma-4-2 · lean-weakened-conclusion",
      "summary": "Blind reconstruction and seven-slot semantic comparison for AutoSamplingTheory.TechnicalLemmas.Analysis.UniformRegularization.uniform_accuracy_and_query_bound.",
      "column": 7,
      "url": "lean-foundations.html?view=semantic",
      "source_url": "",
      "original_theorem": "Assume a first-order method optimizes the alpha-strongly convex beta-smooth class in phi(kappa) log(alpha R²/epsilon) queries, where kappa=beta/alpha. Lemma4.2 reduces the convex beta-smooth class to it with stated order O(phi(2 beta R²/epsilon)). The proof forms W=f+delta||x-x0||²/2 with delta=epsilon/R², solves W to tolerance epsilon/2, and compares its minimizer w with an original minimizer z. W(w)<=W(z) and f(z)<=f(w) show ||w-x0||<=||z-x0||<=R; the two half-error terms then give f(x)-f(z)<=epsilon. In the regime epsilon<=beta R², the regularized smoothness is beta+delta<=2beta and its condition number is at most2 beta R²/epsilon. The existence/comparison, curvature/smoothness and oracle-complexity obligations are separate.",
      "reconstructed_theorem": "Let E be a complete real inner-product space in which closed bounded balls are compact, and let S be any state type. For every nonnegative real β, center u∈E, positive reals R and ε with ε≤βR², every function φ:ℝ→ℕ, every fixed deterministic pair next:S→E⊕E and update:S→E→(ℝ×E)→S, and every initial state s₀∈S, set δ=ε/R² and N=⌈φ(2β/δ) log(δR²/(ε/2))⌉₊. Here the ceiling is the natural-number ceiling, and log is the real natural logarithm.\nAssume the following uniform success guarantee for this same next, update, s₀, φ and N: for every everywhere Fréchet-differentiable g:E→ℝ that is globally δ-strongly convex and whose actual gradient is globally Lipschitz with constant 2β, and for every global minimizer w of g satisfying ‖w−u‖≤R, there exist a terminal state t∈S, an output x∈E and a query count c∈ℕ such that execute next update (y↦(g(y),∇g(y))) N s₀=((t,some x),c) and g(x)−g(w)≤ε/2. The strong convexity convention subtracts (δ/2)θ(1−θ)‖x−y‖² from the convex chord bound.\nDefine corrected(s,y,a)=update(s,y,(a₁+(δ/2)‖y−u‖²,a₂+δ(y−u))). Then, for every everywhere Fréchet-differentiable globally convex f:E→ℝ with globally β-Lipschitz actual gradient, and every global minimizer z of f with ‖z−u‖≤R, there exist t∈S, x∈E and c∈ℕ such that execute next corrected (y↦(f(y),∇f(y))) N s₀=((t,some x),c), f(x)−f(z)≤ε, c≤⌈φ(2βR²/ε) log 2⌉₊, and c≤φ(2βR²/ε).\nThe execution inspects next at each state, returning a successful some x immediately on its left branch even when remaining query fuel is zero. Its right branch requests an oracle answer and increments c by one only when fuel is positive; a right branch at zero fuel instead returns none. Thus the conclusion asserts actual successful termination within the stated query budget, not merely exhaustion of that budget. The terminal state t, returned point x and count c are the components of that successful run; the conclusion places no further condition on t. The same fixed next, update and s₀ must meet the premise uniformly over g and its qualifying minimizers before the conclusion applies uniformly over f and its qualifying minimizers. The theorem is conditional on that solver guarantee and does not assert that such a solver exists for an arbitrary φ. Only explicit calls through the execution oracle are counted; arbitrary computation inside the supplied functions is uncharged.",
      "fidelity_verdict": "lean-weakened-conclusion",
      "blindness": "source hidden from decoder",
      "semantic_slots": [
        {
