ticoptimalcoupling; and hs0, hst, ht1 encode 0 <= s <= t <= 1. explicit-elaboration the source p2 assumption already combines probability with finite second moment. lean separates these into isprobabilitymeasure instances and integrable squared-norm hypotheses. this does not assume absolute continuity. the source optimal-plan condition is likewise bundled by isquadraticoptimalcoupling. the current proof uses finite moments only to establish finite ennreal endpoint distance for valid cancellation. conclusion for 0 <= s <= t <= 1, w2(mu_s,mu_t)=(t-s)w2(mu0,mu1), equivalently the theorem's displayed absolute-value formula because t-s is nonnegative. wassersteindistance (displacementinterpolation gamma s) (displacementinterpolation gamma t) = ennreal.ofreal (t-s) * wassersteindistance mu0 mu1. equivalent under s <= t, ennreal.ofreal(t-s) represents the nonnegative scalar t-s and agrees with |t-s|. source p2 finiteness ensures that the ennreal-valued lean equality represents the same finite w2 equality. the proof follows the source structure: sharp interpolation upper bounds, two triangle inequalities, coefficient partition, and cancellation justified explicitly by endpoint finiteness. scopes the full theorem 7.6 says w2 is a geodesic space and constructs, for every endpoint pair, a constant-speed path from an optimal plan. the reviewed component is only its ordered metric identity conditional on a supplied optimal plan. only the ordered metric identity for a supplied optimal coupling is formalized; optimal-plan existence, path continuity packaging, and the global geodesic-space conclusion are excluded. same the packet explicitly fixes this component boundary. within it, the lean declaration proves the whole ordered identity. it must not be presented as a formalization of optimal-plan existence or of the complete geodesic-space theorem, and the candidate exposition consistently states those exclusions. constant dependencies the speed is exactly w2(mu0,mu1), with time fac