      "url": "lean-foundations.html?view=semantic",
      "source_url": "https://arxiv.org/html/2609.06905v1#A3.SS1",
      "original_theorem": "Samplinglib-authored elementary Euclidean analytic prerequisite, not a quoted paper theorem. PBPS arXiv2609.06905v1 Appendix C.1 uses gradient closedness and conditional Poincare; its weighted-Sobolev route needs actual local square integrability before L2 weak-PDE regularity. Let E be a finite-dimensional real inner-product space with Borel structure and volume dx, W:E→R continuous with integrable exp(-W), and mu its normalized exponential tilt. For every normed additive commutative group F and every a in L2(mu;F), the selected representative has squared norm locally integrable for volume and belongs to L2(volume restricted to K;F) for every compact K, including the required almost-everywhere strong measurability. No completeness or real scalar structure is required on F. The paper's C2 scalar/vector setting is a specialization. Background1310.2526v7 section2.5 provides weighted Sobolev context only; no compact-manifold regularity theorem is transferred. No global unweighted L2 estimate, quantitative constant, H2, Poincare, domain characterization or paper completion is asserted.",
      "reconstructed_theorem": "Let E be any finite-dimensional real inner-product space, equipped with its Borel measurable structure, and let F be any normed additive commutative group. Let W : E → ℝ be continuous, and suppose that x ↦ exp(-W(x)) is integrable with respect to volume on E. Write μ for the tilted volume measure with potential -W, that is, volume weighted by exp(-W) and normalized. For every equivalence class a in L²(μ; F), let f : E → F be the representative selected by the displayed coercions from a. Then x ↦ ‖f(x)‖² is locally integrable with respect to volume on E: at every point it is integrable on some neighborhood. Moreover, for every compact set K ⊆ E, f belongs to MemLp with exponent 2 for volume restricted to K; in particular, f is almost-everywhere strongly measurable for that restricted measure and has finite squared-norm integral there. No global unweighted L² integrability of f is asserted.",
