    {
      "id": "semantic-audit:ASTIS-RT-20260918-ProximalExecution",
      "kind": "semantic-audit",
      "label": "ASTIS-RT-20260918-ProximalExecution",
      "status": "fidelity-exact",
      "subtitle": "companion:sphmc-algorithm-d2-lemma-d3 · equivalent-after-elaboration",
      "summary": "Blind reconstruction and seven-slot semantic comparison for AutoSamplingTheory.ExampleCases.SmoothedPicardHMC.ApproximateProximalExecution.approximate_proximal_execution.",
      "column": 7,
      "url": "lean-foundations.html?view=semantic",
      "source_url": "https://arxiv.org/html/2609.06906v1#A4.SS2",
      "original_theorem": "For a C² potential with kappa-inverse and unit Hessian bounds, the iteration x₀=y, xₙ₊₁=y−eta∇V(xₙ) stops at its first residual at most (1−eta)eps. It returns the current iterate, within eps of the unique proximal minimum. Its gradient-query count, including the successful test, is bounded by C_c[1+log(1+‖∇V(y)‖/eps)], uniformly in y, for fixed 0<eta≤c<1. Samplinglib additionally establishes measurable first-hit/output, quantitative unique minimum and the exact finite-fuel execution equation. Assumptions: The source uses Euclidean space; the formal statement uses a finite-dimensional complete real inner-product space with its Borel measurable structure. V is C², kappa≥1 and kappa⁻¹‖v‖²≤D²V(x)[v,v]≤‖v‖² for every x,v. 0<eta≤c<1 and eps>0. Exact arithmetic and exact gradient queries are the cost model, not floating-point arithmetic.",
      "reconstructed_theorem": "Let E be a finite-dimensional complete real inner-product space equipped with its Borel measurable structure. Let V:E→ℝ be C², and let κ∈ℝ≥0 satisfy κ≥1 and κ⁻¹‖v‖²≤D²V(x)[v,v]≤‖v‖² for every x,v∈E. For every η,c,ε∈ℝ with 0<η≤c<1 and ε>0, define T_y(x)=y−η∇V(x), F_y(x)=V(x)+(η⁻¹/2)‖x−y‖², and x_j(y)=T_y^[j](y). There exist measurable p:E→E and N:E→ℕ such that y↦x_{N(y)}(y) is measurable and, for every y∈E: (1) p(y)+η∇V(p(y))=y; (2) for every z∈E, F_y(p(y))+((κ⁻¹+η⁻¹)/2)‖z−p(y)‖²≤F_y(z), and F_y(z)≤F_y(p(y)) iff z=p(y); (3) ‖x_{N(y)+1}(y)−x_{N(y)}(y)‖≤(1−η)ε, whereas this inequality fails for every natural j<N(y); (4) ‖x_{N(y)}(y)−p(y)‖≤ε; (5) N(y)+1≤[2+(1+log((1−c)⁻¹))/(−log c)] [1+log(1+‖∇V(y)‖/ε)]; and (6) Q(∇V,η,ε,y,N(y)+1,y)=some(x_{N(y)}(y),N(y)+1).",
      "fidelity_verdict": "equivalent-after-elaboration",
      "blindness": "source hidden from decoder",
      "semantic_slots": [
