      "url": "lean-foundations.html?view=semantic",
      "source_url": "https://arxiv.org/html/2609.06906v1#A4.SS2",
      "original_theorem": "Represent one gradient evaluation and residual test per positive-fuel step. With no fuel return none. Otherwise set z=y−eta g(x); on a successful test return the current x with count one, and on failure recurse from z with one less fuel, adding one to a successful count. This is a definition, not a correctness or termination theorem. Assumptions: E is a real normed vector space, g:E→E is an arbitrary oracle, eta and eps are real, y and x are points, and fuel is natural. Exact-real comparison is noncomputable. Source correctness assumptions apply in the separate execution theorem, not in this definition.",
      "reconstructed_theorem": "This is a definition, not a correctness proposition. For every real normed vector space E, define the noncomputable exact-real recursive interpreter Q:(E→E)→ℝ→ℝ→E→ℕ→E→Option(E×ℕ). Its arguments, in order, are g, η, ε, y, fuel, and current state x. Set Q(g,η,ε,y,0,x)=none. At fuel n+1, let z=y−η•g(x). If ‖z−x‖≤(1−η)ε, return some(x,1). Otherwise evaluate Q(g,η,ε,y,n,z) and map each successful result (u,k) to (u,k+1), preserving none.",
      "fidelity_verdict": "exact",
      "blindness": "source hidden from decoder",
      "semantic_slots": [
