          "evidence": "The definition uses classical comparison but has no derivative, Lipschitz, curvature or positivity hypotheses. This is appropriate for a construction, not an unconditional proximal-accuracy claim."
        },
        {
          "slot": "conclusion",
          "original": "Zero fuel returns none; positive fuel forms z, returns (x,1) upon residual success, or recursively advances to z and increments a successful count.",
          "reconstructed": "Exactly these recursive equations, with Option.map preserving none.",
          "relation": "same",
          "evidence": "The successful branch returns x, not z. The failed branch keeps y fixed, reduces fuel by one, and maps (u,k) to (u,k+1). Exhaustion carries no partial count."
        },
        {
          "slot": "scopes",
          "original": "Attributed finite-fuel representation of Algorithm D.2, not a correctness or eventual-success theorem.",
          "reconstructed": "Noncomputable recursive definition with finite recursion depth.",
          "relation": "same",
          "evidence": "Finite fuel guarantees a defined Option result, not some result. The source algorithm is recovered operationally by using g=gradient V and initial x=y with sufficient fuel; those conditions are not silently imposed on every interpreter call."
        },
        {
          "slot": "constant dependencies",
          "original": "One modeled oracle query per positive-fuel step and threshold (1-eta)eps; no asymptotic coefficient is asserted.",
          "reconstructed": "Success count 1, recursive count increment 1, identical threshold.",
          "relation": "same",
          "evidence": "These constants match the body. The count is an explicit exact-oracle cost model, not a measurement of Lean runtime, physical calls or floating-point cost."
