      "subtitle": "companion:sphmc-d4-jensen-expected-proximal-work · equivalent-after-elaboration",
      "summary": "Blind reconstruction and seven-slot semantic comparison for AutoSamplingTheory.ExampleCases.SmoothedPicardHMC.ProximalExpectedWork.approximate_proximal_expected_work.",
      "column": 7,
      "url": "lean-foundations.html?view=semantic",
      "source_url": "https://arxiv.org/html/2609.06906v1#A4.SS2",
      "original_theorem": "Attributed expanded proof component, not the full Lemma D.4: for an actual Algorithm D.2 proximal query at random center Y, Lemma D.3 bounds the number N_prox(Y) of gradient evaluations by C_c[1+log(1+norm(gradient V(Y))/eps)]. If Y has a probability law and E norm(gradient V(Y))^2 is finite, then the actual count is integrable and Jensen together with the probability-space L2-to-L1 inequality gives E N_prox(Y) <= C_c[1+log(1+sqrt(E norm(gradient V(Y))^2)/eps)]. Samplinglib constructs measurable p and first-stop N from the actual exact-real finite-fuel interpreter, retains its proximal equation, eps-accuracy and successful return with count N+1, and uses the explicit admissible C_c=2+(1+log((1-c)^(-1)))/(-log c) already derived for D.3. Assumptions: E is a finite-dimensional complete real inner-product Borel space, V is C2, kappa>=1, kappa^(-1) norm(v)^2 <= D2 V(x)[v,v] <= norm(v)^2, 0<eta<=c<1, eps>0, nu is a probability measure, and y maps to norm(gradient V(y))^2 integrably. The actual Picard center law and numerical D.7 bound, phase counting, D.8, approximate-kernel stability, sampling accuracy and full-paper theorem remain separate. Total variation is not used to transfer unbounded cost.",
      "reconstructed_theorem": "Let E be a finite-dimensional complete real inner-product space, equipped with a measurable structure whose measurable sets are the Borel sets. Let V:E->R and let kappa be a nonnegative real number. Assume kappa>=1, V is twice continuously Frechet differentiable, and, for every x,v in E, (1/kappa)||v||^2 <= D^2 V(x)[v,v] <= ||v||^2. Let eta,c,eps be real numbers satisfying 0<eta<=c<1 and eps>0. Let nu be a probability measure on E such that y |-> ||grad V(y)||^2 is integrable. For y in E define T_y:E->E by T_y(x)=y-eta grad V(x), and define C=2+(1+log((1-c)^(-1)))/(-log c). Then there exist measurable functions p:E->E and N:E->N such that y |-> T_y^[N(y)](y) is measurable and, for every y in E, p(y)+eta grad V(p(y))=y, ||T_y^[N(y)](y)-p(y)||<=eps, and Q(grad V,eta,eps,y,N(y)+1,y)=some((T_y^[N(y)](y),N(y)+1)). Moreover, y |-> N(y)+1 is integrable with respect to nu and integral (N(y)+1) dnu(y) <= C(1+log(1+sqrt(integral ||grad V(y)||^2 dnu(y))/eps)). Here T_y^[n] denotes n-fold iteration (with the zeroth iterate equal to the identity), and Q is the stated exact-real recursive interpreter, so N(y)+1 is the number of gradient-oracle evaluations, including the successful test.",
      "fidelity_verdict": "equivalent-after-elaboration",
      "blindness": "source hidden from decoder",
      "semantic_slots": [
        {
          "slot": "objects",
          "original": "A proximal-query center Y, Algorithm D.2 iterates x^(k), its returned approximate proximal point, the gradient-evaluation count N_prox(Y), and the gradient norm entering the Jensen bound.",
          "reconstructed": "A probability measure nu on E; measurable functions p:E->E and N:E->Nat; the iterates of T_y(x)=y-eta grad V(x); the exact finite-fuel proximalQuery interpreter; and the real-valued count N(y)+1.",
          "relation": "explicit-elaboration",
          "evidence": "Source D.2 defines x^(0)=y, x^(k+1)=y-eta grad V(x^(k)), returns x^(k), and D.3 names N_prox(y). The Lean conclusion retains the proximal equation, accurate returned iterate, and exact interpreter return rather than postulating an abstract cost variable."
        },
        {
          "slot": "domains",
          "original": "The paper works on R^d with the normalized Hessian bounds and a random center arising in the Picard HMC run.",
          "reconstructed": "E is any finite-dimensional complete real inner-product space with a Borel measurable structure; nu is any probability measure satisfying the stated square-gradient integrability premise.",
          "relation": "explicit-elaboration",
          "evidence": "Finite-dimensional real inner-product spaces are the coordinate-free form of the Euclidean source domain, and all operations in this isolated bound are invariant under orthogonal coordinates. Replacing the realized center law by an arbitrary probability law is a valid conditional abstraction of the Jensen step, not an identification with the Picard law."
        },
        {
          "slot": "quantifiers",
          "original": "For every queried center Y along the realized run, D.7 supplies a second-moment bound; Lemma D.3 and Jensen then bound its expected proximal-query work.",
          "reconstructed": "Universally over E,V,kappa,eta,c,eps and a probability measure nu under the displayed hypotheses, there exist measurable p and N, and the pointwise execution facts hold for every y while the cost conclusions hold under nu.",
          "relation": "explicit-elaboration",
          "evidence": "The theorem deliberately factors the source proof: it conditions on an arbitrary center law with a finite square-gradient moment. Instantiating nu with any actual Picard-center law after proving D.7 recovers the source use."
        },
        {
          "slot": "assumptions",
          "original": "V is C2 on R^d, kappa^(-1)I <= Hess V <= I, kappa>=1, 0<eta<=c0<1, eps_prox>0, and for the Jensen step the center has finite E||grad V(Y)||^2 (numerically furnished by D.7 in the paper).",
          "reconstructed": "V is ContDiff R 2, the same quadratic-form Hessian bounds hold for every x,v, kappa is nonnegative with kappa>=1, 0<eta<=c<1, eps>0, nu is a probability measure, and y |-> ||gradient V(y)||^2 is integrable.",
          "relation": "equivalent",
          "evidence": "The full parameter range 0<eta<=c<1 is present; no eta<=1/2 assumption is introduced. ContDiff plus the explicit second-fderivative inequality makes the Hessian assumptions genuine. Integrability of the nonnegative square is the measure-theoretic version of finite second moment and supplies the measurability needed by the expectation."
        },
        {
          "slot": "conclusion",
          "original": "The first displayed inequality after D.7 is E N_prox(Y) <= C[1+log(1+sqrt(E||grad V(Y)||^2)/eps_prox)], obtained from the D.3 pointwise bound and Jensen.",
          "reconstructed": "The actual count N+1 is integrable and its integral is at most C_c[1+log(1+sqrt(integral ||grad V||^2)/eps)]; the same witnesses also satisfy the proximal equation, eps-accuracy, measurability, and exact interpreter return with fuel and reported count N+1.",
          "relation": "explicit-elaboration",
          "evidence": "N+1 matches the actual number of gradient evaluations: testing the stopping condition after x^(N+1) is formed uses gradients at x^(0),...,x^(N), including the successful test. The extra execution and integrability conjuncts make the source's random cost and expectation rigorous and do not weaken the inequality."
        },
        {
          "slot": "scopes",
          "original": "Only the conditional per-proximal-query Jensen estimate in the proof of D.4 after D.7; the source next uses a numerical D.7 estimate and then counts calls and phases to obtain D.8.",
          "reconstructed": "Only construction of one measurable stopped proximal execution and its conditional expected count bound under nu; the theorem does not assert the Picard-center moment estimate, parameter substitution, phase aggregation, approximate-kernel stability, sampling error, or a total-work theorem.",
          "relation": "same",
          "evidence": "The Lean statement contains no h,J,q,L_q,d, phase count, D.7 numerical right-hand side, D.8 total N_grad V, Wasserstein/TV claim, or kernel-transfer premise. In particular it performs no transfer of the unbounded work variable through total variation."
        },
        {
          "slot": "constant dependencies",
          "original": "Lemma D.3 uses a constant C_c0 depending on the fixed contraction ceiling c0; the first Jensen inequality after D.7 writes a generic C before later parameter substitutions.",
          "reconstructed": "C_c is fixed explicitly as 2+(1+log((1-c)^(-1)))/(-log c), depending only on c, while the bound also displays eps and the square-gradient moment; it has no dependence on the realized Picard parameters beyond those explicit inputs.",
          "relation": "explicit-elaboration",
          "evidence": "The explicit formula is an admissible constant obtained from the geometric contraction estimate in the D.3 proof using eta<=c<1. It is provenance-preserving specialization of C_c, not a claim that the paper printed this exact formula."
        }
      ],
