      "url": "lean-foundations.html?view=semantic",
      "source_url": "https://arxiv.org/html/2609.06906v1#S3.SS2",
      "original_theorem": "ASTIS faithful paraphrase (not a quotation): Algorithm 3.1 samples a fresh standard Gaussian for the first partial momentum refresh and a finite independent family of standard Gaussian innovations for the first Picard update. For incoming energy (x,p) maps to norm(x-xstar)^2+norm(p)^2 with expectation at most M, the scoped product-law construction proves refreshed energy at most M+(1-exp(-h))*d and each first-layer Gaussian coordinate has squared-norm expectation d. It does not include the repeated phase history, second innovation family, second refresh, quadrature construction, D.7, D.8, or a main theorem.",
      "reconstructed_theorem": "Let E be any complete finite-dimensional real inner-product space with its Borel measurable structure, let iota be any finite type, let nu be any probability measure on E × E, let xstar belong to E, and let h and M be nonnegative real numbers. Assume that (x,p) ↦ ‖x-xstar‖²+‖p‖² is nu-integrable with integral at most M. Set a = exp(-h/2), sigma = sqrt(1-exp(-h)), and mu = (nu.prod(stdGaussian E)).prod(Measure.pi(fun _ : iota => stdGaussian E)). For w = (((x,p),z),g), define X(w)=x, P0(w)=a p+sigma z, and G_j(w)=g(j), where multiplication of a vector by a real number means scalar multiplication. Set B=M+(1-exp(-h)) dim_R(E). Then B is nonnegative, X and P0 are measurable, and w ↦ ‖X(w)-xstar‖²+‖P0(w)‖² is mu-integrable with integral at most B. Moreover, for every j in iota, G_j is measurable and w ↦ ‖G_j(w)‖² is mu-integrable with integral equal to dim_R(E).",
      "fidelity_verdict": "equivalent-after-elaboration",
      "blindness": "source hidden from decoder",
