      "column": 7,
      "url": "lean-foundations.html?view=semantic",
      "source_url": "",
      "original_theorem": "Faithful scoped paraphrase: in proving Lemma 4.16, the paper couples the initial phase law delta_xref tensor standard Gaussian to the smoothed Gibbs phase reference Pi_eta by coupling the momenta identically. For q=2, the M_kappa quadratic transportation cost of this coupling is at most the position second moment about xref, because the momentum displacement vanishes and the remaining coefficient 1/(2*kappa)+1/2 is at most one for kappa at least one. The full lemma also uses the Gibbs concentration and small-gradient reference-point estimates to give a numeric all-q bound; this ASTIS declaration proves only the conditional coupling-cost edge for a generic position law with finite second moment.",
      "reconstructed_theorem": "For any probability measure pi on a finite-dimensional complete real inner-product Borel space E, any reference point xref in E, and any real kappa at least one, assume the squared distance from xref is pi-integrable. Then the phase quadratic Kantorovich value between the product laws delta_xref tensor standard Gaussian and pi tensor standard Gaussian is at most the ordinary pi-integral of that squared distance, embedded in the extended nonnegative reals. The phase cost uses position coefficient 1/(2*kappa)+1/2, unit position-momentum cross term and unit momentum coefficient. The theorem claims neither an optimal coupling nor equality, and does not assert a numerical Gibbs or all-q bound.",
      "fidelity_verdict": "equivalent-after-elaboration",
      "blindness": "source hidden from decoder",
