      "id": "semantic-audit:ASTIS-RT-20261003-GCCommonKernelSuffix",
      "kind": "semantic-audit",
      "label": "ASTIS-RT-20261003-GCCommonKernelSuffix",
      "status": "fidelity-exact",
      "subtitle": "gaussian-cloud-v1-lemma-6-3-common-suffix · equivalent-after-elaboration",
      "summary": "Blind reconstruction and seven-slot semantic comparison for AutoSamplingTheory.TechnicalLemmas.Probability.KernelHybridTelescope.abs_real_comp_commonSuffix_sub_le_integral_eventBound.",
      "column": 7,
      "url": "lean-foundations.html?view=semantic",
      "source_url": "https://arxiv.org/html/2609.38710v1#S6.SS1",
      "original_theorem": "Faithful ASTIS paraphrase of one component of Chen--Chewi--Lu--Zhang arXiv:2609.38710v1 Lemma 6.3, not quoted prose: two adjacent capped hybrids agree before one call, use ideal versus implemented conditional kernels at that call, and then use the same later adaptive continuation. Data processing through the common continuation cannot increase the discrepancy created at the replaced call, so the final output event discrepancy is at most the ideal-history expectation of the local conditional error. A separate construction must show that the retained history makes the later continuation a common measurable Markov kernel.",
      "reconstructed_theorem": "For every three measurable spaces A, B, and C, every probability measure mu on A, every pair of Markov probability kernels K and L from A to B, every Markov probability kernel S from B to C, and every function epsilon : A -> R integrable with respect to mu, the following implication holds. Suppose that for mu-almost every x in A, simultaneously for every measurable set s in B, |K(x,s) - L(x,s)| <= epsilon(x). Then for every measurable set t in C, |integral_A integral_B S(y,t) K(x,dy) mu(dx) - integral_A integral_B S(y,t) L(x,dy) mu(dx)| <= integral_A epsilon(x) mu(dx). The two integrals inside the absolute value are the real event masses obtained by first applying K or L to mu and then applying the same kernel S. All kernels are probability kernels, so these event masses are finite. The exceptional mu-null set is common to all measurable s. No separate pointwise nonnegativity assumption on epsilon is imposed.",
      "fidelity_verdict": "equivalent-after-elaboration",
      "blindness": "source hidden from decoder",
      "semantic_slots": [
        {
          "slot": "objects",
          "original": "Adjacent hybrids sharing their pre-call history law and later adaptive continuation, but differing at one conditional call.",
          "reconstructed": "A common input probability measure mu, replacement kernels K and L, and a single common downstream Markov kernel S.",
          "relation": "explicit-elaboration",
          "evidence": "The public theorem uses exactly one S on both sides, composing S after K and after L. The conditional discrepancy is measured under the same mu in both branches."
        },
        {
          "slot": "domains",
          "original": "Measurable retained histories and final outputs sufficient to represent the shared adaptive continuation.",
          "reconstructed": "Arbitrary measurable spaces A, B and C with K,L:A to B and S:B to C.",
          "relation": "explicit-elaboration",
          "evidence": "The abstract state B may retain all information required by later adaptation. The theorem does not assert that a current response alone is sufficient; the lesson explicitly requires enough retained history to obtain a common S."
        },
        {
          "slot": "quantifiers",
          "original": "Every admissible common continuation preserves the local mean discrepancy bound for every final event.",
          "reconstructed": "For every supplied Markov S and every measurable final event t, the same bound holds, assuming the conditional premise almost everywhere uniformly over intermediate events.",
          "relation": "equivalent",
          "evidence": "S is an arbitrary parameter, not chosen after t. The proof first obtains hKL for all measurable intermediate events and then applies the same data-processing result to all final events."
        },
        {
          "slot": "assumptions",
          "original": "The one-call conditional bound is integrable under the shared ideal-history law, and later computation is represented by one common measurable probability kernel.",
          "reconstructed": "The mean theorem's probability, Markov, integrability and almost-everywhere assumptions, plus IsMarkovKernel S.",
          "relation": "explicit-elaboration",
          "evidence": "The theorem calls the mean result with exactly mu,K,L,epsilon and hbound, then uses S without altering it between branches. Constructing this common continuation from an adaptive algorithm is not derived from these assumptions."
        },
        {
