t step autosamplingtheory.technicallemmas.probability.kernelhybridtelescope.abs_real_comp_sub_le_integral_eventbound autosamplingtheory/technicallemmas/probability/kernelhybridtelescope.lean true root independent codex source-blind decoder analytic_blind_decoder 08b15663641a613caf6aa388e1dc425195ff92f15e1386e612e12350ae1d03ee accepted analytic_source_review objects an ideal pre-call history law, two conditional probability kernels at one call, and a history-dependent conditional discrepancy budget. a probability measure mu, markov kernels k and l, and an integrable real function epsilon bounding every measurable-event discrepancy almost everywhere. explicit-elaboration the declaration gives these objects as explicit parameters. k and l act on the same input law; the proof compares their output event probabilities. identifying mu with a concrete ideal-history law is not asserted by this generic component. domains measurable histories and one-call outputs of the capped adaptive construction. arbitrary measurable spaces a and b with explicitly supplied probability measure and markov kernels. explicit-elaboration no countability, euclidean, gaussian or regular-conditional-distribution construction is assumed. the general measurable-space statement directly accommodates a supplied history/output kernel representation without claiming to construct it. quantifiers at an accepted history, the conditional total-variation budget controls all output events; average under the ideal-history law and obtain a bound for every output event. for mu-almost every x, simultaneously for all measurable s, the conditional event bound holds; the conclusion quantifies over every measurable t. explicit-elaboration hbound is ordered as almost every x followed by every measurable s, so one common exceptional null set suffices. the proof fixes t and specializes that hypothesis. a total-variation bound at each history implies precisely this uniform eventwise property. assumptions a common ideal-history probability law, genuine conditional probability kernels, and a measurable integrable local error budget. isprobabilitymeasure mu, ismarkovkernel k and l, integrable epsilon mu, and the stated almost-everywhere event bound. explicit-elaboration the embedded proof bounds each conditional event probability by one and proves its integrability. choosing the empty event in hbound forces epsilon to be nonnegative on the accepted histories; a separate nonnegativity premise is therefore unnecessary. no measurability of a conditional-tv supremum is silently inferred. conclusion immediately after the replaced call, the discrepancy is bounded by the ideal-history mean of the local error. for each measurable t, abs((k composed with mu).real t - (l composed with mu).real t) is at most integral epsilon dmu. equivalent happly expresses both composed probabilities as real integrals. integral_sub, abs_integral_le_integral_abs and integral_mono_ae give the exact mean bound. for probability measures, a bound on every event is the usual supremum-over-events total-variation bound; no alternative normalization is introduced. scopes only the mean one-call replacement component of lemma 6.3, excluding history construction, later continuation, the telescope and cap comparison. a generic one-call eventwise averaging theorem, bound only to the mean-one-call-error obligation. same the binding supports only mean-one-call-error. the module expressly leaves dummy-filled histories and the paper's delta_t identification to separate adapters. the broader publication formula is a target, not a conclusion of this declaration; no cap or complexity result is proved here. constant dependencies coefficient one multiplying the expected local budget, with no worst-case substitution or extra cap term. exactly integral epsilon dmu, with unit coefficient and no added constants. same both integration inequalities have coefficient one. the same mu appears in the input law, almost-everywhere premise and budget integral. markov and probability normalization ensure finite real event masses, with no lost mass factor. scopes informational the formal component uses an explicit integrable eventwise budget instead of constructing the paper's measurable conditional-tv function or identifying its expectation with delta_t. this is an explicitly deferred adapter, not a hidden completed source obligation. independent source review classification: explicit-elaboration; nonblocking. domains informational the result is generic over measurable spaces and supplied kernels. its source application requires a separate construction of the appropriate ideal-history law and transition kernels. independent source review classification: documented-generalization; nonblocking.",
