      "summary": "Blind reconstruction and seven-slot semantic comparison for AutoSamplingTheory.TechnicalLemmas.Analysis.GradientAECongruence.not_gradient_ae_congr_for_arbitrary_measure.",
      "column": 7,
      "url": "lean-foundations.html?view=semantic",
      "source_url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/2010.03106v4",
      "original_theorem": "ASTIS formalization-safety prerequisite for the SPHMC Lemma 4.17 route, whose explicit restricted-fiber score calculation is expanded by Lee--Shen--Tian Lemma 2, equation (11): there is no unrestricted theorem asserting that equality of two arbitrary pointwise representatives mu-almost everywhere implies equality of their classical gradients mu-almost everywhere. This prerequisite is not a theorem claimed by either source and does not challenge their explicit smooth representative.",
      "reconstructed_theorem": "It is not the case that for every measure mu on the real line and every two functions f and g from the real line to itself, f equals g mu-almost everywhere implies that the classical gradients of f and g are equal mu-almost everywhere.",
      "fidelity_verdict": "equivalent-after-elaboration",
      "blindness": "source hidden from decoder",
      "semantic_slots": [
        {
          "slot": "objects",
          "original": "The ASTIS safety prerequisite concerns a measure, two pointwise representatives equal almost everywhere, and their classical gradients; the restricted-fiber score is contextual source material.",
          "reconstructed": "The blind statement and Lean theorem quantify over a measure μ and functions f g : ℝ → ℝ, comparing f =ᵐ[μ] g with almost-everywhere equality of gradient f and gradient g.",
          "relation": "equivalent",
          "evidence": "The declaration directly negates the unrestricted gradient-congruence rule and proves it using μ = δ₀, f = 0, and g(x) = x."
        },
        {
          "slot": "domains",
          "original": "The contract rejects an unrestricted representative-to-classical-gradient rule; a real-line counterexample suffices to establish that obstruction.",
          "reconstructed": "The reconstruction and Lean module make the counterexample domain explicit as real-valued functions on ℝ under an arbitrary Measure ℝ.",
          "relation": "explicit-elaboration",
          "evidence": "The theorem type is ¬ ∀ (μ : Measure ℝ) (f g : ℝ → ℝ), f =ᵐ[μ] g → ∀ᵐ x ∂μ, gradient f x = gradient g x."
        },
        {
          "slot": "quantifiers",
          "original": "There is no universal theorem for every measure and every pair of arbitrary pointwise representatives transporting almost-everywhere equality to classical-gradient equality.",
          "reconstructed": "The blind reconstruction and Lean statement negate a universal quantification over μ, f, and g followed by the proposed implication.",
          "relation": "equivalent",
          "evidence": "The outer negation and nested ∀ μ f g precisely encode failure of the unrestricted universal rule, rather than claiming failure for every individual triple."
        },
        {
          "slot": "assumptions",
          "original": "Classical gradients are intended, with no regularity assumptions permitting gradient transport; the source papers retain an explicit smooth representative.",
          "reconstructed": "The Lean theorem uses Mathlib's total classical gradient without differentiability assumptions and supplies the Dirac-at-zero witness in the proof.",
          "relation": "explicit-elaboration",
          "evidence": "The candidate context explicitly distinguishes total classical gradients from Sobolev weak gradients and identifies δ₀, f = 0, and g(x) = x as the counterexample."
        },
        {
          "slot": "conclusion",
          "original": "Arbitrary μ-almost-everywhere equality does not by itself justify μ-almost-everywhere equality of classical gradients.",
          "reconstructed": "It is false that f =ᵐ[μ] g universally forces gradient f =ᵐ[μ] gradient g.",
          "relation": "equivalent",
          "evidence": "At the Dirac atom, f(0) = g(0) = 0 while the module computes gradient f 0 = 0 and gradient g 0 = 1, contradicting the proposed rule."
        },
        {
          "slot": "scopes",
          "original": "The obstruction is an ASTIS-authored formalization-safety prerequisite, is not a theorem attributed to SPHMC or Lee--Shen--Tian, and does not challenge their explicit smooth score calculation.",
          "reconstructed": "The publication context presents the declaration only as a representative-choice blocker supporting explicit-score-representative-safety and separately attributes the affine score calculation to Lee--Shen--Tian and its use to SPHMC.",
          "relation": "equivalent",
          "evidence": "The attribution, source scopes, binding role, and lesson prose consistently isolate the negative theorem as ASTIS safety infrastructure; they neither assign it to either paper nor turn it into a positive sampling inequality."
        },
        {
          "slot": "constant dependencies",
          "original": "The contextual restricted-fiber score depends on η, but the representative-safety obstruction itself requires no η, LSI, T2, W2, or contraction constant.",
          "reconstructed": "The Lean declaration has no ASTIS dependencies and uses only the fixed Dirac counterexample and elementary gradient computations.",
          "relation": "explicit-elaboration",
          "evidence": "The theorem type contains no quantitative constants, the candidate lists astis_dependencies as empty, and nothing in the proof establishes a positive LSI, T2, W2, or contraction result."
        }
      ],
