      "search": "semantic-audit:astis-rt-20261003-quadratictiltfisher semantic-audit astis-rt-20261003-quadratictiltfisher shared:quadratic-tilt-fisher · equivalent-after-elaboration blind reconstruction and seven-slot semantic comparison for autosamplingtheory.technicallemmas.informationtheory.quadratictiltfisher.information_quadratic_representative. equivalent-after-elaboration source hidden from decoder for the two normalized restricted-gaussian fibers with common base nu and quadratic centers y and y', the displayed log-density ratio has spatial gradient eta^{-1}(y-y'). therefore its relative fisher information under the left probability fiber mu_y is the expectation of the constant squared score, namely eta^{-2} times the squared euclidean distance between the centers. in the cited proof this value is subsequently inserted into log-sobolev and talagrand inequalities. the selected astis edge is only the exact fisher-energy evaluation for the explicit smooth nu-normalized representative under a separate probability integration law; it is not the construction of the left fiber, the log-sobolev inequality, the talagrand inequality, or the resulting wasserstein contraction. let iota be any finite index type and let e be the real euclidean space indexed by iota, with its euclidean norm and measurable structure. for every measure nu on e, every probability measure mu on e, every nonzero real eta, and every y,yprime in e, define z_y = integral_e exp(-norm(z-y)^2/(2*eta)) dnu(z) and z_yprime = integral_e exp(-norm(z-yprime)^2/(2*eta)) dnu(z). define the real-valued spatial function r by r(t) = -norm(t-y)^2/(2*eta)-log(z_y)-(-norm(t-yprime)^2/(2*eta))+log(z_yprime). then information(mu,1,r) = integral_e norm(gradient r(x))^2 dmu(x) = (eta^(-1))^2*norm(y-yprime)^2. here information(base,q,r) is the integral against base of q(x) times the squared euclidean norm of the spatial gradient of r, and the weight in this theorem is identically one. the assumptions are that iota is finite, mu has total mass one, and eta is nonzero. the measure nu is arbitrary and is used only in z_y and z_yprime; mu is the measure used to integrate the squared gradient. no equality or other relationship between nu and mu is required. both normalizer logarithms are scalar constants with respect to the differentiation variable t. the statement is universally quantified over all the stated objects and concerns exactly this explicit function r. no positivity of eta, probability or sigma-finiteness assumption on nu, exponential-integrability assumption, or positivity of either normalizer is stated; the integral, logarithm, reciprocal, and powers retain their lean real-operation meanings. the numerical coefficient is exactly (eta^(-1))^2. the answer depends only on eta and the squared distance between the centers, with no explicit dimension factor, no dependence on nu, and no dependence on the distribution of mu beyond its total mass one. this is an identity for the displayed fisher functional, not an assertion identifying r as the log-likelihood ratio of specified measures. accepted lee--shen--tian arxiv:2010.03106v4, lemma 2 proof, fisher-information display immediately before equation (11); used by sphmc arxiv:2609.06906v1 lemma 4.17 autosamplingtheory.technicallemmas.informationtheory.quadratictiltfisher.information_quadratic_representative autosamplingtheory/technicallemmas/informationtheory/quadratictiltfisher.lean true root anonymous_decoder_b adae68c4bc611d837dd51572867934e50c84a30799a088ac365750d025e5eaf5 accepted rgo_invariance_source objects two normalized restricted-gaussian fibers with centers y and yprime share the normalizer base nu; relative fisher information is integrated under the left probability fiber mu_y. the lean theorem has separate measures nu and mu: nu occurs only in the two logarithmic normalizers, while relativefisher.information integrates the explicit representative under mu. explicit-elaboration the signature is (nu mu : measure state) [isprobabilitymeasure mu]; every normalizer integral is with respect to nu, and the first argument of relativefisher.information is mu. domains finite-dimensional euclidean space r^d, positive fiber variance eta, and normalized restricted-gaussian fibers. state is euclideanspace real iota with fintype iota; eta is a nonzero real; nu is any measure and mu is any probability measure. explicit-elaboration euclideanspace real iota with fintype iota is the coordinate finite-dimensional source domain. the broader eta and measure hypotheses are accounted for in the assumptions and quantifiers slots. quantifiers for every pair of centers y and yprime for the two fibers obtained from the common base nu. for every finite index type, every nu, every probability mu, every nonzero eta, and all centers y and yprime. explicit-elaboration the lean identity is universal in the probability integration law mu rather than only the source choice mu = left tilt of nu; this is valid because the score is spatially constant. assumptions eta is positive; the fibers are normalized from a common base nu; the expectation law is the left probability fiber. eta is merely nonzero; mu is required only to be a probability measure; no equality between mu and a tilt of nu and no integrability or positivity condition on the nu-normalizers is assumed. explicit-elaboration the proof uses nu-normalizers only as constants under spatial differentiation and uses isprobabilitymeasure mu only to integrate the resulting constant. the source specialization is obtained by setting mu to the normalized left fiber and using eta > 0 to obtain eta != 0. conclusion the relative fisher energy of the explicit quadratic log-ratio representative is eta^(-2) times norm(y-yprime)^2. relativefisher.information mu 1 r equals (eta^(-1))^2 times norm(y-yprime)^2. equivalent the embedded proof substitutes gradient r = eta^(-1) smul (y-yprime), integrates its constant squared norm under a probability law, and squares the scalar; (eta^(-1))^2 is exactly eta^(-2) for eta != 0. scopes only the explicit smooth-representative fisher evaluation is selected; fiber construction, canonical llr differentiation, lsi, talagrand t2, and wasserstein contraction are excluded. the declaration concludes only an equality for relativefisher.information applied to the displayed explicit function and invokes the previously proved explicit-representative gradient theorem. same neither the theorem statement nor proof identifies the function with a canonical llr or states any log-sobolev, t2, or w2 proposition. the module documentation explicitly preserves these exclusions. constant dependencies the value depends exactly on eta through eta^(-2) and on the centers through norm(y-yprime)^2, with no dimension or base-measure factor. the right side is (eta^(-1))^2 * norm(y-yprime)^2 and is independent of nu, mu, and the finite dimension. equivalent the signs in the representative give gradient eta^(-1)(y-yprime), not its negative; squaring yields the exact eta^(-2) coefficient and removes no additional constants. quantifiers informational lean quantifies over any probability integration law mu independent of the common normalizer base nu. the cited left-fiber case is a direct specialization, and the proof genuinely needs only total mass one after the constant-score rewrite. independent source review classification: faithful-generalization; nonblocking. assumptions informational lean weakens the paper assumption eta > 0 to eta != 0. the gradient and squared-energy identity are algebraically valid on this larger domain, while the source specialization remains available. independent source review classification: faithful-generalization; nonblocking.",
