heory/examplecases/smoothedpicardhmc/initialgibbsphasetransportunconditional.lean true root gibbs_q2_decoder 9a7e9a7d65f878963e163ec94c9fb9fd2d08d70d5eb332f2dec994811ad860de accepted gibbs_q2_source_review objects lemma 4.16 compares the initial dirac-position, standard-gaussian-momentum phase law with the gibbs-position law smoothed by n(0,eta i) and paired with standard-gaussian momentum. the theorem compares (dirac xref).prod(stdgaussian e) to gaussiansmoothing(volume.tilted(-u),sqrt eta).prod(stdgaussian e) through phasemetric.phasewassersteinsq. equivalent the source defines pi_eta=pi*n(0,eta i), pi_eta=pi_eta tensor n(0,i), and the initial phase law at xref; the candidate uses the actual normalized gibbs tilt and the same phase factors. domains the source uses r^d with normalized positive curvature and positive algorithmic smoothing variance eta<=1. lean uses a finite-dimensional complete real inner-product borel space and permits eta in [0,1]. different the coordinate-free space is an isometric generalization of r^d; eta=0 is an additional valid endpoint for this moment-and-coupling subresult, but not the paper's positive-eta algorithmic regime. the lesson explicitly labels this extension. quantifiers the full source lemma is for every q>=2 and selects a minimizer internally; its q=2 instance has no supplied minimizer hypothesis. the lean theorem is universally quantified over admissible u, alpha, eta, and xref, proves only q=2, and constructs a minimizer internally. different the new declaration has no p argument or gradient-zero premise. its proof obtains p from the c2 lower hessian bound and passes it to the conditional q=2 theorem; no q>2 claim appears. assumptions after normalization the source has c2 hessian bounds alpha i<=hessian u<=i with 0<alpha<=1, an admissible xref with ||gradient u(xref)||^2<=alpha d, and eta<=1. lean requires contdiff r 2 u, the quadratic hessian bounds for every x and v, 0<alpha<=1, the same squared-gradient condition, and eta