      "column": 7,
      "url": "lean-foundations.html?view=semantic",
      "source_url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/2010.03106v4",
      "original_theorem": "For two normalized exponential tilts of one common base measure, the log density of the left tilt relative to the right tilt is represented by the left exponent minus its log normalizer, minus the right exponent plus its log normalizer. In the restricted-Gaussian application this identity is used under the left fiber law. ASTIS records the equality almost everywhere with the canonical log-likelihood-ratio representative; it does not infer equality of classical gradients.",
      "reconstructed_theorem": "For every measurable space X, every sigma-finite measure mu on X, and all functions f,g:X->R, assume that f is measurable and that both x->exp(f(x)) and x->exp(g(x)) are mu-integrable. Define Z_f = integral exp(f(z)) dmu(z) and Z_g = integral exp(g(z)) dmu(z), and let mu_f = mu.tilted(f) and mu_g = mu.tilted(g), the normalized exponential reweightings specified by the tilted-measure construction. Then the selected measurable real log-likelihood-ratio representative llr(mu_f,mu_g) satisfies llr(mu_f,mu_g)(x) = f(x)-log(Z_f)-g(x)+log(Z_g) for mu_f-almost every x. Here exp and log are the real exponential and the Lean real logarithm. The almost-everywhere reference measure is mu_f, not an unstated background probability measure. No separate global measurability assumption on g, nonzero-mass assumption on mu, or positive-normalizer assumption is imposed; the statement uses the Lean constructions also in degenerate cases and does not assert a pointwise identity.",
