      "original_theorem": "For real functions interpolation and interpolation' and real parameters rate, s, and t, assume s ≤ t, interpolation has the genuine two-sided derivative interpolation'(u) at every u in the closed interval [s,t], and rate·interpolation(u) ≤ interpolation'(u) for every u in the open interval (s,t). Then interpolation(s) ≤ interpolation(t)·exp(-rate·(t-s)). No sign condition is imposed on rate or interpolation; s=t is allowed; and no behavior outside [s,t] is asserted. This scalar comparison does not construct a semigroup or prove a carré-du-champ identity, Γ₂ curvature bound, or terminal-energy identification.",
