      "url": "lean-foundations.html?view=semantic",
      "source_url": "",
      "original_theorem": "For real functions H and H' and real parameters rate, source, t, and energy, assume rate > 0, t ≥ 0, H has derivative H'(s) at every s in [0,t], H(0)=0, and H'(s) ≤ source·exp(-rate·(t-s))·energy for every s in (0,t). Then H(t) ≤ (source/rate)·(1-exp(-rate·t))·energy. The endpoint derivative hypotheses are two-sided HasDerivAt statements; t=0 is allowed; no separate sign assumption is imposed on source or energy. This scalar calculus result does not construct a semigroup or establish an entropy-production or gradient-contraction estimate.",
      "reconstructed_theorem": "For all real-valued functions H and H' and all real rate, source, t, and energy, if rate > 0, t ≥ 0, H has derivative H'(s) at every s ∈ [0,t], H(0)=0, and H'(s) ≤ source·exp(-rate·(t-s))·energy for every s ∈ (0,t), then H(t) ≤ (source/rate)·(1-exp(-rate·t))·energy. The hypotheses require genuine two-sided derivatives at the endpoints; t=0 is permitted, and source and energy have no separate sign restrictions.",
