      "url": "lean-foundations.html?view=semantic",
      "source_url": "https://arxiv.org/html/2609.06906v1#A2.SS1",
      "original_theorem": "Natural J>=2, real h>0. Fin J is zero-based i=0,...,J-1, corresponding to source j=i+1.\nAll nodes, real Lagrange polynomials, compact Lebesgue supremum and integral coefficients are constructed explicitly. The measure is real Lebesgue measure on the displayed closed-endpoint interval integral. Continuity and interval integrability are conclusions of polynomial regularity, not supplied extra assumptions.\nFor natural J>=2 and real h>0, enumerate i=0,...,J-1 with Fin J and set t_i=h/2[1-cos(i*pi/(J-1))], ell_j=Lagrange.basis(univ,t,j), Lambda=sup_{s in[0,h]} sum_j |ell_j(s)|, omega_ij=integral_0^{t_i}(t_i-s)ell_j(s) ds and b_j=integral_0^h ell_j(s) ds. Each node is in[0,h], the node map is injective, and t_{J-1}=h. The cardinal identity ell_i(t_j)=1_{i=j} and the constant partition sum_j ell_j(s)=1 hold for every real s. The actual compact supremum satisfies Lambda>=1. For each i, sum_j|omega_ij|<=t_i^2*Lambda/2<=h^2*Lambda/2, and sum_j b_j=h.",
      "reconstructed_theorem": "For every natural J>=2 and real h>0, index the J nodes by i in Fin(J)={0,...,J-1} and define\n  t_i=(h/2)(1-cos((i/(J-1))pi)),\nwhere i and J are cast to real numbers in the real quotient. Define the real Lagrange basis polynomial\n  ell_j(X)=product_{k in Fin(J), k!=j} (X-t_k)/(t_j-t_k).\nEquivalently, before injectivity is established, each denominator is interpreted using real inversion in the polynomial product specified by the context. Define\n  Lambda=sup_{s in [0,h]} sum_{j in Fin(J)} |ell_j(s)|,\n  omega_ij=integral_0^{t_i} (t_i-s)ell_j(s) ds,\n  v_j=integral_0^h ell_j(s) ds.\nThen all of the following hold:\n  t_i belongs to [0,h] for every i;\n  i -> t_i is injective;\n  t_{J-1}=h;\n  ell_i(t_j)=1 if i=j and 0 otherwise, for every i,j;\n  sum_j ell_j(s)=1 for every real s, including outside [0,h];\n  1<=Lambda;\n  sum_j |omega_ij| <= (t_i^2/2)Lambda for every i;\n  sum_j |omega_ij| <= (h^2/2)Lambda for every i;\n  sum_j v_j=h.\nThe supremum is the conditionally complete real supremum of the displayed image of the closed interval. Integrals are oriented Lebesgue interval integrals; the node conclusion ensures their displayed endpoints are in nonnegative increasing order. All sums and products use the J actual indices. The theorem gives these interpolation, normalization and absolute-row-bound properties; it asserts no upper asymptotic bound on Lambda, positivity of individual integral weights, general polynomial quadrature exactness, probability kernel, or sampling convergence property.",
