      "original_theorem": "Faithful scoped source restatement: J is a natural number>=2 and h is a positive real. Fin J uses indices i=0,...,J-1 corresponding to source j=i+1.\nt_i=h/2[1-cos(i*pi/(J-1))]; ell_j is the real Mathlib Lagrange cardinal polynomial at those nodes; Lambda is the actual supremum of sum_j|ell_j(s)| on the compact interval[0,h]; b_j=integral_0^h ell_j(s) ds using real Lebesgue measure.\nFor these actual source nodes and integral coefficients, sum_j |b_j| <= h*Lambda. The finite compact supremum and every needed interval integrability are derived from polynomial continuity. No b_j>=0 or supplied coefficient envelope is assumed.\nDerived absolute-integral prerequisite for Appendix D1/D2 using the actual B1 coefficients. This does not prove individual Clenshaw-Curtis weight nonnegativity, logarithmic Lambda, complete B1, interpolation remainder B2 or either main theorem.",
      "reconstructed_theorem": "For every natural J>=2 and every real h>0, index nodes by Fin(J)={0,...,J-1} and define\n  t_i=(h/2)(1-cos((i/(J-1))pi)),\n  ell_j(X)=product_{k in Fin(J),k!=j} ((t_j-t_k)^(-1))(X-t_k),\n  Lambda=sup_{s in [0,h]} sum_{j in Fin(J)} |ell_j(s)|.\nThe quotient in the node definition uses real coercions, so its denominator is the real number J-1. The ell_j are real Lagrange basis polynomials evaluated at real arguments, and Lambda is the real supremum of the indicated interval image. Then\n  sum_{j in Fin(J)} |integral_0^h ell_j(s) ds| <= h Lambda.\nThe integrals use real Lebesgue measure with oriented endpoints; h>0 makes the interval order increasing. The absolute value is applied separately to each integral, followed by a finite sum. This theorem asserts precisely this total absolute integrated-weight bound, without adding signed normalization, individual positivity, an upper growth bound on Lambda, or quadrature exactness conclusions.",
