      "url": "lean-foundations.html?view=semantic",
      "source_url": "https://arxiv.org/html/2609.06906v1#S4.SS3",
      "original_theorem": "Attributed scoped mathematical restatement with explicit generalization:\nE is a complete real inner-product space (a real Hilbert space), including dimension zero. f:E->R is globally C2 in the Frechet sense. x,y and every test vector range over all E.\nalpha,beta are real and may be signed. For every z,v, alpha||v||^2 <= D2f(z)[v,v] <= beta||v||^2. These are bounds on the genuine nested Frechet derivative, not on an independently supplied operator.\nH(x,y) is the ordinary Lebesgue Bochner integral on[0,1] of the Riesz-represented D2f(y+t(x-y)). Its symmetry, continuity/measurability/integrability and secant identity are conclusions derived from C2, not hypotheses. Completeness permits the Riesz identification and Bochner operator integral.\nFor every complete real inner-product space E, every globally C2 real f with signed global genuine-Hessian diagonal bounds alpha,beta, and all x,y, let phi(t) be the Riesz operator of D2f(y+t(x-y)) and H=integral_0^1 phi(t) dt in ordinary Lebesgue measure. Then phi is interval integrable, gradient f x-gradient f y=H(x-y), H is symmetric, all v satisfy alpha||v||^2<=inner(Hv,v)<=beta||v||^2, and ||H||op<=max(|alpha|,|beta|). In finite Euclidean space this specializes to the source actual H with alpha=1/(2kappa),beta=1; V_eta smoothness/curvature must still be proved separately.\nOnly the actual Hessian-segment constructor in Lemma4.6. The source smoothed-potential C2/curvature producer, actual Picard-node weighted average H, first-order endpoint expansion/operator remainder, deterministic K contraction, stochastic Khat/Kbar, repeated history, PBPS and both full main results/composition remain independent. The signed Hilbert generalization is background, not an extra assumption on the finite-dimensional source theorem. No probability/cost guarantee or implemented kernel is asserted here.",
      "reconstructed_theorem": "Let E be any complete real inner-product space with the induced norm; no finite-dimensionality assumption is imposed. Let f:E->R be globally twice continuously Frechet differentiable, and let alpha,beta be arbitrary real numbers. Suppose, for every z,v in E,\n  alpha||v||^2 <= D^2 f(z)[v,v] <= beta||v||^2.\nFor every pair x,y in E define, for all real t,\n  phi(t)=A(y+t(x-y)),\nwhere A(z) is the actual continuous linear Riesz operator associated to the continuous bilinear form D^2 f(z), characterized by\n  <A(z)v,w>=D^2 f(z)[v,w] for all v,w in E.\nDefine the continuous linear operator\n  H=integral_0^1 phi(t) dt\nas an ordinary Lebesgue Bochner interval integral taking values in the complete normed space of continuous real linear operators E->E. Then the following five conclusions hold simultaneously:\n  phi is interval integrable with respect to real Lebesgue measure on 0..1;\n  grad f(x)-grad f(y)=H(x-y);\n  H is symmetric, meaning <Hv,w>=<v,Hw> for all v,w in E;\n  alpha||v||^2 <= <Hv,v> <= beta||v||^2 for every v in E;\n  ||H||_operator <= max(|alpha|,|beta|).\nThe gradient is the Riesz representative of the first Frechet derivative, and D^2 f(z) is the actual nested second Frechet derivative evaluated on its two vector arguments. The operator norm is the induced norm, not a matrix-entry norm.\nThe operator H is fixed by this integral along the segment from y to x, rather than chosen as an existential secant witness. Integrability, the exact gradient-secant identity, symmetry, quadratic-form bounds and the norm estimate are conclusions, not additional certificate assumptions. The theorem covers x=y and permits arbitrary signs of alpha,beta subject to the stated all-point/all-vector inequalities; it does not separately assume alpha>=0, beta>=0 or alpha<=beta. No probability law, finite basis, matrix, stochastic process or sampled mean-value point is supplied.",
