      "id": "semantic-audit:ASTIS-RT-20261005-MonotoneProximalMap",
      "kind": "semantic-audit",
      "label": "ASTIS-RT-20261005-MonotoneProximalMap",
      "status": "fidelity-exact",
      "subtitle": "companion:sphmc-monotone-proximal-nonexpansive · equivalent-after-elaboration",
      "summary": "Blind reconstruction and seven-slot semantic comparison for AutoSamplingTheory.TechnicalLemmas.Analysis.MonotoneProximalMap.nonexpansive_of_monotone_optimality.",
      "column": 7,
      "url": "lean-foundations.html?view=semantic",
      "source_url": "https://arxiv.org/html/2609.06906v1#A4.SS1",
      "original_theorem": "Faithful scoped source restatement: E is any real inner-product normed additive group; g,p:E→E, eta>=0.\nFor every x,y, inner(g(x)-g(y),x-y)>=0, and for every y, p(y)+eta*g(p(y))=y. No differentiability, probability, finite dimension, existence or measurability is assumed or concluded by this abstract leaf.\nThe given exact optimality solution p is 1-Lipschitz. This abstract implication is extracted verbatim from the existing private proof and is consumed by both the existing input-estimator theorem and the actual proximal-phase comparison. It carries no new mathematical-completion credit.\nShared abstract prerequisite for source proximal nonexpansiveness; not an existence, algorithm, stochastic estimator or paper theorem. Actual paper consumers derive monotonicity and construct p themselves.",
      "reconstructed_theorem": "For every real inner-product space E with its normed additive-group structure, any functions g,p:E->E and any real eta>=0, suppose that g is monotone in the precise sense\n  <g(x)-g(y),x-y> >= 0 for every x,y in E,\nand that\n  p(y)+eta g(p(y))=y for every y in E.\nThen p is nonexpansive: for every u,v in E,\n  ||p(u)-p(v)|| <= ||u-v||.\nThe conclusion is LipschitzWith 1 p in the norm-induced metric on E. The theorem assumes that a global p satisfying the equation has been supplied; it does not assert existence of such a p. It imposes no completeness, finite-dimensionality, measurable-space, smoothness, gradient, strong-monotonicity, or strictly positive eta requirement.",
