      "subtitle": "companion:sphmc-first-order-difference · equivalent-after-elaboration",
      "summary": "Blind reconstruction and seven-slot semantic comparison for AutoSamplingTheory.ExampleCases.SmoothedPicardHMC.FirstOrderDifference.source_first_order_difference.",
      "column": 7,
      "url": "lean-foundations.html?view=semantic",
      "source_url": "https://arxiv.org/html/2609.06906v1#S4.SS3",
      "original_theorem": "Attributed scoped mathematical restatement with explicit remaining producer:\nE is a complete finite-dimensional real inner-product space, including dimension zero, with its Borel measurable space. f:E->R is globally C2. kappa>=1 and every genuine nested second derivative satisfies (2kappa)^-1||v||^2 <= D2f(z)[v,v] <= ||v||^2 for every z,v. These are the normalized smoothed-potential curvature interface; f=V_eta and Lemma4.1 are independent prerequisites.\nJ>=2,h>0. The nodes are t_j=h/2(1-cos(j*pi/(J-1))), ell_j their actual Lagrange cardinal polynomials, Lambda=sup_{s in[0,h]} sum_j|ell_j(s)|, omega_ij=integral_0^{t_i}(t_i-s)ell_j(s)ds, b_j=integral_0^h ell_j(s)ds and c_j=integral_0^h(h-s)ell_j(s)ds. Assume only h^2 Lambda<=1. In particular h<=1 is derived, never added as a premise.\na=exp(-h/2),sigma=sqrt(1-exp(-h)),Gamma=stdGaussian(E) product stdGaussian(E). Both Gaussian vectors are independent under Gamma and shared synchronously between compared inputs. P0=a p+sigma zeta0; Y0_j=x+t_j P0; Y1_i=Y0_i-sum_j omega_ij gradient f(Y0_j); Phi=(x+hP0-sum_j c_j gradient f(Y1_j),a(P0-sum_j b_j gradient f(Y1_j))+sigma zeta1). No stochastic gradient/proximal array appears.\nFor every pair z,zprime and every Gaussian vector zeta (not just almost surely), H_j is the ordinary Bochner integral of the Riesz-represented genuine D2f along the Y1prime_j-to-Y1_j segment; H=h^-1 sum_j b_j H_j. The source phase norm is the Euclidean L2 product WithLp2, not the default product max norm.\nAt this explicit normalized C2 curvature interface, the actual exact-gradient two-Picard map Phi is measurable and defines a Markov kernel K(z)=Gamma.map(Phi_z). For every synchronously coupled input pair and every two-vector noise value, the actual integral average H is symmetric, (2kappa)^-1 I<=H<=I and ||H||op<=1. There exists a genuine continuous real linear R on the L2 phase space such that Delta1=Delta0+h*(Delta p,-H Delta x-Delta p)+R Delta0 and ||R||op<=8 Lambda h^2. R may depend on z,zprime,zeta; no measurable choice or global derivative of Phi is asserted. H, Phi, K and the remainder are constructed, not supplied as conclusion certificates.\nThis is the actual deterministic numerical kernel/first-order difference interface in Lemma4.6, conditional on the explicitly exposed normalized C2 curvature producer. The identity f=V_eta and source Lemma4.1 smoothing/curvature production remain open. It does not prove Proposition4.7 contraction/step absorption, Lemma4.8 M_kappa increment, logarithmic Lambda/B2, Khat/Kbar accuracy, invariance, repeated-history/cost, PBPS, either complete main theorem or their actual-input expected-cost composition. Khat and Kbar cannot be replaced by this K; TV closeness cannot transfer unbounded cost.",
      "reconstructed_theorem": "Let E be a complete finite-dimensional real inner-product space with its Borel measurable structure. Let f:E->R be globally twice continuously Frechet differentiable, let kappa,h be real and J be natural. Assume kappa>=1, J>=2, h>0 and, for every z,v in E,\n  (1/(2kappa))||v||^2 <= D^2 f(z)[v,v] <= ||v||^2.\nFor each i in Fin(J)={0,...,J-1}, define\n  t_i=(h/2)(1-cos((i/(J-1))pi)),\nwith real coercions in the quotient. Let ell_j be the actual real Lagrange cardinal polynomial at the defined nodes. Define exactly\n  Lambda=sup{sum_j |ell_j(s)| : s in [0,h]},\n  omega_ij=integral_0^{t_i} (t_i-s)ell_j(s) ds,\n  b_j=integral_0^h ell_j(s) ds,\n  c_j=integral_0^h (h-s)ell_j(s) ds.\nThese are ordinary real Lebesgue interval integrals and the supremum is the real supremum of the displayed image. If h^2 Lambda<=1, put a=exp(-h/2),sigma=sqrt(1-exp(-h)),and let Gamma=G x G be the independent product of two standard isotropic Gaussian probability laws G on E.\nFor an incoming phase state z=(X,P) in E x E and noise zeta=(u,v) in E x E, define the direct-gradient two-stage update by\n  P0(z,zeta)=a P+sigma u,\n  Y0_j(z,zeta)=X+t_j P0(z,zeta),\n  Y1_i(z,zeta)=Y0_i(z,zeta)-sum_j omega_ij grad f(Y0_j(z,zeta)),\n  Phi(z,zeta)=(X+h P0(z,zeta)-sum_j c_j grad f(Y1_j(z,zeta)),\n               a(P0(z,zeta)-sum_j b_j grad f(Y1_j(z,zeta)))+sigma v).\nAll sums run over all J actual indices. Then Phi is jointly measurable in its incoming state and both noise coordinates. There exists a Markov kernel K from E x E to E x E,with probability output measures,satisfying for every incoming state z\n  K(z)=(zeta -> Phi(z,zeta))_* Gamma.\nIn addition,for every pair of incoming states z,zprime and every common noise pair zeta in E x E,define the actual continuous linear operator\n  H=(1/h) sum_j b_j integral_0^1 A(Y1_j(zprime,zeta)+u(Y1_j(z,zeta)-Y1_j(zprime,zeta))) du,\nwhere A(v) is the Riesz operator of the genuine continuous bilinear form D^2 f(v),characterized by <A(v)r,s>=D^2 f(v)[r,s]. The integrals here are Bochner interval integrals with values in continuous real linear operators E->E. In particular the secant segment runs from the primed second-stage point to the unprimed one,and H is fixed by these integrals and weights,not a supplied certificate.\nWrite deltaX=z.1-zprime.1,deltaP=z.2-zprime.2,and d=toLp2(deltaX,deltaP) in the Euclidean phase space L=WithLp 2(E x E). This space uses coordinatewise vector operations and norm sqrt(||X||^2+||P||^2);toLp2 preserves the pair coordinates. Then simultaneously:\n  H is symmetric: <Hr,s>=<r,Hs> for all r,s in E;\n  (1/(2kappa))||v||^2 <= <Hv,v> <= ||v||^2 for every v in E;\n  ||H||_operator <=1;\n  there exists a continuous real linear R:L->L with ||R||_operator<=8 Lambda h^2 such that\n    toLp2(Phi(z,zeta).1-Phi(zprime,zeta).1,\n          Phi(z,zeta).2-Phi(zprime,zeta).2)\n      = d+h toLp2(deltaP,-H(deltaX)-deltaP)+R(d).\nThe R witness is inside the universal quantification over z,zprime,zeta. It may therefore depend on the compared states and common noise,while its displayed operator-norm bound is uniform in those data. No single global R or measurable dependence of R on them is asserted. Both outputs use exactly the same noise pair. Norms of H use the induced E operator norm;the norm of R uses the stated Euclidean L2 phase norm rather than the default product max norm.\nThe conclusions are joint measurability,existence of this Gaussian pushforward probability kernel,and the exact same-noise output-difference representation with the stated averaged-Hessian properties and bounded linear remainder. They assert no invariant law,mixing or transport contraction,time-evolution result,expected evaluation count,proximal interpreter,additional independent Gaussian arrays or matrix certificate.",
