      "url": "lean-foundations.html?view=semantic",
      "source_url": "https://arxiv.org/html/2609.06906v1#S3.SS2",
      "original_theorem": "Attributed implementation component of Algorithm 3.1 with Appendix D.1/D.2 approximate proximal queries, not a convergence theorem. Under the normalized C2 Hessian bounds and 0<eta<=c<1, eps>0, construct one measurable stopped proximal map q, its exact proximal comparison p, and its successful gradient-test count N+1. For h>=0 and supplied finite deterministic node and quadrature arrays, draw independent zeta0,zeta1,G0,G1 from the product of two standard Gaussian laws and two finite products of standard Gaussian laws. Set a=exp(-h/2), sigma=sqrt(1-exp(-h)), P0=a P+sigma zeta0, Y0_i=X+t_i P0, Z0_j=grad V(q(Y0_j)+sqrt(eta)G0_j), Y1_i=Y0_i-sum_j omega_ij Z0_j, Z1_j=grad V(q(Y1_j)+sqrt(eta)G1_j), Xnext=X+h P0-sum_j positionWeight_j Z1_j and Pnext=a(P0-sum_j momentumWeight_j Z1_j)+sigma zeta1. The joint update Phi is measurable and defines a Markov kernel K(s,.)=map(Phi(s,.),gamma); at every queried y the actual finite interpreter with fuel N(y)+1 returns (q(y),N(y)+1) and ||q(y)-p(y)||<=eps. Source node/weight construction and the repeated-run accuracy/cost theorems remain separate.",
      "reconstructed_theorem": "Let E be a complete finite-dimensional real inner-product space, equipped with its Borel measurable structure, and let I be any finite index type. Let V:E->R be C^2 and let kappa be a nonnegative real with 1<=kappa. Suppose that, for every x,v in E,\n  kappa^(-1)||v||^2 <= D^2 V(x)[v,v] <= ||v||^2.\nFix real eta,c,epsilon such that 0<eta<=c<1 and epsilon>0, a real h>=0, and arbitrary deterministic arrays t:I->R, omega:I x I->R, b:I->R, and d:I->R. Here b and d denote positionWeight and momentumWeight.\nThen there exist measurable maps p,q:E->E and N:E->N such that for every y in E,\n  p(y)+eta grad V(p(y))=y,\n  ||q(y)-p(y)||<=epsilon,\n  run(grad V,eta,epsilon,y,N(y)+1,y)=some(q(y),N(y)+1).\nThe finite exact-real interpreter run(g,eta,epsilon,y,fuel,x) fails with none when fuel is zero. With positive fuel it sets next=y-eta g(x). If ||next-x||<=(1-eta)epsilon it returns (x,1); otherwise it recurses from next with one less unit of fuel and adds one to the successful query count. Thus the displayed equality supplies a finite successful run starting at y, using fuel N(y)+1, with output q(y) and reported count exactly N(y)+1.\nFor these witnesses put a=exp(-h/2), sigma=sqrt(1-exp(-h)), and let gamma be the product law of mutually independent standard Gaussian E-valued variables u,v,(xi_j)_{j in I},(xi'_j)_{j in I}; explicitly gamma=(G x G) x ((product_I G) x (product_I G)), where G is the centered Gaussian probability measure with identity covariance. For an incoming state s=(x,r) in E x E and a noise sample z=((u,v),(xi,xi')), define\n  P0=a r+sigma u,\n  Y0_i=x+t_i P0,\n  Z0_j=grad V(q(Y0_j)+sqrt(eta) xi_j),\n  Y1_i=Y0_i-sum_{j in I} omega_{ij} Z0_j,\n  Z1_j=grad V(q(Y1_j)+sqrt(eta) xi'_j),\n  Phi(s,z)=(x+h P0-sum_{j in I} b_j Z1_j,\n            a(P0-sum_{j in I} d_j Z1_j)+sigma v).\nThen 1-exp(-h)>=0, Phi is jointly measurable on (E x E) x ((E x E) x ((I->E) x (I->E))), and there exists a Markov kernel K from E x E to E x E satisfying, for every s,\n  K(s)=(z -> Phi(s,z))_* gamma.\nEquivalently, for every Borel output event A, K(s,A)=gamma({z:Phi(s,z) in A}); each K(s) is a probability measure and its value on each measurable event depends measurably on s. All sums use the entire finite index type. This statement asserts the existence and measurability of these witnesses and transition kernel; it gives no invariant-distribution, convergence, bias, or uniform query-complexity conclusion.",
