      "subtitle": "companion:sphmc-implemented-phase-query-work · equivalent-after-elaboration",
      "summary": "Blind reconstruction and seven-slot semantic comparison for AutoSamplingTheory.ExampleCases.SmoothedPicardHMC.ImplementedPhaseWork.implemented_phase_query_moments_and_work.",
      "column": 7,
      "url": "lean-foundations.html?view=semantic",
      "source_url": "https://arxiv.org/html/2609.06906v1#A4.SS2",
      "original_theorem": "E is a complete finite-dimensional real inner-product space with its Borel sigma-algebra; i ranges over a finite index type of size J.\nV is C2, kappa>=1, and kappa^(-1)||v||^2 <= D2V(x)[v,v] <= ||v||^2 for all x,v. Its actual gradient vanishes at the supplied xstar.\n0<eta<=c<1, eps>0, h>=0, M>=0, S>=0. The incoming probability law nu on (X,P) has integrable ||X-xstar||^2+||P||^2, with integral at most M.\nThe supplied deterministic nodes satisfy |t_i|<=1 and each supplied omega row satisfies sum_j |omega_ij|<=S. Position and momentum weights are arbitrary supplied real arrays; source Chebyshev quadrature is not instantiated.\nFor the full independent driving law gamma=(Gaussian zeta0 product Gaussian zeta1) product (Gaussian array G0 product Gaussian array G1), and mu=nu product gamma, set a=exp(-h/2), sigma=sqrt(1-exp(-h)), MR=M+(1-exp(-h))*d, B=6MR+3eps^2+3eta*d and C=2+(1+log((1-c)^(-1)))/(-log c), d=finrank(E). There exist the same measurable exact proximal map p, actual stopped output q and natural index N, with p(y)+eta grad V(p(y))=y, ||q(y)-p(y)||<=eps and run(grad V,eta,eps,y,N(y)+1,y)=some(q(y),N(y)+1) for every y. Define P0=aP+sigma zeta0, Y0_i=X+t_i P0, Z0_j=grad V(q(Y0_j)+sqrt(eta) G0_j), Y1_i=Y0_i-sum_j omega_ij Z0_j, Z1_j=grad V(q(Y1_j)+sqrt(eta) G1_j), and Phi=(X+h P0-sum_j positionWeight_j Z1_j, a(P0-sum_j momentumWeight_j Z1_j)+sigma zeta1). Phi is measurable and gives a Markov kernel K(s)=gamma.map(z -> Phi(s,z)). For every i both gradient squares are integrable, with E_mu||grad V(Y0_i)||^2<=2MR and E_mu||grad V(Y1_i)||^2<=4MR+2S^2 B. Both actual counts N(Y0_i)+1 and N(Y1_i)+1 are integrable; their respective expectations are at most C[1+log(1+sqrt(2MR)/eps)] and C[1+log(1+sqrt(4MR+2S^2 B)/eps)].",
      "reconstructed_theorem": "Let E be a complete finite-dimensional real inner-product space equipped with its Borel measurable structure, and let I be any finite index type. Let V:E->R be C^2 and kappa be a nonnegative real with 1<=kappa. Assume for every x,v in E that\n  kappa^(-1)||v||^2 <= D^2 V(x)[v,v] <= ||v||^2.\nFix real eta,c,epsilon with 0<eta<=c<1 and epsilon>0. Let nu be a probability measure on E x E, and fix xstar in E with grad V(xstar)=0. Fix h,M,S>=0, and assume that the function (x,r) -> ||x-xstar||^2+||r||^2 is integrable under nu with integral at most M. Let t:I->R satisfy |t_i|<=1 for every i, and omega:I x I->R satisfy sum_j |omega_ij|<=S for every i. Let b,d:I->R be arbitrary deterministic real arrays, denoting positionWeight and momentumWeight.\nWrite D=dim_R E, a=exp(-h/2), sigma=sqrt(1-exp(-h)), G for the centered identity-covariance Gaussian probability measure on E, gi=product_I G, gamma=(G x G) x (gi x gi), and mu=nu x gamma. Thus, under mu, an incoming state (x,r) of law nu is independent of mutually independent standard Gaussian vectors u,v and independent standard Gaussian arrays xi,xi-prime. Define the exact constants\n  MR=M+(1-exp(-h))D,\n  B=6 MR+3 epsilon^2+3 eta D,\n  C=2+(1+log((1-c)^(-1)))/(-log c).\nThen there exist measurable p,q:E->E and N:E->N such that for every y in E,\n  p(y)+eta grad V(p(y))=y,\n  ||q(y)-p(y)||<=epsilon,\n  run(grad V,eta,epsilon,y,N(y)+1,y)=some(q(y),N(y)+1).\nThe exact-real interpreter returns none at zero fuel. With positive fuel it evaluates g(x), sets next=y-eta g(x), and returns (x,1) if ||next-x||<=(1-eta)epsilon. Otherwise it recurses from next with one less fuel and adds one to a successful returned count. Thus the displayed run equality records a successful run initialized at y, with fuel and reported gradient-test count both N(y)+1, including the final successful test.\nFor these same witnesses, for w=((x,r),((u,v),(xi,xi-prime))) define\n  P0(w)=a r+sigma u,\n  Y0_i(w)=x+t_i P0(w),\n  Z0_j(w)=grad V(q(Y0_j(w))+sqrt(eta)xi_j),\n  Y1_i(w)=Y0_i(w)-sum_j omega_ij Z0_j(w),\n  Z1_j(w)=grad V(q(Y1_j(w))+sqrt(eta)xi-prime_j),\n  Phi(w)=(x+h P0(w)-sum_j b_j Z1_j(w),\n          a(P0(w)-sum_j d_j Z1_j(w))+sigma v).\nThen Phi is jointly measurable, and there exists a Markov kernel K from E x E to E x E such that for every incoming state s,\n  K(s)=(z -> Phi(s,z))_* gamma.\nIn addition, for every i in I, all of the following hold with integration over w under mu:\n  ||grad V(Y0_i)||^2 is integrable,\n  integral ||grad V(Y0_i)||^2 dmu <= 2 MR,\n  N(Y0_i)+1 is integrable,\n  integral (N(Y0_i)+1) dmu <= C[1+log(1+sqrt(2 MR)/epsilon)],\n  ||grad V(Y1_i)||^2 is integrable,\n  integral ||grad V(Y1_i)||^2 dmu <= 4 MR+2 S^2 B,\n  N(Y1_i)+1 is integrable,\n  integral (N(Y1_i)+1) dmu <= C[1+log(1+sqrt(4 MR+2 S^2 B)/epsilon)].\nCounts are cast to the reals in integrability and integral statements. Integrability is Bochner integrability, and all sums range over the entire finite index type. Each K(s) is a probability measure, with measurable dependence on s for every measurable output event. The theorem supplies these witnesses, the transition kernel, and these per-index input-gradient moment and interpreter-count bounds. It does not assert an invariant law, sampler convergence, final-state moment or bias bound, or an overall algorithmic gradient-call bound including the Z0 and Z1 evaluations.",
      "fidelity_verdict": "equivalent-after-elaboration",
