      "original_theorem": "Faithful scoped source(4.14)-(4.15) restatement:\nE is a finite-dimensional real inner-product space, including dimension zero; H:E→E is a real linear map. For all v,w, inner(Hv,w)=inner(v,Hw). No supplied eigenbasis or commuting/certified H-square condition is assumed.\nkappa>=1, alpha=1/(2kappa); for every v, alpha||v||²<=inner(v,Hv)<=||v||². These are the actual source symmetric operator bounds. Construction of H from genuine positive integral momentum weights belongs to Lemma4.6 and remains separate.\nx,p range over all E, without probability laws, moment, measure, smoothness or integrability assumptions. The phase norm is the actual M_kappa quadratic form, not the default product max norm.\nFor every finite-dimensional real inner-product E, kappa>=1, symmetric real linear H with (2kappa)^-1 I<=H<=I and every x,p∈E, D_H(x,p)>=Q_kappa(x,p)/(8kappa), where Q_kappa=(1/(2kappa)+1/2)||x||²+inner(x,p)+||p||² and D_H=inner(x,Hx)+2inner(p,Hx)-kappa^-1 inner(x,p)+||p||². With A_H(x,p)=(p,-Hx-p), M_kappa(x,p)=((alpha+1/2)x+p/2,x/2+p), this is exactly -2 inner(M_kappa(x,p),A_H(x,p))>=Q_kappa/(8kappa), hence the full matrix quadratic inequality (4.15). Product inner here is the sum of the two E inner products.\nOnly source full-dimensional drift dissipation(4.14)-(4.15). The exact-gradient kernel K difference expansion/remainder, actual averaged H producer, positivity of integral momentum weights, logarithmic Lambda and actual step adapters remain open. No K/Khat/Kbar interchange, supplied contraction recurrence, kinetic path/invariance or run-wide D3/D7/D8/main/PBPS/composition guarantee is claimed. The private scalar and coordinate helpers share the public whole-module review and gain no separate theorem/leaf credit.",
      "reconstructed_theorem": "For every finite-dimensional real inner-product space E with its normed additive-group structure, every real kappa>=1, and every real linear map H:E->E, assume H is symmetric:\n  <H(v),w>=<v,H(w)> for every v,w in E.\nAssume the actual all-vector quadratic-form bounds\n  (1/(2 kappa))||v||^2 <= <v,H(v)> <= ||v||^2\nfor every v in E. Then for every pair x,p in E,\n  (1/(8 kappa))[(1/(2 kappa)+1/2)||x||^2+<x,p>+||p||^2]\n    <= <x,H(x)>+2<p,H(x)>-(1/kappa)<x,p>+||p||^2.\nAll norms are the induced norms on E and all inner products are real inner products. Both sides are finite real numbers. The dimension of E can be zero, and no eigenbasis or matrix diagonalization is supplied as part of the hypotheses.\nTo spell out the approved quadratic-form context, define alpha=1/(2 kappa)+1/2 and\n  Q(x,p)=alpha||x||^2+<x,p>+||p||^2,\n  A(x,p)=(alpha x+p/2,x/2+p),\n  B(x,p)=(p,-H(x)-p).\nUsing the phase pairing <(u,v),(uprime,vprime)>_phase=<u,uprime>+<v,vprime>, one has Q(x,p)=<(x,p),A(x,p)>_phase and the displayed right-hand expression is exactly -2<A(x,p),B(x,p)>_phase. Thus the inequality is equivalently Q(x,p)/(8 kappa)<=-2<A(x,p),B(x,p)>_phase. The cross term in Q is exactly <x,p>, not twice that term; the off-diagonal action coefficients are 1/2. This phase pairing does not introduce a default product max norm.\nThe theorem is a deterministic pointwise quadratic inequality for arbitrary x,p and the assumed symmetric linear H. It asserts no trajectory, time derivative along a proved dynamics, stochastic law, probability-kernel contraction, eigenbasis existence, positive-determinant hypothesis, or additional supplied lower bound on H squared.",
