      "subtitle": "companion:sphmc-phase-accuracy-work · equivalent-after-elaboration",
      "summary": "Blind reconstruction and seven-slot semantic comparison for AutoSamplingTheory.ExampleCases.SmoothedPicardHMC.PhaseAccuracyWork.source_phase_accuracy_and_work.",
      "column": 7,
      "url": "lean-foundations.html?view=semantic",
      "source_url": "https://arxiv.org/html/2609.06906v1#A4.SS2",
      "original_theorem": "Faithful scoped actual-phase integration restatement:\nE is a complete finite-dimensional real inner-product space with its Borel sigma algebra, including dimension zero. V:E→R is C2; kappa>=1 and kappa^-1||v||²<=D²V(x)[v,v]<=||v||² for every x,v (normalized beta=1).\nJ>=2; h>0; actual t_i=h/2(1-cos(i*pi/(J-1))), i=0,...,J-1; ell_j=Lagrange.basis(univ,t,j); Lambda=sup_{s∈[0,h]}sum_j|ell_j(s)|; omega_ij=integral_0^{t_i}(t_i-s)ell_j(s) ds; pw is the final omega row and b_j=integral_0^h ell_j(s) ds. Assume only h²Lambda<=1; h<=1 is derived.\n0<eta<=c<1 and eps>0. c is a contraction upper-bound parameter in the cost certificate, not an algorithm parameter or additional gradient query.\nnu is the actual incoming phase-state probability measure. xstar∈E satisfies gradient V(xstar)=0; M>=0. The real function ||X-xstar||²+||P||² is nu-integrable and its nu-expectation is <=M. These actual-state moment hypotheses are explicit; no TV approximation or independently sampled proxy law is substituted.\ngamma=((stdGaussian(E) prod stdGaussian(E)) prod (gi prod gi)), where gi=Measure.pi_{Fin J}(stdGaussian(E)); all four factors and each array coordinate are independent. mu=nu prod gamma. Write w=((X,P),((zeta0,zeta1),(G0,G1))), a=exp(-h/2), sigma=sqrt(1-exp(-h)) and P0=aP+sigma*zeta0.\nFor any f:E→E define Y0_i=X+t_iP0, Z0_f,j=gradient V(f(Y0_j)+sqrt(eta)G0_j), Y1_f,i=Y0_i-sum_j omega_ij Z0_f,j, Z1_f,j=gradient V(f(Y1_f,j)+sqrt(eta)G1_j) and Phi_f(w)=(X+hP0-sum_j pw_j Z1_f,j, a(P0-sum_j b_j Z1_f,j)+sigma*zeta1). The exact and approximate branches have their own Y1_f. For the constructed q,N set T(w)=2J+sum_i[(N(Y0_i(w))+1)+(N(Y1_q,i(w))+1)].\nSet d=finrank_R E, S=h²Lambda/2, MR=M+(1-exp(-h))*d, B=6MR+3eps²+3eta*d, C=2+(1+log((1-c)^(-1)))/(-log c), L0=C*(1+log(1+sqrt(2MR)/eps)) and L1=C*(1+log(1+sqrt(4MR+2S²B)/eps)). W_rho(mu1,mu2) is [inf_{pi coupling(mu1,mu2)} integral (sqrt(||DX||²+||DP||²))^rho dpi]^(1/rho), expressed in extended nonnegative reals, for every finite real rho>=1.\nThere exist one measurable exact optimality map p, one measurable stopped output map q and one measurable N:E→Nat such that p(y)+eta*gradient V(p(y))=y, ||q(y)-p(y)||<=eps, p is nonexpansive and the actual residual-stopped interpreter from y returns some(q(y),N(y)+1) with sufficient fuel N(y)+1. The identical p,q,N define actual full-phase Markov Kp,Kq, the actual output Phi_q and the actual total returned gradient count T. For every state s and finite real rho>=1, true Euclidean W_rho(Kq(s),Kp(s))<=3hLambda eps. Under the same actual driving law mu=nu prod gamma, T is measurable, real-integrable and E_mu[T]<=J(2+L0+L1). For every driving input w the counted phase interpreter with fuel(y)=N(y)+1 returns exactly some(Phi_q(w),T(w)). All output/count/kernel/cost conjuncts share these witnesses.\nOne successful exact-real source two-layer phase with actual integral coefficients and all four Gaussian factors, joining real accuracy and expected returned query cost. The exact comparison phase uses exact proximal stochastic gradients, not deterministic gradient V_eta. N(y)+1 is a sufficient execution certificate, not a free runtime computation of N. Counts include terminal residual tests and 2J shifted noisy-gradient calls; arithmetic, random draws and failed-run cost are separate. No TV-to-unbounded-cost transfer. Run-wideD3 contraction/history/D7/D8, B2/depth-K/logarithmic Lambda/weight positivity/source parameter choices/smoothing/Wp/proxy-warmness/initialization/PBPS/main results and actual-input composition remain open.",
      "reconstructed_theorem": "Let E be any complete finite-dimensional real inner-product space with its Borel measurable structure. Let J be a natural number with J>=2. Let V:E->R be genuinely twice continuously Frechet differentiable, and let kappa be a nonnegative real with kappa>=1. Assume, for all x,v in E,\n  kappa^(-1)||v||^2 <= D^2 V(x)[v,v] <= ||v||^2.\nFix real eta,c,epsilon with 0<eta<=c<1 and epsilon>0. Let nu be a probability measure on E x E and fix xstar in E with grad V(xstar)=0. Fix h,M in R with h>0 and M>=0. Assume that s=(X,P) -> ||X-xstar||^2+||P||^2 is Bochner integrable with respect to nu, with its integral <=M.\nFor the J actual indices i,j in Fin(J)={0,...,J-1}, define precisely\n  t_i=(h/2)(1-cos((i/(J-1))pi)),\n  ell_j(X)=product_{k in Fin(J),k!=j} (t_j-t_k)^(-1)(X-t_k),\n  Lambda=sup{sum_j |ell_j(s)| : s in [0,h]},\n  omega_ij=integral_0^{t_i} (t_i-s)ell_j(s) ds,\n  pw_j=omega_{J-1,j},\n  bw_j=integral_0^h ell_j(s) ds,\n  S=(h^2/2)Lambda.\nHere the quotient in the node formula is real; the ell_j are real Lagrange polynomials evaluated at real s; Lambda is the real supremum of the displayed interval image; all interval integrals use real Lebesgue measure with orientation. pw,bw denote positionWeight,momentumWeight.\nWrite D=dim_R E, a=exp(-h/2), sigma=sqrt(1-exp(-h)). Let G be the centered identity-covariance Gaussian probability measure on E. Set gi=product_{Fin(J)} G, gamma=(G x G) x (gi x gi), and mu=nu x gamma. All noise factors are independent: two E-valued Gaussian vectors and two independent J-coordinate Gaussian arrays whose own coordinates are independent. Under mu the incoming state of law nu is independent of this noise. Define exactly\n  MR=M+(1-exp(-h))D,\n  B=6 MR+3 epsilon^2+3 eta D,\n  C=2+(1+log((1-c)^(-1)))/(-log c),\n  L0=C[1+log(1+sqrt(2 MR)/epsilon)],\n  L1=C[1+log(1+sqrt(4 MR+2 S^2 B)/epsilon)].\nIf h^2 Lambda<=1, there exist single global measurable maps p,q:E->E and N:E->N such that, for every y in E,\n  p(y)+eta grad V(p(y))=y,\n  ||q(y)-p(y)||<=epsilon,\n  proximalQuery(grad V,eta,epsilon,y,N(y)+1,y)=some(q(y),N(y)+1),\nand p is nonexpansive: ||p(u)-p(v)||<=||u-v|| for all u,v in E.\nThe interpreter proximalQuery(g,eta,epsilon,y,fuel,x) returns none at zero fuel. With fuel n+1 it evaluates g(x), sets z=y-eta g(x), and tests ||z-x||<=(1-eta)epsilon. On success it returns some(x,1); otherwise it recurses at z with fuel n, propagates failure and increments a successful count by one. The equivalent residual threshold is ||x+eta g(x)-y||<=(1-eta)epsilon. The terminal successful gradient/residual test is counted. N(y)+1 is a finite successful execution certificate with no claim of free precomputation of the fuel/count.\nFor those same p,q,N and full input w=((X,P),((zeta0,zeta1),(G0,G1))), define the approximate phase by\n  P0(w)=a P+sigma zeta0,\n  Y0_i(w)=X+t_i P0(w),\n  Z0_j(w)=grad V(q(Y0_j(w))+sqrt(eta)G0_j),\n  Y1_i(w)=Y0_i(w)-sum_j omega_ij Z0_j(w),\n  Z1_j(w)=grad V(q(Y1_j(w))+sqrt(eta)G1_j),\n  Phi(w)=(X+h P0(w)-sum_j pw_j Z1_j(w),\n          a(P0(w)-sum_j bw_j Z1_j(w))+sigma zeta1),\n  T(w)=2J+sum_i[(N(Y0_i(w))+1)+(N(Y1_i(w))+1)] in N.\nDefine the exact implicit-map phase, using the same P0,Y0 and supplied noise coordinates, by\n  Z0p_j(w)=grad V(p(Y0_j(w))+sqrt(eta)G0_j),\n  Y1p_i(w)=Y0_i(w)-sum_j omega_ij Z0p_j(w),\n  Z1p_j(w)=grad V(p(Y1p_j(w))+sqrt(eta)G1_j),\n  Phip(w)=(X+h P0(w)-sum_j pw_j Z1p_j(w),\n           a(P0(w)-sum_j bw_j Z1p_j(w))+sigma zeta1).\nIn particular the exact second stage uses Y1p, not the approximate Y1.\nOn the state space Q=E x E use the explicit distance\n  d2((X,P),(Xprime,Pprime))=sqrt(||X-Xprime||^2+||P-Pprime||^2).\nFor real rho and measures alpha,beta on Q define the extended-nonnegative quantity\n  W_rho(alpha,beta)=[inf_{tau measure on Q x Q: tau.fst=alpha,tau.snd=beta}\n                       integral^- (ENNReal.ofReal(d2(s,sprime)))^rho dtau(s,sprime)]^(rho^(-1)).\nThis is the infimum over all coupling measures with the two specified marginals of the lower integral of the powered cost, followed by the inverse-rho real power. ENNReal.ofReal embeds max(real value,0); powers are real powers in extended nonnegative reals. The metric is the displayed square-root sum of squared position and momentum norms, not an unspecified default product norm.\nThen Phi and T are measurable, and there exist Markov kernels Kp,Kq:Q->Q with probability outputs satisfying, simultaneously,\n  Kp(s)=(z -> Phip(s,z))_* gamma for every incoming s,\n  Kq(s)=(z -> Phi(s,z))_* gamma for every incoming s,\n  phaseQuery(grad V,eta,epsilon,h,t,omega,pw,bw,(y -> N(y)+1),w)\n    =some(Phi(w),T(w)) for every full input w,\n  w -> (T(w):R) is Bochner integrable under mu,\n  integral (T(w):R) dmu(w) <= (J:R)(2+L0+L1),\n  W_rho(Kq(s),Kp(s)) <= ENNReal.ofReal(3 h Lambda epsilon)\n    for every incoming state s and every real rho>=1.\nphaseQuery is the supplied Option-valued finite program. In increasing Fin(J) order it executes the same proximalQuery at each first-stage Y0_i with fuel(Y0_i), anchor and initial iterate Y0_i. It maps a successful point/count (x,n) to (grad V(x+sqrt(eta)G0_i),n+1), collecting these actual gradient values and adding counts. Any failure returns none. It constructs second-stage inputs using the actually returned first gradients, then repeats the corresponding second array with G1, charging one shifted-gradient evaluation in addition to each actual proximal count. It returns the resulting full position/momentum state and the sum of both array counts. Thus T counts every actual interpreter evaluation, including each terminal successful test, plus one additional shifted-gradient query per node per stage, totaling an overhead of exactly 2J. No desired output or count is given as input to phaseQuery.\nAll sums range over the J actual nodes. The integral count bound uses precisely mu=nu x gamma, whereas the transport bound compares both one-step output laws at the same arbitrary incoming state and holds for every real rho>=1, including noninteger exponents with no upper restriction. These conclusions share the same witnesses p,q,N and kernels Kp,Kq. No assertion is made about an optimal coupling being attained, a free fuel-selection algorithm, elapsed time, sampler invariance or long-run convergence, comparison with a continuous-time dynamics, or the growth of Lambda.",
