      "original_theorem": "Faithful scoped source restatement:\nJ is an integer >=2 and h is a positive real duration. Fin J uses zero-based indices; source indices are1,...,J.\nt_j=(h/2)(1-cos(j*pi/(J-1))), ell_j is the actual Lagrange cardinal polynomial on all these nodes. All j in Fin J are quantified, including both endpoints.\nWeights are actual interval integrals against ordinary real Lebesgue measure. Polynomials are continuous and hence integrable on the finite intervals; these facts are proved, never supplied as premises.\nFor every integer J>=2, every real h>0 and every j in Fin J, with t_i=(h/2)(1-cos(i*pi/(J-1))) and ell_j=Lagrange.basis univ t j, the actual momentum weight omega_j=integral_0^h ell_j(s) ds is nonnegative.\nOnly the momentum-integral positivity clause of Proposition B.1(B2). Constant exactness/sum omega=h and absolute integrated row bounds are earlier separate compiled results. The logarithmic Lebesgue constant and Banach interpolation error, actual smoothed Hessian/averaged H producer, discrete K expansion and contraction, stochastic Khat/Kbar, repeated history, both complete main results and composition remain independent. No probability cost, invariance or kernel guarantee follows from this scalar quadrature edge. Private implementation helpers share whole-module review and receive no separate proof credit.",
      "reconstructed_theorem": "For every natural number J>=2 and every real h>0, take the J zero-based indices Fin(J)={0,...,J-1}. Define\n  t_i=(h/2)(1-cos((i/(J-1))pi))\nfor each i in Fin(J), where i and J are cast exactly to real numbers and the denominator J-1 is real subtraction after casting J. For each j in Fin(J), let ell_j be the actual real Lagrange cardinal polynomial for these nodes,\n  ell_j(X)=product_{i in Fin(J),i!=j} (X-t_i)/(t_j-t_i).\nThen for every j in Fin(J),\n  0 <= integral_0^h ell_j(s) ds.\nThe integral is the oriented interval integral against ordinary real Lebesgue measure; because h>0 it has the usual increasing endpoints 0 and h. It is not a probability integral or a trigonometric weighted integral. Polynomial continuity supplies integrability internally, rather than through an extra hypothesis. The conclusion includes both endpoint indices j=0 and j=J-1, as well as every interior index when present.\nThe assertion is nonnegativity of each signed basis integral. It does not assert strict positivity of the integral, pointwise nonnegativity of ell_j(s), a normalization identity for the total weights, a bound on interpolation growth, or any probability-kernel or algorithmic-cost result. No additional cardinality, positivity-of-coefficients or integrator premise is supplied.",
