      "subtitle": "companion:sphmc-proximal-execution-identity · equivalent-after-elaboration",
      "summary": "Blind reconstruction and seven-slot semantic comparison for AutoSamplingTheory.ExampleCases.SmoothedPicardHMC.ProximalExecutionIdentity.successful_query_unique.",
      "column": 7,
      "url": "lean-foundations.html?view=semantic",
      "source_url": "https://arxiv.org/html/2609.06906v1#A4.SS2",
      "original_theorem": "E is a real normed vector space; g:E->E, eta,eps,y and initial state x are arbitrary. Two supplied finite fuels n,m yield successful returned pairs r,s under the same deterministic interpreter. No positivity, contractivity or termination hypothesis.\nFor the deterministic stopped gradient interpreter of Algorithm D.2, any two successful finite-fuel executions from the same y and initial x, using the same g,eta,eps, return exactly the same pair (output point, actual gradient-test count), independently of the sufficient fuels.",
      "reconstructed_theorem": "Let E be any real normed vector space. Fix any function g:E->E, any real eta and epsilon, any anchor y in E, and any starting point x in E. Let n,m be natural numbers and let r,s be pairs in E x N. Suppose\n  run(g,eta,epsilon,y,n,x)=some(r),\n  run(g,eta,epsilon,y,m,x)=some(s).\nThen r=s: both the returned point and the actual reported evaluation count are identical.\nThe finite exact-real interpreter returns none when its fuel is zero. With positive fuel, it evaluates g(x), sets next=y-eta g(x), and returns (x,1) if ||next-x||<=(1-eta)epsilon. Otherwise it recurses from next with fuel reduced by one, adding one to a successful returned count. The count includes the final successful evaluation. The theorem says that any two successful executions with the same g,eta,epsilon,y,x agree regardless of their possibly different fuel budgets. It imposes no positivity condition on eta or epsilon, no continuity or measurability condition on g, and no inner-product, completeness, finite-dimensionality, or probability-space assumptions. It asserts neither that an execution succeeds nor that n=m; surplus fuel may differ while the successful returned pair agrees.",
      "fidelity_verdict": "equivalent-after-elaboration",
      "blindness": "source hidden from decoder",
