asses. same zero-fuel cases contradict success. for positive fuels the same deterministic residual predicate is used even if eta or eps is nonpositive, so no analytic parameter restriction is necessary. conclusion exact equality of returned point and actual gradient-test count. r=s as pairs in e x nat. same successful stopping branches give the identical pair (x,1). nonstopping branches force successful recursive pairs via option.map_eq_some_iff; induction equates them and congrarg applies the identical count increment. this proves the entire mathematical target. scopes a reusable conditional execution identity extracted to join actual same-witness consumers; no independent paper-progress credit. only successful pair identity, with no termination, proximal error, gaussian law, cost estimate or main theorem. equivalent the current lesson and module explicitly state this boundary. the latest authored lean_statement says no probability law occurs, and lean_proof describes exactly fuel induction. the three displayed proof steps match the whole module. constant dependencies no estimate or numerical constant; all shared interpreter parameters remain fixed between the two runs. no bound or constant conclusion. same the proof compares exact deterministic branches. count increment one is implementation semantics, not a complexity bound. the source routine is recovered by g=gradient v and starting x=y, but those specializations are unnecessary for this identity. scopes informational the formal result is an explicitly scoped deterministic implementation identity, generalized to arbitrary normed real e and arbitrary g/parameters. it does not separately reproduce a named source theorem or receive additional paper-progress credit. the proof uses only exact successful-run equations; current lesson scope retains all termination, accuracy, gaussian and cost obligations separately.",
