    {
      "id": "semantic-audit:ASTIS-RT-20261005-SPHMCProximalPhaseStability",
      "kind": "semantic-audit",
      "label": "ASTIS-RT-20261005-SPHMCProximalPhaseStability",
      "status": "fidelity-exact",
      "subtitle": "companion:sphmc-proximal-phase-stability · equivalent-after-elaboration",
      "summary": "Blind reconstruction and seven-slot semantic comparison for AutoSamplingTheory.ExampleCases.SmoothedPicardHMC.ProximalPhaseStability.source_phase_proximal_stability.",
      "column": 7,
      "url": "lean-foundations.html?view=semantic",
      "source_url": "https://arxiv.org/html/2609.06906v1#A4.SS1",
      "original_theorem": "Faithful scoped source restatement: E is a complete finite-dimensional real inner-product space with its Borel sigma algebra; dimension zero is allowed. V:E→R is genuinely C2, kappa>=1 and kappa^-1||v||²<=D²V(x)[v,v]<=||v||² for every x,v (normalized beta=1).\n0<eta<1, eps>0 for the actual stopped approximate program. J>=2, h>0; the actual nodes, cardinal polynomials, Lambda, omega and b are defined as in the coefficient prerequisite. Position weights are the last omega row, whose node equals h. The sole step condition is h² Lambda<=1.\nThe abstract comparison oracle r is Borel measurable and uniformly satisfies ||r(y)-p(y)||<=delta for every y, with delta>=0 including0. No Lipschitz assumption on r, no state moment or integrability budget, no b positivity, no target-law approximation premise.\nConstruct measurable exact p, actual stopped q and natural N for full0<eta<1, with p+eta gradientV(p)=id, ||q-p||<=eps and proximalQuery(g,eta,eps,y,N(y)+1,y)=some(q(y),N(y)+1). The exact p is nonexpansive. Construct Markov Kp and Kq as the full two-layer source Gaussian pushforwards. For every deterministic phase state s and finite real rho>=1, the Euclidean Wasserstein cost W_rho(Kq(s),Kp(s))<=3h Lambda eps. Moreover every measurable delta-accurate r has its true pushforward Markov Kr with W_rho(Kr(s),Kp(s))<=3h Lambda delta for all s and rho>=1, includingdelta0. Here W_rho is the 1/rho power of the infimum coupling integral of [sqrt(||DX||²+||DP||²)]^rho. It is not the default max-norm product metric.\nAppendix D1/D2 one-phase proximal implementation stability only, explicit universal constant3. Both compared phases use the exact same two stochastic-gradient Gaussian arrays and two half-refresh Gaussian vectors; the exact phase uses exact proximal p, not a deterministic smoothed force. N(y)+1 is a noncomputable successful execution certificate, not free runtime fuel determination or expected-work evidence. Repeated-phase D3, weighted contraction, numerical D7/D8, invariant law, bias/concentration, Wp/proxy-warmness/init/PBPS/composition and both main results remain separate. No TV-to-unbounded-cost transfer.",
      "reconstructed_theorem": "For every complete finite-dimensional real inner-product space E with its Borel measurable structure, consider a twice continuously Frechet differentiable V:E->R and a nonnegative real kappa>=1 such that, for all x,v in E,\n  kappa^(-1)||v||^2 <= D^2 V(x)[v,v] <= ||v||^2.\nChoose eta,epsilon in R with 0<eta<1 and epsilon>0, J in N with J>=2, and h in R with h>0. Index exactly J nodes by Fin(J)={0,...,J-1}. Define\n  t_i=(h/2)(1-cos((i/(J-1))pi)),\n  ell_j(X)=product_{k in Fin(J),k!=j} (t_j-t_k)^(-1)(X-t_k),\n  Lambda=sup{sum_j |ell_j(s)| : s in [0,h]},\n  omega_ij=integral_0^{t_i} (t_i-s)ell_j(s) ds,\n  pw_j=omega_{J-1,j},\n  bw_j=integral_0^h ell_j(s) ds.\nThe node quotient is real, the ell_j are real polynomials, Lambda is the real supremum of the displayed closed-interval image, and the oriented interval integrals use real Lebesgue measure. Assume additionally the exact smallness inequality h^2 Lambda<=1.\nThen there exist measurable p,q:E->E and N:E->N satisfying, simultaneously for every y in E,\n  p(y)+eta grad V(p(y))=y,\n  ||q(y)-p(y)||<=epsilon,\n  proximalQuery(grad V,eta,epsilon,y,N(y)+1,y)=some(q(y),N(y)+1),\nand p is nonexpansive: ||p(u)-p(v)||<=||u-v|| for every u,v in E.\nThe actual interpreter proximalQuery(g,eta,epsilon,y,fuel,x) fails with none at zero fuel. At fuel n+1 it evaluates g(x), sets z=y-eta g(x), and tests\n  ||z-x|| <= (1-eta)epsilon.\nIf this test passes, it returns some(x,1); otherwise it recursively calls the same interpreter with anchor y, iterate z and fuel n, propagates failure and adds one to a successful returned count. Equivalently it compares ||x+eta g(x)-y|| to (1-eta)epsilon. The successful terminal gradient/residual test is counted. The value N(y)+1 in the theorem is an adequate finite execution certificate and its exact reported count; it is not a promise that the count certificate is computed before the run without cost.\nLet G be the centered identity-covariance Gaussian probability measure on E and define\n  gamma=(G x G) x ((product_{Fin(J)} G) x (product_{Fin(J)} G)).\nAll four factors are independent: two Gaussian vectors and two arrays with J independent Gaussian vector coordinates each. For f:E->E and w=((X,P),((zeta0,zeta1),(G0,G1))), define the phase map by\n  a=exp(-h/2), sigma=sqrt(1-exp(-h)),\n  P0=a P+sigma zeta0,\n  Y0_i=X+t_i P0,\n  Z0_j=grad V(f(Y0_j)+sqrt(eta)G0_j),\n  Y1_i=Y0_i-sum_j omega_ij Z0_j,\n  Z1_j=grad V(f(Y1_j)+sqrt(eta)G1_j),\n  Phi_f(w)=(X+h P0-sum_j pw_j Z1_j,\n            a(P0-sum_j bw_j Z1_j)+sigma zeta1).\nAll sums use the entire Fin(J) index set. On the state space S=E x E use the explicit distance\n  d2((X,P),(Xprime,Pprime))=sqrt(||X-Xprime||^2+||P-Pprime||^2).\nThis is the stated square-root sum-of-squares distance, without identifying it with the default product-space norm. For real rho and measures mu,nu on S, define\n  W_rho(mu,nu)=[inf_{tau measure on S x S: tau.fst=mu,tau.snd=nu}\n                  integral^- (ENNReal.ofReal(d2(s,sprime)))^rho dtau(s,sprime)]^(rho^(-1)).\nHere the integral is the lower integral, the infimum is over all coupling measures with precisely those marginals, and the cost and root powers are real powers in extended nonnegative reals. ENNReal.ofReal is the extended-nonnegative embedding of max(value,0).\nThere exist Markov kernels Kp,Kq:S->S with probability outputs such that, for every incoming state s,\n  Kp(s)=(z -> Phi_p(s,z))_* gamma,\n  Kq(s)=(z -> Phi_q(s,z))_* gamma,\nand, for every incoming state s and every real rho>=1,\n  W_rho(Kq(s),Kp(s)) <= ENNReal.ofReal(3 h Lambda epsilon).\nThe exponent rho is an arbitrary real at least one, including noninteger values and with no upper bound.\nFor the same chosen p and Kp there is also this general perturbation conclusion: for every measurable r:E->E, every real delta>=0 and every uniform approximation condition ||r(y)-p(y)||<=delta for all y, there exists a Markov kernel Kr:S->S with probability outputs satisfying for every state s\n  Kr(s)=(z -> Phi_r(s,z))_* gamma,\nand for every state s and every real rho>=1,\n  W_rho(Kr(s),Kp(s)) <= ENNReal.ofReal(3 h Lambda delta).\nEach kernel works simultaneously for all states and all specified exponents. The comparison uses the same incoming state on both sides. No input state distribution or finite-moment assumption is part of this theorem. Its conclusions do not assert optimal-coupling attainment, sampler invariance or convergence, a contraction between distinct incoming states, a total phase-evaluation bound, or machine-precision behavior.",
      "fidelity_verdict": "equivalent-after-elaboration",
