interpreter with anchor y, iterate z and fuel n, propagates failure and adds one to a successful returned count. equivalently it compares ||x+eta g(x)-y|| to (1-eta)epsilon. the successful terminal gradient/residual test is counted. the value n(y)+1 in the theorem is an adequate finite execution certificate and its exact reported count; it is not a promise that the count certificate is computed before the run without cost. let g be the centered identity-covariance gaussian probability measure on e and define gamma=(g x g) x ((product_{fin(j)} g) x (product_{fin(j)} g)). all four factors are independent: two gaussian vectors and two arrays with j independent gaussian vector coordinates each. for f:e->e and w=((x,p),((zeta0,zeta1),(g0,g1))), define the phase map by a=exp(-h/2), sigma=sqrt(1-exp(-h)), p0=a p+sigma zeta0, y0_i=x+t_i p0, z0_j=grad v(f(y0_j)+sqrt(eta)g0_j), y1_i=y0_i-sum_j omega_ij z0_j, z1_j=grad v(f(y1_j)+sqrt(eta)g1_j), phi_f(w)=(x+h p0-sum_j pw_j z1_j, a(p0-sum_j bw_j z1_j)+sigma zeta1). all sums use the entire fin(j) index set. on the state space s=e x e use the explicit distance d2((x,p),(xprime,pprime))=sqrt(||x-xprime||^2+||p-pprime||^2). this is the stated square-root sum-of-squares distance, without identifying it with the default product-space norm. for real rho and measures mu,nu on s, define w_rho(mu,nu)=[inf_{tau measure on s x s: tau.fst=mu,tau.snd=nu} integral^- (ennreal.ofreal(d2(s,sprime)))^rho dtau(s,sprime)]^(rho^(-1)). here the integral is the lower integral, the infimum is over all coupling measures with precisely those marginals, and the cost and root powers are real powers in extended nonnegative reals. ennreal.ofreal is the extended-nonnegative embedding of max(value,0). there exist markov kernels kp,kq:s->s with probability outputs such that, for every incoming state s, kp(s)=(z -> phi_p(s,z))_* gamma, kq(s)=(z -> phi_q(s,z))_* gamma, and, for every incoming state s and every real rho>=1, w_rho(kq(s),kp(s)) <= ennreal.ofreal(3 h lambda epsilon). the exponent rho is an arbitrary real at least one, including noninteger values and with no upper bound. for the same chosen p and kp there is also this general perturbation conclusion: for every measurable r:e->e, every real delta>=0 and every uniform approximation condition ||r(y)-p(y)||<=delta for all y, there exists a markov kernel kr:s->s with probability outputs satisfying for every state s kr(s)=(z -> phi_r(s,z))_* gamma, and for every state s and every real rho>=1, w_rho(kr(s),kp(s)) <= ennreal.ofreal(3 h lambda delta). each kernel works simultaneously for all states and all specified exponents. the comparison uses the same incoming state on both sides. no input state distribution or finite-moment assumption is part of this theorem. its conclusions do not assert optimal-coupling attainment, sampler invariance or convergence, a contraction between distinct incoming states, a total phase-evaluation bound, or machine-precision behavior. accepted d1/d2 html2099-2124; source algorithm3.1, b1 actual coefficient definitions1684-1713; proximal nonexpansiveness lemma4.2(4.4) autosamplingtheory.examplecases.smoothedpicardhmc.proximalphasestability.source_phase_proximal_stability autosamplingtheory/examplecases/smoothedpicardhmc/proximalphasestability.lean true root-samplinglib-writer anonymous_decoder_20261005 60fa2897c1cfc74fcfae69c118d9ec35b2b5c96171244342c98d71c9425af778 accepted phase_source_reviewer_20261005 objects exact and uniformly accurate measurable proximal centers inserted into the two stochastic-gradient layers of algorithm 3.1, using the actual chebyshev-lobatto integrated weights. constructed p,q,n and kp,kq; arbitrary measurable r produces kr. the explicit phase has both refresh vectors and both fin j gaussian arrays. all kernels are full phase pushforwards. explicit-elaboration current statement and phasemap lines18-29, kernel_from_phase lines161-174, main construction lines273-327; anonymous reconstruction specifies exactly these ob