turns (x,1) when ||next-x||<=(1-eta)epsilon; otherwise it recurses from next with fuel decreased by one and adds one to a successful returned query count. arithmetic is exact real arithmetic. consequently n(y)+1 is the reported number of gradient tests, including the final successful test, for this successful run initialized at y. for every measurable y:a->e such that omega -> ||grad v(y(omega))||^2 is integrable with respect to mu, the real-valued test count omega -> n(y(omega))+1 is integrable with respect to mu, and integral_a (n(y(omega))+1) dmu(omega) <= [2+(1+log((1-c)^(-1)))/(-log c)] [1+log(1+sqrt(integral_a ||grad v(y(omega))||^2 dmu(omega))/epsilon)]. natural counts in this formula are cast to the reals. integrability is bochner integrability. the logarithm is the real logarithm and the square root is the real square root. the conclusions concern the integrability and expected count of the stipulated successful exact-arithmetic interpreter; no existence of q or n, explicit pointwise complexity bound, output-error guarantee, sampling-kernel property, or convergence statement is asserted by this theorem. source-reviewed algorithm d.2, lemma d.3 (d.6), and lemma d.4 jensen step after (d.7), html lines 2138-2186 autosamplingtheory.examplecases.smoothedpicardhmc.realizedproximalwork.realized_proximal_expected_work autosamplingtheory/examplecases/smoothedpicardhmc/realizedproximalwork.lean true root-samplinglib-writer anonymous_decoder_20261005 c0e81559f50c4ea5ba808096b02d1c1b668246457416d139a252ab8fb1faa247 accepted phase_source_reviewer_20261005 objects supplied q,n with successful residual-stopped proximal execution; actual center y, its probability law and square-gradient moment; the same successful query count n(y)+1. the identical supplied output/count functions and exact-real interpreter, probability input and integrable realized squared gradient, yielding integrability and expected count. equivalent the interpreter returns the current stopped point and includes the successful final gradient evaluation. the current child invokes the imported proximalexecutionidentity.successful_query_unique to compare the entire pair with the jensen parent witness, then extracts equality of counts. no different existential count substitutes for n. domains finite-dimensional real inner-product borel e, arbitrary measurable probability input space, and a measurable e-valued center. paper centers are euclidean sampler inputs. exactly these e typeclasses and an arbitrary measurable alpha carrying probability mu; natural-valued count and real-valued bochner integrals. explicit-elaboration the source component explicitly admits the coordinate-free and arbitrary probability-space extension. no standard-borel or topological condition on alpha is used: mu.map y is a probability measure because y is measurable. this does not construct the paper repeated-run law. quantifiers every supplied successful q,n at all inputs obeys the bound for every measurable y with the stated moment, for fixed admissible v and parameters. universal supplied q,n and forall-y execution certificate, followed by supplied mu,y,moment and the two work conclusions. same the imported identity proof quantifies over arbitrary g, eta, eps, common center and initial state and any two sufficient fuels. both successes are premises; zero fuel cannot satisfy them. at each y it gives n(y)+1=m(y)+1, hence n=m by natural cancellation. the target does not export existence of a new count. assumptions c2 normalized hessian bounds, kappa>=1, 0<eta<=c<1, eps>0; the actual run equation at every center, probability mu, measurable y and integrable squared gradient. no supplied count measurability, integrability or work bound. exactly those assumptions, including full pointwise run certificate and actual-center squared-gradient integrability. same the explicit moment is the remaining d.7 interface dependency; the publication only claims the implementation/jensen component. the imported execution identity needs no extra assumptions on g or eta and has no termination premise beyond the two successful run equations. equality with the measurable parent count derives measurability/integrability for the supplied count. conclusion integrability of the same actual count and the displayed explicit c_c logarithmic expected-work estimate under its realized law. identical integrable and expectation inequality, with n cast to reals and exact same moment, eps and coefficient. equivalent the child transfers the gradient-square moment to nu=mu.map y, uses the jensen parent under nu, identifies n=m by successful pair uniqueness, then uses integrable_map_measure and integral_map for both count and moment. the parent obtains l1 from l2, proves log(1+g/eps) integrable by domination, applies concave-log jensen and e g<=sqrt(e g^2). no integral is interpreted through an unproved integrability assumption. scopes scoped algorithm d.2/d.3 plus the first d.4 jensen inequality, leaving d.7 and d.8 separate. successful-fuel identity is its explicit implementation mechanism. expected work for the supplied successful exact-arithmetic routine under a measurable input with integrable squared gradient; no sampling convergence, run-wide moment or total-cost conclusion. equivalent the current module introduction, lesson scope and obligations all retain d.7, phase/run aggregation, parameter substitution and d.8. the authored first proof step and astis_dependencies identify the extracted execution-identity theorem; the same mathematical induction is valid there. the bound concerns exactly the pushforward of the supplied y and uses no tv or change-of-law estimate. constant dependencies c_c=2+(1+log((1-c)^(-1)))/(-log c), only c in the multiplicative coefficient; eps and actual gradient-square expectation in the logarithm. precisely that coefficient and dependence, including n+1 rather than n. explicit-elaboration the source uses an unspecified c_c0. the local execution/jensen parent proves