    {
      "id": "semantic-audit:ASTIS-RT-20261006-FullRangeProximalGaussianOracle",
      "kind": "semantic-audit",
      "label": "ASTIS-RT-20261006-FullRangeProximalGaussianOracle",
      "status": "fidelity-exact",
      "subtitle": "companion:sphmc-full-range-proximal-gaussian-oracle · equivalent-after-elaboration",
      "summary": "Blind reconstruction and seven-slot semantic comparison for AutoSamplingTheory.ExampleCases.SmoothedPicardHMC.FullRangeProximalGaussianOracle.full_range_proximal_gaussian_oracle.",
      "column": 7,
      "url": "lean-foundations.html?view=semantic",
      "source_url": "https://arxiv.org/html/2609.06906v1#S4.Thmtheorem2",
      "original_theorem": "Exact independently sealed bounded source statement:\nSource beta=1, alpha=kappa^-1, real kappa>=1; actual C2 V with kappa^-1*normv^2 <= D2V(x)[v,v] <= normv^2 for every x,v.\nFinite real complete Hilbert/Borel full space E, including dimension zero; arbitrary measurable parameter space S; actual measurable eta:S->R and y:S->E with 0<eta(s)<=1 for every s. These coordinate-free/parameter-space extensions are disclosed.\nNo supplied proximal point, minimizer, measurable selector, gradient estimate, Lipschitz bound or Markov kernel. All are produced from the literal potential and actual Gaussian pushforward. The source numerical-prox call cap eta<=1/(2beta) remains a separate computational contract.\nProduce a measurable exact proximal selector p satisfying p+eta*gradientV(p)=y, the genuine quantitative quadratic minimum and uniqueness, and fixed-eta nonexpansivity. The literal Gaussian gradient evaluator G(s,z)=gradientV(p(s)+sqrt(eta(s))*z) is jointly measurable and obeys input/noise Lipschitz constants 1 and sqrteta for equal eta. Produce an actual Markov kernel K(s)=stdGaussian.map(G(s,-)). This covers the exact evaluator (3.2) and only Lemma4.2 (4.4)-(4.5), with no posterior or finite-query claim.\nSource beta1 alpha=kappainv kappa>=1 and0<eta<=1, genuine C2/global allpoint-allvector Hessian. Full-space finite real Hilbert/Borel incl0 and arbitrary measurable parameter spaces are disclosed representation extensions. Actual selector/gradient/kernel witnesses are outputs, never input certificates. Exact Gaussian gradient-output kernel is not RGO posterior or HMC K/Khat/Kbar. Old eta<=1/2 construct remains preserved. Numericalprox calls sourceeta<=1/(2beta) do not grant fullrange finite-query work. No standardized affine posterior law or position moment, history-integrated moment, bias4.2/GaussianMGF4.3/fullLemma4.2/Wp/higher smoothing/sampler/initialization/main/querycost/composition. TV never transfers unbounded cost.",
      "reconstructed_theorem": "Let E be a complete finite-dimensional real inner-product space with its Borel measurable structure, and let S be an arbitrary measurable space. Dimension zero for E is allowed; there is no Nontrivial or nonempty-S assumption. Let V:E->R and kappa:R satisfy kappa>=1, V is twice continuously Frechet differentiable, and for every x,v in E,\n  kappa^(-1)*||v||^2 <= (fderiv R (fderiv R V) x v) v <= ||v||^2.\nLet eta:S->R and y:S->E be measurable, and assume for every s in S that 0<eta(s)<=1. The norm is the given real Hilbert norm and grad V is the genuine Riesz representative of the Frechet derivative.\nDefine, for every s in S and x in E,\n  F(s,x)=V(x)+(eta(s))^(-1)/2*||x-y(s)||^2.\nThen there exists a single map p:S->E such that all the following hold simultaneously:\n(1) p is measurable.\n(2) For every s in S,\n  p(s)+eta(s)*grad V(p(s))=y(s),\nwhere the product eta(s)*grad V is real scalar multiplication in E.\n(3) For every s in S and z in E, both\n  F(s,p(s))+(kappa^(-1)+(eta(s))^(-1))/2*||z-p(s)||^2 <= F(s,z),\nand\n  (F(s,z)<=F(s,p(s))) if and only if z=p(s).\nThus the displayed map is the actual unique global minimizer in each fiber, with the stated strictly positive quadratic growth coefficient; these are conclusions, not supplied minimizer certificates.\n(4) For every s,t in S, if eta(s)=eta(t), then\n  ||p(s)-p(t)|| <= ||y(s)-y(t)||.\nFor this same p define the literal map G:S x E->E by\n  G(s,z)=grad V(p(s)+sqrt(eta(s))*z),\nwith real square root and real scalar multiplication. Then additionally:\n(5) G is measurable on the product measurable space S x E.\n(6) For every s,t in S and z,w in E, if eta(s)=eta(t), then\n  ||G(s,z)-G(t,w)|| <= ||y(s)-y(t)||+sqrt(eta(s))*||z-w||.\n(7) There exists a measurable kernel K:S->Measure(E) which is a Markov kernel (every K(s) is a probability measure), and for every s in S,\n  K(s)=(stdGaussian E).map(z -> G(s,z)).\nHere stdGaussian E is the actual canonical centered covariance-identity Gaussian law in the given finite real Hilbert metric, and map is its literal measurable pushforward. Thus K is the law of the gradient evaluated at p(s)+sqrt(eta(s))*Z for canonical Gaussian Z. It is not asserted to be Gaussian itself, a posterior sampling law, or another transition kernel.\nThe order of quantifiers is: arbitrary E,S,V,kappa,eta,y satisfying the stated premises; one measurable p; all optimality/growth/uniqueness and equal-step comparison claims for that p; the let-defined G using that p; measurable G and all equal-step comparison claims; one Markov K with exact law for every s. No probability law on S, independent sampling sequence, joint continuity in eta, comparison between unequal eta values, posterior identity, approximation/error estimate, query count, or whole-algorithm conclusion is present. The coefficients are exactly 1/2 on each quadratic term, 1 on the center-distance bound, and sqrt(eta(s)) on the noise-distance bound. The upper curvature bound 1 and eta cap 1 remain explicit premises even where a particular conclusion could have a wider validity range.",
