z,w in e, if eta(s)=eta(t), then ||g(s,z)-g(t,w)|| <= ||y(s)-y(t)||+sqrt(eta(s))*||z-w||. (7) there exists a measurable kernel k:s->measure(e) which is a markov kernel (every k(s) is a probability measure), and for every s in s, k(s)=(stdgaussian e).map(z -> g(s,z)). here stdgaussian e is the actual canonical centered covariance-identity gaussian law in the given finite real hilbert metric, and map is its literal measurable pushforward. thus k is the law of the gradient evaluated at p(s)+sqrt(eta(s))*z for canonical gaussian z. it is not asserted to be gaussian itself, a posterior sampling law, or another transition kernel. the order of quantifiers is: arbitrary e,s,v,kappa,eta,y satisfying the stated premises; one measurable p; all optimality/growth/uniqueness and equal-step comparison claims for that p; the let-defined g using that p; measurable g and all equal-step comparison claims; one markov k with exact law for every s. no probability law on s, independent sampling sequence, joint continuity in eta, comparison between unequal eta values, posterior identity, approximation/error estimate, query count, or whole-algorithm conclusion is present. the coefficients are exactly 1/2 on each quadratic term, 1 on the center-distance bound, and sqrt(eta(s)) on the noise-distance bound. the upper curvature bound 1 and eta cap 1 remain explicit premises even where a particular conclusion could have a wider validity range. accepted sphmc arxiv:2609.06906v1 standing s1.e1/s2.p1.1, actual proximal argmin2.1 s2.e1/s4.ex3, exact gaussian gradient estimator3.2 s3.e2, proximal optimalitys4.ss1.p4.1, only input/noise lemma4.2 equations4.4/4.5 s4.e4/e5 and scoped author argument s4.ss1.p5.2; numerical-oracle cap s2.p2.2 excluded. autosamplingtheory.examplecases.smoothedpicardhmc.fullrangeproximalgaussianoracle.full_range_proximal_gaussian_oracle autosamplingtheory/examplecases/smoothedpicardhmc/fullrangeproximalgaussianoracle.lean true root-samplinglib-writer anonymous_decoder_20261