      "source_url": "https://arxiv.org/html/2609.06906v1#S4.SS1",
      "original_theorem": "Attributed equation4.1 restatement:\nE is a finite-dimensional real inner-product space with its Borel measurable space, including dimension zero. mu is an arbitrary probability measure; eta>0 and C>0 are real numbers. No input density, finite input moments, input potential, smoothness, curvature, normalizer or conditional-version certificate is assumed.\nZ(y)=integral exp(-norm(y-x)^2/(2eta))dmu(x), R_y=mu.tilted(-norm(x-y)^2/(2eta)), U(y)=-log(C Z(y)). C is an arbitrary positive constant prefactor; choosing the actual Gaussian C_eta gives its negative-log probability density. Other C values are not asserted to normalize a density.\nR_y is the actual everywhere-selected posterior of the Gaussian augmentation, previously identified as a measurable Markov/disintegration kernel. Probability and finite second moments are derived here; mean is the Bochner integral, covarianceBilin is the true centered bilinear covariance. The new theorem concerns this selected version, never an arbitrary conditional representative.\nFor the source Gibbs input, the actual source unnormalized potential V_eta differs from the normalized density potential U_eta by the already-proved constant log Z_V. The genuine Test derives original Gibbs probability from C2 and positive lower Hessian, proves source V_eta directional derivative/covariance identities by this exact constant, and joins the same augmentation backward kernel. Source Hessian upper bound and eta upper cap are unnecessary for these identities.\nAt every observation y, R_y is a probability measure and the identity map belongs to L2(R_y). For every direction v, DU(y)[v]=inner(y-mean(R_y),v)/eta. For every v,w, D2U(y)[v,w]=inner(v,w)/eta-covarianceBilin(R_y)(v,w)/eta^2. These are actual first and second Frechet derivatives and actual finite posterior moments; the constant positive prefactor cancels exactly.\nActual everywhere-selected quadratic Gaussian posterior has probability and finite second moments, without input moments; true first/second derivatives of negative log of the Gaussian normalizer equal posterior mean and covariance, including the positive constant prefactor and dimension zero. No Brascamp-Lieb/Cramer-Rao covariance inequalities, source Hessian bounds/higher cumulants, stationarity/algorithm accuracy/history/expected work, PBPS process or either complete main/composition. TV never transfers unbounded expected cost.",
      "reconstructed_theorem": "Let E be any finite-dimensional real inner-product space with its Borel measurable structure,including dimension zero. Let mu be any probability measure on E,and let eta,C be strictly positive real numbers. C is a scalar constant,independent of y,and is not required to be a Gaussian density prefactor. Define the actual measures and real function\n  R(y)=mu.tilted(x -> -||x-y||^2/(2eta)),\n  U(y)=-log(C integral_E exp(-||y-x||^2/(2eta)) dmu(x)).\nThe exponential tilt is normalized against its base law mu:for fixed y its density relative to mu is\n  exp(-||x-y||^2/(2eta)) /\n    integral_E exp(-||xprime-y||^2/(2eta)) dmu(xprime).\nAll real/vector integrals are Bochner integrals. For each y write m_y=integral_E x dR(y)(x),an E-valued mean. Then the theorem consists of these three grouped conclusions:\n  1. For every y in E,R(y) is a probability measure and the E-valued identity x->x belongs to L2(R(y)) in the MemLp sense:it is measurable in the appropriate almost-everywhere strong sense and has finite second moment under that actual tilted law.\n  2. For every y,v in E,the genuine Frechet-derivative API satisfies\n       D U(y)[v]=<y-m_y,v>/eta.\n  3. For every y,v,w in E,the nested Frechet derivative,first differentiating the continuous-linear-map-valued function y->D U(y),then applying directions v and w,satisfies\n       D(D U)(y)[v][w]=<v,w>/eta-CovBilin(R(y))(v,w)/eta^2.\nHere D U(y) has type E->continuous-linear[R]R,and D(D U)(y) has type E->continuous-linear[R](E->continuous-linear[R]R). The covariance term is a real-valued bilinear covariance of scalar projections <v,X> and <w,X> under R(y),not a covariance matrix or an E-valued operator supplied as a premise. With the mean m_y,it is the real centered covariance\n  integral_E (<v,x>-<v,m_y>)(<w,x>-<w,m_y>) dR(y)(x).\nIts moment meaning is justified by the first grouped conclusion about the E-valued identity under the probability law R(y).\nAll y,v,w are arbitrary vectors and the derivative equalities are pointwise,not only almost everywhere. The input mu need not possess a Lebesgue density or finite moments. eta has no upper-bound restriction. No original-law L2 assumption,pre-supplied tilted moment,derivative identity,potential,Hessian hypothesis or covariance certificate is assumed. The statement does not separately assert a global ContDiff property,a Gaussian pushforward/density identity,convexity,Hessian bounds,higher smoothness or a sampling algorithm;it asserts exactly the probability/L2 and two derivative identities above.",
