he identity map belongs to l2(r_y). for every direction v, du(y)[v]=inner(y-mean(r_y),v)/eta. for every v,w, d2u(y)[v,w]=inner(v,w)/eta-covariancebilin(r_y)(v,w)/eta^2. these are actual first and second frechet derivatives and actual finite posterior moments; the constant positive prefactor cancels exactly. actual everywhere-selected quadratic gaussian posterior has probability and finite second moments, without input moments; true first/second derivatives of negative log of the gaussian normalizer equal posterior mean and covariance, including the positive constant prefactor and dimension zero. no brascamp-lieb/cramer-rao covariance inequalities, source hessian bounds/higher cumulants, stationarity/algorithm accuracy/history/expected work, pbps process or either complete main/composition. tv never transfers unbounded expected cost. let e be any finite-dimensional real inner-product space with its borel measurable structure,including dimension zero. let mu be any probability measure on e,and let eta,c be strictly positive real numbers. c is a scalar constant,independent of y,and is not required to be a gaussian density prefactor. define the actual measures and real function r(y)=mu.tilted(x -> -||x-y||^2/(2eta)), u(y)=-log(c integral_e exp(-||y-x||^2/(2eta)) dmu(x)). the exponential tilt is normalized against its base law mu:for fixed y its density relative to mu is exp(-||x-y||^2/(2eta)) / integral_e exp(-||xprime-y||^2/(2eta)) dmu(xprime). all real/vector integrals are bochner integrals. for each y write m_y=integral_e x dr(y)(x),an e-valued mean. then the theorem consists of these three grouped conclusions: 1. for every y in e,r(y) is a probability measure and the e-valued identity x->x belongs to l2(r(y)) in the memlp sense:it is measurable in the appropriate almost-everywhere strong sense and has finite second moment under that actual tilted law. 2. for every y,v in e,the genuine frechet-derivative api satisfies d u(y)[v]=<y-m_y,v>/eta. 3. for every y,v,w in e,the nested frechet derivative,first differentiating the continuous-linear-map-valued function y->d u(y),then applying directions v and w,satisfies d(d u)(y)[v][w]=<v,w>/eta-covbilin(r(y))(v,w)/eta^2. here d u(y) has type e->continuous-linear[r]r,and d(d u)(y) has type e->continuous-linear[r](e->continuous-linear[r]r). the covariance term is a real-valued bilinear covariance of scalar projections <v,x> and <w,x> under r(y),not a covariance matrix or an e-valued operator supplied as a premise. with the mean m_y,it is the real centered covariance integral_e (<v,x>-<v,m_y>)(<w,x>-<w,m_y>) dr(y)(x). its moment meaning is justified by the first grouped conclusion about the e-valued identity under the probability law r(y). all y,v,w are arbitrary vectors and the derivative equalities are pointwise,not only almost everywhere. the input mu need not possess a lebesgue density or finite moments. eta has no upper-bound restriction. no original-law l2 assumption,pre-supplied tilted moment,derivative identity,potential,hessian hypothesis or covariance certificate is assumed. the statement does not separately assert a global contdiff property,a gaussian pushforward/density identity,convexity,hessian bounds,higher smoothness or a sampling algorithm;it asserts exactly the probability/l2 and two derivative identities above. accepted arxiv:2609.06906v1 section4.1 equation4.1 html403-412; section3.1 unnormalized convolution convention; pbps2609.06905v1 section2.2 actual gaussian augmentation/selected backward quadratic tilt autosamplingtheory.technicallemmas.measure.gaussianconvolutionregularity.gaussian_convolution_derivatives autosamplingtheory/technicallemmas/measure/gaussianconvolutionregularity.lean true root-samplinglib-writer anonymous_decoder_20261006 7e933c3a2f5f1b910ab6e14132812939eddfadbee6575cb68ffbbc7b2bdcc11e accepted phase_source_reviewer_20261005 objects actual gaussian quadratic posterior r_y, normalizer z, u=-log(cz), actual bochner mean and centered covariance. finit