      "source_url": "https://arxiv.org/html/2609.06905v1#A3.SS1",
      "original_theorem": "Explicit authored prerequisite restatement:\nFull finite real Hilbert/Borel E including dimension0. Fix SAME original real weighted-L2 partial-linear gradient D with exact compact-C-infinity genuine gradient graph and closability BEFORE all queried functions or classes.\nmu is exactly volume.tilted(-W); W is C1 and exp(-W) is genuinely integrable against canonical full-space volume. Positive normalized density and both AE transfer directions come from the accepted one-way theorem.\nu is in SAME D.closure.domain. Constant-domain converse is produced, not assumed as a final kernel certificate.\nFor EVERY u in SAME original Gibbs closed-gradient domain, D.closure u=0 IFF its weighted representative is AE ONE real constant. True domain converse is proved from the new constant producer. No spectral-gap/range/Poincare claim.\nActual finite real Hilbert/Borel E including0. Fix actual finite mu and SAME original closable exact compact-C-infinity gradient D BEFORE ALL C1 f with actual MemLp f2mu and MemLp gradientf2mu. Genuine smooth cutoff exhaustion/derivative-product/weighted L2 limits put actual class pair into SAME closure graph, without compact support or extra spatial moment. Finite mu constants are genuinely in original closure domain with outputzero. For actual C1 Gibbs W/exp(-W) integrable, combine actual constants-domain with accepted one-way implication for full zero-output iff AEconstant on SAME D. Actual PBPS common Gaussian J/R/S/Sdensity/positive partitions/sourceC2 alpha beta eta cap and original D_y beforeALL constants/u/directions; real unnormalized y-score C1/L2/boundedgradient from accepted score producer transfers along ONLY explicit reflected density equality S2_y=S_y and enters SAME original closed graph. No R2=R or alternateD equality, global ordinary L1, C-infinity potential, gradient of rough representative, Nontrivial E, spatial moment, supplied domain/fullkernel/spectralgap/Poincare certificate, epsilon0/range/centering/measurablejointselector/core/BL/main/composition. TV never transfers unbounded cost.",
      "reconstructed_theorem": "Let E be the full finite-dimensional real Hilbert space with its Borel measurable structure and canonical ordinary volume, including dimension zero. All approved section instances belong to the contract. Let W:E->R be C1 and assume exp(-W) is volume-integrable. For readability write mu=volume.tilted(x->-W(x)); in the Lean signature this same measure is written directly throughout. It is the actual normalized exp(-W)/partition Gibbs measure, with the positive normalized density supplied by the approved full-space context; a separate positive-partition binder is not a displayed additional premise.\nLet D:Lp R 2 mu ->partialLinear[R] Lp E 2 mu be supplied and closable, with exactly this original graph: For every scalar class a and vector class H in these actual quotient spaces, (a,H) belongs to D.graph if and only if there exists a smooth compactly supported real function phi:E->R with a=phi almost everywhere for the stated measure and H=gradient(phi) almost everywhere for that same measure. D is the genuine real-linear partial gradient on its submodule domain; D.closure is its actual same-graph closure operator. For every supplied u in this SAME D.closure.domain,\n  D.closure(u)=0  if and only if  there exists a real c such that the scalar quotient's standard representative equals the constant function x->c mu-almost everywhere.\nThe equivalence is genuine in both directions for an already closure-domain u. D is fixed before u, and the existential c in the right-hand proposition may depend on u. The zero on the left is the actual vector L2 output, not a totalized pointwise derivative of rough u. W is only C1; no curvature/spectral-gap, mean-zero, dense-domain or rough differentiability premise is added. This statement is not an existential construction of D or a standalone separate construction of each constant's domain witness. The AE equivalence does not assert pointwise constancy, uniqueness of c, measurable parameter selection, a centered/epsilon-zero/range theorem, further functional inequality or complete adjoint/core/composition statement.",
