      "id": "semantic-audit:ASTIS-RT-20261006-GibbsGradientMoment",
      "kind": "semantic-audit",
      "label": "ASTIS-RT-20261006-GibbsGradientMoment",
      "status": "fidelity-exact",
      "subtitle": "companion:sphmc-gibbs-gradient-moment · equivalent-after-elaboration",
      "summary": "Blind reconstruction and seven-slot semantic comparison for AutoSamplingTheory.TechnicalLemmas.Analysis.GibbsGradientMoment.gibbs_gradient_moment.",
      "column": 7,
      "url": "lean-foundations.html?view=semantic",
      "source_url": "https://arxiv.org/html/2609.06906v1",
      "original_theorem": "E is a complete finite-dimensional real inner-product space with its Borel sigma-algebra and canonical Lebesgue volume. Dimension zero is included. U:E->R is twice continuously differentiable.\nThe nonnegative real parameters alpha,beta satisfy 0<alpha and alpha<=beta. At every x,v the genuine Hessian obeys alpha*norm(v)^2<=D2U(x)[v,v]<=beta*norm(v)^2. The alpha<=beta assumption remains in the public statement, despite its unused binder name.\nDefine mu=volume.tilted(-U), the actual normalized exponential Gibbs measure. For the standard orthonormal basis (e_i), define H(x)=sum_i D2U(x)[e_i,e_i]. No minimizer, normalizing constant, weighted integrability or moment bound is supplied as an assumption.\nThe actual measure mu is a probability measure. Both norm(grad U)^2 and H are integrable under mu. Their expectations are equal, and the expected squared gradient is at most beta*finrank(E). These are five public conclusions; bare exponential integrability, weighted linear integrability and directional integration by parts are proved internally.",
      "reconstructed_theorem": "For every finite-dimensional complete real inner-product space E with its Borel structure and canonical Lebesgue volume, let d=dim_R E, with no assumption d>0. For every U:E->R and nonnegative real alpha,beta, assume 0<alpha, alpha<=beta, U is globally C2, and alpha||v||^2<=D2U(x)[v,v]<=beta||v||^2 for all x,v. Put Z=integral exp(-U) against volume, a real Bochner integral with value zero if not integrable. Define mu=volume.withDensity(x -> ENNReal.ofReal(exp(-U(x))/Z)), with real division by zero equal to zero and ofReal truncating negative values to zero. This is precisely the given tilted measure. For the full standard orthonormal basis e_i indexed by Fin(d), set H(x)=sum_i D2U(x)[e_i,e_i]. Then five conclusions hold under the same mu: mu is probability; x->||grad U(x)||^2 is Bochner integrable; H is Bochner integrable; integral ||grad U||^2 dmu=integral H dmu; and this integral<=beta*d. All derivatives and gradients are actual. The numeric upper bound explicitly depends only on beta,d; alpha remains a strictly positive assumption parameter. Dimension zero uses the empty basis sum.",
      "fidelity_verdict": "equivalent-after-elaboration",
      "blindness": "source hidden from decoder",
      "semantic_slots": [
