      "original_theorem": "Explicit analytic prerequisite restatement:\nE is a finite-dimensional real inner-product space with Borel measurable structure and canonical full-space volume, including dimension zero. W is C1 and exp(-W) is genuinely integrable for volume; mu is exactly volume.tilted(-W).\nD is the same real-linear partial gradient on actual L2(mu), closable with original graph exactly compact smooth scalar representatives and their genuine gradients, identified mu-a.e. Epsilon is positive and forcing f is a genuine scalar L2(mu) class. Neither a weak solution, test-domain membership, ordinary PDE nor local-L2 certificate is supplied.\nAll ordinary weak derivatives, compact restrictions and distribution test integrals use volume; the variational identity uses actual L2(mu). The actual closed gradient G=D.closure u and the same u serve every test/compact. C2 compact tests suffice; no regularity of the selected L2 representative u is assumed.\nFor actual mu=volume.tilted(-W), the same exact closable compact smooth gradient D, every epsilon>0 and genuine L2(mu) forcing f, construct u in D.closure.domain satisfying the all-domain variational equation. Its actual G=D.closure u is the ordinary weak gradient, with locally volume-integrable u/G and every C1 compact directional identity and both integrable products. Put k=epsilon u+inner(gradient W,G)-f. Prove locally integrable squared norm of k and actual u/G/k in L2(volume.restrict K) for every compact K. For every C2 compact phi, both u Delta phi and k phi are integrable for volume and integral u Delta phi = integral k phi. This is a distribution identity, not a classical Laplacian of arbitrary u.\nSame actual D/u/G and forcing yield ordinary volume distribution Delta u=epsilon u+inner(gradient W,G)-f, all C2 compact tests with genuine L1 products, and genuine local L2 of u/G/right-hand side. Full existing variational and ordinary weak-gradient identities retained. Actual PBPS R/S/reflected law and fixed fiber D before epsilon/input. No local H2, global generator core/Bochner, Poincare/BL, centered epsilon limit, noncompact constant/linear domains, measurable y-selection, SPHMC Hessian lower/full4.1 or complete paper/main/composition. TV never transfers unbounded expected cost.",
      "reconstructed_theorem": "Let E be a finite-dimensional real inner-product space with its Borel measurable space and canonical Lebesgue measure dx; dimension zero is allowed, and the approved ambient instances are part of the contract. Let W:E->R be C1 and assume x->exp(-W(x)) is Lebesgue integrable. Define mu=volume.tilted(x->-W(x)), namely exp(-W)/integral exp(-W) times volume; positivity of its normalizing integral is not built into the definition.\nFor every real-linear partial operator D:L2(mu;R)->L2(mu;E), assume D is closable and has exactly the following graph: for every a in L2(mu;R) and H in L2(mu;E), (a,H) belongs to graph(D) if and only if there exists a smooth compactly supported phi:E->R with a=phi mu-almost everywhere and H=gradient(phi) mu-almost everywhere. The closure is the same operator defined by the topological graph closure, on its submodule domain.\nFor every real epsilon>0 and every scalar L2 input f for this same D, there exists u in domain(D.closure) satisfying the following simultaneous conclusions. Write u_rep and f_rep for the standard measurable representatives of the actual scalar L2 classes, and G_rep for the standard representative of G=D.closure(u) in the actual vector L2 quotient. In the formulas below set r(x)=epsilon*u_rep(x)+<gradient(W)(x),G_rep(x)>_E-f_rep(x); this notation abbreviates the exact displayed expression.\n(1) For every v in domain(D.closure),\n  epsilon*<u,v>_{L2(mu;R)}+<D.closure(u),D.closure(v)>_{L2(mu;E)}=<f,v>_{L2(mu;R)}.\n(2) u_rep and G_rep are locally integrable with respect to canonical Lebesgue volume.\n(3) For every C1 compactly supported psi:E->R and every direction v in E, both x->psi(x)*<G_rep(x),v>_E and x->u_rep(x)*(fderiv R psi x v) are Lebesgue integrable, and\n  integral psi(x)*<G_rep(x),v>_E dx = - integral u_rep(x)*(fderiv R psi x v) dx.\n(4) x->||r(x)||^2 is locally Lebesgue integrable, with the real scalar norm in this squared expression.\n(5) For every compact set K contained in E, u_rep, G_rep, and r each satisfy MemLp with exponent 2 and the actual restricted measure volume.restrict K. Each assertion includes a.e. strong measurability and finite L2 norm with respect to that restricted measure.\n(6) For every C2 compactly supported phi:E->R, both x->u_rep(x)*Laplacian.laplacian(phi)(x) and x->r(x)*phi(x) are Lebesgue integrable, and\n  integral u_rep(x)*Laplacian.laplacian(phi)(x) dx = integral r(x)*phi(x) dx.\nHere the test-function Laplacian is the actual finite-basis trace of its derivative. The distribution equation tests u_rep against this C2 test Laplacian; it makes no pointwise total-Laplacian assertion about u_rep. The Hilbert variational inner products use mu, while all local integrability and distribution integrals use canonical Lebesgue volume. One u and its actual output G serve all variational v, all C1 directional tests, every compact K, and all C2 distribution tests.\nThe operator D is quantified before epsilon and f and works for all their permitted values. Its density or existence is not an additional premise or an existential conclusion of this universal implication. The exact forcing expression is epsilon*u_rep+<gradient(W),G_rep>-f_rep. There is no mean-zero restriction on f, no C2 assumption on W, and no classical differentiability or H2 regularity conclusion for u_rep. The statement asserts existence of such u, without a uniqueness, quantitative bound, or jointly measurable selection conclusion.",
