    {
      "id": "semantic-audit:ASTIS-RT-20261006-PBPSConditionalC1Resolvent",
      "kind": "semantic-audit",
      "label": "ASTIS-RT-20261006-PBPSConditionalC1Resolvent",
      "status": "fidelity-exact",
      "subtitle": "companion:pbps-conditional-c1-resolvent · equivalent-after-elaboration",
      "summary": "Blind reconstruction and seven-slot semantic comparison for AutoSamplingTheory.ExampleCases.ProximalBPS.ConditionalC1Resolvent.conditional_c1_resolvent.",
      "column": 7,
      "url": "lean-foundations.html?view=semantic",
      "source_url": "https://arxiv.org/html/2609.06905v1#A3.SS1",
      "original_theorem": "Explicit analytic prerequisite restatement:\nE has the same full-space finite-dimensional real Hilbert/Borel structure, including dimension zero. V is C2 with true all-point/all-direction alpha I<=Hess V<=beta I, alpha,beta NNReal, 0<alpha<=beta, eta>0 and beta eta<=1.\nThe original Gibbs law, genuine independent Gaussian augmentation, selected R,S kernels, reflected W_y, finite positive partition and fixed original gradient D per fiber are derived by the existing actual conditional producer. Neither normalization nor a convenient replacement operator is supplied.\nThe potential is reflected, with quarter Hessian scaling. The unreflected SPHMC RGO potential/law is different. Existence is fiberwise for every y; no joint measurable resolvent selection, unbounded cost transfer or macroscopic H1/Poincare theorem is asserted.\nFor actual C2 V with 0<alpha<=beta global Hessian bounds, eta>0 and beta eta<=1, construct the actual Gibbs-Gaussian augmentation and common Markov conditional kernels R,S, with disintegration R and S_y=(R_y)#(x->2x-y). S_y is the true normalized reflected Gibbs law for W_y(u)=V((y+u)/2)+norm(u-y)^2/(8eta), with C2 regularity and positive integrable weight. For every y, retain one dense closable exact compact-smooth gradient D, fixed before epsilon/input, and derive every positive-epsilon L2 forcing weak solution, ordinary weak gradient/local integrability and all C1 compact weighted volume test equations. No measurable solution selection in y is claimed.\nSame actual compact-gradient graph closure admits C1 compact function/gradient pairs; actual positive-epsilon weak resolvent has all C1 compact weighted volume tests and genuine ordinary weak gradient. Actual PBPS reflected conditional R/S law and original fixed D precede epsilon/input. No local H2, global operator-core/Bochner, conditional Poincare/BL, centered epsilon limit, noncompact constant/linear domains, measurable y-selection, SPHMC Hessian lower/full4.1, PBPS hypocoercivity or either complete main/composition. TV never transfers unbounded expected cost.",
      "reconstructed_theorem": "Let E be a finite-dimensional real inner-product space, equipped with its Borel measurable space and canonical Lebesgue measure dx; dimension zero is allowed. Let V:E->R and alpha,beta be nonnegative real scalars and eta a real scalar. Assume alpha>0, alpha<=beta, V is C2, and, for every x,v in E,\n  alpha*||v||^2 <= (fderiv R (fderiv R V) x v) v <= beta*||v||^2.\nAssume eta>0 and beta*eta<=1. Define\n  mu = volume.tilted(x -> -V(x)),\n  J = map((x,g) -> (x,x+sqrt(eta)*g), mu.prod(stdGaussian E)),\n  W_y(z) = V((y+z)/2) + ||z-y||^2/(8*eta).\nHere volume.tilted(-F) is the volume measure with density exp(-F)/integral exp(-F); the Gaussian/product/pushforward are the actual measures.\nThere exist kernels R,S:E->Measure E with all the following properties: R and S are Markov kernels; R is a conditional kernel for J.map Prod.swap, disintegrating the swapped joint (Y,X); and for every y in E, S(y) is the pushforward of R(y) under x -> 2*x-y.\nFor every y in E, S(y)=volume.tilted(z -> -W_y(z)), W_y is C2, exp(-W_y) is Lebesgue integrable, and its Lebesgue integral is strictly positive. Moreover, for this y there exists a real-linear partial operator\n  D:L2(S(y);R) -> L2(S(y);E)\nwhose domain is dense in L2(S(y);R), which is closable and whose closure is closed, and whose graph is characterized exactly as follows: for every a in L2(S(y);R) and H in L2(S(y);E),\n  (a,H) belongs to graph(D) iff there exists phi:E->R that is smooth and has compact support, with a=phi S(y)-almost everywhere and H=gradient(phi) S(y)-almost everywhere.\nFor this same D, for every real epsilon>0 and every f in L2(S(y);R), there exists u in domain(D.closure) satisfying all the following conclusions simultaneously. Write u_rep and f_rep for the usual representatives of the scalar L2 classes, and G_rep for the usual representative of G=D.closure(u) in L2(S(y);E).\n(1) For every v in domain(D.closure),\n  epsilon*<u,v>_{L2(S(y);R)} + <D.closure(u),D.closure(v)>_{L2(S(y);E)} = <f,v>_{L2(S(y);R)}.\n(2) u_rep and G_rep are locally integrable with respect to Lebesgue volume.\n(3) For every C1 compactly supported psi:E->R and every direction v in E, both x -> psi(x)*<G_rep(x),v>_E and x -> u_rep(x)*(fderiv R psi x v) are Lebesgue integrable and\n  integral psi(x)*<G_rep(x),v>_E dx = - integral u_rep(x)*(fderiv R psi x v) dx.\n(4) For every C1 compactly supported psi:E->R, all three functions\n  x -> exp(-W_y(x))*(u_rep(x)*psi(x)),\n  x -> exp(-W_y(x))*<G_rep(x),gradient(psi)(x)>_E,\n  x -> exp(-W_y(x))*(f_rep(x)*psi(x))\nare Lebesgue integrable, and\n  epsilon*integral exp(-W_y(x))*(u_rep(x)*psi(x)) dx\n  + integral exp(-W_y(x))*<G_rep(x),gradient(psi)(x)>_E dx\n  = integral exp(-W_y(x))*(f_rep(x)*psi(x)) dx.\nOne pair of kernels R,S works for all y. For each y one D is chosen before epsilon and f, and works for all of them. For each epsilon and f the same u serves every variational test v, every directional weak-gradient test, and every weighted test psi. The Hilbert inner products use S(y), whereas all local-integrability assertions, directional weak-gradient integrals, partition integrals, and the final weighted test integrals use Lebesgue volume. The displayed weighted equation uses exp(-W_y) without dividing by its partition integral. The conclusions about S(y), W_y, and D hold for every y, not merely almost every y; the conditional-kernel conclusion itself has its usual disintegration meaning.",
      "fidelity_verdict": "equivalent-after-elaboration",
