      "url": "lean-foundations.html?view=semantic",
      "source_url": "https://arxiv.org/html/2609.06905v1#A3.SS1",
      "original_theorem": "Explicit analytic prerequisite restatement:\nE has the same finite real Hilbert/Borel/full-space structure including dimension zero. Source V is C2 with all-point/all-direction alpha I<=Hess V<=beta I, alpha,beta NNReal, 0<alpha<=beta, eta>0 and beta eta<=1.\nActual common Gibbs/Gaussian augmentation J and Markov disintegrating R/S are constructed. S_y=(2x-y)#R_y is precisely volume.tilted(-W_y), W_y(z)=V((y+z)/2)+norm(z-y)^2/(8eta), with true C2 regularity and positive integrable partition.\nFor every y one original dense closable compact-smooth gradient D_y with closed closure is fixed before epsilon/input. For each epsilon>0 and true f in L2(S_y), one u/G/r serves all compact sets, ordinary tests and all C2 compact cutoffs. Existence is fiberwise; no jointly measurable solution selector, RGO operator identity or localized weighted-domain membership is concluded.\nFrom actual source V/alpha/beta/eta construct true common augmentation R/S and reflected law, and for each y the original dense closable D_y before epsilon/forcing. Construct one actual u before all cutoffs in that same closure domain. Retain the actual all-domain variational equation, ordinary locally integrable u/G, all C1 compact directional weak-gradient tests, squared local integrability of r=epsilon*u+inner(grad W_y,G)-f, compact-volume L2 of u/G/r and every C2 original distribution test. For every C2 compact chi define v=chi*u, Gchi=chi*G+u*grad chi, Fchi=chi*r+2 inner(grad chi,G)+u Delta chi. Prove genuine global-volume L2 of v/Gchi/Fchi and each topological support contained in tsupport chi. For every C1 compact psi/direction a both weak-gradient products are L1 with integral psi inner(Gchi,a)=-integral v Dpsi[a]. For every C2 compact phi both PDE products are L1 with integral v Delta phi=integral Fchi phi. The same u is chosen before all cutoffs; only smooth test/cutoff functions have classical derivatives. Reflected and unreflected RGO laws/operators/scales remain distinct.\nSame actual original D/u/G/r, one u before all C2 compact chi: derive actual compact support/global volume L2 of chi*u, chi*G+u*gradchi, chi*r+2<gradchi,G>+u*Delta chi, all C1 compact weak-gradient and C2 compact distribution PDE with every L1 term legal. No localized weighted closure-domain membership, H2, Schwartz/tempered adapter, global core/Bochner, BL/Poincare, epsilon0, noncompact domain, measurable y-selector or either main/composition. Reflected/RGO law/scale remain distinct; TV never transfers unbounded expected cost.",
      "reconstructed_theorem": "Let E be a finite-dimensional real inner-product space with NormedAddCommGroup, InnerProductSpace R, FiniteDimensional R, MeasurableSpace and BorelSpace instances and canonical Lebesgue volume; dimension zero is allowed. Let V:E->R, alpha,beta:NNReal and eta:R. Assume alpha cast to R is strictly positive, alpha<=beta, V is C2, and for every x,a in E,\n  alpha*||a||^2 <= (fderiv R (fderiv R V) x a) a <= beta*||a||^2.\nAssume eta>0 and beta*eta<=1. Define actual measures and potential by\n  mu=volume.tilted(x->-V(x)),\n  J=map((x,g)->(x,x+sqrt(eta)*g),mu.prod(stdGaussian E)),\n  W_y(z)=V((y+z)/2)+||z-y||^2/(8*eta).\nThe tilt of -F is exp(-F)/integral exp(-F) times volume; positive normalization is not built into the definition. The Gaussian is the actual standard real Hilbert Gaussian and the product and pushforward are actual independent product and map.\nThere exist kernels R,S:E->Measure E such that R and S are Markov kernels, R disintegrates the swapped actual joint J.map Prod.swap, and for every y in E, S(y)=(R(y)).map(x->2*x-y).\nFor every y in E, S(y)=volume.tilted(z->-W_y(z)), W_y is C2, exp(-W_y) is Lebesgue integrable, and its Lebesgue integral is strictly positive. For this y there exists a real-linear partial operator D:L2(S(y);R)->L2(S(y);E) with dense domain in scalar L2(S(y)), D closable and D.closure closed, and with the exact graph characterization: for every scalar class a and vector class H, (a,H) belongs to graph(D) iff there exists a smooth compactly supported phi:E->R with a=phi S(y)-almost everywhere and H=gradient(phi) S(y)-almost everywhere.\nFor each y this same D is chosen before epsilon and f.\nFor every real epsilon>0 and every f in L2(S(y);R), there exists u in D.closure.domain satisfying all conclusions below simultaneously. Write u_rep and f_rep for the standard measurable representatives of the actual scalar L2 classes, and G_rep for the standard representative of G=D.closure(u) in L2(S(y);E). Abbreviate the exact scalar expression by\n  r(x)=epsilon*u_rep(x)+<gradient(W_y)(x),G_rep(x)>_E-f_rep(x).\n(a) For every z in D.closure.domain,\n  epsilon*<u,z>_{L2(S(y);R)}+<D.closure(u),D.closure(z)>_{L2(S(y);E)}=<f,z>_{L2(S(y);R)}.\n(b) u_rep and G_rep are locally integrable with respect to Lebesgue volume.\n(c) For every C1 compactly supported psi:E->R and every direction a in E, both x->psi(x)*<G_rep(x),a>_E and x->u_rep(x)*(fderiv R psi x a) are Lebesgue integrable, and\n  integral psi(x)*<G_rep(x),a>_E dx = - integral u_rep(x)*(fderiv R psi x a) dx.\n(d) The real scalar function x->||r(x)||^2 is locally Lebesgue integrable.\n(e) For every compact set K in E, u_rep, G_rep and r each satisfy MemLp at exponent 2 with respect to volume.restrict K, including a.e. strong measurability and finite L2 norm for that actual restricted measure.\n(f) For every C2 compactly supported phi:E->R, the two functions x->u_rep(x)*Laplacian.laplacian(phi)(x) and x->r(x)*phi(x) are Lebesgue integrable and\n  integral u_rep(x)*Laplacian.laplacian(phi)(x) dx = integral r(x)*phi(x) dx.\n(g) For every C2 compactly supported chi:E->R, define the following actual functions, with the notation v_chi corresponding to the let-defined v in the statement:\n  v_chi(x)=chi(x)*u_rep(x),\n  G_chi(x)=chi(x) scalar-multiplied by G_rep(x) + u_rep(x) scalar-multiplied by gradient(chi)(x),\n  F_chi(x)=chi(x)*r(x)+2*<gradient(chi)(x),G_rep(x)>_E+u_rep(x)*Laplacian.laplacian(chi)(x).\nThen v_chi, G_chi and F_chi each satisfy MemLp at exponent 2 with respect to the full volume measure, with actual a.e. strong measurability and finite global L2 norm. All three topological supports are contained in the topological support of chi:\n  tsupport(v_chi) subset tsupport(chi),\n  tsupport(G_chi) subset tsupport(chi),\n  tsupport(F_chi) subset tsupport(chi).\nFor every C1 compactly supported psi:E->R and every a in E, both x->psi(x)*<G_chi(x),a>_E and x->v_chi(x)*(fderiv R psi x a) are Lebesgue integrable, and\n  integral psi(x)*<G_chi(x),a>_E dx = - integral v_chi(x)*(fderiv R psi x a) dx.\nFor every C2 compactly supported phi:E->R, both x->v_chi(x)*Laplacian.laplacian(phi)(x) and x->F_chi(x)*phi(x) are Lebesgue integrable, and\n  integral v_chi(x)*Laplacian.laplacian(phi)(x) dx = integral F_chi(x)*phi(x) dx.\nAll unannotated local-integrability, integrability and integral assertions use canonical Lebesgue volume. The Hilbert inner products use the actual law S(y). Laplacian.laplacian on chi and phi is the actual finite-basis test-function trace; no classical Laplacian of the arbitrary representative u_rep or v_chi is asserted. The same u and G serve every test and every cutoff. chi is any real C2 compact-support function, without positivity, value-range or plateau assumptions. The localized functions are given by these three formulas, not independent existential choices; no membership of v_chi in a weighted partial-operator domain is asserted.\nOne R,S pair works for all y, and every y has the potential/density identity and a suitable D; these conclusions are not limited to almost every y. Conditional disintegration has its usual scope and does not assert this for every possible version of R on exceptional y. D is selected fiberwise once and works for all epsilon>0 and all scalar L2 forcing f; u is chosen before every cutoff chi. There is no joint measurable y-dependent D or u selection, uniqueness, H2 or classical-Laplacian conclusion, core characterization, invariance/iteration or quantitative bound.",
