      "source_url": "https://arxiv.org/html/2609.06905v1#A3.SS1",
      "original_theorem": "Explicit analytic prerequisite restatement:\nE has the same real finite-dimensional Hilbert/Borel/full-space structure, including dimension zero. V is C2 with all-point/all-direction alpha I<=Hess V<=beta I, alpha,beta NNReal, 0<alpha<=beta; eta>0 and beta eta<=1.\nThe Gibbs/Gaussian augmentation, common Markov R/S, disintegration R, reflection S_y=(2x-y)#R_y, and true reflected W_y(u)=V((y+u)/2)+norm(u-y)^2/(8eta) are derived. Each selected S_y is precisely volume.tilted(-W_y), with positive integrable partition and C2 W_y.\nFor every y there is one original dense closable exact compact smooth gradient D_y with closed closure, fixed before epsilon and forcing. Every epsilon>0 and genuine f in L2(S_y) has one u/G serving all ordinary tests and compact sets. Existence is fiberwise; no joint measurable solution selection in y is concluded.\nFor actual source V/alpha/beta/eta, construct genuine common augmentation R/S and exact reflected conditional laws. For every y, retain the true positive integrable C2 W_y and original dense closable compact smooth gradient D_y before epsilon/input. Every epsilon>0 and actual f in L2(S_y) has u in that same closure domain, the full variational and ordinary weak-gradient identities, genuine local volume L2 of u/G/k_y, locally integrable squared norm of k_y, and for all C2 compact phi the legal ordinary volume distribution identity integral u Delta phi = integral k_y phi, where k_y=epsilon u+inner(gradient W_y,G)-f. Reflected/RGO laws and scales remain distinct.\nSame actual D/u/G and forcing yield ordinary volume distribution Delta u=epsilon u+inner(gradient W,G)-f, all C2 compact tests with genuine L1 products, and genuine local L2 of u/G/right-hand side. Full existing variational and ordinary weak-gradient identities retained. Actual PBPS R/S/reflected law and fixed fiber D before epsilon/input. No local H2, global generator core/Bochner, Poincare/BL, centered epsilon limit, noncompact constant/linear domains, measurable y-selection, SPHMC Hessian lower/full4.1 or complete paper/main/composition. TV never transfers unbounded expected cost.",
      "reconstructed_theorem": "Let E be a finite-dimensional real inner-product space with NormedAddCommGroup, InnerProductSpace R, MeasurableSpace and BorelSpace instances and canonical Lebesgue volume; dimension zero is allowed. Let V:E->R, alpha,beta:NNReal and eta:R. Assume alpha cast to R is strictly positive, alpha<=beta, V is C2, and for every x,v in E,\n  alpha*||v||^2 <= (fderiv R (fderiv R V) x v) v <= beta*||v||^2.\nAssume eta>0 and beta*eta<=1. Define the actual measures and potential\n  mu=volume.tilted(x->-V(x)),\n  J=map((x,g)->(x,x+sqrt(eta)*g), mu.prod(stdGaussian E)),\n  W_y(z)=V((y+z)/2)+||z-y||^2/(8*eta).\nThe tilt of -F has density exp(-F)/integral exp(-F) against volume; nonzero normalization is not built into the definition. The Gaussian is the standard real Hilbert Gaussian, the product is independent, and map is actual pushforward.\nThere exist kernels R,S:E->Measure E such that R and S are Markov kernels, R is a conditional kernel disintegrating J.map Prod.swap, and for every y in E,\n  S(y)=(R(y)).map(x->2*x-y).\nFor every y in E, S(y)=volume.tilted(z->-W_y(z)), W_y is C2, exp(-W_y) is Lebesgue integrable, and its Lebesgue integral is strictly positive. For this y there exists a real-linear partial operator D:L2(S(y);R)->L2(S(y);E) with dense domain in the actual scalar L2 space, with D closable and D.closure closed, and with exactly this graph: for every a in scalar L2(S(y)) and H in vector L2(S(y)), (a,H) belongs to graph(D) if and only if there exists a smooth compactly supported phi:E->R with a=phi S(y)-almost everywhere and H=gradient(phi) S(y)-almost everywhere.\nFor every real epsilon>0 and every scalar L2 input f for this same D, there exists u in domain(D.closure) satisfying the following simultaneous conclusions. Write u_rep and f_rep for the standard measurable representatives of the actual scalar L2 classes, and G_rep for the standard representative of G=D.closure(u) in the actual vector L2 quotient. In the formulas below set r(x)=epsilon*u_rep(x)+<gradient(W_y)(x),G_rep(x)>_E-f_rep(x); this notation abbreviates the exact displayed expression.\n(1) For every v in domain(D.closure),\n  epsilon*<u,v>_{L2(S(y);R)}+<D.closure(u),D.closure(v)>_{L2(S(y);E)}=<f,v>_{L2(S(y);R)}.\n(2) u_rep and G_rep are locally integrable with respect to canonical Lebesgue volume.\n(3) For every C1 compactly supported psi:E->R and every direction v in E, both x->psi(x)*<G_rep(x),v>_E and x->u_rep(x)*(fderiv R psi x v) are Lebesgue integrable, and\n  integral psi(x)*<G_rep(x),v>_E dx = - integral u_rep(x)*(fderiv R psi x v) dx.\n(4) x->||r(x)||^2 is locally Lebesgue integrable, with the real scalar norm in this squared expression.\n(5) For every compact set K contained in E, u_rep, G_rep, and r each satisfy MemLp with exponent 2 and the actual restricted measure volume.restrict K. Each assertion includes a.e. strong measurability and finite L2 norm with respect to that restricted measure.\n(6) For every C2 compactly supported phi:E->R, both x->u_rep(x)*Laplacian.laplacian(phi)(x) and x->r(x)*phi(x) are Lebesgue integrable, and\n  integral u_rep(x)*Laplacian.laplacian(phi)(x) dx = integral r(x)*phi(x) dx.\nHere the test-function Laplacian is the actual finite-basis trace of its derivative. The distribution equation tests u_rep against this C2 test Laplacian; it makes no pointwise total-Laplacian assertion about u_rep. The Hilbert variational inner products use S(y), while all local integrability and distribution integrals use canonical Lebesgue volume. One u and its actual output G serve all variational v, all C1 directional tests, every compact K, and all C2 distribution tests.\nOne pair R,S works for every y. For each y one D is chosen before every positive epsilon and every L2 input f, and the same D works for all of them; it is not chosen anew for each epsilon or f. The density and operator conclusions hold for all y, not merely almost every y. The conditional-kernel property itself has the usual disintegration scope and does not make a claim about every possible version on exceptional y. The exact r is epsilon*u_rep+<gradient(W_y),G_rep>-f_rep. No classical Laplacian or H2 conclusion for u, core theorem, measurable y-dependent operator/solution selection, kernel invariance, or quantitative solution bound is asserted.",
