rdinary volume distribution delta u=epsilon u+inner(gradient w,g)-f, all c2 compact tests with genuine l1 products, and genuine local l2 of u/g/right-hand side. full existing variational and ordinary weak-gradient identities retained. actual pbps r/s/reflected law and fixed fiber d before epsilon/input. no local h2, global generator core/bochner, poincare/bl, centered epsilon limit, noncompact constant/linear domains, measurable y-selection, sphmc hessian lower/full4.1 or complete paper/main/composition. tv never transfers unbounded expected cost. let e be a finite-dimensional real inner-product space with normedaddcommgroup, innerproductspace r, measurablespace and borelspace instances and canonical lebesgue volume; dimension zero is allowed. let v:e->r, alpha,beta:nnreal and eta:r. assume alpha cast to r is strictly positive, alpha<=beta, v is c2, and for every x,v in e, alpha*||v||^2 <= (fderiv r (fderiv r v) x v) v <= beta*||v||^2. assume eta>0 and beta*eta<=1. define the actual measures and potential mu=volume.tilted(x->-v(x)), j=map((x,g)->(x,x+sqrt(eta)*g), mu.prod(stdgaussian e)), w_y(z)=v((y+z)/2)+||z-y||^2/(8*eta). the tilt of -f has density exp(-f)/integral exp(-f) against volume; nonzero normalization is not built into the definition. the gaussian is the standard real hilbert gaussian, the product is independent, and map is actual pushforward. there exist kernels r,s:e->measure e such that r and s are markov kernels, r is a conditional kernel disintegrating j.map prod.swap, and for every y in e, s(y)=(r(y)).map(x->2*x-y). for every y in e, s(y)=volume.tilted(z->-w_y(z)), w_y is c2, exp(-w_y) is lebesgue integrable, and its lebesgue integral is strictly positive. for this y there exists a real-linear partial operator d:l2(s(y);r)->l2(s(y);e) with dense domain in the actual scalar l2 space, with d closable and d.closure closed, and with exactly this graph: for every a in scalar l2(s(y)) and h in vector l2(s(y)), (a,h) belongs to graph(d) if and only if there exists a smooth compactly supported phi:e->r with a=phi s(y)-almost everywhere and h=gradient(phi) s(y)-almost everywhere. for every real epsilon>0 and every scalar l2 input f for this same d, there exists u in domain(d.closure) satisfying the following simultaneous conclusions. write u_rep and f_rep for the standard measurable representatives of the actual scalar l2 classes, and g_rep for the standard representative of g=d.closure(u) in the actual vector l2 quotient. in the formulas below set r(x)=epsilon*u_rep(x)+<gradient(w_y)(x),g_rep(x)>_e-f_rep(x); this notation abbreviates the exact displayed expression. (1) for every v in domain(d.closure), epsilon*<u,v>_{l2(s(y);r)}+<d.closure(u),d.closure(v)>_{l2(s(y);e)}=<f,v>_{l2(s(y);r)}. (2) u_rep and g_rep are locally integrable with respect to canonical lebesgue volume. (3) for every c1 compactly supported psi:e->r and every direction v in e, both x->psi(x)*<g_rep(x),v>_e and x->u_rep(x)*(fderiv r psi x v) are lebesgue integrable, and integral psi(x)*<g_rep(x),v>_e dx = - integral u_rep(x)*(fderiv r psi x v) dx. (4) x->||r(x)||^2 is locally lebesgue integrable, with the real scalar norm in this squared expression. (5) for every compact set k contained in e, u_rep, g_rep, and r each satisfy memlp with exponent 2 and the actual restricted measure volume.restrict k. each assertion includes a.e. strong measurability and finite l2 norm with respect to that restricted measure. (6) for every c2 compactly supported phi:e->r, both x->u_rep(x)*laplacian.laplacian(phi)(x) and x->r(x)*phi(x) are lebesgue integrable, and integral u_rep(x)*laplacian.laplacian(phi)(x) dx = integral r(x)*phi(x) dx. here the test-function laplacian is the actual finite-basis trace of its derivative. the distribution equation tests u_rep against this c2 test laplacian; it makes no pointwise total-laplacian assertion about u_rep. the hilbert variational inner products use s(y), while all local integrability and distribution integrals use canonical lebesgue volume. one u and its actual output g serve all variational v, all c1 directional tests, every compact k, and all c2 distribution tests. one pair r,s works for every y. for each y one d is chosen before every positive epsilon and every l2 input f, and the same d works for all of them; it is not chosen anew for each epsilon or f. the density and operator conclusions hold for all y, not merely almost every y. the conditional-kernel property itself has the usual disintegration scope and does not make a claim about every possible version on exceptional y. the exact r is epsilon*u_rep+<gradient(w_y),g_rep>-f_rep. no classical laplacian or h2 conclusion for u, core theorem, measurable y-dependent operator/solution selection, kernel invariance, or quantitative solution bound is asserted. accepted pbps arxiv:2609.06905v1 section2.2 equations2.6-2.14 actual reflected conditional law and appendixc.1 lemmab.1/c.1 omitted weighted-h1 analytic prerequisite. sphmc2609.06906v1 section4.1 bl dependency route only; the ordinary pde is not a quoted numbered source theorem. autosamplingtheory.examplecases.proximalbps.conditionalordinaryresolvent.conditional_ordinary_resolvent autosamplingtheory/examplecases/proximalbps/conditionalordinaryresolvent.lean true root-samplinglib-writer anonymous_decoder_20261006 8aa59139fb50d182ee1116e0745a9ef86c944412dab5bcbb8ac7b92094dc74f9 accepted phase_source_reviewer_20261005 objects actual gibbs/gaussian j, common markov r/s and reflected w_y, original dense exact d_y, actual ordinary weak solution. same actual measures/pushforwards/disintegration and normalized s_y; actual u/g and r_y. equivalent complete conditional proof preserves conditionalgradient r,s,d_y witnesses then applies shared producer via exact s_y law equality. domains finite hilbert/borel full space including0; true reflected conditional law, not original-coordinate unreflected rgo. l2(s_y) classes; all local/distribution integrals use volume. equivalent w_y(z)=v((y+z)/2)+no