      "url": "lean-foundations.html?view=semantic",
      "source_url": "https://arxiv.org/html/2609.06906v1#S4.SS2",
      "original_theorem": "Attributed scoped prerequisite/interface restatement:\nE is a normed additive Borel group with second-countable topology. These topological/algebraic conditions are exposed API requirements of the reused shared marginal interface, not hidden moment or optimizer assumptions. State and noise use this same space.\nPhi:E×E->E and the extended nonnegative cost c:E×E->ENNReal are jointly measurable. The noise Gamma and both input laws mu,nu are probability measures. 0<A<infinity in extended reals.\nFor every x,y,zeta, c(Phi(x,zeta),Phi(y,zeta))<=A c(x,y). This is the explicit general lifting premise; the actual source algorithm consumer proves it from its genuine normalized potential, not a supplied kernel certificate.\nThe true randomized output laws map(Phi,mu×Gamma) and map(Phi,nu×Gamma) obey T_c(output_mu,output_nu)<=A T_c(mu,nu). Here T_c is the raw Kantorovich infimum over actual couplings. Input/output costs may be infinite and no optimizer or finite moments are assumed.\nActual Gaussian exact-gradient numerical K at the explicit normalized global C2 curvature interface. Actual f=V_eta smoothing/C2/curvature remains separate. No target invariance/stationarity, numerical target bias, stochastic Khat/Kbar, local errors/history/expected query cost, Wp/proxy-warmness/initialization, PBPS or either complete main/composition result. TV proximity does not transfer unbounded cost.",
      "reconstructed_theorem": "Let E be a normed additive group with its Borel measurable space and second-countable topology. Fix a measurable map Phi:E x E->E,a measurable cost c:E x E->ENNReal,and an extended nonnegative factor A with A!=0 and A!=infinity. Assume the following inequality pointwise for every x,y,zeta in E:\n  c(Phi(x,zeta),Phi(y,zeta))<=A c(x,y).\nFor every three probability measures mu,nu,Gamma on E,let the two updated laws be the measurable pushforwards\n  muOut=(mu x Gamma)_*Phi,\n  nuOut=(nu x Gamma)_*Phi.\nThus in each law the input and noise are sampled independently,and both laws use the same noise law Gamma. Define the raw extended-nonnegative transport cost\n  T_c(alpha,beta)=inf{integral^- c dpi : pi is a measure on E x E,\n                     pi.fst=alpha and pi.snd=beta}.\nThe infimum is over the lower-integral values of all measures having the two specified marginals. Then\n  T_c(muOut,nuOut)<=A T_c(mu,nu).\nThe pointwise premise uses the same zeta on both compared outputs and holds for every noise value,not only almost everywhere. A is positive and finite in ENNReal but is not assumed less than one. The cost c may take infinite values and is not required to be a metric,quadratic cost or finite cost. Transport values and their products use extended-nonnegative conventions;there is no finite-cost replacement,optimal-coupling or attainment assumption. Probability normalization applies to all three laws. No Gaussian law,noise distribution beyond Gamma,iteration history,invariant law or root/power conversion is included.",
      "fidelity_verdict": "equivalent-after-elaboration",
      "blindness": "source hidden from decoder",
