      "summary": "Blind reconstruction and seven-slot semantic comparison for AutoSamplingTheory.ExampleCases.SmoothedPicardHMC.PhaseMetric.wassersteinDistance_sq_le_phaseWassersteinSq.",
      "column": 7,
      "url": "lean-foundations.html?view=semantic",
      "source_url": "https://arxiv.org/html/2609.06906v1",
      "original_theorem": "Attributed scoped source restatement:\nThe paper's exact twisted phase transport cost controls ordinary phase transport and therefore transfers reference position/momentum moments to an incoming law.\nReal inner-product phase space.\nkappa>=1.\nExplicit reference second moments for the final adapter.\nOne-way explicit comparison only; no D.3.",
      "reconstructed_theorem": "Let E be any real inner-product space with a measurable Borel structure and second-countable topology. For every real kappa>=1 and arbitrary measures mu,nu on phase space S=E x E,define\n  Q_kappa(x,p)=(1/(2kappa)+1/2)||x||^2+<x,p>+||p||^2,\n  C_kappa(s,sprime)=ENNReal.ofReal(Q_kappa(s-sprime)),\n  P_kappa(mu,nu)=inf_{pi coupling of mu,nu} integral^- C_kappa(s,sprime) dpi.\nCouplings are measures on S x S with first marginal mu and second marginal nu. Let the ordinary phase product norm be the default maximum norm,so for s=(x,p),sprime=(xprime,pprime),\n  dmax(s,sprime)=max(||x-xprime||,||p-pprime||).\nDefine the ordinary quadratic transport cost cmax(s,sprime)=ENNReal.ofReal(dmax(s,sprime)^2),and let\n  Wmax(mu,nu)=[inf_{pi coupling of mu,nu} integral^- cmax(s,sprime) dpi]^(1/2:R)\nin extended nonnegative reals. This is precisely WassersteinSpace.wassersteinDistance for the stated product norm. Then\n  Wmax(mu,nu)^2 <= ENNReal.ofReal(6) P_kappa(mu,nu).\nThe left side is the unrooted quadratic-cost coupling infimum,and P_kappa is phaseWassersteinSq,also unrooted,using the explicit tilted quadratic energy. The integral is the lower integral and the root is ENNReal real exponentiation. Both measures are arbitrary: no probability normalization,finite-moment,finite-mass or coupling-existence assumption is separately supplied. No completeness or finite-dimensionality assumption is stated. The theorem is one comparison of transport quantities,not a contraction theorem for any numerical map or a finiteness conclusion.",
