    {
      "id": "semantic-audit:ASTIS-RT-ANDI-CURRENT-FirstOrder",
      "kind": "semantic-audit",
      "label": "ASTIS-RT-ANDI-CURRENT-FirstOrder",
      "status": "fidelity-mismatch",
      "subtitle": "chewi-opt-v1-prop-1-6 · lean-weakened-conclusion",
      "summary": "Blind reconstruction and seven-slot semantic comparison for AutoSamplingTheory.TechnicalLemmas.Analysis.StrongConvexFirstOrder.firstOrder_lower_bound_of_strongConvexOn.",
      "column": 7,
      "url": "lean-foundations.html?view=semantic",
      "source_url": "",
      "original_theorem": "For a continuously differentiable real-valued function on Euclidean space and a nonnegative modulus, the chord inequality, quadratic lower model and gradient monotonicity inequality below are equivalent. Under twice continuous differentiability they are also equivalent to the Hessian bound.",
      "reconstructed_theorem": "Let E be any complete real inner-product space, s any subset of E, f : E → ℝ any real-valued function, m any real number, and grad : E → E any supplied vector field. Assume s is convex and f is m-strongly convex on s in the following precise sense: for every u,v ∈ s and every a,b ≥ 0 with a+b=1, f(a u+b v) ≤ a f(u)+b f(v)−a b (m/2) ‖u−v‖². Assume also that for every z ∈ s, f has an ambient Fréchet derivative at z represented by grad(z) under the real inner product. Then for all x,y ∈ s, f(y) ≥ f(x)+⟨grad(x),y−x⟩+(m/2)‖y−x‖². This is a one-way implication from strong convexity plus ambient differentiability to a quadratic lower support inequality. It asserts no converse, minimizer existence, or uniqueness. There is no requirement that m be positive, s be open, closed, bounded, or nonempty, or E be finite-dimensional. The gradient condition holds at every point of s and is ambient, including at boundary points; it is not just differentiability relative to s. No continuity or Lipschitz condition on grad is assumed. The conclusion concerns only points of s.",
