      "original_theorem": "For a continuously differentiable real-valued function on Euclidean space and a nonnegative modulus, the chord inequality, quadratic lower model and gradient monotonicity inequality below are equivalent. Under twice continuous differentiability they are also equivalent to the Hessian bound.",
      "reconstructed_theorem": "Let E be any complete real inner-product space, s any subset of E, f : E → ℝ any real-valued function, m any real number, and grad : E → E any supplied vector field. Assume s is convex and f is m-strongly convex on s in the following precise sense: for every u,v ∈ s and every a,b ≥ 0 with a+b=1, f(a u+b v) ≤ a f(u)+b f(v)−a b (m/2) ‖u−v‖². Assume also that for every z ∈ s, f has an ambient Fréchet derivative at z represented by grad(z) under the real inner product. Then for all x,y ∈ s, m‖y−x‖² ≤ ⟨grad(y)−grad(x),y−x⟩. This is a one-way implication from strong convexity plus ambient differentiability to the displayed gradient difference lower bound. When m>0 this gives strong monotonicity on s with constant m, and when m=0 ordinary monotonicity; for negative m the displayed inequality must not be read as ordinary monotonicity. It asserts no converse, minimizer existence, uniqueness, or Lipschitz bound for grad. There is no requirement that m be positive, s be open, closed, bounded, or nonempty, or E be finite-dimensional. The gradient condition holds at every point of s and is ambient, including at boundary points; it is not just differentiability relative to s. No continuity assumption on grad is imposed. The conclusion concerns only pairs in s.",
