      "source_url": "https://chewisinho.github.io/main.pdf",
      "original_theorem": "Let B be a real pre-Brownian process under a probability measure and let T≥0. For each level m, partition [0,T] into 2^m equal deterministic cells of length T/2^m and let Q_m be the sum over the cells of the squared Brownian increment minus the cell length. Then E[Q_m^2] tends to zero as m tends to infinity. The conclusion is scalar mean-square convergence on these canonical dyadic grids; it does not assert almost-sure or ucp convergence, predictable-weight estimates, vector cross-variation, stochastic integration, or Ito's formula.",
      "reconstructed_theorem": "Let Ω be a measurable space, let μ be a measure on Ω, and let B assign to each nonnegative real time a real-valued random variable on Ω. Assume IsPreBrownianReal B μ, and fix T ∈ ℝ≥0. At level ℓ ∈ ℕ, partition the interval from 0 to T into 2^ℓ equal cells, using the regular grid with mesh T / 2^ℓ, and form the quadratic-variation error by summing, over all cells, each squared increment of B minus that cell's length. Then the μ-integral of the square of this full error converges to 0 as ℓ tends to infinity: lim_{ℓ→∞} ∫ ω, (quadraticVariationError B (regularGridTimes (dyadicMesh T ℓ) (2^ℓ)) ω)² ∂μ = 0.",
