      "original_theorem": "Let B be a real pre-Brownian process under a probability measure and let t₀ ≤ ⋯ ≤ tₙ be a deterministic finite grid in nonnegative time. Define Q = ∑ᵢ ((B(tᵢ₊₁)-B(tᵢ))²-(tᵢ₊₁-tᵢ)). The centered Gaussian increment moments and independence of disjoint grid increments give E[Q²] = 2 ∑ᵢ (tᵢ₊₁-tᵢ)². Repeated grid points and an empty grid are allowed. This finite-grid identity alone does not assert convergence along refining grids.",
      "reconstructed_theorem": "Let Ω be a measurable space, let μ be a measure on Ω, and let B assign to every nonnegative real time a real-valued random variable on Ω. Assume that B satisfies IsPreBrownianReal B μ. For every n ∈ ℕ and every monotone map times : Fin (n + 1) → ℝ≥0 (with repeated grid points allowed), write Δtᵢ = times(i.succ) − times(i.castSucc) in ℝ≥0 and ΔBᵢ(ω) = B(times(i.succ), ω) − B(times(i.castSucc), ω). If Q(ω) = ∑ i : Fin n, ((ΔBᵢ(ω))² − (Δtᵢ : ℝ)), then ∫ ω, Q(ω)² ∂μ = 2 · ∑ i : Fin n, ((Δtᵢ : ℝ))².",
