    {
      "id": "semantic-audit:ASTIS-RT-20261003-unit-sphere-unit-sphere-geodesic-derivatives-and-bounds",
      "kind": "semantic-audit",
      "label": "ASTIS-RT-20261003-unit-sphere-unit-sphere-geodesic-derivatives-and-bounds",
      "status": "fidelity-exact",
      "subtitle": "unit-sphere-explicit-geodesics:unitSphereGeodesic_derivatives_and_bounds · exact",
      "summary": "Blind reconstruction and seven-slot semantic comparison for AutoSamplingTheory.TechnicalLemmas.Geometry.UnitSphere.unitSphereGeodesic_derivatives_and_bounds.",
      "column": 7,
      "url": "lean-foundations.html?view=semantic",
      "source_url": "",
      "original_theorem": "At a unit base point and nonzero velocity, the first four derivatives have explicit formulas and satisfy ‖γ^(q)(t)‖≤2‖z‖^q for q=1,2,3,4. Formula: \\|\\gamma_{w,z}^{(q)}(t)\\|\\le2\\|z\\|^q\\quad(q=1,2,3,4) Assumptions: E is any real inner-product space. ‖w‖=1. z≠0 selects the displayed closed form. No finite-dimensional or probabilistic hypothesis is used. Boundary: The constant 2 is a simple uniform envelope, not an optimality claim.",
      "reconstructed_theorem": "Let w,z lie in a real inner-product space E with ‖w‖ = 1 and z ≠ 0, put r = ‖z‖, and fix t ∈ ℝ. For γ(s) = γ_{w,z}(s), its first four successive derivative vectors at t are v₁(t) = -(r sin(tr))·w + cos(tr)·z, v₂(t) = -(r² cos(tr))·w -(r sin(tr))·z, v₃(t) = (r³ sin(tr))·w -(r² cos(tr))·z, and v₄(t) = (r⁴ cos(tr))·w + (r³ sin(tr))·z. More precisely, γ has derivative v₁ at t, the curve v₁ has derivative v₂ at t, the curve v₂ has derivative v₃ at t, and the curve v₃ has derivative v₄ at t. These vectors satisfy ‖v₁(t)‖ ≤ 2r, ‖v₂(t)‖ ≤ 2r², ‖v₃(t)‖ ≤ 2r³, and ‖v₄(t)‖ ≤ 2r⁴.",
      "fidelity_verdict": "exact",
      "blindness": "source hidden from decoder",
      "semantic_slots": [
        {
          "slot": "objects",
          "original": "At a unit base point and nonzero velocity, the first four derivatives have explicit formulas and satisfy ‖γ^(q)(t)‖≤2‖z‖^q for q=1,2,3,4.",
          "reconstructed": "Let w,z lie in a real inner-product space E with ‖w‖ = 1 and z ≠ 0, put r = ‖z‖, and fix t ∈ ℝ. For γ(s) = γ_{w,z}(s), its first four successive derivative vectors at t are v₁(t) = -(r sin(tr))·w + cos(tr)·z, v₂(t) = -(r² cos(tr))·w -(r sin(tr))·z, v₃(t) = (r³ sin(tr))·w -(r² cos(tr))·z, and v₄(t) = (r⁴ cos(tr))·w + (r³ sin(tr))·z. More precisely, γ has derivative v₁ at t, the curve v₁ has derivative v₂ at t, the curve v₂ has derivative v₃ at t, and the curve v₃ has derivative v₄ at t. These vectors satisfy ‖v₁(t)‖ ≤ 2r, ‖v₂(t)‖ ≤ 2r², ‖v₃(t)‖ ≤ 2r³, and ‖v₄(t)‖ ≤ 2r⁴.",
          "relation": "equivalent",
          "evidence": "The blind text names and expands the same target objects as AutoSamplingTheory.TechnicalLemmas.Geometry.UnitSphere.unitSphereGeodesic_derivatives_and_bounds; the lesson title is “A fourth-order certificate for sphere geodesics”."
        },
        {
